window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"p":"Home","t":"Home","u":"index.html","x":"What you need to be useful on the vascular service: the list and the pager, the operating room and the CTV ICU, the anatomy you will be asked and the notes you will write. Written for residents first, and for every sub-intern and student who joins them."},{"p":"Home","t":"Start where you are","u":"index.html#start","x":"Start where you are Pick your role for a reading order that fits it. Everything else can wait until a case or a page makes it relevant. Intern or resident Sub-intern Third-year student Intern or resident You will carry the list, answer the pager and write most of the notes. Read the first four before your first call night, then keep the rest open on your phone. Your first day PowerChart access, the pager, sign-out times and who to call for what. The week before. History, exam and charting Copy-ready consult, brief op, post-op check, progress and discharge notes, ready to save as PowerChart Auto Text. On call and cross-cover The overnight calls that come up again and again, the first moves for each, and when to wake your senior. Before your first night. The CTV ICU Who goes there, the OR-to-ICU handoff, admission orders, CSF drains, and what to watch after each operation. Rounds and presenting The numbers the team wants every morning and how they want them presented. Wires, catheters and devices So that “give me a Bentson” or “go up and over with an Omni” means something the first time you hear it. Before your first angio day. Quick reference Classifications, thresholds and reversal agents on one screen. Keep it open. Sub-intern You will carry patients, present them like an intern and take some first calls with backup. Everything you practice here is what intern year asks of you in July. Your first day Badge, scrubs, PowerChart and where the team meets. The week before. Rounds and presenting Pre-rounding, the vascular numbers, and a presentation that ends on a plan. History, exam and charting The focused vascular exam and the notes you will be writing, with templates. Presentations PAD, CLTI, acute limb ischemia, aneurysm, carotid, dissection, mesenteric and venous disease. First week. Open operations Steps, consent risks and what to watch after each case. The night before you scrub. On call and cross-cover How residents triage the pager overnight. Learn it now and your first call night as an intern is far less frightening. Questions you'll be asked Answer out loud, then reveal. Ten minutes before rounds. Third-year student You will follow a few patients closely, pre-round, present and scrub. Aim to be useful and to learn the logic of the service; the habits you build here (a specific pulse exam, a note that is true today, a plan you commit to) carry straight into residency. Your first day The logistics that decide whether your first morning goes smoothly. The week before. Rounds and presenting How to pre-round, what to gather, and how to present a vascular patient. Foundations Anatomy, the pulse exam, the ABI and the anticoagulants. First week. Presentations The diseases behind the consult pager. Open operations Read the case the night before and name the structures in the wound. Before each case. Endovascular Access, angiography and radiation safety, so the angio suite makes sense. Questions you'll be asked The classic teaching questions with the answers attendings want."},{"p":"Home","t":"What the guide saves you","u":"index.html#why","x":"Built to make the service easier to run Most of what goes wrong for a new resident on vascular is predictable: a stale pulse exam, a groin bleed nobody expected, an ICU handoff missing the clamp time. These pages are organized around those moments. Fewer 2 a.m. guesses First moves for the lost pulse, the groin hematoma, the swollen neck and the low urine output, and a clear list of when to call. On call and cross-cover Notes in minutes Consult, brief op, post-op check, progress and discharge templates you can paste into PowerChart Auto Text once and reuse all rotation. Charting Clean ICU handoffs The items the CTV ICU team will ask for, in the order they ask, plus targets for blood pressure, MAP and CSF drains. The handoff Devices by name What a Rosen, a KMP, an Omni Flush or a ProGlide is, so you can hand the right thing over and follow the case. Devices Answers on rounds Classifications, thresholds and reversal agents on one page, and the teaching questions as self-tests. Quick reference Search every page Press / anywhere, or tap the search icon, and type HIT, Rutherford, endoleak or Bentson."},{"p":"Home","t":"Contents","u":"index.html#contents","x":"Contents Start here 1 Your first day Badge, scrubs, PowerChart, parking, the team, and what to carry. 2 Rounds and presenting Pre-rounding, the numbers a vascular team wants, presenting, and the operating room. Running the service 3 History, exam and charting New The vascular history by complaint, the focused exam, and copy-ready notes. 4 The CTV ICU New Admission, the OR-to-ICU handoff, orders, CSF drains, and what to watch after each operation. 5 On call and cross-cover New Pager triage, sign-out, and first moves for the calls that come up again and again. The knowledge 6 Foundations Anatomy, hemodynamics, the exam, noninvasive testing, imaging, anticoagulation, and cardiac risk. 7 Presentations PAD, CLTI, acute limb ischemia, aneurysm, carotid, dissection, mesenteric, and venous disease. 8 Open operations Steps, consent risks, and what to watch afterwards for every major open case. 9 Endovascular Access, angiography, radiation and contrast, stenting, EVAR and TEVAR, and clot management. 10 Wires, catheters and devices New Sheaths, wires, catheters, balloons, stents, grafts and closure devices, by name. Sharpen up 11 Questions you'll be asked The classic teaching questions, as self-tests. 12 Quick reference Every table, classification, and number worth memorizing."},{"p":"Home","t":"The vascular mindset","u":"index.html#mindset","x":"The vascular mindset Before the facts, the frame. Vascular is a small, intense service with a logic of its own. Get the logic and the facts hang on it. Everything reduces to inflow, outflow and conduit. A leg, a graft, a bypass, a fistula, all of it. Is enough blood arriving (inflow), is there a healthy bed for it to drain into (outflow, or runoff), and is the channel between the two intact (conduit)? When a graft clots or a wound will not heal, the question is always which of the three failed. The vascular patient is a cardiac patient who happens to have a leg or carotid problem. Atherosclerosis is systemic. The person with claudication almost certainly has coronary disease too, which is why the leading cause of death after nearly every vascular operation is myocardial infarction rather than anything in the operated territory. Optimize the heart, and treat the limb in that context. Time is tissue. Acute limb ischemia, a ruptured aneurysm, acute mesenteric ischemia, a cold mottled foot after a catheterization: these are clocks running against muscle, bowel and brain. The reflex is to anticoagulate early, image fast, and call for help before the window closes. Pearl When you present a patient on this service, lead with the vascular bed, the symptom severity, the pulse exam and the hemodynamics (ABI or duplex). An attending wants the limb characterized before the social history. Rounds and presenting turns this into a script."},{"p":"Home","t":"How to read the guide","u":"index.html#reading","x":"How to read the guide Each section heading carries a short colored bar that tells you which vascular bed it is about. Arterial Venous Both General or logistics The boxes recur on every page, and each one means something specific. Red flag A do-not-miss finding, an emergency, or a classic trap. These are the things that hurt patients. Pearl A clinical or operative point that separates understanding the disease from memorizing a list. Resident tip A specific habit that saves time or prevents an error when you are the one carrying the pager. Confirm locally Practice that varies between hospitals and services, such as order sets, drain protocols or who writes which orders. Ask your senior or the unit. Mnemonic A memory hook. Keep the ones that stick. Questions look like this. Try to answer before you open one. The answer is the one an attending wants to hear, usually followed by the reason. If you can answer these cold, you are ahead of most rotators."},{"p":"Home","t":"About this guide","u":"index.html#about","x":"About this guide Read me first This is an educational orientation and study aid for trainees rotating on vascular surgery. It is not a clinical protocol and it is not hospital policy. Thresholds, classifications and trial data drift over time and vary by institution, so check specifics against current society guidelines (SVS, ESVS), your attendings, and local pathways before applying anything to a patient. Logistics such as access and onboarding change often, so confirm day-one details with your clerkship or program coordinator. Compiled by Hashim Salar A medical trainee from the Aga Khan University with an interest in vascular surgery, who put this guide together to make the first weeks on the service less disorienting for whoever comes next."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"Your first day","u":"first-day.html","x":"The unglamorous logistics that decide whether your first morning goes smoothly: badge, scrubs, PowerChart, parking, and finding the team before rounds. Sort these out before day one and you can spend your attention on patients instead of paperwork."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"Before day one","u":"first-day.html#before","x":"Before day one A short email a week or two ahead saves you a frantic first morning. The goal is to arrive already credentialed, already able to log in, and already knowing where to stand. Email your coordinator or the chief resident and ask the four questions that matter: what time and where the team meets in the morning, how you will get a badge and scrubs, whether your PowerChart access is set up, and who your direct contact is for the first day. Everything else can be sorted once you are there. Pearl Visiting and away students usually have onboarding paperwork (background check, immunization records, HIPAA and EHR training, N95 fit testing) that must be finished days ahead before a badge or a PowerChart login will work. Start it the moment you are accepted, not the week you arrive."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"The day-one checklist","u":"first-day.html#checklist","x":"The day-one checklist Seven things to have working before rounds on your first morning. Most can be done the day before if you arrive early. Get your photo ID badge. Badging or ID office Your hospital badge is also your door access, and at many sites it unlocks the scrub dispenser and parking gates too. Wear it where it is visible. If a door will not open on day one, your access for that unit may not be active yet, so flag it early. Confirm your PowerChart login. IT service desk, 505-272-3282 The hospital runs on Cerner PowerChart. Log in once before day one so you are not locked out during rounds. If your username or password does not work, or access has not been provisioned, call the service desk. This is the single most common day-one snag. Find out how you draw scrubs. OR locker rooms Most ORs use a badge-linked dispenser that issues a set and credits you when you return the used ones. Learn where the locker rooms are, how many sets you are allowed, and where dirty scrubs go, before you need to change in a hurry. Sort parking or transit. Parking and Transportation Services The hospital sits on the university's north campus and parking is tight. Arrange a permit or a transit plan in advance, and build in extra time on day one regardless. Set up contact with the team. Ask your senior Find out how the team communicates: pager, secure messaging inside PowerChart, or the senior resident's phone. Give them your number too. Being reachable and responsive is half of being useful as a junior. Clear OR and isolation access. Student or employee health If you will scrub or see patients in airborne isolation, make sure N95 fit testing and OR onboarding are done. A respirator you were never fitted for is not protecting anyone. Confirm the schedule. Coordinator Know your rounding time, where to meet, and whether day one is an OR, clinic or consult day. Walking in knowing the plan signals that you take the rotation seriously."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"If you are a resident","u":"first-day.html#residents","x":"If you are a resident Interns and rotating residents carry more than students do, so a few extra things are worth settling in the first 24 hours. Get the sign-out times and format. When and where morning and evening sign-out happen, and which tool the service uses for its written handoff. What a vascular sign-out needs . Learn the call structure. Who holds the vascular pager overnight, who your backup is, and which calls go straight to the senior or attending. When to call . Know the numbers you will dial. OR front desk, the angio suite or hybrid OR, the vascular lab for urgent duplex, the CTV ICU, and the ward charge nurses. Save them in your phone on day one. Check your order and note access. Make sure you can place orders, use the service's PowerPlans, and sign notes. Build your patient list and your Auto Text phrases before your first call. Working faster in PowerChart . Find the OR schedule. Know where to see tomorrow's cases so you can read the operation, check the consent and the pre-op labs, and know which patients go to the CTV ICU afterwards. Resident tip By the end of your first day, have a one-line summary, the baseline pulse exam and the anticoagulation status written down for every patient on the list. Those three facts answer most of the calls you will get that night."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"Getting into PowerChart","u":"first-day.html#powerchart","x":"Getting into PowerChart PowerChart is where you will live: vitals, labs, notes, orders, imaging and the patient list. The faster you are fluent in it, the more useful you are on rounds. On your first login, do three things before rounds. Find the vascular service patient list or build a custom list of the patients you are following. Open one chart and find where vitals, labs, ins and outs, medications and notes live. Practice opening the most recent imaging so you are not fumbling when an attending asks for the CT angiogram. Patient list Find the service list, or make a custom list of your patients so you can pre-round efficiently. Flowsheets Know where the vitals and the ins and outs are documented by nursing; these are the numbers you report first. Results Pull the morning labs and learn to view a trend rather than a single value. Imaging Practice opening duplex, CTA and angiography reports and images quickly. Notes Read the team's note style and write yours to match. Do not copy forward stale data. Templates are on History, exam and charting . Orders As a student, understand the orders being placed before you ever pend one. Your order access depends on your role. Red flag: copy-forward The fastest way to lose trust on rounds is a note that carries yesterday's exam and numbers forward unchanged. Re-examine the patient, pull today's vitals and labs, and write what is true this morning. A wrong number reported confidently is worse than no number. Pearl: locked out If you are locked out, your password has expired, or your access has not been provisioned, the IT service desk (505-272-3282) handles login and account problems. Sort it the day before if you can, because the workroom computers fill up early."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"Scrubs and the OR","u":"first-day.html#scrubs","x":"Scrubs and the OR The operating room has its own rules of dress and movement, and getting them right on day one earns quiet credibility. Drawing scrubs. Badge in at the dispenser, take only what you need, and return used sets so your account stays in credit. Carry a spare set for the day a case runs long or messy. Changing and storing. Change in the locker room, not the hallway, and keep your coat, phone and badge somewhere you can reach between cases. OR attire. Scrub top tucked in, hair fully covered, mask on before you enter a room with an open field, and a fresh mask for each case. Shoe covers and eye protection as the hospital requires. Moving in the room. Once sterile, keep your hands above your waist and in front of you, do not turn your back to the field, and say so immediately if you think you have contaminated. Before you scrub. Introduce yourself to the scrub tech and the circulating nurse, tell them it is your first day, and ask where to stand. They run the room, and they will help you if you are respectful. Pearl The best way to be welcomed in the OR is to be early, quiet and clean at the field. Open operations walks through how each vascular case flows, so read the entry the night before and you will understand why each instrument is being asked for."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"Where to be, who to find","u":"first-day.html#where","x":"Where to be, who to find Knowing the shape of the team tells you who to ask for what, and where you fit in the morning. A vascular service usually runs as a layered team. The attending sets the plan and operates. The chief or senior resident runs the list, the OR and the consults. Junior residents and interns carry the floor patients and the notes. Students and sub-interns follow a handful of patients closely, pre-round on them and present. There is often an advanced practice provider who knows the floor and the systems better than anyone, and a coordinator who owns the schedule. Patients in the CTV ICU are co-managed with the intensivist team. In the morning, find the workroom where the list is reviewed, get there before the stated round time, and have your patients pre-rounded and ready to present. For clinical questions, ask the junior resident or APP first; save the chief for decisions and the attending for teaching, unless something is urgent. For logistics, the coordinator and the APP are your people. They have answered every first-day question before. When you do not know, say so plainly and offer to find out. Confident guessing on a vascular service can hurt a patient."},{"p":"Your first day","t":"What to carry","u":"first-day.html#carry","x":"What to carry A vascular trainee's pockets are slightly different from everyone else's, because the exam runs on one small device. Handheld Doppler essential The vascular exam lives on the continuous-wave Doppler. Know where the team keeps one, and learn to describe a signal as triphasic, biphasic or monophasic. Skin marker essential Mark the pulses you found by Doppler (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) so the next person checks the same spot. Shears and tape useful For taking down and reapplying dressings on wound and ulcer checks, plus a pen and a printed list. This guide useful Quick reference is built to be screenshotted: ABI cutoffs, Rutherford categories, endoleak types and the numbers attendings expect on demand."},{"p":"Rounds and presenting","t":"Rounds and presenting","u":"rounds.html","x":"The part of the rotation nobody formally teaches. How to pre-round, which numbers a vascular team actually wants, how to turn them into a clean presentation, and how to be useful in the operating room. Do this well and you will be trusted with more, faster."},{"p":"Rounds and presenting","t":"Pre-rounding","u":"rounds.html#preround","x":"Pre-rounding The quiet hour before the team gathers, when you see your patients, gather their data, and decide what you think. Done properly it is the difference between presenting and reciting. Arrive early enough to see every patient you are following before rounds. Work the same way each time so nothing is missed under time pressure. Pull the overnight events and the new vitals and labs from PowerChart, then go to the bedside, because the chart does not tell you that the foot is now cool or the dressing is soaked. Form an assessment and a plan for each patient before you present, even if it turns out to be wrong. The attending is teaching you to think, not to read a screen back to them. Get the list and the overnight story. Note events overnight, new consults, and anyone who went to or came back from the OR, the angiography suite, or the CTV ICU. Pull the numbers. Vitals and trends, ins and outs, morning labs, and the status of any drips or drains. Gather them before you walk in so the bedside visit is for examining, not charting. See the patient and the limb. Lay eyes on them, examine the relevant vascular bed, Doppler the pulses if they are not palpable, and look under the dressing if a wound or incision needs checking. Decide what you think. Frame each patient in one line, then an assessment and a plan. Walk in ready to commit to a recommendation. Resident tip Build a PowerChart custom patient list for the service on day one and keep a one-line running summary for each patient in your sign-out tool. When the list is twenty patients long at 5 a.m., the people who pre-round fastest are the ones who never re-read a chart from the top."},{"p":"Rounds and presenting","t":"The numbers to get","u":"rounds.html#numbers","x":"The numbers to get Every service wants vitals and labs. A vascular team also wants the limb, the lines, and the anticoagulation, because those are what change the plan. Collect these on every patient, every morning. Vitals, with the trend Temperature, heart rate and rhythm (atrial fibrillation matters here), blood pressure and oxygen. Report the trend, not just the last value, and note blood pressure in both arms when carotid or subclavian disease is in play. Ins and outs Fluid balance and especially urine output, which matter after open aneurysm repair, after contrast, and in anyone whose kidneys are at risk. Falling urine output is an early warning. Labs Hemoglobin and platelets (a falling platelet count raises the question of HIT), the basic metabolic panel for creatinine after contrast and potassium after revascularization, and coagulation studies if the patient is on heparin. Lactate Only if ischemia is possible. A rising lactate in the right setting points to ischemic tissue, whether a threatened limb or threatened bowel. The limb Pulses at each level, the Doppler signal where pulses are not palpable, capillary refill, and the look of the foot. This is the heart of a vascular exam and it should be specific. The wound Incision or ulcer appearance, drainage, the dressing or wound vac, and fasciotomy sites. Look under the dressing when it matters rather than reporting yesterday's finding. Lines, drains and access sites Arterial and central lines, drain outputs, and the puncture site after any catheter procedure: hematoma, bleeding, or a new bruit. Anticoagulation Is the heparin drip therapeutic, and at what rate? Is the patient on aspirin and a statin? Was anything held for a planned procedure? These change the plan most mornings. The basics Pain control, diet, mobility and disposition. The plan for going home or to rehab starts being built on day one. Pearl Anchor every post-operative patient to the operation and the day. Post-operative day two from an open aneurysm repair means watching urine output, potassium and the first passage of stool. Post-operative day one from a carotid endarterectomy means watching the blood pressure and the neck. Open operations lists what to anticipate after each case, and the CTV ICU covers the first night."},{"p":"Rounds and presenting","t":"Presenting a patient","u":"rounds.html#present","x":"Presenting a patient A vascular presentation is organized around a vessel and the tissue it feeds. Lead with the vascular bed and how threatened it is, then build the rest of the story around that. The attending wants to know how worried to be and how quickly, so your first two sentences should locate the problem and grade it. Move from the one-liner, through the vascular symptom and the pulse exam, to the hemodynamic and cardiac status, then the imaging framed as inflow, outflow and target, and finish with a clear assessment and plan. One-line summary. Age, the relevant risk factors (smoking, diabetes, prior revascularization), and the problem framed by severity. Say “68-year-old smoker with rest pain and a non-healing toe ulcer, so tissue-loss CLTI,” not “leg pain.” The symptom, in vascular terms. For arterial disease, claudication versus rest pain versus tissue loss. For an aneurysm, asymptomatic, symptomatic or ruptured. For carotid disease, symptomatic or asymptomatic, and when the event was. The pulse and perfusion exam. Pulses at each level, graded consistently, the ABI or toe pressures, capillary refill, the skin and ulcer description, and the other side for comparison. This is the part that earns credibility. Hemodynamic and cardiac status. Because the vascular patient is a cardiac patient: blood pressure, heart rate and rhythm, and any active cardiac, pulmonary or renal issue that bears on operative risk. Imaging, then the plan. Summarize the duplex, CTA or angiogram as inflow, outflow and target, then close with an assessment and a plan rather than trailing off. Ending on a recommendation is what separates a strong presentation from a weak one. A model post-operative presentation This is [name] , a [age] -year-old with [smoking, diabetes, CAD] , now post-operative day [2] from a [right fem-pop bypass with reversed saphenous vein] . Overnight, [no events; pain controlled] . This morning the patient is afebrile and hemodynamically stable, [HR 78 in sinus, BP 132/74] , and urine output is [adequate] . The [right] leg is warm and well perfused, with a [palpable graft pulse and a biphasic dorsalis pedis signal on Doppler] , and the incision is clean, dry and intact. Labs are notable for [hemoglobin 10.4, creatinine 1.1, potassium 4.1] . The patient is on aspirin and a statin, [no heparin drip] . Assessment: stable post-operative day two with a patent graft. Plan: [advance diet, continue aspirin and statin, out of bed today, graft duplex before discharge, home tomorrow] . Pearl Whenever you describe a revascularization candidate, speak in inflow, outflow and conduit. Naming the inflow source, the outflow target and the conduit you would use shows you understand the operation, not just the diagnosis."},{"p":"Rounds and presenting","t":"Writing the note","u":"rounds.html#note","x":"Writing the note A good vascular progress note reads the way the team thinks. It is short, it is current, and it puts the limb and the perfusion exam near the top. Open with the operative day and the operation. “POD 2, right fem-pop bypass” orients every reader in five words. Put the focused vascular exam high. Pulses by level, Doppler signals, the wound or incision, and the access site. This is what the team makes decisions on. Report numbers that are true today. Re-pull the vitals, the ins and outs and the labs. Never copy yesterday's exam forward. State the anticoagulation plan explicitly. Whether the heparin drip is therapeutic, whether aspirin and a statin are on board, and whether anything is held for a procedure. Close with a problem-based assessment and plan. Tie each plan to inflow, outflow or conduit where it applies, and name the disposition target. Copy-ready progress note, consult note and post-op check templates are on History, exam and charting . Red flag: the stale exam The most common and most dangerous documentation error on a vascular service is carrying forward a pulse exam or wound description that is no longer true. If the note says the foot has a palpable pulse and the foot is in fact cool and pulseless, that error can delay recognition of a clotted graft. Examine, then write."},{"p":"Rounds and presenting","t":"In the operating room","u":"rounds.html#or","x":"In the operating room Your job as a student or junior is to be useful, unobtrusive and anticipatory: protect the field, follow the flow of the case, and be ready half a step before you are asked. Scrub, glove and stay sterile. Hands above the waist and in front of you, never turn your back to the field, and say so immediately if you think you have contaminated rather than hoping no one noticed. Hold retraction steadily and without complaint. Exposure is the operation. A still, well-placed retractor that gives the surgeon a view of the anastomosis is a genuine contribution. Keep the tension you were given until you are asked to change it. Follow the rhythm of the case. Expose, control proximal and distal, heparinize, clamp, do the anastomosis or endarterectomy, flush, unclamp, and confirm flow. Knowing the sequence tells you why each instrument is being asked for. Anticipate heparin, clamps and flushing. Systemic heparin precedes clamping, the team checks an activated clotting time, and flow is restored in an order that flushes debris away from the brain or the distal bed. Know the anatomy of the case you are in. Read the operation the night before and be able to name the structures in the wound. Answering in the room is far more impressive than answering in the workroom. Manage the small things. Cut suture at the length the surgeon prefers, keep the field dry with suction when asked, and handle tissue gently. Rough handling of an artery or a vein graft has real consequences. Resident tip Before you leave the room, know three things for the post-op orders and the handoff: the heparin dose and last ACT, whether protamine was given, and the distal signals at the end of the case. Those three facts answer most of the night's calls about that patient. The ICU handoff checklist lists the rest."},{"p":"Rounds and presenting","t":"The common consults","u":"rounds.html#consults","x":"The common consults A handful of consults make up most of the pages to a vascular service, and each has a predictable workup. Knowing the first few moves lets you start before the senior arrives. In every case the questions are how acute it is, how threatened the tissue is, and what the perfusion exam and imaging show. The acutely cold, painful leg Treat this as acute limb ischemia until proven otherwise; it is the most time-sensitive vascular consult. Run the six Ps, establish the onset and whether the patient is in atrial fibrillation or has prior claudication, examine and Doppler both legs, and start systemic heparin immediately unless contraindicated. Then grade the limb with the Rutherford acute ischemia categories to decide whether there is time for imaging and catheter-directed therapy, or whether a category IIb limb demands the operating room now. The non-healing foot ulcer Frame it around neuropathy, ischemia and infection, because management hinges on which are present. Examine pulses and get an ABI and toe pressures (diabetic vessels can be falsely noncompressible), probe the wound for deep infection or osteomyelitis, and look for the rocker-bottom deformity of Charcot. The plan runs three threads in parallel: offload and debride, treat infection, and revascularize if perfusion is inadequate. The asymptomatic aneurysm found on imaging Two questions: how big, and is it symptomatic? Confirm the diameter, ask specifically about back or abdominal pain and tenderness that would reclassify it as symptomatic, and apply the repair thresholds (about 5.5 cm in men, 5.0 cm in women, rapid growth, or symptoms). Below threshold the work is surveillance and risk-factor control, principally smoking cessation and cardiovascular optimization. The carotid bruit or asymptomatic stenosis First decide whether the patient is symptomatic: a stroke, TIA or amaurosis fugax in the matching territory. Get a carotid duplex to grade the stenosis and make sure the patient is on best medical therapy (antiplatelet, statin, blood pressure control, smoking cessation) whatever else is decided. Symptomatic stenosis of 50 to 99 percent generally warrants intervention, ideally within about two weeks of the event; asymptomatic disease is treated more selectively. The venous consult A swollen leg, a DVT on duplex, or a question about an IVC filter. Confirm the extent of thrombus (calf, femoropopliteal or iliofemoral), check for phlegmasia, and confirm anticoagulation has been started if there is no contraindication. The questions for the vascular team are usually whether an extensive iliofemoral DVT needs thrombus removal and whether a filter is truly indicated. Red flag: the consults you do not sit on A category IIb acutely ischemic limb, a ruptured or symptomatic aneurysm, acute mesenteric ischemia with pain out of proportion to the exam, phlegmasia cerulea dolens, and an acute aortic dissection are emergencies. Start resuscitation and heparin or impulse control as appropriate and call the senior or attending at once. Do not finish a leisurely workup first. The overnight versions of these calls, and the floor calls that come with them, are on On call and cross-cover ."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"History, exam and charting","u":"charting.html","x":"How to take a vascular history, do and document a focused vascular exam, and write each note the service produces, from the consult to the discharge summary. Every note has a copy-ready template sized for PowerChart. Read the history and exam sections before your first consult, then come back to the templates as you need them."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"The vascular history","u":"charting.html#history","x":"The vascular history Every vascular history answers three questions: which vascular bed is involved, how threatened the tissue is, and what the patient can tolerate. A short set of core questions belongs in every history, whatever the complaint, because they decide operative risk, the conduit and the medical therapy. The core questions High yield Smoking Current, former or never, with pack-years (packs per day times years smoked) and the quit date, not just \"former smoker.\" Ask about vaping and other tobacco too. Smoking is the strongest modifiable risk factor for PAD and for aneurysm growth and rupture, and continued smoking shortens bypass and stent patency, so every vascular note records it and the cessation help offered. Diabetes Type, insulin or oral agents, the most recent hemoglobin A1c, and the end-organ effects: neuropathy (can the patient feel the feet?), retinopathy and nephropathy. Diabetes shifts disease to the tibial vessels, hides ischemic pain behind neuropathy and falsely raises the ABI. Note any SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist, since both carry pre-procedure hold rules. Kidney function and contrast Baseline creatinine and eGFR, CKD stage, and dialysis status (modality, schedule, and which arm carries the access). Ask about recent contrast, prior contrast-associated kidney injury, and contrast allergy: what happened, and whether premedication worked. This decides between duplex, CTA and angiography, and how much contrast the team can use. Cardiac history and functional capacity Coronary disease, prior MI, coronary stents (when placed and on which antiplatelet), heart failure with the latest ejection fraction, atrial fibrillation and valve disease. Estimate functional capacity in METs: climbing a flight of stairs or walking up a hill without stopping is about 4 METs, the usual threshold in perioperative risk assessment. A claudicant may be stopped by the legs before the heart is ever tested, so ask what limits them. Prior vascular interventions Every bypass, endarterectomy, stent, angioplasty, aneurysm repair, dialysis access and amputation: what, which side, when, where and by whom, so the operative notes and angiograms can be found. Then ask about conduit: has the saphenous vein been used? It may have gone to an earlier leg bypass or a coronary bypass, or been stripped or ablated for varicose veins. Look for the scars. The vein that is left often decides which operation is possible. Antithrombotics, with the last dose Aspirin, P2Y12 inhibitors (clopidogrel, ticagrelor, prasugrel), warfarin, DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban, including low-dose rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily used with aspirin in PAD) and cilostazol. Record the indication and the date and time of the last dose. That time decides whether the patient can go to the OR or the angiography suite today, and whether neuraxial anesthesia is an option. Statin Name, dose and adherence. Patients with PAD, carotid disease or an aneurysm should be on a high-intensity statin unless there is a documented reason not to be. If they are not, that is a recommendation in your note. Allergies that matter here Iodinated contrast; heparin, because a history of HIT changes how the patient is anticoagulated in the OR; the prophylactic antibiotics (penicillin and cefazolin); latex; and chlorhexidine. Ambulatory and living status Walks independently, with a cane or walker, uses a wheelchair, or is bedbound. Lives alone, with family, or in a facility, and who helps at home. This is not social filler: whether a patient walks shapes the choice between revascularization and amputation, the amputation level, and the discharge plan. Goals of care Code status, the health care decision-maker, and any advance directive. Before a major amputation or a high-risk aortic repair, ask what matters most to the patient (walking again, staying at home, avoiding more operations) and document the conversation and who was present. Leg pain Sort the pain into claudication, rest pain or tissue loss before anything else, because that grade drives the decision. Then localize it. Claudication. Reproducible cramping, aching or fatigue in a muscle group with walking, relieved within about 10 minutes by standing still. Ask the distance (blocks or meters) and whether it is shrinking, which muscles hurt (buttock and thigh point to aortoiliac disease, the calf to femoropopliteal disease), and what it stops the patient doing. Ask men about erectile dysfunction, part of the Leriche picture. Rest pain. Burning pain in the forefoot and toes, not the calf, worse at night or lying flat, and relieved by hanging the foot over the side of the bed or sleeping in a chair. Dependency helps because gravity adds a little perfusion pressure. Ask how long it has been present (CLTI requires more than two weeks) and how the patient sleeps; ankle swelling from sleeping upright is a clue. Tissue loss. Any ulcer or gangrene: when it started, what started it, and whether it is healing. A wound that has not healed in two weeks in a patient with PAD is CLTI until shown otherwise. Vascular claudication Brought on by walking, at a fairly constant distance Relieved by standing still within minutes Cramping or fatigue in the calf, thigh or buttock Not brought on by standing alone Diminished or absent pulses, low ABI Neurogenic claudication (spinal stenosis) Brought on by walking or by prolonged standing, at a variable distance Relieved by sitting or bending forward; leaning on a shopping cart helps Tingling, numbness or weakness, often radiating from the back or buttock Often bilateral, with back pain Pulses and ABI may be normal Other mimics: hip or knee arthritis (pain at the joint, worse with weight bearing, variable from day to day), venous claudication (bursting thigh pain after an old iliofemoral DVT, eased by elevation), painful diabetic neuropathy (burning in a stocking distribution, not relieved by dependency), a symptomatic Baker's cyst, and in a young at"},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"The focused vascular exam","u":"charting.html#exam","x":"The focused vascular exam Examine in the same order every time, compare both sides, and write it down so the next person can tell whether anything has changed. The documented exam is what the team decides on and what the night team compares against. Expose and look. Both legs uncovered from groin to toes, socks off, heels and the spaces between the toes inspected. Note color, scars (prior bypass, vein harvest, sternotomy), amputations, swelling, wounds and dressings. Vital signs with a vascular eye. Rhythm (an irregularly irregular pulse is atrial fibrillation, an embolic source) and blood pressure in both arms. A difference of more than about 15 mmHg suggests subclavian stenosis; use the higher arm for decisions and for the ABI. Palpate the pulses, head to toe. Carotid (gently, one side at a time), brachial and radial, the abdominal aorta for a widened pulsatile mass, the femoral at the midinguinal point, the popliteal with the knee slightly flexed and the fingertips of both hands deep in the fossa, the dorsalis pedis just lateral to the extensor hallucis longus tendon, and the posterior tibial behind the medial malleolus. Feel any bypass graft along its course. Listen. Bruits over the carotids, the abdomen and the femorals, and over any dialysis access. Doppler what you cannot feel. Every nonpalpable pulse gets a handheld Doppler check and a recorded signal. Skin, temperature and capillary refill. Run the back of your hand down both legs to find a temperature change, check capillary refill on the toes, and look for trophic changes. Sensation and motor. Light touch on the dorsum and sole, and toe flexion and extension. In an acute limb these two findings set the Rutherford category; in a diabetic foot, a monofilament test documents neuropathy. Measure when it matters. The ABI, and toe pressures when the vessels are calcified. The provocative tests (Buerger's test, the Allen test before radial access or harvest) are described in Foundations . Grading and documenting pulses Several scales are in use, and the one that matters is the one your team uses. A common one is below; some services use 0 to 4+, with 4+ meaning bounding. Whatever the scale, write each pulse by name and side, and make Doppler-only signals unmistakable so no one reads them as palpable. Many vascular services skip numbers for the foot altogether and write \"palpable,\" \"Doppler signal\" with its phase, or \"absent,\" which is more reproducible between examiners. A common pulse grading scale Grade Meaning Note 0 Absent to palpation Always follow with a Doppler check and record the signal 1+ Diminished, barely palpable Suggests disease upstream 2+ Normal Compare with the other side 3+ Increased or bounding A prominent, wide popliteal or femoral pulse suggests an aneurysm D Not palpable, Doppler signal present Record the phase: triphasic, biphasic or monophasic Vascular exam and pulse grid Vascular exam (examined __:__ on __/__) BP: R arm __/__ L arm __/__ Rhythm: ___ Pulses: 2+ normal, 1+ diminished, 0 absent, D = Doppler only (tri / bi / mono) R L Carotid ___ ___ Bruit: R __ L __ Brachial ___ ___ Radial ___ ___ Aorta [nonpalpable / normal / widened ___] Femoral ___ ___ Bruit: R __ L __ Popliteal ___ ___ DP ___ ___ PT ___ ___ Graft ___ ___ Doppler sites marked: [Y / N] ABI ___ ___ (ankle __ / __ mmHg) Toe pressure ___ ___ Temperature ___ ___ Cap refill __ s __ s Sensation ___ ___ Motor ___ ___ Skin: [hair loss / rubor / mottling / edema / ___] Wound: [site], __ x __ x __ cm, base ___, probe-to-bone [+/-], exposed ___, cellulitis __ cm beyond edge, drainage ___ Recording Doppler signals Triphasic Forward flow in systole, a brief reversal in early diastole, then a small forward wave. The normal signal in a resting peripheral artery. Biphasic Forward and reverse components without the third. Often normal in older patients, or a sign of mild disease upstream. Monophasic A single, blunted forward signal, often continuing through diastole. Significant disease upstream: the tissue is perfused, but through collaterals. Absent No arterial signal. In an acute limb, inaudible pedal arterial signals with an audible venous signal is category II territory, where the sensory and motor exam separates IIa from IIb; losing the venous signal as well marks category III. Write the artery, the side and the phase: \"R PT monophasic Doppler signal, not palpable.\" Avoid \"dopplerable pulses\" and \"signals present,\" which say nothing about where or what. A venous signal is a low, continuous rushing sound that varies with breathing and gets louder when you squeeze the calf; an arterial signal beats with the heart. Mistaking one for the other is the classic way a dead foot gets documented as having signals. Resident tip When you find a faint pedal signal, mark the spot on the skin with a pen and write \"Doppler sites marked\" in the note. The nurse doing hourly checks and the night intern will listen over the same artery you did, so a change in the signal means a change in the patient, not a change in examiner. The ABI at the bedside With a manual cuff and a handheld Doppler, measure systolic pressure in both brachial arteries, then at each ankle over both the dorsalis pedis and the posterior tibial, with the cuff just above the malleoli. For each leg, divide the higher of that leg's two ankle pressures by the higher brachial pressure. Record the pressures as well as the ratio, because an ankle pressure under 50 mmHg means something on its own. An ABI of 0.90 or less confirms PAD; above 1.40 the vessels are noncompressible and the ratio is unreliable, so get toe pressures. A toe pressure under 30 mmHg predicts poor wound healing. Full interpretation is in Testing and the ABI . Skin and trophic changes Chronic arterial insufficiency. Hair loss, thin shiny skin, thickened nails, muscle wasting, pallor on elevation with dependent rubor (Buerger's test), and capillary refill slower than about 3 seconds. Acute ischemia. A pale or waxy white foot, a level where the"},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"The consult note","u":"charting.html#consult-note","x":"The consult note A consult note answers the question asked, grades the threat, and gives recommendations the primary team can act on without calling you to interpret them. It follows the same order as the presentation on Rounds and presenting . Restate the question in the first line, and make sure the recommendations answer it. Frame the HPI by vascular bed and severity. \"Right leg CLTI with tissue loss,\" not \"foot wound.\" Keep the history pertinent. The core questions above, not a transcription of the whole chart. Describe imaging as inflow, outflow and target, and say which study is still needed. Grade the severity in the assessment: Rutherford and WIfI for chronic limb disease, the Rutherford acute category for an acute limb, symptom status and percent stenosis for carotid disease, diameter and symptoms for an aneurysm. Number the recommendations and make each one specific: the drug and dose, the study, the NPO time, and who will place the order. Record who staffed it and when, and whether the team will follow or sign off. Vascular surgery consult note Vascular surgery consult note Seen: __/__ __:__ Requesting: Dr ___, ___ service Reason for consult: ___ HPI: [age] [M/F] with [risk factors] seen for [question]. Vascular bed: [aortoiliac / fem-pop / tibial / carotid / aortic / mesenteric / venous / dialysis access] Presentation: [claudication at __ blocks / rest pain x __ wk / tissue loss: ___ / acute, onset __:__ on __/__] Prior vascular procedures: [what, side, date, where] Conduit used: ___ GSV status: R ___ L ___ Pertinent ROS: ___ PMH: CAD/stents ___ CHF (EF __%) ___ AF ___ DM (A1c __) ___ CKD (Cr __) ___ dialysis ___ Stroke/TIA ___ COPD ___ other ___ PSH: ___ (incl. CABG, vein harvest, amputations) Meds: antiplatelet ___ (last dose ___) anticoagulant ___ (last dose __/__ __:__) statin ___ mg other relevant ___ Allergies: ___ Contrast: ___ HIT history: [Y/N] Social: tobacco ___ pk-yr [current / quit __] lives [alone / with ___ / facility] ambulation [independent / cane / walker / w/c] Functional capacity: [4+ METs / under 4 / leg-limited] Code status: ___ Surrogate: ___ Exam T __ HR __ (rhythm ___) BP R __/__ L __/__ SpO2 __ General: ___ Heart/lungs: ___ Abdomen: [soft, nontender; aorta ___] Pulses (2+ / 1+ / 0; D = Doppler only, with phase): R L Femoral ___ ___ Popliteal ___ ___ DP ___ ___ PT ___ ___ [carotid / radial / graft as relevant] Limb: temp ___ cap refill __ s sensation ___ motor ___ calf/compartments ___ Wound: [site], __ x __ x __ cm, base ___, probe-to-bone [+/-], exposed ___, cellulitis __ cm, drainage ___ Data Labs: Hgb __ Plt __ WBC __ Cr __ K __ INR __ aPTT __ lactate __ A1c __ ABI: R __ (ankle __ mmHg) L __ (ankle __ mmHg) Toe pressure: R __ L __ Imaging: [study, date] Inflow: ___ Outflow: ___ Target / runoff: ___ ECG: ___ Assessment [age] [M/F] with [diagnosis, side], [Rutherford __ / WIfI W_ I_ fI_ / ALI category __ / symptomatic __% stenosis / __ cm AAA], [one line on operative risk]. Recommendations 1. ___ 2. ___ 3. Antithrombotic: ___ 4. Statin: ___ 5. Tobacco: [counseled / Rx ___ / N/A] 6. Diet: [NPO after midnight for ___ / regular] Seen and staffed with Dr ___ (attending) at __:__ on __/__. Urgent recommendations discussed with Dr ___ (primary team) at __:__. Vascular surgery will [follow daily / sign off]. A model consult presentation This is [name] , a [72] -year-old [man] with [insulin-treated diabetes, CKD stage 3b, coronary stents in 2021, and a 50 pack-year smoking history, quit two years ago] , consulted by [hospital medicine] for a [non-healing right great toe ulcer] . The ulcer appeared [six weeks] ago after [a new shoe rubbed it] , and for [three weeks] he has had burning forefoot pain at night that he relieves by hanging the foot off the bed. He has had no vascular procedures, and both great saphenous veins are intact. He takes [aspirin and atorvastatin 40] and no anticoagulant. He walks without an aid and lives with his wife. He is afebrile, heart rate [82] in sinus rhythm. Femoral pulses are [2+ bilaterally] and popliteal pulses are [not palpable] . On the right there is [a monophasic posterior tibial signal and no dorsalis pedis signal] ; the left foot has [biphasic pedal signals] . At the tip of the right great toe is a [1.5 by 1 by 0.3 cm] ulcer with a [pale base] that [does not probe to bone] , with [1 cm] of surrounding erythema and no drainage. The ABI is [0.35 on the right and 0.78 on the left] . White count is [9.8] , creatinine [1.8] and A1c [8.9] , and the foot film shows [no osteomyelitis] . There is no arterial imaging yet. So this is [right] leg chronic limb-threatening ischemia with tissue loss, Rutherford [5] , WIfI [wound 1, ischemia 3, foot infection 1] . I would recommend [diagnostic angiography with possible intervention, using limited contrast and pre-procedure fluids given his kidney function] , [vein mapping of both legs] , [continuing aspirin and increasing atorvastatin to 80] , [antibiotics for mild cellulitis per the primary team] , and [offloading with a dry dressing to the toe] . We will follow daily. Resident tip For anything time-sensitive (start heparin, keep NPO, get the patient to the angiography suite), call the primary team with the recommendation first, then write it, and record in the note whom you spoke to and when. A recommendation that sits unread in a signed note at 6 p.m. has not happened."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"The admission H&P","u":"charting.html#admission-hp","x":"The admission H&P Vascular patients come in two ways, and the documentation differs. Elective The patient was seen in clinic, the H&P was written there, and the patient arrives on the day of surgery. Before the operation you write or confirm the interval update (see the pre-op checklist note ), then the post-operative orders and checks. Some services also write a short admission note after the case. Urgent Admitted from the ED, clinic or another hospital with CLTI, an infected foot, acute limb ischemia, a symptomatic aneurysm or a recent TIA. Write a full H&P. If you already wrote the consult note, it is the skeleton: add the full medication reconciliation, family history and review of systems, and an admission plan that covers every active problem, not only the vascular one. Vascular surgery admission H&P Vascular surgery admission H&P Admitted: __/__ __:__ Attending: Dr ___ Type: [urgent from ED / clinic / transfer from ___] Chief complaint: ___ HPI: [framed by vascular bed and severity, as consult] PMH: ___ PSH: ___ (incl. vascular procedures, vein harvest) Home meds (reconciled __/__): ___ Antithrombotics, last dose: ___ Held on admission: ___ Allergies: ___ (contrast ___, HIT ___) Social: tobacco ___ pk-yr; alcohol ___; drugs ___ lives ___; ambulation ___ Family history: [aneurysm / dissection / VTE / ___] ROS: ___ Exam: [focused vascular exam with pulse grid] Data: [labs; ECG; imaging as inflow/outflow/target] Assessment: [one line with severity grade] Plan 1. Vascular: [procedure ___ on __/__ / workup ___] 2. Antithrombotic: [ASA ___; heparin gtt per protocol / ___ held since __/__] 3. Cardiac: [beta-blocker ___; statin ___; ECG ___] 4. Renal: [Cr __; contrast plan ___] 5. Diabetes: [insulin plan ___; A1c __] 6. Infection: [antibiotics ___; cultures ___] 7. VTE prophylaxis: [heparin SQ / on therapeutic anticoagulation; no SCD on an ischemic leg] 8. Diet ___ Activity ___ Lines ___ 9. Code status ___ Surrogate ___ 10. Disposition: [anticipated ___] Confirm locally Whether an elective post-operative admission needs its own admission note, which service writes the admission orders for a patient going to the CTV ICU, and whether a recent consult note can stand in for parts of the H&P all vary by hospital and service. Ask the senior on day one."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"Pre-op checklist note","u":"charting.html#pre-op","x":"Pre-op checklist note The night before or the morning of an operation, someone confirms that the patient can safely go: paperwork, blood, drugs held or continued, and the planning studies the surgeon will want in the room. On most services that is the intern, and the pre-op note is the record that it was done. The H&P is current. Team Under CMS rules the H&P must be done no more than 30 days before surgery (or within 24 hours after admission, but before the operation). If it is older than 24 hours, an update documenting any change in the patient's condition must be written after admission and before surgery. An H&P older than 30 days is redone. Hospital bylaws may be stricter. Consent is signed. Operating surgeon or a delegated resident The procedure written in full with side and level and the likely additions (\"possible prosthetic graft,\" \"possible angiogram\"); risks, benefits and alternatives discussed; blood product consent if separate; an interpreter or a surrogate decision-maker when needed. Students do not obtain surgical consent. The site is marked. Operating surgeon or delegate For any procedure with laterality, per the Universal Protocol, with the patient awake and taking part where possible. Type and screen or crossmatch. Intern A type and screen for most open cases; crossmatched units for open aortic and redo operations per the attending and blood bank; cell saver requested for open aortic repair. Check the expiry (commonly about 3 days) and the antibody screen, since a positive screen delays crossmatched blood. Antiplatelets. Attending decides Aspirin is continued through almost all vascular surgery. Whether a P2Y12 inhibitor continues depends on the operation and why it was started. Never stop dual antiplatelet therapy after a recent coronary stent without cardiology input. Anticoagulants held per protocol. Intern, with pharmacy Warfarin is typically stopped about 5 days before, with an INR the day before or the morning of surgery. DOACs are held 1 to 4 days depending on the drug, renal function and bleeding risk. Bridging is rarely needed, and neuraxial anesthesia has longer hold windows. Record the last dose. A heparin drip for acute ischemia often runs into the OR; confirm the stop time with the surgeon and anesthesia. Beta-blocker and statin continued. Intern Continue a beta-blocker the patient already takes, but do not start one on the day of surgery. Continue the statin, and start one if it is missing. Renal function and the contrast plan. Intern A recent creatinine and potassium. For contrast cases, stop nephrotoxins, give isotonic fluids to patients at risk per protocol, plan to minimize contrast, hold metformin per local policy, and premedicate contrast allergy per protocol. Dialysis patients are usually dialyzed the day before, with a potassium on the morning of surgery. Vein mapping. Intern orders Duplex mapping of the great (and if needed small) saphenous veins before a planned bypass, and arm vein and artery mapping before access creation. NPO and the diabetes plan. Intern, with anesthesia Fasting per anesthesia guidance (ASA guidance allows clear liquids until 2 hours and a light meal until 6 hours before), though many services still order NPO after midnight. Adjust insulin per protocol. SGLT2 inhibitors are usually held 3 to 4 days before surgery because of euglycemic ketoacidosis; follow current anesthesia guidance for GLP-1 receptor agonists, which has changed recently. Antibiotic prophylaxis ordered. Intern Commonly cefazolin (2 g, or 3 g at 120 kg or more) within 60 minutes before incision, or vancomycin started within 120 minutes for a beta-lactam allergy or MRSA colonization; cefazolin redosed every 4 hours or after heavy blood loss, and stopped within 24 hours. Per local protocol. Cardiac evaluation. Team, with anesthesia An ECG, and further testing only when it would change management, following the 2024 ACC/AHA perioperative guideline. Operation-specific items. Intern Carotid: the baseline neuro exam is documented. Open or complex aortic: a CTV ICU bed requested, blood and cell saver arranged, and the spinal drain decision made (see spinal drains ). Endovascular: the device is ordered and in the building. All cases: the CTA, angiogram or vein map is available in the room. Confirm locally The H&P window, type and screen expiry, who may obtain consent and mark the site, the perioperative anticoagulant table, metformin and contrast rules, and NPO orders all follow hospital policy and the anesthesia department. Use the hospital's perioperative anticoagulation protocol or ask pharmacy, and confirm the plan for every anticoagulated patient with the senior. Vascular surgery pre-op note Vascular surgery pre-op note Procedure: [side] ___ Date: __/__ Surgeon: Dr ___ Diagnosis: ___ H&P: dated __/__; interval update __/__ __:__ Consent: signed __/__ [incl. blood products Y/N] Site marked: [Y / N/A] Labs (__/__): Hgb __ Plt __ Cr __ K __ INR __ Glu __ Type and screen: __/__ (expires __/__) Crossmatch: __ u PRBC Cell saver: [Y / N/A] ECG: ___ Echo: ___ Cardiac eval: ___ Antiplatelet: ASA [continued]; P2Y12 ___ [cont / held] Anticoagulant: ___ last dose __/__ __:__ [held per ___] Heparin gtt: [N/A / stop __:__ / continue to OR] Beta-blocker: [continued / N/A] Statin: [continued] Renal: Cr __, eGFR __; contrast plan ___ [metformin held / fluids per protocol / premed ___] Vein mapping: [GSV R ___ L ___ / arm veins ___ / N/A] Imaging for OR: [CTA __/__ / angiogram __/__] NPO: [after midnight / per anesthesia]; insulin ___ SGLT2i / GLP-1 RA: [N/A / held since __/__] Antibiotic: [cefazolin __ g / vancomycin] for OR Post-op bed: [CTV ICU requested / floor / same day]"},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"The brief op note","u":"charting.html#brief-op-note","x":"The brief op note Write it before the patient leaves the operating room or the PACU, because the full operative report may not be in the chart for hours. The Joint Commission requires this note before transfer to the next level of care when the full report is not yet available. The required elements are the surgeon and assistants, the procedure, the findings, the estimated blood loss, the specimens and the post-operative diagnosis. A vascular service adds the heparin and protamine, the end-of-case distal exam, implants, and where the patient is going, because those are what the night team and the CTV ICU need. Record the implant details from the implant log rather than from memory. Brief operative note Brief operative note Date: __/__ Pre-op diagnosis: ___ Post-op diagnosis: [same / ___] Procedure: [side, specific: e.g. R CFA to BK popliteal bypass with reversed ipsilateral GSV] Surgeon: Dr ___ Assistant(s): ___ Anesthesia: [general / regional / MAC / local] Findings: [inflow, outflow, conduit quality; completion angiogram or duplex result] Heparin: ___ units IV at __:__ Last ACT: ___ Protamine: [none / __ mg at __:__] EBL: __ mL Cell saver returned: __ mL Fluids: crystalloid __ mL PRBC __ u other ___ UOP: __ mL Specimens: [none / ___ to pathology / cultures ___] Drains: [none / ___ in ___] Implants: [graft, stent, patch, device: type, size] Complications: [none apparent / ___] End-of-case exam: R DP __ PT __ | L DP __ PT __ [graft __; neuro exam ___ for carotid/aortic] Condition: [stable / ___] Disposition: [PACU then CTV ICU / floor / home] Pearl The end-of-case exam in this note is the baseline every later check is compared against. Write the actual findings (\"R DP biphasic Doppler signal, PT palpable\"), not \"foot perfused.\" A signal that was monophasic in the OR and is monophasic at midnight is reassuring; the same signal after a palpable pulse at the end of the case is not."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"The post-op check","u":"charting.html#post-op-check","x":"The post-op check A few hours after the patient reaches the floor or the unit, and always before you sign out, see the patient and compare everything with the end of the case. The question is simple: is anything worse than when the patient left the OR? Patients in the CTV ICU are managed with the intensivists, but the vascular team still checks the limb, the neck or the incision, and the operation-specific problems (see The CTV ICU ). What to focus on, by operation Carotid endarterectomy or stenting The neuro exam against the pre-op baseline, the cranial nerves, the neck (hematoma, tracheal deviation, voice, stridor), and blood pressure within the surgeon's target range; both hypotension and hypertension matter. See On call: after carotid surgery . Open aortic repair Urine output, hemoglobin, lactate, the abdomen (distension, and bladder pressure if abdominal compartment syndrome is a concern), pulses and motor function in both legs, and the stool, since bloody diarrhea suggests colon ischemia. See On call: after aortic surgery and the CTV ICU by operation . EVAR or TEVAR Access sites, distal pulses, creatinine after contrast, and leg strength and sensation, because spinal cord ischemia after thoracic coverage can appear hours later. If a spinal drain is in, its output and pressure per protocol. Leg bypass or endarterectomy The graft pulse or signal and the distal signals compared with the end of the case, the incisions (hematoma, lymph leak), and the compartments after revascularizing an acutely ischemic leg. Endovascular case through the groin or arm The puncture site (hematoma, bleeding, a new bruit, or back or flank pain suggesting retroperitoneal bleeding), distal pulses, and the bed rest orders for the closure method used. See On call: the groin . Dialysis access Thrill and bruit, hematoma, and the hand: color, temperature, sensation, strength and pain. Amputation Bleeding through the dressing, pain control, the knee kept straight after a below-knee amputation, and the other foot and both heels, which are at risk in bed. Vascular surgery post-op check Vascular surgery post-op check POD 0 s/p ___ (out of OR __:__) Seen: __:__ S: Pain __/10 [controlled / not]; [nausea, chest pain, dyspnea, new numbness or weakness: ___] O: T __ HR __ (rhythm ___) BP __/__ (goal ___) RR __ SpO2 __% on ___ UOP __ mL since OR (__ mL/kg/h); last 2 h __ mL Neuro (carotid/aortic): [alert, oriented, speech normal, face symmetric, tongue midline, voice normal; strength R __ L __; LE motor/sensory ___] Neck/incision/groin: [soft, dry, no hematoma / ___] Pulses now end of case R DP ___ ___ R PT ___ ___ L DP ___ ___ L PT ___ ___ Graft ___ ___ Limb: [warm], cap refill __ s, motor/sensory ___ Compartments: [soft / ___] Drains: __ mL [serosanguineous / ___] Spinal drain (if present): __ mL/h, CSF __ mmHg Labs: Hgb __ (pre-op __) Plt __ K __ Cr __ lactate __ glucose __ aPTT/ACT __ A/P: [stable post-op check / concern: ___] - ___ - Discussed with Dr ___ at __:__ Red flag: worse than the end of the case A pedal signal present at the end of the operation that is now absent, a graft that has lost its pulse, a new neurologic deficit after a carotid or aortic operation, or a growing neck hematoma after carotid surgery: call the senior immediately, before you finish the note. A lost signal is graft or vessel thrombosis until proven otherwise. A neck hematoma with any change in breathing or voice is an airway emergency that may need the wound opened at the bedside. The overnight versions of these calls are on On call and cross-cover ."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"The progress note","u":"charting.html#progress-note","x":"The progress note The daily note reads the way the team thinks: operation and day first, the limb near the top, true numbers, an explicit antithrombotic plan, and a problem-based plan that ends in a disposition. Writing the note explains the reasoning; this is the template. POD and operation in the first line. \"POD 2 s/p R CFA to BK popliteal bypass with reversed GSV\" orients every reader. For a nonoperative patient, use the hospital day and the problem. The focused vascular exam high, with the time you did it. Pulses by level, Doppler signals with phase, the incision or wound, and the access site. True numbers. Ranges for the last 24 hours, measured urine and drain output, and today's labs. Antithrombotics stated outright. Aspirin, statin, the heparin drip rate and whether it is therapeutic, and anything held, with when it restarts. A problem-based assessment and plan, tied to inflow, outflow or conduit where it applies, ending with the disposition and a target date. Vascular surgery progress note Vascular surgery progress note POD __ s/p [side] ___ (__/__) | HD __ [or: HD __, admitted for ___] Interval/overnight: ___ S: ___ [pain, diet, flatus/BM, ambulation, new sx] O: Tmax __ HR __-__ (rhythm ___) BP __-__/__-__ SpO2 __% on ___ I/O 24 h: __ in / __ out UOP __ mL Drains: __ mL [character] Vascular exam (examined __:__): Pulses: R DP __ PT __ | L DP __ PT __ | graft __ Limb: [warm / cool], cap refill __ s, motor/sens ___ Incision: ___ Wound: [site], __ x __ cm, base ___, drainage ___ Access site: ___ Other exam: ___ Labs: Hgb __ Plt __ WBC __ Cr __ K __ Mg __ aPTT / anti-Xa __ INR __ Imaging/studies: ___ Antithrombotics: ASA [Y/N] statin [Y/N] Heparin gtt: [N/A / __ units/h, therapeutic Y/N] Held: ___ since __/__, restart ___ A/P: [age] [M/F] POD __ s/p ___ for ___, [stable / ___] 1. [Vascular problem]: ___ 2. Antithrombotic: ___ 3. ___ 4. ___ VTE prophylaxis: ___ Diet: ___ Activity / PT / OT: ___ Lines/drains: ___ Dispo: [home / home health / SAR / SNF], target __/__ Red flag: copy-forward Copy-forward and auto-populating templates make it easy to sign a note that says \"palpable DP\" or \"incision clean and dry\" from yesterday. If you start from yesterday's note, delete the exam, the wound, the I&O and the labs before you see the patient, and rebuild them from today's findings. A signed note is a legal record that you examined what it says you examined, and a stale pulse exam can hide a clotted graft."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"Discharge","u":"charting.html#discharge","x":"Discharge summary and instructions The discharge summary is the note the next doctor reads: the primary care physician, the nursing facility, the emergency physician who sees the patient in two weeks. For a vascular patient it must carry the antithrombotic plan and the surveillance plan, because those are what get lost. The procedure Each operation or intervention with the date, side, conduit or device, and any complication. \"Right CFA to below-knee popliteal bypass with reversed ipsilateral GSV\" is useful; \"bypass\" is not. Antiplatelet and statin Name and dose. If either is missing, say why (true allergy, intolerance, bleeding); otherwise the next physician will assume it was an oversight, or never notice. Anticoagulation Agent, dose (including any loading phase), indication, planned duration, and who monitors it. Say explicitly whether aspirin continues alongside it, and if anticoagulation was stopped, say so and why. Wound care Dressing instructions, when the patient may shower, no soaking until healed, when staples or sutures come out and who removes them, and any negative pressure wound therapy settings and home health orders. Activity Weight-bearing status (protecting a fresh amputation or a heel wound), lifting limits after open abdominal surgery, driving (not on opioids, and not until the patient can brake safely), and leg elevation for post-bypass swelling. Follow-up and surveillance The clinic visit and the study that goes with it, named precisely. Typical SVS-based intervals: a vein bypass gets a duplex at about 1 month, then around 3, 6 and 12 months, then yearly; after EVAR, a CTA at about 1 month, then yearly imaging (often duplex) if there is no endoleak and the sac is stable; after carotid endarterectomy, a baseline duplex within the first few months, then periodic surveillance; after open aortic repair, imaging about every 5 years. Follow your attending's protocol and write the study and the interval. Smoking cessation Counseling given, pharmacotherapy prescribed (nicotine replacement, varenicline), and a referral to a quitline or cessation program. Continued smoking lowers graft patency and raises amputation risk. Return precautions Specific to the operation: a cold, pale, numb or weak foot; increasing leg pain; wound redness, drainage or fever; groin swelling or bleeding; and after carotid surgery, new weakness, numbness, speech or vision change, which means calling 911. Discharge summary: vascular content Procedure(s): __/__ [side] ___ [conduit / device] Findings / complications: ___ Hospital course by problem: 1. ___ 2. ___ Discharge antithrombotics: Antiplatelet: ___ Statin: ___ mg Anticoagulant: ___ dose ___ for ___ (indication) Duration: ___ Monitored by: ___ Held meds and restart dates: ___ Wound: [care ___; shower from __/__; no soaking until healed]; staples/sutures out __/__ by ___ Activity: [weight bearing ___; no lifting over __ lb for __ wk; no driving on opioids; elevate legs ___] Tobacco: [never / quit / counseled, Rx ___] Follow-up: vascular clinic in __ wk with [study] Surveillance plan: ___ Pending at discharge: ___ (follow-up by ___) Return precautions: [cold, pale, numb or weak foot; wound redness, drainage or fever; groin swelling or bleeding; carotid: new weakness, speech or vision change, call 911] Disposition: [home / home health / SAR / SNF] Before the patient leaves Antiplatelet and statin prescribed. Or the reason they are not is documented. Anticoagulation plan written. Drug, dose, duration and who manages it, with the first INR or lab check booked if needed. Medication reconciliation complete. Every held drug restarted or given a restart date: anticoagulants, metformin after contrast, antihypertensives held for low blood pressure. Wound plan and supplies arranged. Dressings, negative pressure wound therapy, home health or wound clinic, and a staple or suture removal date. Follow-up booked and imaging ordered. Clinic visit and surveillance study on the same day where possible. Activity and weight bearing written. Therapy recommendations (PT, OT, equipment) acted on. Smoking cessation addressed. Counseling documented and pharmacotherapy offered. Return precautions taught back. The patient can say which symptoms mean calling the clinic and which mean calling 911. Pending results assigned. Cultures, pathology and any study still out, with the person responsible for following them up. Disposition and handoff complete. Home, home with services, rehab or skilled nursing, and the summary sent to the primary care physician and any facility."},{"p":"History, exam and charting","t":"Working in PowerChart","u":"charting.html#powerchart","x":"Working faster in PowerChart The hospital runs on Cerner PowerChart (Cerner is now part of Oracle Health). Most of the time saved on a busy service comes from a handful of features used well. Names and layouts vary with each hospital's build, so treat this as a map of what exists, not click-by-click instructions. Logging in and first-day setup are on Your first day . Patient lists The service may already have a shared team list. Build a custom list of the patients you follow as well, so pre-rounding starts from one screen, and add each consult the moment you get the page. Results Review and flowsheets Labs, vitals and other results laid out by time. Set the date range to the last 24 to 72 hours, and use the trend or graph view for hemoglobin, platelets and creatinine rather than reading single values. iView Interactive View and I&O Where nursing documents vitals, intake and output, drain output, neuro checks and pulse checks. This is where you see what the nurse heard on the Doppler at 3 a.m. and how the urine output ran hour by hour. The MAR The medication administration record shows what was actually given and when, which is not always what was ordered: whether the heparin drip was running or paused, when the last anticoagulant dose went in, how much PRN opioid the patient needed. Orders and PowerPlans PowerPlans are PowerChart's order sets: grouped orders for a situation such as post-operative care, a heparin nomogram or a contrast study. Save orders you place often to your favorites. Dynamic Documentation The usual note-writing tool. Note templates can pull vitals, labs, medications and I&O into the note automatically; check what each one pulls and over what time window. Some builds still use older structured PowerNote templates. Auto Text Personal shortcut phrases: type a short abbreviation and it expands into a block of text. Personal Auto Text is yours to create and edit; shared phrases may also exist. Message Center The inbox: results to review, notes to sign or forward for co-signature, and messages. Unsigned notes and co-signature requests wait here until you act on them. The handoff tool An electronic sign-out, usually organized along I-PASS lines, where the build includes one. Update the one-liner, the active issues and the \"if this, then that\" plan for each patient before you leave. Habits that save time Let templates pull data, never the exam. Auto-populated vitals, labs and medications are fine once you have checked them. The exam, the wound and the plan are typed fresh every day. Never copy forward an exam. Check iView before you see a post-op patient. The overnight pulse checks and urine output tell you what to look at first. Read the source documents. For a returning patient, open the prior operative note and angiogram report rather than relying on the last progress note; filter the documents view by note type to find them. Check the MAR before you write the antithrombotic plan. \"On heparin\" in yesterday's plan does not mean the drip ran overnight. Clear Message Center daily. Unsigned notes and co-signature requests go stale quickly. Resident tip In your first week, turn each template on this page into a personal Auto Text phrase with a short abbreviation: one for the progress note, one for the post-op check, one for the pulse grid, one for the consult. Keep the blanks as ___ so an unfilled field stands out, and delete lines that do not apply rather than leaving them empty. Ten minutes of setup pays back on every note for the rest of the rotation. Confirm locally Order sets (PowerPlans), note templates and shared Auto Text are built by each institution, and the vascular service may have its own post-operative PowerPlans and note templates. Ask your senior which ones the team uses before you build your own. Whether students write notes in PowerChart, and how those notes are reviewed and co-signed, is also set locally."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"The CTV ICU","u":"ctv-icu.html","x":"Which vascular patients go to the cardiothoracic and vascular ICU, how they get there, how to hand them off, and what to watch after each kind of operation. Read it before your first open aortic or complex endovascular case, and again before your first night covering the unit."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"How the unit works","u":"ctv-icu.html#model","x":"How the CTV ICU works The CTV ICU is the hospital's dedicated cardiothoracic and vascular intensive care unit, in the newer critical care tower, staffed by intensivists who look after post-operative cardiac, thoracic and vascular patients every day. Your patients there have two teams, and the care goes well when each team knows what the other owns. Most academic surgical ICUs run a closed or collaborative model. The ICU team (the intensivist, fellows or residents, advanced practice providers and the bedside nurses) manages the critical care. The vascular team remains the surgical owner: it makes the operative decisions, sets the operation-specific plan and targets, and owns the wounds, grafts, surgical drains and the antiplatelet and anticoagulation decisions. Neither team should ever be surprised by an order the other wrote. The vascular team owns Surgical decisions: return to the OR, reintervention, imaging, a second-look operation. The operation-specific targets: blood pressure range, MAP floor when the spinal cord is at risk, pulse and neuro check schedule. Incisions, wounds, grafts, fasciotomies and surgical drains. Antiplatelet and anticoagulation decisions, including when heparin starts or stops. Diet advancement and drain removal timing after aortic and mesenteric surgery. Updating the family about the operation and the surgical outlook. The ICU team owns Airway, ventilation, sedation and delirium. Vasoactive drips, titrated to the agreed targets. Fluids, electrolytes, glucose and nutrition delivery. Lines, monitoring and the ICU prevention bundles. The day-to-day critical care orders. Goals-of-care discussions, together with the surgeon. Confirm locally Whether the CTV ICU runs a closed or collaborative model for vascular patients, who writes the admission orders and the daily orders, which service is the attending of record, when the vascular team must be called, and who manages the CSF drain. Ask your senior on day one, because it decides what you are allowed to order."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"Who goes","u":"ctv-icu.html#who-goes","x":"Who goes to the CTV ICU ICU admission after vascular surgery is driven by three things: the physiological insult of the operation, the need for drips, ventilation or invasive monitoring, and a risk that needs hourly eyes on the patient. Most vascular patients never need it. Typical CTV ICU admissions from the vascular service Patient Why the ICU First-night focus Open AAA repair, elective Aortic cross-clamp, blood loss, fluid shifts, often an epidural, high cardiac risk Bleeding, urine output, potassium, cardiac events, distal perfusion Ruptured AAA, after open repair or EVAR Shock, massive transfusion, coagulopathy, hypothermia, often still ventilated Abdominal compartment syndrome, colon ischemia, bleeding, renal and respiratory failure Thoracoabdominal and complex open aortic repair Renal and visceral ischemia time, thoracoabdominal incision, large transfusion Spinal cord, kidneys, bowel, bleeding, lungs TEVAR, fenestrated or branched EVAR with a CSF drain or high spinal cord ischemia risk Hourly leg neuro checks, a MAP floor, drain management Spinal cord ischemia, stroke, access sites, target-vessel and kidney function Acute aortic dissection on impulse-control drips IV beta-blocker and vasodilator titration on an arterial line Heart rate and pressure targets, pain, malperfusion Mesenteric revascularization, or a planned second look Bowel at risk, sometimes an open abdomen, sepsis Lactate, abdominal exam, timing of the second look Acute limb ischemia after revascularization, with reperfusion concerns or fasciotomy Reperfusion injury after prolonged ischemia Potassium, acidosis, CK and urine, compartments, pulses CEA or TCAR with labile blood pressure needing drips, or a neuro change IV antihypertensive or pressor titration, frequent neuro checks Blood pressure, the neck, the neuro exam Major vascular trauma Hemorrhagic shock and associated injuries; some go to a trauma or surgical ICU depending on the other injuries Bleeding, perfusion beyond the repair, compartments Any vascular patient needing vasoactive drips, ventilation or invasive monitoring Level of care follows the need, not the operation Whatever the drips are treating Most uncomplicated carotid endarterectomies, standard EVARs, lower extremity bypasses and amputations go from the operating room to PACU and then to a step-down unit or the floor. The level of care is decided by the attending surgeon with anesthesia and the ICU team: before the day of surgery for elective cases, and again at the end of the case if something changed. Pearl Level of care follows physiology, not the name of the operation. A \"routine\" bypass that needed four units of blood and a norepinephrine drip belongs in the ICU, and a straightforward open aneurysm repair in a fit patient may be ready to leave the next day. Confirm locally Which operations are booked to the CTV ICU by default (for example, every open aortic case and every CSF drain), where carotid patients recover overnight, and which drips, if any, a step-down bed can take."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"Getting admitted","u":"ctv-icu.html#admission","x":"Getting a patient admitted The ICU bed is part of the operative plan. For elective cases it is requested before the patient is in the room; for emergencies, the call to the ICU goes out as the decision to operate is made. From the operating room Decide the level of care. For elective open aortic and complex endovascular cases the attending decides before the day of surgery and the ICU bed is requested with the case. In the OR the decision changes when the case does: unexpected blood loss, a new pressor, a long cross-clamp, or a plan to stay intubated. Request the bed and call the ICU. Place the bed request the way the hospital does it, then give the ICU team a heads-up call: the operation, the expected arrival time, and what is coming with the patient (ventilator, drips, a CSF drain, ongoing transfusion, an open abdomen). A call before the end of the case lets the nurse set up the room. Transport with monitoring. Anesthesia usually transports with a member of the surgical team, on a transport monitor with the arterial line transduced, drips running on pumps, and oxygen or a transport ventilator. A CSF drain is clamped for the move. Hand off at the bedside. Monitors and drips are connected first, then the structured handoff happens with everyone present. See the OR-to-ICU handoff . Reconcile the admission orders. The ICU admission PowerPlan and the surgical post-operative orders must agree. Check that the vascular items are there (pulse checks, blood pressure range, drain orders, aspirin and statin, the antibiotic stop time), that home medications such as beta-blockers and anticoagulants are addressed, and that nothing is ordered twice. See admission orders . Update the family. The surgeon usually speaks to the family after the case. Make sure they know the patient is going to the CTV ICU, when they can visit, and who will call them if something changes overnight. Do the post-op check. A focused exam a few hours after arrival: perfusion compared with the end-of-case exam, the incisions, drains, urine output and the first set of labs. A template is on History, exam and charting . Direct admissions from the ED or a consult Some vascular patients reach the CTV ICU without passing through the operating room: an acute type B dissection that needs impulse control, a symptomatic aneurysm awaiting repair, a patient with acute limb ischemia on heparin who needs hourly checks, or a transfer from another hospital. The resident's job is to make the calls early and to make the plan explicit. Call the vascular attending, through your senior if that is the service norm, with a one-line summary, the vitals and the imaging, and agree on the plan and the targets. Call the CTV ICU team (the intensivist or fellow) to request the bed. Give the same summary, the drips that are running, and the targets you want, for example a heart rate and systolic pressure for a dissection. Start the time-critical treatment while the bed is found: impulse control and an arterial line for a dissection, heparin for acute limb ischemia, and analgesia, because pain drives heart rate and pressure. Write the note with the surgical plan and the call parameters, and make sure everyone knows which service is primary. Consult note templates are on History, exam and charting . Red flag: a rupture goes to the OR, not the ICU A ruptured or unstable aneurysm goes straight to the operating room or hybrid suite with permissive hypotension and blood on the way. Never let a bed request or an ICU admission delay the repair; the ICU bed is arranged for after it. See ruptured AAA . Confirm locally How ICU beds are requested, whether ED and outside-hospital transfers go through a transfer center or directly to the intensivist, which service admits a medically managed type B dissection, and who writes the admission orders."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"OR-to-ICU handoff","u":"ctv-icu.html#handoff","x":"The OR-to-ICU handoff The handoff is the moment information is most easily lost. A structured bedside handoff with the surgeon, anesthesia, the ICU nurse and the intensivist all present catches the omissions that cause harm overnight. Structured OR-to-ICU handoffs reduce missed information and early errors. The pattern most units use is equipment first, then a short protected period in which one person speaks at a time and nobody else works on the patient unless something is urgent, then questions, then a spoken summary of the plan and the targets. Monitors, lines and drips transferred first. ICU nurse and anesthesia Arterial line transduced on the ICU monitor, drips confirmed on ICU pumps, ventilator connected. Only then start talking. Anesthesia report. Anesthesia Airway and ventilation, lines, fluids and blood products, vasoactives, hemodynamic events, analgesia including any epidural, last antibiotic dose, and reversal agents given. Surgical report. Surgeon, often the resident The items in the template below: what was done, what was found, what worries you, the targets, and what the vascular team wants to be called for. Nursing questions and bedside checks. ICU nurse Drains identified and labeled, CSF drain and epidural told apart, dressings and skin checked, pulse sites marked, and the Doppler signals confirmed together at the bedside. Plan and read-back. Intensivist Summarizes the plan and the targets; the surgeon confirms or corrects; anyone with a concern says it now. Who to call. Surgeon The name and number of the vascular team member covering tonight. OR-to-ICU handoff: surgical items Patient: ___ Age: __ Weight: __ kg Allergies: ___ Operation: ________________________________ Indication and key findings: _______________ Complications or surprises: ________________ Aorta Cross-clamp level: [infrarenal / suprarenal / supraceliac] Cross-clamp time: ___ min Renal ischemia: ___ min Visceral ischemia: ___ min Reimplanted or sacrificed: [IMA / renals / hypogastrics] Blood and fluids EBL: ___ mL Cell saver returned: ___ mL Crystalloid: ___ L Other: ___ Products: PRBC __ FFP __ Plt __ Cryo __ Urine output: ___ mL total, last hour ___ mL Anticoagulation in the OR Heparin: ___ units total, last at __:__ Last ACT: ___ s Protamine: [no / yes, ___ mg] Hemodynamics Vasoactives now: ___________ Trend: [up / down / off] SBP goal: ___ to ___ MAP floor: ___ Limbs at end of case (and baseline before surgery) R: Fem ___ DP ___ PT ___ (baseline ___) L: Fem ___ DP ___ PT ___ (baseline ___) Doppler sites marked on skin: [yes / no] Drains and catheters Surgical drains: [site, type] Output so far: ___ CSF drain: [no / yes] Opening pressure: ___ mmHg CSF pressure goal: ___ Max drainage: ___ mL/h Epidural: [no / yes, level, infusion] Plan Antiplatelet: ___ Statin: ___ Anticoagulation: ___ (start when, any bolus) Antibiotic: ___ stop by __:__ (within 24 h) Diet / NG: ___ Labs or imaging due: ___ Call vascular for: [loss of signal, bleeding, drain > ___ mL/h, SBP out of range, leg weakness, neck swelling, bloody stool, low UOP] Vascular contact tonight: ___ Resident tip Before you leave the OR, write the cross-clamp position and time, the renal and visceral ischemia times, the blood products and the end-of-case Doppler signals on your patient list. They answer most of the questions you will get at 2 a.m., when the urine output drops, the lactate climbs or the nurse thinks the foot looks cool. Why does the ICU team care where the aortic clamp was and for how long? The clamp position tells you which organs were ischemic. A suprarenal clamp makes the kidneys ischemic for the clamp time; a supraceliac clamp adds the liver and gut. Longer ischemia means more risk of acute kidney injury, lactic acidosis, coagulopathy and a larger load of potassium and acid at unclamping. A long infrarenal clamp means more ischemia and reperfusion of the pelvis and legs, and the colon is at risk if the inferior mesenteric and internal iliac arteries were sacrificed. It also frames the night. A modest creatinine rise after a suprarenal clamp is common and often transient, whereas oliguria after a short infrarenal clamp needs another explanation."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"Admission orders","u":"ctv-icu.html#orders","x":"Admission orders: what the vascular team cares about The ICU admission PowerPlan covers the critical care basics. Your job is to make sure the vascular items are in the orders, correct for this operation, and not duplicated by a second set. Use the template as a check of the combined orders rather than a script. Most items follow ICU or hospital protocol; the bracketed ones are the surgeon's call for this patient. Who actually enters each order depends on the unit's model. Vascular items in the ICU admission orders Operation: ____________ Attending: ________ POD 0 Vascular checks Pulse / Doppler checks [sites] q__h x __ h, then q__h Doppler sites marked; call for loss or change of signal Incision, groin or neck checks with each vital set Compartment checks [which legs] q__h if reperfused Neuro Neuro checks q1h [carotid: face, speech, grip] Leg motor and sensory checks q1h [cord at risk] Sedation light enough to examine, per ICU Cardiovascular SBP goal ___ to ___ mmHg; call if out of range MAP floor ___ mmHg [cord at risk: about 80-90+] Telemetry; ECG and troponin per protocol Continue home beta-blocker, with hold parameters Vasoactives per ICU, titrated to the goals above Respiratory Ventilator and extubation per ICU Incentive spirometry once extubated Renal, fluids and labs Foley, strict I/O; UOP goal >= 0.5 mL/kg/h Bladder pressure q__h [rupture, open abdomen] On arrival: CBC, BMP, Mg, ionized Ca, PT/INR, PTT, fibrinogen, lactate, ABG if ventilated Then: CBC and BMP q__h; lactate until normal CK q__h [revascularized limb] Active type and screen GI NPO [ice chips?]; NG to low suction [yes / no] Stress ulcer prophylaxis if indicated, per ICU Bowel regimen when appropriate Antithrombotics and VTE Aspirin ___ mg daily (continue unless told) P2Y12 inhibitor [stents, TCAR]: ___ Statin, high intensity, daily Therapeutic heparin: [no / start ___, bolus?] VTE prophylaxis: [SQ heparin / LMWH] start ___ (timing with CSF drain or epidural per protocol) SCDs [which legs] Infection [Cefazolin] last dose __:__; stop within 24 h Drains are not a reason to continue antibiotics Endocrine Glucose goal 140-180 mg/dL; insulin per protocol Pain Epidural / PCA / multimodal per anesthesia, ICU [Avoid motor block if legs need neuro checks] Drains [Site]: record q__h; call if > ___ mL/h CSF drain: per CSF drain protocol and orders Activity [Bed rest / HOB __ / flat __ h after sheath out] Out of bed POD __ if stable Call vascular for Loss or change of a pulse or Doppler signal New neuro deficit (face, arm, speech or legs) Neck swelling, bleeding, drain > ___ mL/h Hgb drop > ___, UOP < ___ for 2 h, bloody stool Bladder pressure > ___, tense compartments Aspirin and statin continue. Nearly every vascular patient stays on aspirin and a statin through surgery; stopping them raises perioperative cardiovascular events. Patients with a recent stent or a TCAR also need their P2Y12 inhibitor, and only the surgeon stops it. Beta-blockers continue, they do not start. Continue a beta-blocker the patient already takes, with hold parameters for bradycardia and hypotension. Do not start one on the day of surgery. VTE prophylaxis starts when hemostasis is secure, usually within about 24 hours and on the surgeon's word. Around a CSF drain or epidural catheter, the timing of each dose follows the neuraxial protocol. Mechanical prophylaxis goes on legs without fresh incisions, fasciotomies or ischemia. Stress ulcer prophylaxis is for patients with risk factors, such as mechanical ventilation, coagulopathy or shock, per ICU practice, and stops when they resolve. Perioperative antibiotics stop within 24 hours. Longer courses do not prevent graft or wound infection and add resistance and C. difficile . Treat a real infection as a real infection, with cultures. Glucose goal is about 140 to 180 mg/dL. Tighter control in ICU patients caused more hypoglycemia without benefit. Insulin infusions run per ICU protocol. Resident tip An order for hourly pulse checks is only as good as the baseline. At the handoff, Doppler the feet with the bedside nurse, mark each signal on the skin with a skin marker, and write what you heard (\"R DP biphasic, PT monophasic, L DP absent at baseline\") in the orders or the post-op note. It prevents a 3 a.m. call about a signal that was never there. Why continue a beta-blocker the patient already takes, but not start one on the morning of surgery? Stopping a chronic beta-blocker causes rebound tachycardia and hypertension and can provoke myocardial ischemia, so it is continued with hold parameters. Starting one acutely is a different matter: in the POISE trial, high-dose metoprolol begun just before noncardiac surgery reduced myocardial infarction but increased stroke and death, largely through hypotension and bradycardia. If a beta-blocker is indicated for a new reason, it is started well before surgery and titrated, not on the day."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"Watch by operation","u":"ctv-icu.html#by-operation","x":"What to watch, by operation High yield Each operation has its own first-night failure modes. Know the list for your patient's operation before the patient arrives, and say it out loud at the handoff so the ICU team watches for the same things you are. Open AAA repair The operation is described in Open operations . The first 48 hours are about blood, kidneys, gut, legs and heart. Bleeding. Falling hemoglobin, tachycardia, a rising pressor requirement or a distending abdomen. Check platelets, fibrinogen, coagulation studies, temperature and ionized calcium, correct what is abnormal, and call early; ongoing transfusion despite corrected coagulation means a conversation about returning to the OR. Urine output and the kidneys. Aim for at least 0.5 mL/kg/h. Oliguria has a differential: hypovolemia or bleeding, renal ischemia from a suprarenal clamp or embolization, a blocked or kinked Foley, rising abdominal pressure, low cardiac output, and rarely a ureteral injury. Flush the Foley before giving fluid blindly. Abdominal compartment syndrome. Suspect it when the abdomen is tight, airway pressures rise, urine output falls or the patient needed large-volume resuscitation, and measure a bladder pressure. Intra-abdominal hypertension is a sustained pressure of 12 mmHg or more; abdominal compartment syndrome is a sustained pressure over 20 mmHg with new organ dysfunction. The definitive treatment is decompressive laparotomy, so call at once. Colon ischemia. The left colon and sigmoid depend on the inferior mesenteric artery and internal iliac collaterals. Bloody stool, or any early diarrhea, unexplained acidosis, a rising lactate, fever, leukocytosis, distension or a growing fluid requirement should prompt a bedside flexible sigmoidoscopy without delay. Mucosal ischemia is managed with bowel rest, fluids, antibiotics and a repeat scope; transmural necrosis needs resection, which is why early diagnosis matters. Lower limb ischemia and trash foot. Compare the feet with the end-of-case exam. A lost signal suggests limb thrombosis or a problem at the distal anastomosis; mottled, painful toes with palpable pulses suggest distal microembolization (trash foot). Either way, call. Myocardial infarction. It remains the leading cause of death after open aneurysm repair and is often silent under sedation and an epidural. Watch the rhythm, the ST segments and the hemodynamics, and check troponin per protocol. New atrial fibrillation is common. Ileus. Expected after a transperitoneal repair and less marked after a retroperitoneal one. Keep the patient NPO with an NG tube if the surgeon wants one, and advance the diet on the surgeon's word. Hyperkalemia and acidosis. From reperfusion of the pelvis and legs, transfusion and kidney injury. Check potassium on arrival and repeat it. Red flag: bloody stool after aortic surgery Bloody diarrhea after aortic repair is colon ischemia until a sigmoidoscopy says otherwise. Tell the vascular team the same hour, not on morning rounds. Transmural colon necrosis after aortic surgery carries a very high mortality. Ruptured AAA Highest risk Everything that can go wrong after an elective repair is more likely after a rupture, whether the repair was open or endovascular. Three problems dominate the first 48 hours. The effects of massive transfusion. Patients arrive cold, acidotic and coagulopathic. Keep warming, replace calcium (citrate in stored blood binds it), and guide further products with labs or viscoelastic testing per protocol. Ongoing bleeding once coagulation is corrected means the OR. Abdominal compartment syndrome. Common after both open repair and EVAR for rupture: the retroperitoneal hematoma stays in place after EVAR, resuscitation swells the bowel, and an open repair may be closed under tension or left open by plan. Bladder pressures are measured on a schedule (commonly every 4 to 6 hours for the first day or two, per protocol) and whenever airway pressures rise or urine output falls. Colon ischemia. Several times more common than after elective repair. Most teams have a low threshold for sigmoidoscopy, and some scope every ruptured patient in the first day or two. Organ failure. Acute kidney injury (sometimes needing dialysis), prolonged ventilation and multiorgan failure are common, and many of these patients have long ICU stays. After EVAR specifically. Check the large-bore access sites and both legs. A falling hemoglobin may be access-site bleeding or continued filling of the sac through a type I or III endoleak; call rather than simply transfusing. TEVAR and complex EVAR The devices and steps are in Open operations and Endovascular . Spinal cord ischemia. Risk rises with the length of thoracic aorta covered, prior aortic repair, coverage of the left subclavian artery without revascularization, occluded internal iliac arteries, and perioperative hypotension. It can appear on waking or hours to days later, often after a hypotensive episode. Hourly leg checks and a MAP floor are the defense; the rescue is in CSF drains and spinal cord protection . Stroke. From wire and device manipulation in the arch, or posterior circulation ischemia after left subclavian coverage. Check the full neuro exam, not only the legs, and compare the arm pulses and pressures. Access complications. Large femoral or iliac sheaths cause groin hematoma, pseudoaneurysm, limb ischemia from dissection or thrombosis, and retroperitoneal bleeding (back or flank pain, falling hemoglobin, hypotension with an unimpressive groin). Brachial or axillary access, common for branched cases, can bleed into the arm and compress the median nerve; new hand numbness or weakness after arm access needs the surgeon urgently. Target vessels after fenestrated or branched repair. A renal or visceral stent can occlude. Watch urine output, creatinine, lactate and abdominal pain, and the contrast load on the kidneys. Post-implantation syndrome. Fever, leukocytosis and a raised CRP in the first days without infection, "},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"CSF drains","u":"ctv-icu.html#spinal-drain","x":"CSF drains and spinal cord protection A lumbar CSF drain protects the spinal cord after extensive thoracic and thoracoabdominal aortic repair by lowering the pressure around the cord. It is also the most protocol-driven device in the CTV ICU, so learn your unit's protocol and treat every number below as typical, not local. Why it works Spinal cord perfusion pressure is roughly the mean arterial pressure minus the CSF pressure (or minus the central venous pressure, if that is higher). When an aortic repair covers or sacrifices intercostal and lumbar arteries, the cord depends on a collateral network that stays pressure-dependent for days. You can raise perfusion pressure from either side: raise the MAP, or lower the CSF pressure. Drains are usually placed by anesthesia before the operation, for open thoracoabdominal repair and for TEVAR or fenestrated and branched repair when the spinal cord risk is high: long coverage, prior aortic repair, or left subclavian or internal iliac arteries that are covered or occluded. Some centers place them selectively, or only as a rescue once a deficit appears. 10 to 12 mmHg Typical CSF pressure goal; some protocols use 10 or lower, and lower during a rescue MAP 80 to 90+ Typical MAP floor while the cord is at risk 10 to 15 mL/h Common hourly drainage cap; more than the cap means clamp and call Every hour Leg motor and sensory checks while the drain is in 48 to 72 h Typical time before a clamp trial and removal Note These are typical published values, not orders. Units differ in the targets, in the reference level, and in whether pressure is recorded in mmHg or cm H 2 O (1 mmHg is about 1.36 cm H 2 O, so 10 mmHg is about 14 cm H 2 O). Follow the local protocol and this patient's written orders. Bedside rules Level and zero. The transducer and drip chamber are leveled at the reference point the protocol names, commonly the tragus, and re-leveled whenever the head of the bed or the patient's position changes. Some protocols use a different reference, so check. Clamp for every move. Clamp the drain for transport, repositioning and head-of-bed changes, then re-level and unclamp. An open drain that suddenly ends up below the patient drains fast. Record every hour: CSF pressure, the volume drained, and the color of the CSF. Check the legs every hour. Hip flexion, knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion and plantar flexion on each side, graded the same way each time, plus sensation. Compare with the last check and with the baseline. Never let the MAP fall below the floor, including during sedation, dialysis, transport or antihypertensive dosing. Know which catheter is which. A patient may have both an epidural and a CSF drain in the back. Both are labeled, and nothing is injected into a CSF drain. Watch for the epidural confounder. A local-anesthetic epidural can itself cause leg weakness, which is why many protocols use dilute or opioid-only infusions in these patients. New weakness is treated as cord ischemia until proven otherwise. New leg weakness: the rescue Emergency Delayed spinal cord ischemia can improve, sometimes completely, if treated within hours. Every step below happens at once, not in sequence over an afternoon. Call immediately. Call the vascular team and the ICU team with the time of onset and the exact exam. Do not wait for the next scheduled check to confirm it. Raise the MAP. Fluids and vasopressors to a higher target, commonly 90 to 100 mmHg or more, per protocol and the team. Stop any antihypertensive. Drain CSF. Open the drain and lower the pressure goal (often to about 8 to 10 mmHg) within the protocol's volume limits. If no drain is in, the team will discuss placing one urgently. Optimize oxygen delivery. Many protocols transfuse to a hemoglobin of about 10 g/dL in a rescue, alongside good oxygenation and adequate cardiac output. Look for other causes. Stop a local-anesthetic epidural infusion. Consider a spinal or epidural hematoma, especially after drain insertion or manipulation or with anticoagulation, which needs urgent imaging. Consider a stroke if the deficit is not confined to the legs. Reassess and document. Recheck the legs frequently and record the response to each step; the team decides how long to hold the higher targets. Complications of the drain Overdrainage and intracranial bleeding Draining too much CSF too fast can tear bridging veins and cause a subdural or other intracranial hemorrhage. Headache, falling consciousness or new focal signs mean clamp the drain, call, and expect a CT head. Headache A positional, post-dural puncture headache is common and usually managed conservatively. A severe or changing headache needs the overdrainage questions above. Bloody CSF New blood-tinged or frankly bloody CSF can signal intracranial or spinal bleeding. Clamp, call, check the neuro exam and coagulation, and let the team decide on imaging. Spinal or epidural hematoma Rare but devastating, usually around insertion or removal or with anticoagulation. It mimics cord ischemia: new leg weakness or numbness, sometimes back pain. Urgent MRI and spine surgery evaluation. Infection Meningitis risk rises with time, which is one reason drains come out promptly. Fever, neck stiffness, confusion or cloudy CSF. Catheter problems Occlusion, dislodgement, disconnection (clamp, cover, call) and catheter fracture at removal. Whoever removes the drain confirms and documents that the tip is intact. Removal Drains typically stay 48 to 72 hours. If the legs are intact, the drain is clamped for a trial period (often 12 to 24 hours) with neuro checks continuing, and removed if no deficit appears. Before removal the protocol checks coagulation and platelets and the timing of every anticoagulant dose, usually based on ASRA neuraxial guidance: prophylactic and therapeutic heparin, LMWH and P2Y12 inhibitors all have rules, while aspirin alone usually does not. Neuro checks continue for a period after removal, because a hematoma at removal causes new weakness t"},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"Daily rounding","u":"ctv-icu.html#daily","x":"Rounding on vascular patients in the ICU Two teams round on the same patient. The vascular team's job each morning is to examine what only it owns, set the surgical plan, and make sure the ICU team hears that plan before the day's orders go in. What the vascular team checks every day Perfusion Pulses and Doppler signals at each level, compared with the end-of-case baseline; a graft pulse if it is palpable; the color and temperature of the feet. Incisions and wounds The abdomen, groins, neck and fasciotomy wounds, under the dressing when it matters, and the seal and output of any wound vac. Drains Volume and character (bloody, serous, milky, the color of the CSF) and the plan for removal. The operation-specific neuro exam Face, speech and grip after carotid work; leg strength while the cord is at risk. The gut Abdominal exam, distension, flatus or stool and any blood, and the bladder pressure trend if it is being measured. Kidneys and potassium Urine output, creatinine and potassium, and CK after a reperfused limb. Antithrombotics Aspirin, statin, any P2Y12 inhibitor, the heparin plan, and anything held for a procedure or for drain or epidural removal. The surgical plan Imaging due, return to the OR, diet, activity, and readiness for step-down. Communicating the plan Talk to the ICU team, not just the chart. After you round, or by joining ICU rounds for your patients, give the ICU resident, fellow or intensivist the vascular plan in a few sentences: the targets, what to call for, and what changes today. Write the plan as targets. \"SBP 100 to 140, MAP at least 85 until the drain is out, Doppler checks every hour, call for loss of the DP signal\" is actionable. \"BP control\" is not. Progress note templates are on History, exam and charting . One owner per order. In a closed or collaborative unit, do not place orders for drips, fluids, electrolytes or the ventilator; ask the ICU team. When a vascular order needs to change, such as starting heparin, agree who enters it. Duplicate heparin orders, two sets of blood pressure parameters, and a VTE prophylaxis dose that collides with a planned drain removal are the classic errors. Tell the bedside nurse. The nurse does the hourly checks, so the nurse needs the baseline, the targets and the call parameters. Close the loop on overnight events. Read the ICU note, and if a pulse or neuro check was called overnight, examine the patient yourself. Agree who updates the family, so they hear one consistent story from both teams. Resident tip Before you leave the unit each morning, say the vascular plan out loud to the ICU resident and to the bedside nurse, one sentence each: the targets, what to call for, and anything held or started. Thirty seconds at the bedside prevents an afternoon of conflicting orders."},{"p":"The CTV ICU","t":"Transfer out","u":"ctv-icu.html#transfer-out","x":"Transfer to step-down or floor Leaving the ICU is a handoff in the other direction, and it is where vascular details get lost: the Doppler baseline, the antiplatelet plan, the blood pressure targets. Typical readiness criteria Off vasoactive drips. Or on a dose the receiving unit can manage under local policy, with blood pressure in range on oral agents. Breathing comfortably. Extubated, with oxygen needs the floor can meet. Stable hemoglobin. No ongoing transfusion and no sign of active bleeding. Adequate urine output. Creatinine stable or improving, potassium controlled. Lactate normal and abdomen settled. Bladder pressures no longer needed. Drains out or stable. CSF drain removed with the post-removal neuro checks complete and the legs unchanged; surgical drains removed or with low, stable output. Neuro exam stable. Especially after carotid surgery and when the spinal cord was at risk. Pain controlled. On a regimen the receiving unit can deliver. Checks the unit can do. Pulse and neuro checks needed no more often than the receiving unit's nurses can perform them. What to update in the transfer note Write a fresh exam, not the one from the post-op check three days ago. The receiving team needs the operation, the ICU course in a few lines, today's perfusion exam against the baseline, and the plan in targets. ICU transfer: vascular items Operation and date: ____________ POD __ Key intraop facts: clamp ___, EBL ___, issues ___ ICU course: [drips, transfusion, complications] Exam today vs baseline Pulses / Doppler: R ___ L ___ (baseline ___) Incisions, wounds, fasciotomies: ___ Neuro: ___ Lines and drains: [removed / remaining, plan] Antiplatelet: ___ Statin: ___ Anticoagulation: ___ (start, hold, monitoring) VTE prophylaxis: ___ BP goal: ___ Beta-blocker: ___ Diet: ___ Bowel function: ___ Activity: ___ PT/OT: ___ Floor checks: pulses q__h, neuro q__h Pending: [imaging, labs, repeat scope, etc.] Call vascular for: ___ Disposition plan: ___ Confirm locally Which drips and monitoring the step-down unit accepts, who writes the transfer orders (the ICU team, the vascular team, or both), and who becomes primary on the floor. Night calls about these patients once they reach the floor are covered on On call and cross-cover , and discharge planning on History, exam and charting ."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"On call and cross-cover","u":"on-call.html","x":"How to triage the vascular pager overnight, the calls that come up again and again with the first moves for each, and when to wake your senior. Read it before your first night of cross-cover, then keep it open on your phone."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"Triage on the phone","u":"on-call.html#triage","x":"Triage on the phone Most overnight pages about vascular patients come down to a short list: the limb, the incision or puncture site, the blood pressure, the urine and the heparin. Your first decision is made on the phone: how fast do you need to be at the bedside? When you are unsure, go and look. The daytime versions of the common consults (the cold leg, the foot ulcer, the incidental aneurysm, the carotid stenosis, the venous consult) are on Rounds and presenting . This page covers the calls about patients already on the service, or just back from the operating room, the angiography suite or the CTV ICU . How fast to go Category Typical pages Your move Come now A pulse, graft pulse or Doppler signal that was present and is now gone; a cold, pale, mottled, numb or weak limb; neck swelling, stridor or trouble swallowing after carotid surgery; any new neurologic deficit, including leg weakness after aortic repair; bleeding that pressure is not controlling, or a growing hematoma; hypotension, especially with back, flank or abdominal pain; chest pain; bloody diarrhea after aortic surgery; a tense, painful calf after revascularization; bleeding from the skin over a fistula; any rapid response criteria. Give a holding instruction (hold pressure, sit the patient up, oxygen, call a rapid response), say you are on your way, and go. Call your senior as soon as you have seen the patient, or before if anything will delay you. See within the hour A small, stable groin hematoma with pressure held; blood pressure outside the ordered range without symptoms; urine output below target; a new fever after surgery; new atrial fibrillation with a stable blood pressure; a falling platelet count on heparin; a fistula or graft with no thrill; a dusky incision edge; a wound vac that will not hold a seal. Give the first orders by phone (labs, an ECG, a bladder scan), then see the patient. Decide at the bedside whether it has become a come-now problem. Can wait Routine heparin adjustments per the nomogram, electrolyte replacement per protocol, sleep, bowel and routine pain orders, a dry dressing that needs changing, questions about the morning plan. Handle by phone with a read-back, and see the patient on your next pass if there is any doubt. If the nurse says they are worried, it is no longer a can-wait call. The questions to ask every time Who, and what operation. Name, room, the operation and post-operative day, or the reason for admission. “POD 1 right fem-pop bypass” already tells you what to worry about. Vitals now, and the trend. Heart rate and rhythm, blood pressure against the ordered target, oxygen saturation, temperature, and urine output over the last few hours. The limb. Color, temperature, pain, movement and sensation, and whether the pulse or Doppler signal matches the baseline marked after surgery. The incision or access site. Swelling, bleeding, a soaked dressing, a new lump or a pulsatile mass. What changed, and when. When the patient was last normal, what has been given or stopped (heparin, antihypertensives, opioids), and whether this is sudden or has been building all evening. “What are you worried about?” A nurse who has been in the room all shift often knows something is wrong before the numbers show it. Treat that as data. Closed-loop orders Every order given by phone, or at a bedside in a hurry, is a chance for error. Close the loop: say the order, have the nurse read it back, confirm it, and then enter it in PowerChart yourself as soon as you can. Give parameters, not adjectives. “Call me if the systolic is above 160 or below 100” can be acted on; “call me if anything changes” cannot. Resident tip End every phone call with three things: when you will be there, what the nurse should do until you arrive, and the number that should make them call again. “I'll be there in ten minutes. Keep firm pressure just above the puncture, recheck the blood pressure every five minutes, and call a rapid response if the systolic drops below 90.” Then set a timer on your phone for any reassessment you promised. SBAR: how to call your senior Nurses page you in SBAR, and your senior wants the same structure from you. It forces you to commit to an assessment and to a specific request. Situation Who you are, which patient, and the problem in one sentence. Background The operation and post-operative day, the baseline exam, the anticoagulation, and anything relevant from the day. Assessment What you found at the bedside and what you think it is. Say it even if you are unsure: “I'm worried the graft has thrombosed.” Recommendation What you have done and what you need: an order, a decision, or the senior at the bedside. Calling your senior at 3 a.m. “This is [name] , covering vascular tonight. I'm calling about [patient] , post-operative day [1] from a [right fem-pop bypass with reversed vein] . The nurse called because the [dorsalis pedis] signal marked after surgery is gone. I've examined the leg: the foot is [cool to the ankle with no DP or PT signal; motor and sensation intact] , the incisions are soft, and the blood pressure is [138/72 in sinus rhythm] . The patient is [on aspirin, no heparin] . I'm worried the graft has thrombosed. I've [made the patient NPO and sent a CBC, BMP, coagulation studies and a type and screen] . Do you want a heparin bolus and drip, and are you coming in?” Document every call Write a short event note for every call that needed a bedside visit or changed management: when you were called and why, what you found, what you did, who you discussed it with and when, and when you will reassess. The day team learns what happened overnight, and the record protects both the patient and you. A one-line entry is enough for a routine order; a cold leg or a neck hematoma deserves a full note. Cross-cover event note Vascular surgery cross-cover event note Called at [time] by [RN] for: ____________ Seen at bedside at [time] Context: POD __ s/p ____________ Baseline exam per sign-out: [__] S: [what "},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"A vascular sign-out","u":"on-call.html#sign-out","x":"A vascular sign-out The night goes well or badly depending on what was handed over at the end of the day. A vascular sign-out uses the usual I-PASS structure and adds three things: the baseline limb exam, the anticoagulation, and an explicit plan for the bad call. I-PASS stands for illness severity, patient summary, action list, situation awareness and contingency planning, and synthesis by the receiver. Label each patient stable, watcher or unstable. Watchers are the patients you see on your first walk-through, before anyone calls about them. For a vascular patient, the summary and the contingencies need more detail than on most services. What must be in it Operation and post-operative day The exact operation, the side and the conduit: “POD 0 left fem-AT bypass with reversed great saphenous vein,” not “bypass.” Add anything unusual from the case: a difficult anastomosis, a shunt, a long clamp time, a large blood loss. Baseline pulse and Doppler exam What is palpable, what is present on Doppler and where those signals were marked, plus the graft pulse if there is one, with the time and who examined it: “right DP biphasic, marked with an X; PT absent at baseline.” Without this nobody can tell at 3 a.m. whether a signal is lost or was never there. Anticoagulation What is running and its target (a heparin drip with its aPTT or anti-Xa range, or prophylaxis only), the antiplatelets, whether protamine was given in the operating room, and anything held for a procedure with its restart time. Blood pressure target Specific numbers, the reason and the drug to use: a systolic ceiling after carotid surgery, a mean arterial pressure floor with a spinal drain or a fresh graft. Drains and lines Each drain, its location, the output so far, and how much would be too much. Arterial and central lines, and where heparin flushes are running. CSF drain, if present The drainage parameters from the order (the pressure threshold and the maximum volume per hour), the leveling, the frequency of neurologic checks, and who adjusts it. See New leg weakness . Labs to follow The timed hemoglobin, the next aPTT or anti-Xa, the potassium after reperfusion, the platelet count on heparin, and the creatinine after contrast. Contingencies If-then statements with a name attached: “If the graft pulse is lost, examine, make NPO, send labs and call Dr. ___ directly.” “If the neck swells, call immediately.” Plus the code status. Vascular sign-out (I-PASS) Illness severity: stable / watcher / unstable Patient: [name], [age], room [__], code status [__] Operation: POD __ s/p [side] [operation], conduit [__] Case notes: [shunt, clamp time, EBL, hard anastomosis] Baseline exam at [time] by [who] (P palp, D Doppler, 0 none) R: fem __ pop __ DP __ PT __ graft __ L: fem __ pop __ DP __ PT __ graft __ Marked signals: [e.g. R DP and graft, X on skin] Incision / access site: [soft, no hematoma; closure device] Anticoagulation: [heparin gtt __ U/kg/h, target __ / ppx] Antiplatelet, statin: [__] Protamine in OR: [y/n] Held for procedure: [__], restart at [__] BP target: SBP __ to __ / MAP > __ PRN: [__] Drains, lines: [type, site, output __, call if > __ mL/h] CSF drain: [none / level __, drain if > __ mmHg, max __ mL/h] Labs to follow: [Hb at __, K, aPTT or anti-Xa at __, plt] Action list: [ ] ____________ [ ] ____________ If / then: Graft pulse or signal lost: examine, NPO, labs, call [__] BP outside target: [__] [Neck swelling / leg weakness / UO < __]: call [__] Synthesis: receiver read back [ ] Resident tip At the end of every revascularization, mark each Doppler signal on the skin with a marker and an X, and write the baseline into the sign-out in words. That one line (“right DP and PT biphasic, both marked; graft pulse palpable above the knee”) is what lets the night team tell a lost signal from one that was never there. The post-op check is the natural place to record it. Confirm locally How and when the vascular service signs out (a written list in PowerChart, a verbal handoff at a set time, or both), who carries the vascular pager overnight (a vascular resident taking call from home, a surgery night float, or an in-house senior), and who covers the patients in the CTV ICU. In many academic programs the night intern or cross-cover resident takes floor calls first, and the vascular senior or fellow is the next call. The ICU handoff is covered in The CTV ICU ."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"Lost pulse or cold leg","u":"on-call.html#cold-leg","x":"Lost pulse or cold leg after bypass or intervention Emergency The call you never sit on. A pulse, graft pulse or Doppler signal that was present after surgery and is now gone means the revascularization may have failed, and the window to save it is measured in hours. Go now, compare with the baseline, and call. The page. “The foot feels cold and I can't find the pulse you marked,” or “I can't get the DP on Doppler anymore.” Ask first. “Was that signal there at the last check, and when was that?” Then: is the leg painful, numb or weak, what are the blood pressure and rhythm, and is heparin running? Tell the nurse you are coming now, to keep the leg level and warm with blankets (no heating pads on an ischemic limb), and to give nothing by mouth until you have seen the patient. At the bedside Compare with the documented baseline. Read the sign-out and the post-op check, then examine the leg yourself. A signal that was never there is not a lost signal, and a hurried Doppler can miss one that is present. If the chart is silent and the foot is cold, assume the change is new. Doppler properly, both legs. Use plenty of gel at the marked spot, angle the probe at about 45 to 60 degrees to the vessel, and check the DP, the PT, the peroneal, and the graft itself where it runs superficially. A change in character (biphasic to monophasic) matters, not just present or absent. Use the other leg as the control. Look at the whole leg. Color, temperature and where it changes, capillary refill, motor function (toe and ankle movement), sensation (including the first web space), and the calf compartments: tense, tender, painful on passive stretch. Check the incisions and the graft tunnel for a hematoma. After an endovascular case, look at the access site for a hematoma, a pseudoaneurysm or loss of the femoral pulse. Check the rest of the patient. Blood pressure and heart rate, the rhythm (new atrial fibrillation suggests an embolus), volume status and urine output. Send a CBC with platelets, a BMP for potassium, coagulation studies, a type and screen and a lactate, plus a CK if the leg has been ischemic for a while. Call your senior from the bedside. Give the finding, the baseline and the time course. Heparin (commonly an 80 U/kg bolus, then an infusion per the nomogram) is usually started for a suspected acute occlusion, but in a fresh post-operative patient that is the senior's call, weighed against bleeding. Keep the patient NPO for a possible return to the operating room or the angiography suite. What it might be Graft or stent thrombosis Abrupt loss of the graft pulse and the distal signals. Failure in the first days is usually technical: a kink, a twist, a retained valve cusp, an intimal flap, a clamp injury or poor runoff. If the patient has been on heparin and the platelets are falling, think of HIT. Embolus Sudden onset, often with new atrial fibrillation, or debris dislodged during the procedure. Patchy mottling of the toes with palpable pedal pulses after aortic or endovascular work is microembolization (“trash foot”). An access-site problem After an endovascular case: thrombosis or dissection at the puncture, a closure device that has narrowed or occluded the common femoral artery, or a hematoma compressing the vessel. Compartment syndrome After reperfusion of a limb that was ischemic for hours: pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, tense compartments and numbness. Pulses are often still present. Low flow state Hypotension, low cardiac output, hypovolemia, vasopressors or hypothermia soften the signals in both legs. Correct the pressure and recheck quickly, remembering that a graft with low flow can also clot. A change in one leg only is not explained by low flow. When to call: now Any loss of a previously present pulse, graft pulse or Doppler signal, or a cold, pale, numb or weak foot after revascularization: call your senior from the bedside, immediately. Do not wait for a duplex, a CT or the morning team. Imaging, heparin and a return to the operating room are decisions to make with the senior, quickly. Pearl: compartment syndrome does not wait for the pulse After revascularizing a limb that was ischemic for hours, a tense, painful calf with pain on passive stretch is compartment syndrome until proven otherwise, even with palpable pulses. Pulselessness is a late sign. Measured pressures can support the diagnosis (a gap between the diastolic blood pressure and the compartment pressure of less than about 30 mmHg), but the decision for a four-compartment fasciotomy is clinical. Watch the potassium, the CK and the urine color for reperfusion injury. See Fasciotomy and Acute limb ischemia . On the first night after a fem-pop bypass, the foot is cool and the DP signal is faint, and the blood pressure is 78/40. Is this graft failure or low flow? It could be either, and the low pressure can itself make the graft clot. Treat the hypotension and look for its cause, bleeding first (the incisions, the tunnel, the hemoglobin). Compare both legs and recheck the signals as soon as the pressure recovers. Call your senior now, not after the fluid bolus: if the signal does not come back with a normal blood pressure, or only the operated leg is affected, assume the graft has thrombosed."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"Groin bleeding and hematoma","u":"on-call.html#groin","x":"Groin bleeding, hematoma or a new mass after access Most endovascular complications happen at the puncture, and many of them are found overnight. Good manual pressure stops most groin bleeding. The dangerous bleed is the one you cannot see. The page. “The groin is oozing,” “the dressing is soaked,” or “there's a lump in the groin after the angiogram.” Ask first. “Is it bleeding right now, and is someone holding pressure?” If it is bleeding, tell the nurse to press firmly with the fingers just above the puncture until you arrive. Then ask for the vitals, which side, the procedure and the sheath size, whether a closure device was used or a sheath is still in, and what anticoagulants, antiplatelets or lytics are running. Manual pressure, done properly Find the arteriotomy, not the skin hole. The needle enters the skin and travels upward at an angle, so the hole in the artery lies above the skin puncture, typically 1 to 2 cm higher. Feel for the femoral pulse just above the skin nick and press there, over the femoral head. Press with your fingers. Use the fingertips of two or three fingers, or both hands stacked, firmly enough to stop the bleeding. Once it has stopped, ease off just enough for a distal pulse or Doppler signal to return, so the leg is not ischemic for the whole hold. Hold for about 15 to 20 minutes without peeking. Watch the clock; it always feels longer than it is. Hold longer if the patient is anticoagulated, the sheath was large, or the bleeding restarts. Release slowly and look. Check the site, the distal pulses and the vitals. If it bleeds again, hold again and call your senior. Do not rely on a sandbag. A sandbag or a pressure dressing is no substitute for direct manual pressure, and a bulky dressing can hide a growing hematoma. Some units use mechanical compression devices per local protocol. At the bedside Mark the hematoma. Outline its edges with a skin marker and write the time, so the next person can tell whether it is growing. Check the distal pulses. Compare with the baseline. A large hematoma can compress the artery, and the puncture site can thrombose. Send labs. Hemoglobin (repeat it in a few hours, because the first value lags behind the bleeding), platelets, coagulation studies and a type and screen. Keep the patient flat with the leg straight, per the post-procedure bed rest order. Raise reversal with the senior. Pausing a heparin infusion, giving protamine for procedural heparin, and holding antiplatelets are the senior's decisions, weighed against thrombosis of a fresh stent or graft. Never stop dual antiplatelet therapy after a new stent on your own. Bleeding on a lytic infusion. If the patient is on catheter-directed thrombolysis, anything more than minor oozing at the sheath means stopping the lytic, holding pressure, sending a fibrinogen with the CBC and coagulation studies, and calling immediately. See Thrombolysis . What it might be Groin problems after femoral access Problem Clues Next step Hematoma Local swelling and bruising at the puncture, with stable vitals and pulses Pressure, mark the edges, serial exams and hemoglobin. Call if it grows or the patient becomes unstable Retroperitoneal bleed Hypotension and tachycardia, back, flank or lower abdominal pain, a falling hemoglobin with a soft groin; follows a high puncture above the inguinal ligament. Sometimes thigh numbness or weakness from femoral nerve compression Call immediately. Two large-bore IVs, resuscitate, type and cross, hold anticoagulation. CT (a CT angiogram shows active bleeding) once stable enough to travel; an unstable patient goes to the angiography suite or operating room instead. May need a covered stent or surgery Pseudoaneurysm A pulsatile, tender mass with a systolic bruit; to-and-fro flow in its neck on duplex; follows a low puncture or anticoagulation Duplex. Small ones may thrombose on their own; most others are treated with ultrasound-guided thrombin injection; surgery if rapidly expanding, infected, or threatening the skin Arteriovenous fistula A continuous (machinery) bruit and a thrill at the puncture, where both the artery and the vein were punctured Duplex, usually during the day. Most are observed and many close on their own; repair if it persists, grows or causes symptoms Why puncture location predicts the complication, and the closure devices themselves, are covered in Endovascular: closure and complications . Red flag: the soft groin with a falling hemoglobin A patient who becomes hypotensive or develops back or flank pain after a femoral procedure, with a groin that looks unremarkable, has a retroperitoneal bleed until proven otherwise. The blood is tracking up behind the peritoneum, where you can neither see it nor compress it. Do not be reassured by the skin. Why do you press above the skin puncture rather than on it? The needle enters the skin and runs upward at roughly 45 degrees, so the arteriotomy sits a centimeter or two above the skin nick. Pressing on the skin hole squeezes the tract while the artery keeps bleeding into the thigh. Pressure over the arteriotomy, against the femoral head, is what stops it. The same geometry explains why a puncture above the inguinal ligament, with no bone behind it, can bleed into the retroperitoneum. When to call: now Bleeding that 15 to 20 minutes of good pressure does not control, a hematoma growing past its marked edge, hypotension or tachycardia, a suspected retroperitoneal bleed, loss of distal pulses, or any significant bleeding on a lytic infusion. A stable, marked, non-expanding hematoma with normal vitals and pulses can be reported at a natural break or at sign-out, as your senior prefers."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"After carotid surgery","u":"on-call.html#post-cea","x":"After carotid surgery: blood pressure, neck swelling, new deficit Three calls follow a carotid endarterectomy, and to a lesser degree carotid stenting or TCAR: the blood pressure is out of range, the neck is swelling, or the neurologic exam has changed. The last two are among the most time-critical calls in surgery. Whatever the page was about, look at the neck and repeat the neurologic exam. Blood pressure out of range The page. “BP is 184/92 after the carotid,” or “BP is 88/50 and the heart rate is 48.” Ask first. “What is the target in the orders, and does the patient have a headache, any neurologic change, or any neck swelling?” The surgeon sets the target, so follow the order. A common pattern is a systolic ceiling somewhere between 140 and 160 mmHg and a floor around 100, with a lower ceiling when the risk of hyperperfusion is high. Swings are common because the operation disturbs the carotid sinus baroreceptors. Hypertension. It drives both neck hematoma and hyperperfusion syndrome. Treat the causes first: pain, a full bladder, hypoxia or hypercarbia, anxiety, missed home antihypertensives. Then treat to target with the ordered agent, typically IV labetalol or hydralazine on the floor, or a nicardipine or clevidipine infusion in the ICU or step-down unit. Hypotension and bradycardia. Usually baroreceptor stimulation, more common after carotid stenting because the stent stretches the bulb. Still exclude bleeding and myocardial ischemia with an exam and an ECG. Fluids, atropine or glycopyrrolate for symptomatic bradycardia, and a vasopressor such as phenylephrine if it persists, with the senior and usually in a monitored bed. Hypotension risks stroke and myocardial ischemia, so do not ignore it. When to call When the pressure does not respond to the first ordered doses or an infusion is needed, and at once if the blood pressure problem comes with a headache, a neurologic change or neck swelling. Neck swelling Emergency The page. “Her neck looks bigger than it did,” “he says it's hard to swallow,” or “his breathing sounds noisy.” Ask first. “Is the patient breathing comfortably and talking normally? Any stridor?” If there is any airway concern, have the nurse call a rapid response now and sit the patient up. You are going now either way. Call your senior or attending immediately. An expanding neck hematoma after carotid surgery is an airway emergency. Call for airway help early, from anesthesia or the airway team, through a rapid response if needed. Intubation can be very difficult once the larynx is pushed aside and swollen. Assess the airway. Sit the patient up and give oxygen. Look for stridor, voice change, tracheal deviation, difficulty handling secretions and rising anxiety. Mark the edges of the swelling. Control the blood pressure. Hypertension feeds the bleeding, but the airway comes first. If the airway is compromised, open the incision at the bedside. Cut the skin sutures or remove the staples and open the layer beneath (the platysma) to let the hematoma out, which decompresses the airway. Pack gently with gauze, hold pressure, and go to the operating room. When the airway is failing, this is done by whoever is at the bedside; it is not saved for the operating room. Even a stable but enlarging hematoma usually goes back to the operating room. That decision belongs to the attending, which is why the call is still immediate. After TCAR the incision is small, but it can still hide an expanding hematoma. When to call: now Any new or growing neck swelling after carotid surgery, and any stridor, voice change or trouble swallowing. Call immediately, call for airway help, and stay at the bedside. Know where the suture removal kit or staple remover is before you need it. New neurologic deficit Emergency The page. “He isn't moving his right hand like he was,” “her face looks droopy,” or “he's having trouble getting his words out.” Ask first. “When was the last normal neurologic check?” Then the blood pressure and a fingerstick glucose. You are coming now. Examine quickly and precisely. Level of consciousness, speech and language, facial symmetry, strength in both arms and legs, and vision. Record the time you found the deficit and the last known normal. Call the attending immediately. A deficit after carotid surgery may mean thrombosis or a flap at the endarterectomy site, and the surgeon may want an immediate duplex or a return to the operating room for re-exploration without waiting for other imaging. If hospital policy calls for a stroke alert on any new inpatient deficit, call it too, but the vascular attending must hear about it directly and at once. Protect perfusion. Keep the blood pressure in the ordered range, avoid hypotension, and correct glucose and oxygenation. Do not start heparin or any stroke treatment on your own. What it might be Internal carotid thrombosis or an intimal flap at the repair, more likely when the deficit appears on waking or within the first hours. An embolus from the endarterectomy site. Hyperperfusion syndrome or intracerebral hemorrhage: headache or seizure, usually later and with high blood pressure. Hypoperfusion from hypotension, hypoglycemia, or residual sedation, which is a diagnosis of exclusion. When to call: now Any new neurologic deficit after carotid surgery, however subtle. Call first; do not wait for a CT before the attending knows. Headache or seizure: hyperperfusion syndrome Restoring normal pressure to a hemisphere that has been underperfused for a long time can overwhelm its impaired autoregulation. The picture is an ipsilateral headache, often severe, followed by confusion, seizures, focal deficits or intracerebral hemorrhage. It usually appears in the first days to a week, most often after a very tight stenosis, with poor collateral flow or a contralateral occlusion, and with poorly controlled blood pressure. Call your senior for any severe new headache after carotid revascularization. The workup is a CT of the head to exclude he"},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"After aortic surgery","u":"on-call.html#post-aaa","x":"After aortic surgery: urine output, abdomen, legs After open aortic repair the night calls are about the urine, the abdomen and the legs. After EVAR they are mostly about the groins, the limbs and a fever. Most open repairs spend the first night or two in the CTV ICU , so many of these calls come after transfer to the floor. Low urine output The page. “Urine output has been 15 mL an hour for the last two hours.” Ask first. “What is the trend over the last four to six hours, what are the blood pressure and heart rate, and is the abdomen distended?” Ask the nurse to check the catheter and tubing, and to bladder scan, while you walk over. Check the catheter. Kinked or clamped tubing, a full bag, a clot. Flush it or bladder scan: a full bladder with a catheter in place means the catheter is blocked. Sudden anuria with a working catheter is a vascular problem until proven otherwise. Assess volume and perfusion. Heart rate, blood pressure and mean arterial pressure, capillary refill, the drain and nasogastric output, the fluid balance since surgery, and the hemoglobin. Low urine output with hypotension after an aortic operation means bleeding until proven otherwise. Feel the abdomen. A tense, distended abdomen with falling urine output (and, on a ventilator, rising airway pressures) needs a bladder pressure. See abdominal compartment syndrome below. Send labs. A BMP for potassium and creatinine, a CBC, a lactate, and a CK if the legs were ischemic. Dark, tea-colored urine suggests myoglobin. Give fluid if the patient is dry. A crystalloid bolus (for example 250 to 500 mL) once you are sure the patient is not overloaded, with the senior's agreement in an aortic patient. Do not reach for furosemide to treat a number. Think of the aorta. A suprarenal clamp, a renal artery or graft problem (including a stented renal branch after complex EVAR), embolization to the kidneys, or contrast after EVAR. Imaging of the renal arteries is the senior's call. When to call Urine output below about 0.5 mL/kg per hour for two hours that does not respond to the first steps, anuria with a working catheter, or low output with a rising potassium or lactate, hypotension, or a tense abdomen. Abdominal compartment syndrome Emergency Most likely after repair of a ruptured aneurysm (open or endovascular) with massive resuscitation, or with a large retroperitoneal hematoma. The signs are a tense, distended abdomen, falling urine output, hypotension, a rising lactate and, on a ventilator, rising peak airway pressures. Measure the bladder pressure: patient supine, at end-expiration, transducer zeroed where the mid-axillary line crosses the iliac crest, with no more than 25 mL of saline instilled. A pressure of 12 mmHg or more is intra-abdominal hypertension; a sustained pressure above 20 mmHg with new organ dysfunction is abdominal compartment syndrome. Call immediately. Treatment ranges from sedation, gastric and rectal decompression and limiting fluid to decompressive laparotomy, which is the senior's decision. Diarrhea, especially bloody: colon ischemia Emergency The page. “He just had a loose, bloody bowel movement,” two days after an open repair. Ask first. “What are the vitals, and is the abdomen more painful or distended?” Then go and see. When the inferior mesenteric artery and the internal iliac supply are interrupted and the collaterals are not enough, the left colon and sigmoid can become ischemic. Bloody diarrhea after aortic surgery is colon ischemia until proven otherwise. Any early diarrhea, a rising lactate, unexplained acidosis, fever, a rising white count, or worsening abdominal pain or distension should raise the same question. It is far more common after a ruptured repair. Call your senior now. The diagnosis is made by bedside flexible sigmoidoscopy, arranged by the senior. A CT can miss early mucosal ischemia. In the meantime. NPO, IV fluids, a CBC, BMP and lactate, and broad-spectrum antibiotics if the senior agrees. Avoid hypotension. Why the urgency. Mucosal ischemia may settle with support; transmural necrosis needs a colectomy and is often fatal when found late. C. difficile or laxatives are diagnoses to make after ischemia has been considered, not before. Cold, mottled or painful feet After aortic surgery, check the feet against the baseline. Patchy mottling of the toes with palpable pulses is usually microembolization (trash foot), managed supportively. Loss of a femoral or pedal pulse that was present suggests limb thrombosis, a kinked graft limb, a dissection or a large embolus, and is handled exactly like a lost pulse after bypass : call now. A tense, painful calf after a long clamp time is compartment syndrome until proven otherwise. Back pain or hypotension After open repair, hypotension with a falling hemoglobin means bleeding (an anastomosis, a lumbar artery, coagulopathy or hypothermia) until proven otherwise. After EVAR, think of the access sites, a retroperitoneal bleed from an iliac injury, and, rarely, rupture from a type I or III endoleak. Myocardial infarction is the other early killer, so get an ECG and a troponin. Resuscitate, send a CBC, coagulation studies and a type and cross, and call now. The full workup is under Hypotension . After EVAR The access sites Large-bore femoral punctures closed with a suture-mediated device, or groin cutdowns. Check them with the distal pulses as in Groin bleeding . Limb occlusion A new cold, painful leg or loss of a femoral pulse on one side, most often in the first weeks to months, and sometimes on the first night. Treat it as a lost pulse and call now. Post-implantation syndrome Fever, malaise and a raised white count and CRP in the first few days without infection: an inflammatory response to the graft. It is self-limited and treated with antipyretics, but it is a diagnosis of exclusion, so examine the groins, lines, lungs and urine and culture as you would for any post-operative fever. The kidneys Check the creatinine after the contrast load, "},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"New leg weakness","u":"on-call.html#spinal","x":"New leg weakness after aortic repair Emergency Spinal cord ischemia can follow TEVAR, fenestrated or branched EVAR and open thoracoabdominal repair, and occasionally an infrarenal repair. It often appears hours to days after surgery rather than on waking, and quick action can reverse it. New weakness or numbness in the legs is an emergency until proven otherwise. The page. “She can't lift her legs the way she could at the last check,” “he says his legs feel numb,” or “the spinal drain is alarming, leaking or draining a lot.” Ask first. “When were the legs last normal, what is the MAP right now, and is there a spinal drain in?” You are coming now. At the bedside Call the senior and attending immediately, and the CTV ICU team if the patient is there. This is a team response that usually needs ICU-level care. Examine and time it. Strength in each leg by muscle group, sensation and any sensory level, and the time of the last normal exam. Check the pulses: a cold, weak leg may be acute limb ischemia, not the spinal cord. Raise the MAP. To the protocol's rescue target, commonly 90 mmHg or higher, with fluid, blood if the hemoglobin is low, and a vasopressor such as norepinephrine. Stop antihypertensives and treat any hypotension at once. Drain CSF per protocol if a drain is in. Check that it is patent, leveled and zeroed, then lower the drainage threshold to the protocol's rescue setting and drain within its hourly maximum (commonly 10 to 20 mL per hour). Over-draining risks intracranial hemorrhage. Optimize oxygen delivery. Hemoglobin to the protocol target, oxygenation, and cardiac output. If there is no drain, discuss emergent placement. The attending and anesthesia weigh emergent lumbar drain placement against the timing of anticoagulants and antiplatelets and the platelet count, under neuraxial guidelines. Repeat the exam often, at least hourly, and document each one with the time and the MAP. Pearl: two levers on cord perfusion Spinal cord perfusion pressure is roughly the mean arterial pressure minus the CSF pressure. That is why there are two levers, raising the MAP and lowering the CSF pressure, and why most protocols pull both at the first sign of weakness. What it might be Spinal cord ischemia from coverage or loss of the intercostal and lumbar supply, made worse by hypotension or anemia. A spinal epidural hematoma from the drain or an epidural catheter, especially on anticoagulation. This needs urgent MRI and neurosurgery. Acute limb ischemia: a cold, pulseless, weak leg. Motor block from an epidural infusion. Stop it and reassess with the acute pain service, but do not settle on this until the cord has been considered. Stroke if an arm is involved; less often hypokalemia or a peripheral nerve injury. When to call: now Any new leg weakness or numbness after aortic repair. Any CSF drain problem: blood-tinged CSF, a disconnection or leak, or drainage over the hourly maximum. Any headache, confusion or drowsiness in a patient with a drain, which can signal intracranial hemorrhage from over-drainage. For a leak, a disconnection or a suspected bleed, clamp the drain and cover the site with a sterile dressing while you call. Confirm locally Spinal drain protocols vary: the routine and rescue MAP targets, the drainage pressure threshold, the hourly maximum, the hemoglobin target, whether nurses or physicians adjust the drain, and who places and removes it. Removal needs the same anticoagulation timing as placement. Read the hospital protocol before your first night, and see Spinal drains on the CTV ICU page."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"Heparin, bleeding and HIT","u":"on-call.html#heparin","x":"Heparin drips, bleeding and HIT Heparin is behind a large share of overnight pages on a vascular service. Most are routine adjustments. Bleeding on heparin and a falling platelet count are not, and both need your senior. Routine nomogram calls Follow the hospital nomogram. Adjust the rate to the measured aPTT or anti-Xa level exactly as the protocol says. Most hospitals monitor with one test or the other, not both. Do not freelance doses. Know which intensity was ordered. Targets, and whether to bolus, differ by indication: a full therapeutic nomogram with a bolus (commonly 80 U/kg, then about 18 U/kg per hour) for acute limb ischemia or venous thromboembolism, and often no bolus and a lower target soon after surgery. The order should say which. Know the range. A common anti-Xa target for unfractionated heparin is 0.3 to 0.7 IU/mL. aPTT ranges are calibrated by each laboratory, so use the number in the order set. Check that the level is real. It should be drawn about six hours after a start or a rate change, and never from the arm or line the heparin is running into. A recent dose of apixaban or rivaroxaban falsely raises the anti-Xa level, so tell the pharmacist and the senior if the patient was taking one. Resident tip Before you adjust a drip for an out-of-range level, check two things in PowerChart: when the sample was drawn relative to the last rate change, and where it was drawn from. A level drawn two hours after a change, or from the heparin line, will have you chasing a number that is not real. Holding before procedures Many vascular procedures are done with heparin running, so never hold it without a clear instruction: who said to hold it, from when, and when to restart. For neuraxial procedures, including placing or removing a spinal drain or an epidural, the American Society of Regional Anesthesia guidance applies: an IV heparin infusion is typically stopped 4 to 6 hours beforehand, with normal coagulation tests, and restarted no sooner than an hour afterward. Confirm the plan with anesthesia, and write the hold and restart times in the order and the sign-out. Bleeding on heparin Assess how bad it is. Where, how much, and the vitals. Apply direct pressure to anything external. Stop the infusion for significant bleeding and call your senior. IV heparin has a short half-life (roughly an hour to an hour and a half at therapeutic doses), so its effect fades within a few hours of stopping. For minor oozing the senior may prefer a pause or a lower target. Send labs. CBC with platelets, aPTT or anti-Xa, fibrinogen, and a type and screen or crossmatch. Discuss protamine with the senior. About 1 mg reverses 100 units of heparin. For an infusion the dose covers the heparin given over roughly the last 2 to 3 hours: a drip at 1,200 units per hour has delivered about 3,000 units in the last two and a half hours, so about 30 mg. The maximum single dose is 50 mg, given slowly (no faster than about 5 mg per minute) because it can cause hypotension, bradycardia and anaphylaxis, more so with prior protamine exposure, NPH insulin use or fish allergy. It only partly reverses low-molecular-weight heparin. Weigh the graft and the limb. Stopping or reversing heparin can let a fresh graft or an ischemic limb clot. That trade-off is why reversal is the senior's decision. Falling platelets and HIT Platelets fall after most operations from hemodilution and consumption, usually in the first few days, and then recover. Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is different. It is an immune reaction to the complex of platelet factor 4 and heparin that typically drops the count by more than half, 5 to 10 days after heparin was started (or within a day if the patient had heparin in the past month), and it causes thrombosis, not bleeding. On a vascular service that thrombosis may be the graft. Use the 4T score to estimate the probability. The 4T score (0 to 8 points; platelet counts in ×10 9 /L) Feature 2 points 1 point 0 points Thrombocytopenia Fall of more than 50% and nadir 20 or higher Fall of 30 to 50%, or nadir 10 to 19 Fall of less than 30%, or nadir below 10 Timing of the fall Clear onset on days 5 to 10, or within 1 day with heparin exposure in the past 30 days Consistent with days 5 to 10 but not clear, after day 10, or within 1 day with exposure 30 to 100 days ago Within 4 days with no recent exposure Thrombosis New proven thrombosis, skin necrosis at injection sites, or an acute systemic reaction after an IV bolus Progressive or recurrent thrombosis, red skin lesions, or suspected but unproven thrombosis None Other causes None apparent Possible Definite A score of 0 to 3 is low probability, and current ASH guidance is not to test and to continue heparin. A score of 4 to 5 is intermediate and 6 to 8 is high: treat as HIT while the tests are pending, with your senior. Stop, all of it The heparin drip and subcutaneous heparin. Low-molecular-weight heparin. Heparin flushes, including arterial line pressure bags. Heparin locks in central lines and dialysis catheters (ask for a citrate or saline lock). Heparin-coated catheters, where an alternative exists. Platelet transfusion, unless the patient is bleeding or needs a high-risk procedure. Warfarin: do not start it until the platelets recover, and give vitamin K if it was already started. Start, with the senior and pharmacy A non-heparin anticoagulant at a therapeutic dose, because stopping heparin alone leaves the thrombotic risk untreated: argatroban (cleared by the liver, so usable in renal failure) or bivalirudin (short-acting, often chosen around procedures). Later, in stable patients, fondaparinux or a direct oral anticoagulant, per hematology or pharmacy. The PF4-heparin antibody test, then a functional assay such as the serotonin release assay if it is positive. Bilateral leg duplex to look for silent DVT. A heparin allergy entry in PowerChart, so no one restarts it. Pearl HIT clots, it does not bleed. A patient on heparin whose platelets fall by half o"},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"Dialysis access calls","u":"on-call.html#access-calls","x":"Dialysis access calls Fistulas and grafts generate their own calls. A clotted access can usually wait for a planned procedure, as long as the potassium and the volume status allow. Bleeding from an access and a cold, weak hand cannot wait. The operations are described in Hemodialysis access . No thrill: a clotted fistula or graft The page. “Dialysis says there's no thrill in his left arm graft,” or “the patient says the buzz is gone.” Ask first. “When was it last used or felt, when is dialysis due, and what is the potassium?” Examine it. Feel along the access for a thrill and listen for a bruit. No pulse and no thrill means thrombosis; a strong, bounding pulse with no thrill means the outflow is blocked. Check the hand, and look for infection (redness, tenderness or drainage over a graft), which changes the plan. Protect the arm. No blood pressure cuffs, IVs or blood draws on that side. Check the potassium and volume. Send a BMP, CBC and coagulation studies. Hyperkalemia or fluid overload that cannot wait for a declot means a temporary dialysis catheter and a call to nephrology tonight. Plan for thrombectomy. Endovascular or open, ideally within a day or two and before the next dialysis, so the patient avoids a catheter. Keep the patient NPO after midnight if the senior plans it for the morning. When to call Let your senior know tonight so the case can be planned, and call right away if the potassium is high, the patient is overloaded, or the access looks infected. Bleeding from a fistula or graft Can be an emergency The page. “The fistula is still bleeding after dialysis,” or “there's blood everywhere from his arm.” Ask first. “Is someone pressing on it right now?” Tell the nurse to press firmly and directly on the bleeding point with two fingers, keep pressing, and not to use a tourniquet. Then ask for the vitals. You are coming now. Firm digital pressure on the bleeding point. Two fingers directly over the hole, firm enough to stop the bleeding while, ideally, the thrill persists. Hold for 10 to 15 minutes with the arm raised. A tourniquet can thrombose the access and is a last resort only for life-threatening bleeding that direct pressure cannot stop. Look at the skin. Bleeding through thin, shiny, scabbed or ulcerated skin over an aneurysmal fistula or graft is a herald of rupture. That is an emergency even if the bleeding has stopped: call now, and keep the patient monitored until the senior has seen them. Send labs and ask about heparin. CBC and coagulation studies, and find out whether heparin was given during dialysis. Protamine is the senior's decision. Close the hole if pressure fails. A figure-of-eight or purse-string skin suture around a needle site, or a topical hemostatic agent, controls most needle-site bleeding. Your senior may ask you to place one. When to call: now Any bleeding through skin breakdown over an access, bleeding that 10 to 15 minutes of good pressure does not control, or bleeding with hypotension. A ruptured fistula can exsanguinate a patient in minutes. Prolonged bleeding after every dialysis session points to an outflow stenosis, which needs a fistulogram during the day. Cold, painful or weak hand: steal and neuropathy The page. “Her hand is cold and numb since the fistula operation,” or “it hurts during dialysis.” Ask first. “Is it new since today's surgery, and can she move and feel her fingers?” Compare both hands: color, temperature, capillary refill, the radial pulse or Doppler signal, and motor and sensory function. In steal, the hand improves when you compress the access. Access-related hand ischemia steal A cool, pale, painful hand, worse during dialysis, with a weak radial signal that improves when the access is compressed. Mild coolness with normal motor and sensory function can be documented and reviewed in the morning. Rest pain, a new motor or sensory deficit, or tissue loss needs a call now for revision or ligation. Ischemic monomelic neuropathy Sudden severe pain, weakness and numbness of the hand immediately after the operation, typically with a brachial artery-based access in a patient with diabetes and neuropathy, yet with a warm hand and a palpable pulse or Doppler signal. It is a surgical emergency, usually treated by ligating the access: call now. Swollen arm after access Some swelling after access surgery is expected and settles with elevation. A markedly swollen arm, sometimes with swelling of the face or breast and dilated veins over the chest wall, suggests central venous stenosis unmasked by the new high flow, usually from previous catheters or pacemaker leads. It is worked up with a fistulogram or venogram during the day. Check the hand perfusion and the forearm compartments, consider DVT, a hematoma or infection, and call if the hand is threatened or the arm is tense and painful."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"Wounds and wound vacs","u":"on-call.html#wounds","x":"Wounds, amputations and wound vacs Wound calls are rarely emergencies, with three exceptions: bleeding, especially from a wound over a graft or an artery; a rapidly spreading infection; and an injured stump. Wound vac alarms and leaks The page. “The wound vac keeps alarming,” or “it won't hold a seal.” Ask first. “Which alarm, how long has suction been off, and is there blood in the tubing or the canister?” Look for blood first. Frank blood in the canister or tubing means stopping the suction, holding pressure over the wound and calling now, especially if the vac sits over a groin, an exposed vessel or a graft. Find the leak. Listen for the hiss, smooth or patch the drape with extra film, and check for kinked tubing, a full or unseated canister, and a dead battery. If you cannot restore the seal. Most manufacturers and wound teams advise that foam left without suction for more than about two hours be replaced with a moist saline gauze dressing until the vac can be reapplied. Check with the senior first if the vac covers a graft, an exposed vessel, a fasciotomy or an open abdomen. Count the foam. If you remove it, record how many pieces came out against the number documented at placement. Bleeding or injured stump The page. “The BKA dressing is soaked through,” or “he fell trying to get to the bathroom.” Ask first. “How much, is it still bleeding, and what are the vitals?” Then: what anticoagulants are running? Take the outer dressing down and look. Most bleeding is ooze from the skin edge and stops with firm direct pressure and a fresh compressive dressing. Brisk bleeding. Hold firm pressure and call now. For arterial bleeding that pressure cannot control, a tourniquet above the stump is a reasonable bridge to the operating room. A fall onto the stump. New amputees often forget the leg is gone when they get up at night. Look for dehiscence, a hematoma or bleeding, and call if the incision has opened. Labs. CBC, coagulation studies and a type and screen; anticoagulation decisions are made with the senior. Dusky flap or incision edge The page. “The skin edges on the amputation look dark,” or “the flap looks purple.” Check that the dressing or elastic wrap is not too tight and loosen it, look for a hematoma under the flap, and check the limb perfusion against the baseline. Outline the area with a marker, note the time, and photograph it if hospital policy allows, so the morning team can judge the trend. A dusky edge is usually reviewed on rounds. Call if it is spreading quickly, if a pulse or signal has changed, or if there is crepitus, foul drainage or systemic illness. Fever with a wound Fever in the first 48 hours after surgery is usually inflammatory, but on a vascular service always look at every wound: take down the dressings, including the groins and the vein harvest sites. Then work through the usual sources (lines, lungs, urine, C. difficile if there is diarrhea, and DVT), with blood cultures if the patient is febrile and unwell. Some findings change the urgency. Necrotizing soft tissue infection. Pain out of proportion, rapidly spreading erythema, bullae, crepitus, thin gray drainage and systemic toxicity. Call now: it needs operative debridement. An infected groin over a graft. Purulent drainage, a groin wound that opens down toward a graft, or any bleeding from an infected groin, which can herald anastomotic disruption. Call now. Wet gangrene or a foot abscess. Sepsis from the foot needs source control, sometimes a guillotine amputation. Send cultures, start antibiotics and call. See The diabetic foot ."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"Chest pain, AF, hypotension","u":"on-call.html#cardiac","x":"Chest pain, new AF, hypotension The vascular patient is a cardiac patient. Myocardial infarction is the leading cause of death after open aortic surgery and a major cause after other vascular operations, and most perioperative myocardial injury is silent. Take every cardiac call seriously, and think about the limbs and grafts at the same time. Chest pain The page. “He's complaining of chest pressure.” Ask first. “What are the vitals and the rhythm? Please get a 12-lead ECG now.” Then go. ECG within 10 minutes, compared with the old one. ST elevation means activating the cardiology or cath lab pathway per local protocol and calling your senior at the same time, because anticoagulation and antiplatelets after fresh surgery are a joint decision. Examine. Vitals, lungs, volume status, the incisions and the limbs. Consider the other causes: pulmonary embolism, aortic dissection (especially with known aortic disease), pneumonia, reflux and musculoskeletal pain. Send labs. A troponin, repeated per the hospital's high-sensitivity protocol, with a CBC, BMP and magnesium. Treat the cause. Much perioperative ischemia is a supply and demand mismatch from anemia, tachycardia, hypotension, hypertension or pain. Continue the home aspirin, statin and beta-blocker unless the patient is hypotensive or bleeding, and do not give nitroglycerin to a hypotensive patient. Call your senior and involve medicine or cardiology per the service's usual pattern for a troponin rise. New atrial fibrillation The page. “Heart rate is 140 and irregular. That's new.” Ask first. “What's the blood pressure, and is the patient short of breath, confused or having chest pain?” Ask for an ECG. Decide whether it is unstable. Rapid AF with hypotension, ischemia, pulmonary edema or altered mental status calls for a rapid response and synchronized cardioversion. Look for the trigger. Pain, bleeding or hypovolemia, hypoxia, sepsis, pulmonary embolism, ischemia, a missed home beta-blocker, and electrolytes. Replace potassium to about 4 and magnesium to about 2. Control the rate. IV metoprolol or diltiazem if the blood pressure allows, or amiodarone if the pressure is borderline or the ejection fraction is low, with the senior. Restart home rate control. Think embolism. New AF is an embolic source, so check the feet, the grafts and the neurologic exam. Anticoagulation for new post-operative AF balances bleeding against stroke and limb risk, and it is rarely started overnight without discussion with the senior. Hypotension The page. “Blood pressure is 82/45.” Ask first. “What was it before, what's the heart rate, is the patient awake and making urine, and is there new pain, bleeding or drain output?” Bleeding first. Look at every incision, both groins, the drains and the abdomen, and ask about back or flank pain. Send a CBC (the first hemoglobin can be normal in acute bleeding), coagulation studies and a type and cross, and place two large-bore IVs. Resuscitate while you think. A fluid bolus if there is no sign of overload, and blood if the patient is bleeding, with the senior. Work through the rest. Cardiac causes (an ECG for ischemia or arrhythmia), sepsis, medications (antihypertensives, opioids, and an epidural infusion, a common and easily missed cause), baroreceptor effects after carotid surgery, pulmonary embolism, and adrenal insufficiency in patients on chronic steroids. Check the limbs and grafts. Low flow can clot a fresh graft. Recheck the signals once the pressure is restored. When to call: now Chest pain with ECG changes or a rising troponin, any unstable arrhythmia, and any hypotension that does not respond promptly to a fluid bolus or comes with a falling hemoglobin, back or flank pain, or a cold limb. Call a rapid response first if the patient is unstable."},{"p":"On call and cross-cover","t":"When to call your senior","u":"on-call.html#when-to-call","x":"When to wake your senior or attending On a vascular service the rule is simple: if you are wondering whether to call, call. No one is ever criticized for calling about the situations below. A senior woken for a false alarm is far better than a graft or a limb lost to a call put off until morning. Always call, right away Any loss of a previously present pulse, graft pulse or Doppler signal, or a cold, pale, mottled, numb or weak limb after revascularization. Any new neurologic deficit, including new leg weakness after aortic repair and any change after carotid surgery. Neck swelling after carotid surgery, or any trouble breathing or swallowing. Suspected rupture or retroperitoneal bleeding: hypotension with back, flank or abdominal pain, a falling hemoglobin, or bleeding that pressure does not control. Suspected compartment syndrome, in a limb or in the abdomen. Bloody diarrhea after aortic surgery, or any early diarrhea after aortic repair. Bleeding from the skin over a fistula or graft, or significant bleeding on a lytic infusion. Suspected HIT (a 4T score of 4 or more) or a new thrombosis on heparin. A spinal drain problem, or headache or drowsiness in a patient with a drain. A rapid response, a code, an unplanned ICU transfer, a STEMI or an unstable arrhythmia in a vascular patient. Any patient you are worried about, or whom the nurse is worried about, even if you cannot yet say why. Making the call count Call from the bedside or with the chart open, use SBAR, and end with a specific question. Have the vitals, your exam compared with the baseline, the relevant labs, and what you have already done. But never delay a come-now call to collect data. “I'm at the bedside, the graft pulse is gone, I'll call back with the labs” is the right first call. Resident tip If your senior does not answer within a few minutes for a come-now problem, page again, then call the fellow or the attending directly. The chain of command exists so that an emergency never waits on one phone. Write down whom you called and when. Confirm locally On your first day, ask your senior how the service wants to be called overnight: who is first call (the senior resident, the fellow or the attending), which events the attending wants to hear about directly whatever the hour, and how long to wait before going up the chain. Most academic services also expect the attending to be told about a death, an unplanned return to the operating room or transfer to the ICU, a patient leaving against medical advice, and a family asking to speak with the attending. Hospital policy may define some of these."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Foundations","u":"foundations.html","x":"The anatomy, hemodynamics, exam, testing, pharmacology and cardiac risk assessment that sit underneath every case on the service. Everyone will assume you already speak this language, so read it in your first week and come back to it before cases and consults."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Arterial anatomy","u":"foundations.html#anatomy","x":"Arterial anatomy, head to toe Memorize You will be asked to name vessels in order, in the operating room, while holding a retractor. Learn the path of blood from the aorta to the toes and from the arch to the fingertips, then the neck and the gut, because those are the beds the service operates on. The aorta and the arch The aortic root gives the coronary arteries, and the ascending aorta then becomes the arch, which gives three branches before it descends. The classic arch order, right to left, is the brachiocephalic (innominate) trunk, the left common carotid, and the left subclavian. The brachiocephalic then splits into the right common carotid and right subclavian. Each subclavian gives a vertebral artery, which climbs through the cervical transverse foramina (usually entering at C6) and joins its partner to form the basilar artery. Mnemonic Arch order, right to left, is the \"ABCs of the arch\": Arteria innominata (brachiocephalic), left common Carotid, left Subclavian. And the celiac trunk's three branches are the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic. Arch variants worth knowing Bovine arch common origin The brachiocephalic trunk and left common carotid share a single origin, or the left common carotid arises from the brachiocephalic trunk itself. It is the most common variant, seen in roughly 10 to 25 percent of people depending on the series. The name is a misnomer (cattle have a different arrangement) but everyone uses it. It changes how the left carotid is catheterized for angiography or stenting. Left vertebral from the arch The left vertebral arises directly from the arch between the left common carotid and left subclavian in about 5 percent of people. It matters for catheter selection and for thoracic stent-graft planning near the left subclavian. Aberrant right subclavian arteria lusoria Arises as the last arch branch, beyond the left subclavian, and usually crosses behind the esophagus (about 1 percent of people). It can cause dysphagia, may arise from an aneurysmal Kommerell diverticulum, and comes with a non-recurrent right laryngeal nerve that is at risk in neck surgery. Arch type I to III Describes how far below the top of the arch the great vessels arise. In a type I arch they come off the top; in a type III arch the innominate origin sits well below it. Steeper, elongated arches are more common with age and hypertension and make transfemoral carotid work harder. The carotid bifurcation The common carotid normally gives no branches in the neck. It divides, usually at about the upper border of the thyroid cartilage (roughly C3 to C4), into the internal and external carotid arteries. The level varies, and a high bifurcation near the angle of the mandible makes distal exposure harder, so look for it on the preoperative imaging. The origin of the internal carotid is dilated as the carotid bulb, home of the carotid sinus baroreceptors and the carotid body. That is why handling the bifurcation can drop the heart rate and blood pressure. The internal carotid (ICA) has no branches in the neck; its first branch, the ophthalmic artery, arises inside the skull. At its origin the ICA usually lies posterolateral to the external carotid. The external carotid (ECA) supplies the face, scalp and neck through eight branches, and it becomes an important collateral to the brain when the ICA is occluded. Mnemonic: external carotid branches \"Some Anatomists Like Freaking Out Poor Medical Students\": superior thyroid, ascending pharyngeal, lingual, facial, occipital, posterior auricular, maxillary, superficial temporal. The superior thyroid is usually the first branch and is the one you will see controlled during an endarterectomy. Structures met during carotid endarterectomy Through an incision along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid, roughly in the order you meet them: Great auricular nerve Crosses the top of the incision toward the earlobe. Injury leaves a numb earlobe. Facial vein common facial vein Crosses the field to drain into the internal jugular vein and reliably overlies the carotid bifurcation, so it is the landmark. It is ligated and divided, and the internal jugular is retracted laterally. Carotid sheath Common carotid medially, internal jugular vein laterally, vagus nerve posteriorly between them. Ansa cervicalis A nerve loop on the front of the sheath, descending from the hypoglossal to the strap muscles. It can be divided to free the hypoglossal with little consequence. Vagus nerve (X) Usually posterior in the sheath but occasionally anterior, so it must be seen before clamps go on. Injury, including clamp injury, paralyzes the ipsilateral vocal cord (hoarseness, weak cough) because the recurrent laryngeal fibers travel within it. Hypoglossal nerve (XII) Crosses superficial to the ICA and ECA a short distance above the bifurcation, beneath the posterior belly of the digastric, tethered by the sternocleidomastoid branch of the occipital artery. Injury makes the tongue deviate toward the injured side. This is the classic carotid nerve injury. Superior laryngeal nerve Runs deep (medial) to the ICA and ECA near the bifurcation; its external branch travels with the superior thyroid artery. Injury causes voice fatigue and loss of high pitch, which matters a great deal to a singer. Marginal mandibular nerve branch of VII Runs just below the angle of the mandible and is stretched by retraction at the top of the incision. Injury droops the lower lip on that side. Curving the upper incision back toward the mastoid helps protect it. Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) Met only with very high exposure toward the skull base. Injury causes dysphagia and aspiration risk. Pearl Most cranial nerve injuries after carotid endarterectomy are traction or clamp injuries that recover over weeks to months. Still, new facial or tongue asymmetry after a carotid operation is a stroke until a full neurologic exam says otherwise. A drooping lower lip with an otherwise normal exam points to the m"},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Venous anatomy","u":"foundations.html#venous","x":"Venous anatomy Veins are divided into a deep system (paired with the named arteries, carrying roughly 85 to 90 percent of return), a superficial system, and the perforators that connect them. Naming them correctly is not pedantry: whether a clot is in a deep or a superficial vein decides whether the patient is anticoagulated. Deep veins by name Below the knee the deep veins are paired companions (venae comitantes) of the anterior tibial, posterior tibial and peroneal arteries, plus the muscular gastrocnemius and soleal veins, where many calf DVTs begin. They join to form the popliteal vein, which becomes the femoral vein at the adductor hiatus. The femoral vein joins the deep femoral (profunda) vein to form the common femoral vein, which receives the great saphenous vein at the saphenofemoral junction and becomes the external iliac vein under the inguinal ligament. The external and internal iliac veins form the common iliac veins, which join at about L5, to the right of the aorta, to form the inferior vena cava. Red flag: the \"superficial femoral vein\" The vein running with the superficial femoral artery was long called the superficial femoral vein. It is a deep vein, and current nomenclature calls it the femoral vein. Thrombus there is a proximal DVT that needs anticoagulation, not a superficial phlebitis. If a report or a colleague says \"superficial femoral vein,\" read it as femoral vein, and write it that way yourself. In the arm, paired radial, ulnar and brachial veins drain to the axillary vein, which becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib and joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein. The superficial cephalic vein (lateral, ending in the axillary vein through the deltopectoral groove) and basilic vein (medial, diving deep in the mid-arm to join the brachial veins) are the dialysis access veins; see Open operations . Superficial veins and their nerves The great saphenous vein begins in front of the medial malleolus and runs up the medial leg and thigh to join the common femoral vein at the saphenofemoral junction, just below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. The small saphenous vein begins behind the lateral malleolus and runs up the posterior calf to join the popliteal vein in the popliteal fossa, at a level that varies. Each runs with a sensory nerve that is at risk during vein harvest, stripping or thermal ablation. Great saphenous vein Runs with the saphenous nerve below the knee, where the two lie close together. Injury numbs the medial leg and foot. One reason thermal ablation usually treats the above-knee segment and works carefully below the knee. Small saphenous vein Runs with the sural nerve in the posterior calf. Injury numbs the lateral foot and heel. The sural nerve is also the nerve harvested for nerve grafts, so its course gets asked. Pearl The great saphenous vein is the workhorse conduit of vascular and cardiac surgery, the preferred graft for infrainguinal bypass and for coronary bypass. Before you ablate or harvest it, someone always asks whether the patient might need it as a conduit later. A vein mapped and spared today can be a limb saved tomorrow. On mapping, a vein of at least about 3 mm is generally wanted for a bypass. Superficial vein thrombosis is usually treated less aggressively than DVT, with one exception worth remembering: thrombus in the great saphenous vein within about 3 cm of the saphenofemoral junction is generally managed like a DVT, because it is a step away from the common femoral vein. Perforators Perforating veins pierce the deep fascia and connect the superficial and deep systems. Their valves normally direct flow from superficial to deep, so the calf muscle pump can empty the leg. When they fail, high pressure from the deep system is transmitted to the skin. The medial lower-leg perforators (historically called Cockett perforators, now posterior tibial perforators) drain the gaiter area above the medial malleolus, which is exactly where venous ulcers cluster. Others sit just below the knee (Boyd) and in the thigh (Dodd). A perforator is usually considered pathologic, and worth treating, when it shows outward flow of 500 milliseconds or more, a diameter of about 3.5 mm or more, and lies beneath a healed or open ulcer. May-Thurner anatomy The right common iliac artery crosses in front of the left common iliac vein and compresses it against the fifth lumbar vertebra. Chronic pulsatile compression can leave webs and spurs inside the vein. The result is a predisposition to left iliofemoral DVT, classically in younger women. Some degree of this compression is common on imaging in people with no symptoms, so the anatomy alone is not the diagnosis; intravascular ultrasound at venography is the usual way to judge it. An extensive, symptomatic left iliofemoral DVT may be treated with thrombus removal and stenting of the compressed segment in selected patients. See Presentations . Why is it the left leg in May-Thurner syndrome? Because the inferior vena cava lies to the right of the aorta, the left common iliac vein must cross the midline, passing behind the right common iliac artery and in front of the spine. That is where it gets pinched."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Collateral pathways","u":"foundations.html#collaterals","x":"Collateral pathways High yield Collaterals explain why a patient can occlude a major vessel and still keep a viable limb, brain or gut. Named collateral routes are favorite teaching material. Circle of Willis Anastomotic ring at the base of the brain linking the anterior (carotid) and posterior (vertebrobasilar) circulations. It explains why some patients tolerate carotid occlusion or clamping and others stroke. It is complete in only a minority of people. External to internal carotid When the ICA is occluded, ECA branches around the face and orbit can fill the ophthalmic artery backward and supply the intracranial ICA. One reason the ECA is worth preserving. Vertebral reversal subclavian steal With a proximal subclavian occlusion, blood flows up one vertebral artery and down the other into the arm. Arm exercise can then steal from the brainstem. Suspect it when the arm pressures differ by more than about 15 to 20 mmHg. Pancreaticoduodenal arcades Link the celiac (through the gastroduodenal artery) to the superior mesenteric artery. They keep the liver and foregut alive when the celiac origin is stenosed, and can become aneurysmal under the extra flow. Marginal artery of Drummond Continuous arcade along the mesenteric border of the colon connecting the SMA and IMA territories. Arc of Riolan meandering mesenteric artery A shorter, more central SMA to IMA connection that enlarges dramatically when one mesenteric vessel occludes chronically. Aortoiliac collaterals In Leriche-type occlusion, flow reaches the legs through lumbar to iliolumbar routes and through the IMA to superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal (internal iliac) routes. Internal thoracic to epigastric Winslow pathway Internal thoracic (mammary) to superior epigastric to inferior epigastric to external iliac. In aortoiliac occlusion it can carry much of the leg's flow, so it should not be divided carelessly. Profunda femoris and the geniculates The main collateral around an occluded superficial femoral artery; geniculate branches reconstitute the popliteal. A good profunda can carry a limb despite a dead SFA. Pearl Before any vessel is covered, ligated or embolized, someone asks what supplies its territory afterward. Covering both internal iliacs risks buttock claudication and colon ischemia; covering the left subclavian matters more if the patient has a left internal mammary coronary graft or a dominant left vertebral; ligating the IMA depends on an intact marginal artery. Answering that question is how you show you understand collaterals."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Hemodynamics","u":"foundations.html#hemodynamics","x":"Hemodynamics and physiology A handful of physical principles get asked again and again because they explain disease. See also the teaching questions on physiology . Poiseuille's law Flow is proportional to the fourth power of the radius and inversely proportional to length and viscosity. The punchline is that small changes in lumen diameter cause large changes in flow, which is why a focal stenosis can be so hemodynamically costly, and why a slightly larger conduit dramatically improves flow. Critical stenosis A stenosis generally becomes hemodynamically significant once it narrows the diameter by about 50 percent (roughly 75 percent of cross-sectional area), at which point a measurable pressure and flow gradient appears across it. This is the threshold that duplex velocity criteria are designed to detect. Because the same volume of blood must pass through a smaller opening, velocity rises within a stenosis (flow equals area times velocity). That jump in velocity is what duplex ultrasound measures, which is why reports grade stenoses by peak systolic velocity and by the ratio of velocity in the stenosis to velocity just upstream. Why disease shows up with exercise At rest, collaterals around a stenosis or occlusion may carry enough flow for resting muscle. Exercise dilates the muscle bed and multiplies demand, but the collaterals have a fixed, high resistance, so pressure beyond them falls and the muscle becomes ischemic. That is claudication, and it is why an exercise ABI can reveal disease that a resting ABI misses. Laplace's law and aneurysms Wall tension rises with both pressure and radius. As an aneurysm enlarges, wall tension climbs, which raises rupture risk and accelerates further dilation, a self-reinforcing loop that is the entire rationale for size-based repair thresholds. Why does aneurysm rupture risk rise so steeply with diameter? By Laplace's law, tension in the wall is proportional to pressure times radius, so a larger radius means higher wall stress at the same blood pressure. That both predisposes to rupture and drives continued expansion."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"History and exam","u":"foundations.html#exam","x":"The vascular history and physical exam The exam is where you earn credibility. Do it the same way every time. Documentation templates for the exam are on History, exam and charting . History: localizing claudication The level of arterial disease predicts where the patient hurts when walking, because the pain occurs one level below the occlusion. Buttock and hip claudication points to aortoiliac disease, thigh claudication to the common femoral or iliac level, and the most common pattern, calf claudication, to the superficial femoral and popliteal segment. The full presentations are on Presentations . Mnemonic: Leriche syndrome Aortoiliac occlusive disease gives the triad of buttock and thigh claudication, erectile dysfunction, and absent or diminished femoral pulses. Picture the blockage sitting at the aortic bifurcation, starving everything downstream. The pulse exam Palpate and grade every pulse, comparing sides. A common scale is 0 absent, 1+ diminished, 2+ normal, 3+ bounding (some services add a fourth grade, so use the one your team uses). Document the sites: radial, brachial, carotid, femoral, popliteal, dorsalis pedis, and posterior tibial. If you cannot feel a distal pulse, reach for the handheld Doppler and describe the signal. Handheld Doppler waveforms Signal What you hear What it usually means Triphasic A sharp forward systolic sound, a brief reversal in early diastole, then a small forward sound Normal peripheral artery at rest: a compliant vessel feeding a high-resistance bed. Biphasic Forward systolic flow and early diastolic reversal, without the late forward component Often normal, especially in older patients with stiffer arteries; can accompany mild proximal disease. Interpret alongside the other leg and the ABI. Monophasic A single forward sound with no reversal, often soft and drawn out (dampened) Usually distal to a significant stenosis or occlusion, with flow arriving through collaterals. Also normal in low-resistance beds (the ICA, the kidney) and in a hyperemic limb after exercise, with infection, or just after revascularization. Absent No arterial signal at that site Occlusion or severe ischemia. In an acutely cold leg, no arterial signal with a preserved venous signal suggests a threatened limb (Rutherford category II); no venous signal either suggests an irreversible one (category III). See acute limb ischemia . A 2020 consensus from the Society for Vascular Medicine and the Society for Vascular Ultrasound recommends describing waveforms as multiphasic or monophasic, with descriptors such as high or low resistance, rather than counting phases. You will still hear triphasic, biphasic and monophasic at the bedside every day. Pearl \"Doppler signals\" and \"palpable pulses\" are not the same thing, and saying so precisely matters. A monophasic Doppler signal in a foot that has no palpable pulse still tells you the foot is perfused, which can be the difference between watchful waiting and an emergent trip to the angiography suite. Always state both what you felt and what you heard. Resident tip When you find a faint pedal signal, mark the spot on the skin with a pen and write the vessel and waveform (\"DP monophasic, PT biphasic, marked\"). The next examiner, often you at 3 a.m., then compares like with like, and a lost signal means a real change rather than a different spot on the foot. Provocative and inspection findings Buerger's test. Elevation causes pallor in an ischemic limb, and dependency causes a dusky dependent rubor. The angle at which pallor appears tracks severity. Signs of chronic ischemia. Capillary refill beyond 2 to 3 seconds, along with loss of hair, shiny atrophic skin, and thickened nails. Allen test. Before radial access or radial-artery harvest, confirms that the ulnar artery and palmar arch can supply the hand alone. A Doppler or pulse-oximetry version is more objective than watching the palm color. Bruits. Auscultate over the carotids, abdomen, and femorals; a bruit signals turbulent flow across a stenosis."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Testing and the ABI","u":"foundations.html#testing","x":"Noninvasive testing and the ABI Core skill The ankle-brachial index is the bedside number that quantifies perfusion. Measure the higher of the two ankle systolic pressures (dorsalis pedis or posterior tibial) in each leg and divide by the higher of the two brachial pressures. ABI interpretation ABI value Interpretation Typical correlate > 1.40 Noncompressible Calcified, incompressible vessels (diabetes, kidney failure); ABI unreliable, so use toe pressures 1.00 to 1.40 Normal No significant occlusive disease 0.91 to 0.99 Borderline Equivocal; consider exercise ABI 0.40 to 0.90 Mild to moderate PAD Typical claudication range < 0.40 Severe PAD Rest pain and tissue loss range (chronic limb-threatening ischemia) How to measure an ABI Rest the patient. Supine and still for 5 to 10 minutes, legs at heart level. Measurements taken right after a walk from the waiting room run low. Choose the right cuff. Cuff width should be at least 40 percent of the limb circumference. The ankle cuff goes just above the malleoli, with the lower edge about 2 to 3 cm above them. Measure both brachial pressures. Cuff on the upper arm, Doppler probe over the brachial artery. A difference of more than about 15 mmHg suggests subclavian stenosis on the lower side. Measure both ankle pressures in each leg. Probe at about 45 to 60 degrees over the dorsalis pedis, then the posterior tibial. Inflate about 20 mmHg past the point where the signal disappears, deflate slowly, and record the pressure at which the signal returns. Calculate each leg separately. The higher of that leg's two ankle pressures divided by the higher brachial pressure. Some labs also report the lower ankle pressure, which is more sensitive for disease. Document the raw pressures as well as the ratio. If an ABI is above 1.40 or does not fit the foot in front of you, move on to toe pressures. Red flag: the falsely reassuring ABI A normal or high ABI in a diabetic or dialysis patient may be an artifact of medial calcinosis making the vessels incompressible. If the ABI is above 1.40 or does not match a clearly ischemic foot, get a toe-brachial index, since the small digital arteries are usually spared from calcification. A toe-brachial index below 0.70 is abnormal, and a toe pressure below 30 mmHg predicts poor wound healing. Why divide by the higher of the two brachial pressures? The brachial pressure stands in for central aortic pressure. If one subclavian is stenosed, that arm reads falsely low and would inflate the ABI, so the higher arm is the better estimate. The rest of the noninvasive toolbox Segmental limb pressures and pulse-volume recordings. Cuffs at the thigh, calf, and ankle localize the level of disease by where the pressure drops or the waveform flattens. Exercise ABI. A normal resting ABI that falls after treadmill walking reveals significant disease and confirms that leg symptoms are vascular. A fall in ankle pressure of more than 30 mmHg, or in the ABI of more than 20 percent, is generally considered diagnostic. Arterial duplex ultrasound. Combines B-mode imaging with Doppler velocities, and a rise in peak systolic velocity across a segment grades the stenosis. This is the first-line tool for surveillance. Venous duplex. DVT is diagnosed mainly by a vein that will not compress under the probe. Reflux is generally defined as reversed flow lasting more than 0.5 seconds in superficial veins and more than 1 second in the femoral and popliteal veins; thresholds vary slightly between guidelines. Duplex criteria you will hear quoted Note Velocity criteria differ between vascular labs, which validate their own. The tables below are widely used starting points, not your lab's official criteria. When the report and these numbers disagree, the report's criteria are the ones the team acts on. Carotid duplex: Society of Radiologists in Ultrasound consensus criteria (2003) ICA stenosis ICA peak systolic velocity Plaque ICA/CCA PSV ratio ICA end-diastolic velocity Normal < 125 cm/s None visible < 2.0 < 40 cm/s < 50% < 125 cm/s Under 50% diameter reduction < 2.0 < 40 cm/s 50 to 69% 125 to 230 cm/s 50% or more 2.0 to 4.0 40 to 100 cm/s 70% or more, short of near occlusion > 230 cm/s 50% or more > 4.0 > 100 cm/s Near occlusion High, low or undetectable Significant Variable Variable Total occlusion No flow No detectable lumen Not applicable Not applicable Peak systolic velocity and the visible plaque are the primary criteria; the ratio and end-diastolic velocity help when the picture is mixed. A tight stenosis or occlusion on the other side can raise velocities on this side and overcall it. Percentages refer to the NASCET method of measurement, which is what the trials and repair thresholds use. The clinical side is on Presentations . Native arteries and bypass grafts In native leg arteries, a peak systolic velocity ratio of about 2 or more (velocity in the stenosis divided by velocity in the normal segment just upstream) suggests a stenosis of 50 percent or more. Vein grafts are surveilled with duplex because a stenosis found and fixed before the graft clots has far better results than a graft that has already failed. Vein graft duplex surveillance (commonly used thresholds) Risk Duplex findings Usual response Highest PSV over about 300 cm/s or ratio over about 3.5 at a stenosis, plus low velocities (under about 45 cm/s) elsewhere in the graft Prompt angiography and repair; the graft is close to thrombosing High PSV over about 300 cm/s or ratio over about 3.5, graft velocities preserved Plan repair soon Moderate PSV about 180 to 300 cm/s, ratio over about 2 Repeat duplex in about 4 to 6 weeks Low PSV under about 180 cm/s, ratio under 2, normal graft velocities Routine surveillance Uniformly low velocities throughout a graft (under about 45 cm/s) with no focal stenosis also mark a graft at risk, usually from inflow or outflow disease. A drop in ABI of more than about 0.15 between visits should prompt a duplex. Many labs require both the velocity and the ra"},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Imaging","u":"foundations.html#imaging","x":"Imaging modalities Each test answers a different question. Pick the one that answers yours with the least contrast, radiation and risk. Duplex ultrasound first-line, no contrast Cheap, repeatable, operator-dependent. The workhorse for carotid disease, graft surveillance, aneurysm screening and follow-up, DVT, venous reflux and vein mapping. CT angiography the anatomy roadmap Fast and detailed, the go-to for aneurysm sizing, dissection, trauma, and operative planning. The costs are iodinated contrast and radiation, and heavy calcium in small tibial vessels can bloom and hide the lumen. MR angiography contrast-sparing option Good soft-tissue detail without iodinated contrast or radiation. Slower, tends to overestimate stenosis, and degraded by stents and some implants. The gadolinium concern in kidney failure (nephrogenic systemic fibrosis) is now very small with the newer agents, but follow radiology's policy for advanced kidney disease. Digital subtraction angiography the gold standard Catheter angiography has the highest spatial resolution and is the only modality that is also therapeutic. Invasive, so it is reserved for when intervention is likely or other imaging is equivocal. Carbon dioxide angiography can spare iodinated contrast below the diaphragm, never in the cerebral or coronary circulation. Confirm locally Contrast precautions (kidney-function cutoffs, hydration, holding metformin, premedication for contrast allergy) follow radiology and hospital protocols. Confirm locally before you order or promise anything to a patient. What is the gold-standard imaging test for the arterial tree, and why is it not first-line? Catheter-based digital subtraction angiography. It is invasive and carries access-site, contrast, and radiation risk, so noninvasive duplex and CTA come first; angiography is chosen when you intend to treat in the same setting. More on Endovascular ."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Anticoagulation","u":"foundations.html#anticoag","x":"Anticoagulation and antiplatelets Asked often The pharmacology of clotting is asked constantly, both on the wards and in the operating room, and it changes the plan on most vascular patients most mornings. Agents at a glance Drug Mechanism Monitor Reversal Unfractionated heparin Activates antithrombin, inhibiting IIa and Xa aPTT or anti-Xa (ward), ACT (OR) Protamine, about 1 mg per 100 units of heparin still circulating (maximum 50 mg per dose) LMWH (enoxaparin) Antithrombin, preferentially inhibiting Xa Anti-Xa (only if needed: kidney failure, obesity, pregnancy) Protamine (partial) Warfarin Inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase (lowers II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S) INR Vitamin K; 4-factor PCC for urgent reversal (plasma if PCC is unavailable) Apixaban, rivaroxaban Direct factor Xa inhibitor None routine Andexanet alfa or 4-factor PCC, per local protocol Dabigatran Direct thrombin (IIa) inhibitor None routine Idarucizumab; dialyzable Argatroban, bivalirudin IV direct thrombin inhibitors (used for HIT) aPTT (bivalirudin also ACT in procedures) No specific antidote; short half-lives. Argatroban raises the INR, which complicates the switch to warfarin Aspirin Irreversible COX-1 inhibition, lowering thromboxane A2 None Platelet transfusion for serious bleeding Clopidogrel Irreversible P2Y12 (ADP) receptor blocker None Platelet transfusion for serious bleeding For VTE, a weight-based heparin infusion classically starts with an 80 units per kg bolus, then is adjusted to an aPTT or anti-Xa nomogram per local protocol. The overnight version of managing a drip is on On call . Intraoperative heparin Before clamping any artery, the surgeon asks anesthesia to give systemic heparin, classically about 80 to 100 units per kg, and the team confirms an activated clotting time of roughly 250 seconds or more before the clamp goes on (targets vary by surgeon and procedure). At the end, heparin may be reversed with protamine. Red flag: protamine reaction and HIT Protamine, derived from fish sperm, can cause hypotension, bradycardia, and anaphylaxis, especially in patients with prior protamine exposure, fish allergy, or NPH-insulin use, so give it slowly. And never forget heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, an immune reaction (antibodies to the PF4 and heparin complex) appearing about 5 to 10 days after exposure (sooner if the patient had heparin in the past 100 days), causing a falling platelet count and paradoxical thrombosis. Stop all heparin, do not transfuse platelets unless the patient is bleeding, and anticoagulate with a non-heparin agent: argatroban or bivalirudin in the acutely ill or peri-procedural patient, with fondaparinux or a DOAC as options in stable patients. Use the 4T score to gauge probability. The 4T score 4T score for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia Component 2 points 1 point 0 points Thrombocytopenia Fall over 50% and nadir 20 or more (x10 9 /L) Fall 30 to 50%, or nadir 10 to 19 Fall under 30%, or nadir under 10 Timing Clear onset day 5 to 10, or within 1 day if heparin in the past 30 days Probably day 5 to 10 but unclear, after day 10, or within 1 day with heparin 30 to 100 days ago Fall within 4 days with no recent heparin Thrombosis or other sequelae New proven thrombosis, skin necrosis at injection sites, or acute systemic reaction after an IV bolus Progressive or recurrent thrombosis, red skin lesions, or suspected but unproven thrombosis None Other causes None apparent Possible Definite A total of 0 to 3 is low probability, 4 to 5 intermediate, and 6 to 8 high. A low score makes HIT very unlikely; an intermediate or high score means stopping heparin and sending a PF4 immunoassay, with a functional test (serotonin-release assay) to confirm. Resident tip When the platelets fall in a heparinized patient, write the 4T score in your note before you send the antibody test, with a line for each component. If it is intermediate or high, stop heparin everywhere at the same time: the drip, the subcutaneous doses, the line flushes and any heparin-coated catheter. A missed heparin flush is the usual reason \"we stopped heparin\" turns out not to be true. A heparinized patient's platelets drop on day 7 and they clot a graft. Diagnosis and next step? HIT. Stop every source of heparin (including flushes and coated catheters), start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban, send the HIT antibody and serotonin-release assay, and do not give platelet transfusions, which can worsen thrombosis. Do not start warfarin until the platelet count has recovered. Around procedures Whether and when to hold an anticoagulant depends on the drug, kidney function, the bleeding risk of the procedure and the patient's clotting risk. As a common pattern, warfarin is stopped about 5 days before surgery with an INR check the day before, and bridging with heparin is reserved for the highest clotting risk (for example a mechanical mitral valve or a VTE in the past 3 months). Apixaban and rivaroxaban are commonly held about 1 day before a low-bleeding-risk procedure and 2 days before a high-risk one, longer with poor kidney function or neuraxial anesthesia; dabigatran depends heavily on kidney function. Aspirin is usually continued through vascular operations. Confirm locally Periprocedural holds and restarts, bridging, and the intervals before spinal or epidural anesthesia are protocol-driven and differ between hospitals, anesthesia teams and attendings. Confirm locally, and write the plan explicitly in the note: what is held, when the last dose was, and when it restarts."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Medical therapy","u":"foundations.html#medical","x":"Medical and risk-factor therapy Revascularization treats the plumbing; medical therapy treats the disease. Every patient with PAD should be on best medical therapy regardless of whether they get an operation, and it is a fair question on rounds for any vascular patient. Smoking cessation. The single highest-impact intervention, slowing progression and improving graft patency more than any drug. Offer counseling and pharmacotherapy (nicotine replacement, varenicline) at every encounter. High-intensity statin. For every atherosclerotic patient, for plaque stabilization and cardiovascular and limb event reduction, not just lipids. Antiplatelet therapy. A single agent, aspirin or clopidogrel, for symptomatic PAD. Dual antiplatelet therapy after endovascular intervention is common for a period, with practice varying. Low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin. In selected patients, to reduce limb and cardiovascular events (below). Blood pressure and diabetes control. Plus an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 agonist where indicated for cardiovascular benefit, and daily foot inspection and podiatry care for diabetic patients. Supervised exercise therapy. First-line for claudication; it rivals revascularization for walking distance (below). Cilostazol. A phosphodiesterase-3 inhibitor that improves claudication distance, but is contraindicated in heart failure of any severity. Rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily plus aspirin This \"vascular dose\" of rivaroxaban, a quarter of the usual VTE dose and given with low-dose aspirin, is a different strategy from full anticoagulation. In COMPASS, patients with stable coronary disease or PAD had fewer major cardiovascular events and fewer major adverse limb events, including major amputation, at the cost of more major bleeding. In VOYAGER PAD, patients who had just undergone lower-extremity revascularization (open or endovascular) had fewer events in a composite of acute limb ischemia, major amputation, heart attack, stroke and cardiovascular death, driven largely by less acute limb ischemia, again with more bleeding. The 2024 ACC/AHA PAD guideline recommends it for symptomatic PAD and after revascularization in patients who are not at high bleeding risk. What is the VOYAGER PAD regimen, and who is it for? Rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily plus aspirin, started after lower-extremity revascularization, in patients without high bleeding risk and without another indication for full-dose anticoagulation. Short-term clopidogrel (up to 6 months) was permitted in the trial at the investigator's discretion. Statin intensity and LDL goals Statin intensity Intensity Expected LDL reduction Examples High 50% or more Atorvastatin 40 to 80 mg; rosuvastatin 20 to 40 mg Moderate 30 to 49% Atorvastatin 10 to 20 mg; rosuvastatin 5 to 10 mg; simvastatin 20 to 40 mg; pravastatin 40 to 80 mg Every patient with PAD should be on a high-intensity statin, or the highest dose they tolerate. The 2026 ACC/AHA dyslipidemia guideline restored LDL goals: for patients at very high risk, which includes many with symptomatic PAD and other atherosclerotic disease, the target is an LDL below 55 mg/dL (non-HDL below 85 mg/dL), adding ezetimibe or a PCSK9 inhibitor when a maximally tolerated statin does not get there. European guidance uses the same 55 mg/dL (1.4 mmol/L) target with at least a 50 percent reduction. The 2024 ACC/AHA PAD guideline's older trigger for add-on therapy was an LDL of 70 mg/dL or more. Supervised exercise therapy Supervised exercise is a class 1 recommendation for claudication and the first treatment to offer. A typical program is 30 to 60 minutes of treadmill walking at least three times a week for at least 12 weeks: walk to moderate or near-maximal claudication pain, rest until it settles, and repeat. Medicare covers up to 36 sessions over 12 weeks for symptomatic PAD with a referral. When a supervised program is not available, a structured home- or community-based walking program is the alternative. What is the most important intervention for a smoker with claudication? Smoking cessation, paired with a statin, antiplatelet therapy, and a supervised exercise program. For pure claudication, medical therapy and exercise come before any consideration of intervention."},{"p":"Foundations","t":"Perioperative cardiac risk","u":"foundations.html#cardiac-risk","x":"Perioperative cardiac risk The vascular patient is a cardiac patient. Atherosclerosis rarely confines itself to one bed, and myocardial infarction is the leading cause of death both around vascular operations and in the years after. Assessing and managing cardiac risk is part of every preoperative workup on this service. Pearl When patients scheduled for vascular surgery underwent routine coronary angiography in a classic Cleveland Clinic series (Hertzer, 1984), only about 8 percent had normal coronary arteries. Yet in the CARP trial (2004), revascularizing stable coronary disease before elective vascular surgery did not improve long-term survival. The lesson: assume coronary disease, optimize medically, and revascularize only when the heart needs it on its own merits. The stepwise approach The 2024 ACC/AHA perioperative guideline follows a sequence you can apply to any elective vascular patient. Is it an emergency? A ruptured aneurysm or an immediately threatened limb goes to the operating room; cardiac risk is managed around the operation, not before it. Is there an active cardiac condition? Acute coronary syndrome, decompensated heart failure, a significant arrhythmia or severe valve disease needs evaluation and treatment first unless the surgery is an emergency. Estimate the risk. Use a validated tool such as the RCRI or the NSQIP calculator. Two or more RCRI points, or a predicted risk of major cardiac events of 1 percent or more, is elevated risk. Assess functional capacity. A patient who can do 4 METs or more (climb two flights of stairs, walk up a hill, do heavy housework) without symptoms can proceed without further testing. The guideline also accepts a Duke Activity Status Index score above 34. Test only if it changes management. In an elevated-risk patient with poor or unknown functional capacity, pharmacologic stress testing is reasonable only if the result would change care: coronary revascularization indicated regardless of the operation, a different operation (endovascular rather than open), or not operating. Routine screening stress tests are not recommended. The guideline also supports measuring BNP or NT-proBNP before surgery in selected higher-risk patients for added prognostic information, and troponin surveillance after surgery in elevated-risk patients, because myocardial injury after noncardiac surgery is common, usually painless, and carries real mortality. Revised Cardiac Risk Index (one point each) Factor What counts High-risk surgery Intraperitoneal, intrathoracic, or suprainguinal vascular. Open aortic repair counts; an infrainguinal bypass does not. Ischemic heart disease Prior MI, positive stress test, current angina, nitrate use, or pathologic Q waves Heart failure History of heart failure, pulmonary edema, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, S3, bilateral rales, or vascular redistribution on chest film Cerebrovascular disease Prior stroke or TIA Diabetes on insulin Insulin-treated diabetes Kidney dysfunction Preoperative creatinine above 2.0 mg/dL The RCRI is known to underestimate risk in vascular surgery patients, which is why vascular-specific tools such as the Vascular Quality Initiative cardiac risk index exist. It remains the score you will be asked to recite. Pearl Functional capacity is often unknowable in vascular patients. Someone who stops at half a block because of calf pain, or who has rest pain or an amputation, cannot tell you whether their heart would let them climb two flights of stairs. That is why vascular patients reach the \"test only if it changes management\" step more often than most surgical patients. Medications around surgery Beta-blockers Continue them in patients already taking them; abrupt withdrawal causes rebound tachycardia and ischemia. Do not start a beta-blocker on the day of surgery: in POISE, starting metoprolol just before surgery reduced heart attacks but increased strokes and deaths, largely through hypotension and bradycardia. If a patient needs one for another reason, it is started well ahead of surgery and titrated. Statins Continue them. Vascular patients who are not on a statin almost always have an indication, and guidelines support starting one before vascular surgery. Antiplatelets Aspirin is usually continued through vascular operations. Never stop a P2Y12 inhibitor in a patient with a recent coronary stent without talking to cardiology; elective surgery is usually deferred after a recent stent. Anticoagulants Held and restarted according to a written plan; see Anticoagulation . SGLT2 inhibitors Held for 3 days before surgery (4 days for ertugliflozin) because of the risk of euglycemic ketoacidosis. ACE inhibitors and ARBs Practice varies; some teams hold them the morning of surgery to reduce hypotension after induction, while continuing them in heart failure is reasonable. Confirm locally Which patients go through a preoperative anesthesia clinic, who orders an echocardiogram or stress test, and the medication-hold rules (DOACs, SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 agonists, ACE inhibitors) follow anesthesia and hospital protocols. Confirm locally before telling a patient what to take on the morning of surgery. Resident tip Put four things in every preoperative note: the RCRI points by name, functional capacity in METs (or why it cannot be assessed, such as \"limited by claudication at one block\"), the cardiac medications with the time of the last beta-blocker dose, and any coronary stents with the date and the antiplatelet agent. Those are the questions anesthesia and your attending will ask, and having them written saves a phone call on the morning of surgery. A claudicant with two RCRI points cannot climb a flight of stairs because of calf pain. Does she need a stress test before a fem-pop bypass? Only if the result would change management. She is elevated risk with unknown functional capacity, so pharmacologic stress testing is reasonable if a positive result would lead to coronary revascularization she needs anyway, to an "},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Presentations","u":"presentations.html","x":"The diseases that fill the consult list. For each one: what it is, how it shows up, how it is classified, and what is done about it. Read these in your first week, because they are most of what the service does and most of what you will be asked."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"PAD and claudication","u":"presentations.html#claudication","x":"Peripheral arterial disease and claudication PAD is atherosclerotic narrowing of the limb arteries. It runs on a spectrum from asymptomatic, through intermittent claudication, to chronic limb-threatening ischemia at the severe end. Intermittent claudication is reproducible exertional muscle pain, cramping or fatigue that is relieved by a few minutes of standing still. It is a quality-of-life problem with a low rate of limb loss (on the order of 1 to 2 percent a year). The real danger to these patients is their coexisting coronary and cerebrovascular disease, which is why every claudicant is a cardiovascular risk-reduction patient first. The two historical staging systems are worth knowing because attendings switch between them freely. Fontaine stages and Rutherford categories (chronic ischemia) Fontaine Rutherford Clinical picture I 0 Asymptomatic IIa 1 Mild claudication IIb 2 to 3 Moderate to severe claudication III 4 Ischemic rest pain IV 5 to 6 Tissue loss: minor ulcer (5), then major tissue loss or gangrene (6) Management is medical first Risk-factor therapy for everyone. Smoking cessation, a high-intensity statin, an antiplatelet, and control of blood pressure and diabetes. Low-dose rivaroxaban (2.5 mg twice daily) with aspirin is an option in selected patients to cut limb and cardiovascular events. Details are in Foundations . Supervised exercise therapy. First-line for walking distance, and it rivals revascularization for claudication. Cilostazol adds a modest benefit if there is no heart failure. Revascularization only for lifestyle-limiting symptoms that persist despite medical therapy and exercise, in a patient who understands the trade-off. The choice between endovascular and open repair depends on the location and length of the disease. Interventions below the knee for claudication alone are generally discouraged, because durability is poor and failure can make the limb worse. A 64-year-old smoker can walk two blocks before calf pain stops him. What do you offer first? Smoking cessation, a high-intensity statin, an antiplatelet, and a supervised exercise program, with cilostazol if there is no heart failure. Claudication is treated medically first; intervention is reserved for lifestyle-limiting symptoms that persist after a fair trial."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"CLTI","u":"presentations.html#clti","x":"Chronic limb-threatening ischemia CLTI, the modern term for \"critical limb ischemia,\" is the severe end of PAD: ischemic rest pain for more than two weeks, a non-healing ulcer, or gangrene, together with objectively reduced perfusion. Rest pain is classically in the forefoot and toes, worse lying flat at night, and relieved by hanging the foot off the bed, because gravity restores a trickle of perfusion. Many patients sleep in a chair. Ask about it specifically, because they rarely volunteer it. Pearl: WIfI The WIfI classification stages limb threat along three axes: Wound, Ischemia (ABI, ankle pressure or toe pressure) and foot Infection, each graded 0 to 3. It reframes the decision from \"is there a pulse\" to \"what is this limb's risk of amputation and how much would revascularization help,\" which is how vascular surgeons now think. Grades are in Quick reference and how to document them is in charting . GLASS (the Global Limb Anatomic Staging System) is the anatomic partner to WIfI. It grades the femoropopliteal and infrapopliteal disease along a chosen target artery path, to estimate how likely an endovascular approach is to succeed. Together, WIfI and GLASS frame the patient, the limb and the anatomy, the structure used by the Global Vascular Guidelines. What the trials changed BEST-CLI (2022). In patients with an adequate single-segment great saphenous vein, a surgical bypass-first strategy led to fewer major adverse limb events or deaths than an endovascular-first strategy. Without good vein, the two strategies performed similarly. This revived vein bypass in the right patient and made vein mapping a routine part of the workup. BASIL-2 (2023). For CLTI needing an infrapopliteal revascularization, a best-endovascular-first strategy gave better amputation-free survival than vein bypass first, driven mainly by fewer deaths. It applies to a different, more distal population than BEST-CLI. The practical upshot is individual: the patient's risk, the limb's WIfI stage, the anatomy, and the availability of good vein all shape the choice. CLTI demands revascularization wherever feasible, plus aggressive wound care, offloading and infection control. Red flag Untreated CLTI carries a high one-year risk of major amputation and death. Rest pain, a non-healing wound, or any gangrene is not something to watch; it needs a revascularization plan. Resident tip On every CLTI consult, ask whether the great saphenous veins are still present and order vein mapping early if bypass is possible. \"Has the vein been used for a CABG or a prior bypass?\" is the question that most often changes the plan."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Acute limb ischemia","u":"presentations.html#ali","x":"Acute limb ischemia Emergency ALI is a sudden loss of arterial perfusion that threatens limb viability, presenting within two weeks of onset. Muscle tolerates roughly 4 to 6 hours of severe ischemia before irreversible injury begins. Mnemonic: the six Ps Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis, poikilothermia (the perishingly cold limb). The order matters clinically, because paresthesia and paralysis are the late, ominous signs. A limb with sensory or motor loss is on the clock; pain and pallor alone are earlier and more recoverable. Embolic or thrombotic: the central distinction The mechanism changes the operation, so this comparison is asked relentlessly. Embolic versus in situ thrombotic ALI Feature Embolic Thrombotic Onset Sudden and dramatic, an acute event in a previously normal limb More gradual; often acute on chronic Source or setting The heart: atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus after MI, valve disease Pre-existing atherosclerotic plaque or a bypass graft Prior symptoms Usually none; no claudication history Pre-existing claudication or known PAD Other leg Normal pulses Also diseased, with diminished pulses Collaterals Few, so ischemia is severe Well developed, so sometimes better tolerated Classic site Common femoral bifurcation, also the popliteal trifurcation A prior stenosis or graft What is the most common source of an arterial embolus, and where does it lodge in the leg? The heart, most often from atrial fibrillation. Emboli lodge at branch points where the vessel narrows; in the leg the common femoral bifurcation is the classic site. Rutherford classification of ALI This is the table that decides what happens next. Rutherford acute limb ischemia categories Class Status Sensory loss Motor loss Doppler, arterial and venous What it means I Viable None None Audible, audible Not immediately threatened; time to image and plan IIa Marginally threatened Minimal (toes) or none None Often inaudible, audible Salvageable if treated promptly IIb Immediately threatened More than toes, with rest pain Mild to moderate Inaudible, audible Salvageable only with immediate revascularization III Irreversible Profound, anesthetic Paralysis, rigor Inaudible, inaudible Not salvageable; primary amputation Management Anticoagulate immediately. A systemic heparin bolus and infusion the moment ALI is suspected, unless contraindicated, to stop clot propagation. Do not wait for imaging. Grade the limb. The Rutherford class decides the path: I and IIa allow time for CTA and possibly catheter-directed therapy; IIb needs revascularization now, usually in the OR; III is not salvageable. Revascularize. Balloon embolectomy with a Fogarty catheter (classic for embolic disease), catheter-directed thrombolysis or mechanical thrombectomy (often suited to thrombotic disease and category I to IIa limbs), surgical bypass, or a hybrid of these. Anticipate reperfusion. Restoring flow to ischemic muscle releases potassium, myoglobin and acid. Watch for hyperkalemia, rhabdomyolysis, acute kidney injury and compartment syndrome, and keep a low threshold for fasciotomy. Find the source. After an embolus: ECG and telemetry for atrial fibrillation, an echocardiogram, and a plan for long-term anticoagulation. Red flag: reperfusion injury After revascularizing a limb that was ischemic for several hours, a tense, painful compartment with pain on passive stretch is compartment syndrome until proven otherwise. Pulses may still be present, so do not wait for them to disappear. The treatment is urgent four-compartment fasciotomy. Watch the potassium and the kidneys at the same time. Resident tip: the first ten minutes Examine and Doppler both legs and write down exactly what you find, including sensation and toe movement. Establish the time of onset and whether there is atrial fibrillation. Start heparin unless there is a clear contraindication, send a CBC, BMP, coags, CK, lactate and type and screen, and call your senior with the Rutherford class. Overnight versions of this call are on On call ."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm","u":"presentations.html#aaa","x":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm An aneurysm is a focal dilation more than 1.5 times the normal vessel diameter. For the infrarenal aorta that means 3.0 cm or more. Most are infrarenal and degenerative. Risk factors are smoking (the strongest modifiable one), male sex, older age, a family history in a first-degree relative, and other atherosclerotic disease. Diabetes is, curiously, protective. Pearl: true and false aneurysms A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the wall: intima, media and adventitia. A pseudoaneurysm, or false aneurysm, is a contained rupture held in only by the outer layer or the surrounding tissue, classically at an anastomosis or a femoral puncture site. The distinction is a frequent question. Screening and surveillance The USPSTF recommends a one-time screening ultrasound for men aged 65 to 75 who have ever smoked, with selective screening for men who never smoked. The SVS recommends a broader net: men and women aged 65 to 75 with a smoking history, and people with a first-degree relative with an AAA. Small aneurysms are followed by ultrasound at intervals that shorten as they grow. Surveillance intervals by diameter (SVS 2018) Diameter Repeat imaging 2.5 to 2.9 cm Rescreen in about 10 years (an ectatic aorta; European guidance suggests about 5) 3.0 to 3.9 cm Every 3 years 4.0 to 4.9 cm Every 12 months 5.0 to 5.4 cm Every 6 months When to repair electively Diameter of 5.5 cm or more in men. Diameter of 5.0 cm or more in women (European guidance; the SVS suggests considering repair from 5.0 to 5.4 cm in women). Rapid growth: more than 1 cm in a year, or more than 0.5 cm in six months. Symptoms at any size: tenderness, back or abdominal pain attributable to the aneurysm, or distal embolization. Repair is by EVAR when the anatomy suits it, or open repair. EVAR has lower early mortality, the long-term survival of the two converges, and EVAR needs lifelong imaging surveillance because of endoleak and late failure. Both are covered in Open operations and Endovascular . At what size do you repair an asymptomatic infrarenal AAA in a man? 5.5 cm (5.0 cm in women), or sooner for rapid growth or symptoms. Below threshold the rupture risk is lower than the operative risk, so you survey the aneurysm and optimize medical therapy. Red flag: ruptured AAA The classic triad is sudden abdominal, back or flank pain, hypotension, and a pulsatile mass. An older patient with hypotension and back pain is a ruptured AAA until proven otherwise. Call the attending, the OR and the blood bank (massive transfusion) at once. Practice permissive hypotension, keeping systolic pressure only high enough to keep the patient awake and perfusing (around 70 to 90 mmHg), because aggressive fluid and a higher pressure can blow out a contained rupture. A stable patient may get a CTA to plan EVAR, which is preferred when the anatomy allows; an unstable one goes straight to the OR, where an aortic occlusion balloon can buy control. Variants to recognize Inflammatory AAA A thick, inflamed wall with a peri-aortic rind, raised inflammatory markers, often pain, and classically ureteral entrapment. The inflammation often settles after repair. Mycotic (infected) AAA Saccular, often in an unusual location, rapidly expanding, in a febrile patient. Salmonella and Staphylococcus are classic organisms. Needs prolonged antibiotics and repair, with attention to infected-field reconstruction."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Carotid disease","u":"presentations.html#carotid","x":"Carotid disease and stroke prevention Plaque at the carotid bifurcation causes stroke mainly by embolization of plaque or thrombus to the brain, and less often by flow limitation from a tight stenosis. The whole point of carotid intervention is stroke prevention, not symptom relief. Symptomatic or asymptomatic Symptomatic disease means an ipsilateral neurologic event in the previous six months: a TIA, a stroke, or amaurosis fugax (transient monocular blindness, \"a curtain coming down,\" from a retinal artery embolus). Asymptomatic disease is found on screening or because of a bruit. What is amaurosis fugax and what does it signify? Transient, painless loss of vision in one eye from an embolus to the ophthalmic or retinal artery, a branch of the internal carotid. It is a form of TIA and makes that carotid symptomatic, lowering the threshold to intervene. Indications, and what the trials showed NASCET and ECST established the benefit of endarterectomy for symptomatic stenosis. ACAS and ACST-1 showed a smaller benefit in asymptomatic patients, in an era before modern medical therapy. Carotid revascularization thresholds Group Stenosis Recommendation Symptomatic 70 to 99% Clear benefit from revascularization, ideally within about 2 weeks of the event Symptomatic 50 to 69% Moderate benefit; individualize (greater in men and with recent hemispheric events) Symptomatic Under 50% Best medical therapy; do not operate Asymptomatic 70 to 99% Selective: consider in low-risk patients with a reasonable life expectancy, alongside intensive medical therapy Occlusion 100% Generally not revascularized CREST-2 (reported in 2025) compared intensive medical management alone with revascularization plus intensive medical management in asymptomatic patients with severe stenosis. In the stenting comparison, adding stenting lowered the rate of stroke; in the endarterectomy comparison, the benefit did not reach statistical significance. How this changes practice for asymptomatic disease is still being worked out, so expect your attendings to discuss it and guidelines to move. Endarterectomy, transfemoral stenting or TCAR Carotid endarterectomy remains the reference standard. Transfemoral carotid stenting is an alternative, particularly for a hostile neck (prior radiation or neck surgery, a high lesion) or high surgical risk, but CREST showed stenting carried more periprocedural strokes and surgery more myocardial infarctions, with stenting faring worse in older patients. Transcarotid artery revascularization (TCAR) uses a small incision at the base of the neck, direct carotid access and flow reversal for embolic protection, avoiding the aortic arch, and has become a widely used middle path. The operations are described in Open operations . Red flag: timing after a stroke After a stroke or TIA, revascularization is generally done within about two weeks in stable patients, because recurrence risk is front-loaded. It is deferred in the hyperacute setting of a large infarct or hemorrhagic transformation, where restored flow can cause bleeding. Coordinate the timing with neurology."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Mesenteric ischemia","u":"presentations.html#mesenteric","x":"Mesenteric ischemia Reduced blood flow to the gut, split into an acute emergency and a chronic, indolent form. The vessels at stake are the celiac trunk, the SMA and the IMA. Acute mesenteric ischemia Emergency The hallmark is pain out of proportion to the exam: a patient in severe abdominal pain with a soft, relatively benign-feeling abdomen early on. Peritonitis, bloody stool and shock are late signs that usually mean dead bowel. Causes of acute mesenteric ischemia Mechanism Setting Note Arterial embolism (most common) Atrial fibrillation, after MI Lodges in the SMA, classically sparing the proximal jejunum Arterial thrombosis Pre-existing mesenteric atherosclerosis Often a prior history of chronic mesenteric ischemia; the occlusion is at the origin Non-occlusive (NOMI) Low flow or shock, vasopressors, dialysis, cardiac surgery Splanchnic vasoconstriction with no clot to remove; treat the hemodynamics Mesenteric venous thrombosis Hypercoagulable states, portal hypertension, malignancy More subacute; anticoagulation is the mainstay The workup shows leukocytosis, a rising lactate and a metabolic acidosis, although a normal lactate early does not exclude it. CTA is the imaging test of choice. Management is to resuscitate, start heparin, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, revascularize urgently (embolectomy, bypass, or endovascular stenting, sometimes retrograde through an open SMA), and resect frankly necrotic bowel, with a planned second-look operation at 24 to 48 hours for marginal segments. Why does an SMA embolus spare the proximal jejunum? The embolus typically lodges a few centimeters into the SMA, distal to the first jejunal branches and the middle colic origin, so the proximal jejunum (and sometimes the proximal colon) keeps its perfusion while the rest of the small bowel infarcts. A thrombosis at the origin, by contrast, takes the whole SMA territory. Chronic mesenteric ischemia \"Intestinal angina.\" The triad is postprandial abdominal pain, food fear (sitophobia) and weight loss, in an older patient with vascular disease. Because the collateral network is rich, symptoms usually need significant disease in at least two of the three vessels. Treatment is revascularization, now mostly endovascular stenting of the SMA, with open bypass for selected patients. Pearl: median arcuate ligament syndrome Extrinsic compression of the celiac artery by the median arcuate ligament of the diaphragm, classically in younger, thinner patients with postprandial pain and an epigastric bruit that changes with respiration. It is a diagnosis of exclusion, and treatment is surgical release of the ligament, not stenting, because a stent is crushed by the ligament."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Aortic dissection","u":"presentations.html#dissection","x":"Aortic dissection Emergency A tear in the intima lets blood track into the media, creating a false lumen that can propagate, occlude branch vessels (malperfusion), or rupture. The presentation is sudden, severe, tearing chest or back pain, sometimes with a pulse or blood pressure difference between the arms, or signs of organ malperfusion: a cold leg, abdominal pain with a rising lactate, falling urine output, or a neurologic deficit. Dissection classifications Stanford DeBakey Involves Default management Type A I and II The ascending aorta (I: ascending plus arch and beyond; II: ascending only) Emergency open repair by cardiac surgery; risk of tamponade, aortic insufficiency and coronary involvement Type B III The descending aorta, distal to the left subclavian Medical first; TEVAR if complicated The 2020 SVS/STS reporting system classifies by the location of the entry tear : type A if it is in zone 0 (the ascending aorta), type B if it is beyond zone 0, with subscripts for the proximal and distal extent by aortic zone (for example B 3,10 ). A type B tear whose dissection extends back into the arch is written B 0 or B 1 or B 2 , the group older papers called \"non-A non-B.\" Chronicity is hyperacute (under 24 hours), acute (1 to 14 days), subacute (15 to 90 days) and chronic (over 90 days). Zones are explained in Endovascular . Initial medical management The goal is to reduce the force of each heartbeat on the aortic wall (the rate of rise of pressure) and the pressure itself. Start an IV beta-blocker first (esmolol or labetalol), then add a vasodilator such as nicardipine, clevidipine or nitroprusside if the pressure is still high. The 2022 ACC/AHA targets are a heart rate of 60 to 80 and a systolic under 120 mmHg, or the lowest pressure that keeps the brain, kidneys and gut perfused. Older teaching used a heart rate under 60, so confirm the target your attending wants. Treat the pain, place an arterial line, and admit to the CTV ICU . Red flag: sequence matters Never give a vasodilator before beta-blockade. A vasodilator alone triggers reflex tachycardia, which increases the shear on the wall and can extend the dissection. Beta-blocker first, always. If a beta-blocker is contraindicated, a non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker (diltiazem or verapamil) controls the rate. Type A dissections go to cardiac surgery emergently. Type B dissections are managed medically unless complicated by malperfusion, rupture, rapid expansion, or pain or hypertension that will not settle, in which case TEVAR to cover the entry tear is the usual intervention. Uncomplicated type B dissections with high-risk features (such as a total aortic diameter of 40 mm or more, or a large entry tear) are increasingly considered for TEVAR in the subacute window. A patient with a type B dissection on an esmolol drip develops a cold, pulseless left leg. What has happened, and what changes? Limb malperfusion: the false lumen is compressing or the flap is obstructing the iliac flow. The dissection is now complicated, which moves it from medical management toward urgent intervention, usually TEVAR to cover the entry tear and re-expand the true lumen, with or without a direct limb procedure. Call the attending now."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"DVT and VTE","u":"presentations.html#vte","x":"Deep vein thrombosis and venous thromboembolism Venous thromboembolism spans DVT and pulmonary embolism. Its pathophysiology is captured by Virchow's triad. Mnemonic: Virchow's triad Stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability. Every DVT risk factor (immobility, surgery, malignancy, estrogen, pregnancy, inherited thrombophilia) maps onto one of the three. Diagnosis combines pretest probability (the Wells score), D-dimer (useful to rule out disease when probability is low) and compression duplex ultrasound, the confirmatory test, which shows a non-compressible vein. Treatment Anticoagulation is the treatment. A direct oral anticoagulant is first-line for most patients, including many with cancer. Low-molecular-weight heparin or warfarin suits others (pregnancy, severe renal failure, antiphospholipid syndrome). Duration. About three months for a DVT provoked by a transient risk factor such as surgery. For an unprovoked DVT or a persistent risk factor, extended anticoagulation is considered, balanced against bleeding risk. Isolated calf DVT. Either anticoagulate or repeat the ultrasound in one to two weeks to look for extension, depending on symptoms and risk. Thrombus removal. Catheter-directed lysis or mechanical thrombectomy for selected patients with acute iliofemoral DVT, severe symptoms and low bleeding risk. ATTRACT (2017) did not reduce post-thrombotic syndrome overall but did reduce its severity, with the best signal in iliofemoral disease; CAVA (2020) found no reduction. So it is a selective, not a routine, therapy. IVC filters. The clear indication is acute proximal DVT or PE in a patient who cannot be anticoagulated. Placing a filter for recurrent PE despite anticoagulation is debated, and PREPIC2 found no benefit from adding a filter to anticoagulation. Plan the retrieval when the filter goes in. Red flag: phlegmasia Massive iliofemoral DVT can cause phlegmasia cerulea dolens: a swollen, blue, painful leg from near-total venous outflow obstruction that can progress to venous gangrene and compartment syndrome. It is a limb-threatening emergency that needs urgent thrombus removal (lysis or thrombectomy), not anticoagulation alone. Its milder, pale precursor is phlegmasia alba dolens. Pearl: May-Thurner May-Thurner syndrome is compression of the left common iliac vein by the overlying right common iliac artery against the spine, predisposing to left-leg DVT, classically in younger women. It is why an \"unprovoked\" left iliofemoral DVT deserves a look at the iliac vein, often treated with thrombus removal and venous stenting under IVUS guidance. Superficial thrombophlebitis of the great saphenous vein extends to within 2 cm of the saphenofemoral junction. How do you treat it? As a DVT: therapeutic anticoagulation, because thrombus within about 3 cm of the junction can propagate into the common femoral vein. Longer superficial thromboses farther from the junction (5 cm or more in length) are commonly treated with prophylactic-dose fondaparinux for about 45 days."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Chronic venous insufficiency","u":"presentations.html#cvi","x":"Chronic venous insufficiency and varicose veins When venous valves fail, blood refluxes and venous pressure stays high (venous hypertension), producing varicose veins, heaviness, edema, skin changes and ultimately ulceration. Mnemonic: CEAP Clinical, etiologic, anatomic, pathophysiologic. In practice the clinical class is the part everyone quotes. CEAP clinical classes (2020 revision) Class Findings C0 No visible or palpable signs C1 Telangiectasias or reticular veins C2 Varicose veins (C2r if recurrent) C3 Edema C4a Pigmentation or eczema C4b Lipodermatosclerosis or atrophie blanche C4c Corona phlebectatica (a fan of small veins at the ankle) C5 Healed venous ulcer C6 Active venous ulcer (C6r if recurrent) Venous or arterial ulcer? Venous ulcer Medial malleolus, the gaiter area Irregular, shallow, granulating and exudative Mild pain, better with elevation Pigmentation, edema, lipodermatosclerosis around it Pulses present Cornerstone: compression Arterial ulcer Toes, heel, pressure points and distal tips Punched out, dry, with a pale or necrotic base Severe pain, worse with elevation, better dependent Shiny, hairless, cool, atrophic skin Pulses diminished or absent Cornerstone: revascularization Red flag: check the inflow before you compress Never apply compression to a leg you have not confirmed has adequate arterial inflow, because compressing an ischemic leg can cause tissue loss. Check the ABI before wrapping a \"venous-looking\" ulcer. As a rough guide, full compression is avoided when the ABI is below about 0.5 to 0.6, and reduced compression is used in the mixed range below 0.8. Management Treatment starts with compression and elevation. For refluxing superficial veins, the options are endovenous thermal ablation (radiofrequency or laser), non-thermal ablation (cyanoacrylate glue or mechanochemical ablation), foam sclerotherapy, and phlebectomy for the varicosities themselves. For a venous ulcer, the EVRA trial (2018) showed that early ablation of superficial reflux, added to compression, healed ulcers faster and reduced recurrence. After thermal ablation, a duplex looks for endovenous heat-induced thrombosis (EHIT) extending toward the deep vein."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"The diabetic foot","u":"presentations.html#diabetic-foot","x":"The diabetic foot Diabetic foot disease is the convergence of three processes: neuropathy, ischemia and infection. Each worsens the others, and together they make the diabetic foot the leading cause of non-traumatic amputation. Neuropathy. Loss of protective sensation leads to unnoticed trauma. Neuropathic ulcers sit under pressure points (metatarsal heads, heel) in a foot that may have intact circulation. Ischemia. Diabetic PAD favors the tibial vessels below the knee, often sparing the foot arteries, and calcification can make the ABI falsely high, so use toe pressures. Infection. Can spread fast along tendon sheaths and compartments with surprisingly little systemic response. A deep space infection or wet gangrene is a surgical emergency for drainage first. Charcot foot. Progressive neuropathic joint destruction producing the collapsed \"rocker-bottom\" foot. In the acute phase it can mimic infection (red, hot, swollen) without a wound. Severity is staged with WIfI, which has largely replaced older systems such as Wagner and the University of Texas classification for limb-threat decisions. A positive probe-to-bone test suggests osteomyelitis; X-ray and then MRI help confirm it. Pearl Management is multidisciplinary and follows the same logic as the rest of the service: drain and debride and control infection first, offload pressure, and revascularize if there is ischemia. Because diabetic disease favors the tibial vessels with patent pedal targets, a distal bypass or a pedal intervention can save a foot that looks unsalvageable. Many centers run this as a \"toe and flow\" team with podiatry."},{"p":"Presentations","t":"Other presentations","u":"presentations.html#other-pres","x":"Other key presentations Briefer, but each is a known consult and a known question. Popliteal artery aneurysm clots, not ruptures The most common peripheral aneurysm. The danger is thrombosis and distal embolization causing limb loss, not rupture. Often bilateral and associated with an AAA, so image the other knee and the aorta. Repair is generally offered at about 2 cm or more, or with mural thrombus or symptoms at any size. Splenic and other visceral aneurysms pregnancy risk The splenic artery is the most common visceral aneurysm, with a female predominance and a notable risk of rupture in pregnancy. Treat at about 3 cm or more, and at smaller sizes in women who are or may become pregnant. Visceral pseudoaneurysms (after pancreatitis or trauma) are treated at any size, usually by embolization. Renal artery stenosis two populations Atherosclerotic (older patients, ostial lesions) and fibromuscular dysplasia (younger women, a \"string of beads\" in the mid vessel). Can cause renovascular hypertension and flash pulmonary edema. CORAL found no routine benefit from stenting atherosclerotic disease, so intervention is selective; FMD is treated with balloon angioplasty without a stent. Fibromuscular dysplasia A non-atherosclerotic arteriopathy of medium arteries, mainly renal and carotid or vertebral. It can cause dissections and aneurysms elsewhere, so patients are imaged from head to pelvis once. Thoracic outlet syndrome three types Neurogenic (most common, brachial plexus compression). Venous: Paget-Schroetter \"effort thrombosis\" of the subclavian vein, treated with thrombolysis then first rib resection. Arterial (rarest): subclavian aneurysm or stenosis from a cervical rib, with embolization to the hand. Decompression usually means first rib resection. Subclavian steal the arm steals from the brain A proximal subclavian stenosis causes reversed flow down the vertebral artery to feed the arm, giving posterior circulation symptoms with arm exercise and an arm-to-arm blood pressure difference. More common on the left. Atheroembolism blue toe Cholesterol crystals showered from an aortic or iliac plaque, often after a catheterization: painful blue toes with palpable pulses, livedo reticularis, sometimes kidney injury and eosinophilia. Find and treat the source, statin and antiplatelet; anticoagulation does not help. Buerger disease young smoker, digits Thromboangiitis obliterans: inflammatory, non-atherosclerotic occlusion of small and medium vessels in young smokers, causing digital ischemia and ulcers. The only treatment that works is complete abstinence from tobacco, including nicotine replacement in some patients. Popliteal entrapment young athlete An abnormal relationship between the popliteal artery and the gastrocnemius (or popliteus) compresses flow with exercise. Suspect it in a fit young person with calf claudication and normal pulses at rest that fade with plantar flexion. Vascular trauma hard and soft signs Hard signs (pulsatile bleeding, an expanding hematoma, a bruit or thrill, an absent pulse, distal ischemia) go to the OR. Soft signs (a diminished pulse, a stable hematoma, proximity to a vessel, a neurologic deficit) get CTA or an injured extremity index (ABI below 0.9 is abnormal). Large-vessel vasculitis Takayasu arteritis (\"pulseless disease,\" younger women, aortic arch branches) and giant cell arteritis (over 50, temporal artery, jaw claudication, threat to vision, but also the subclavian and axillary arteries). Avoid operating during active inflammation. The most common consults, with their first moves, are on Rounds and presenting ."},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Open operations","u":"operations.html","x":"The cases you will scrub and the patients you will look after afterward. Every entry follows the same pattern: indications, positioning and exposure, the steps in order, the questions you will be asked in the room, the risks discussed at consent, and what to watch for after the case. Read the entry the night before, and know the anatomy in the wound; that answers most of what you will be asked while scrubbed."},{"p":"Open operations","t":"CEA and TCAR","u":"operations.html#cea","x":"Carotid endarterectomy High yield Removal of the atherosclerotic plaque at the carotid bifurcation to prevent stroke: open the artery, peel out the plaque, and close, usually with a patch. TCAR, the hybrid stenting operation done through a smaller neck incision, closes out this section. Indications Symptomatic stenosis. A stroke, TIA or amaurosis fugax in the matching territory with 50 to 99 percent stenosis (NASCET method), in a patient with a reasonable life expectancy. The benefit is greatest at 70 to 99 percent and when the operation is done within about two weeks of the event. Asymptomatic stenosis. Treated more selectively: usually 70 to 99 percent (some guidelines use 60 percent) in a low-risk patient expected to live at least three to five years. The size of the benefit over modern intensive medical therapy is being re-examined in recent trials, so expect these decisions to be individualized. Always on top of best medical therapy. Antiplatelet, high-intensity statin, blood pressure control and smoking cessation, whatever else is decided. Thresholds and the workup are on Presentations . Positioning and exposure Supine with a shoulder roll, the neck extended and the head turned away from the operative side, with the head of the bed slightly up. Anesthesia is either general, with EEG, evoked potentials or a stump pressure used to judge brain perfusion during the clamp, or regional with the patient awake, where the patient's own neuro exam is the monitor. The incision runs along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. Through the platysma, the dissection follows the sternocleidomastoid and the internal jugular vein down to the carotid sheath. The facial vein crosses the bifurcation and is divided, which makes it the reliable landmark. The steps Expose the carotid sheath. Open it to find the common, internal and external carotid arteries and the vagus nerve coursing within, usually posterior in the groove between the carotid and the internal jugular vein. Dissect with as little handling of the bulb as possible: manipulating it can dislodge plaque and can trigger reflex bradycardia through the carotid sinus. Identify and protect the nerves. The hypoglossal nerve crosses the internal and external carotids superiorly, and the ansa cervicalis descends from it on the front of the carotid (the ansa can be divided to free the hypoglossal). Loop the common, external and internal carotids and the superior thyroid artery, with the internal carotid controlled beyond the end of the plaque. Heparinize, then clamp. Give systemic heparin and confirm the activated clotting time, then clamp in a deliberate order: usually the internal carotid first, so debris loosened by clamping cannot travel to the brain, then the common and external carotids. Decide on a shunt. A temporary shunt carries flow to the brain during the clamp. Some surgeons shunt routinely; others shunt selectively, based on a low stump pressure, a change on neuromonitoring, or a new deficit in an awake patient. Arteriotomy and endarterectomy. Open the artery longitudinally from the common carotid onto the internal carotid past the plaque, develop the endarterectomy plane with an elevator, and remove the plaque with a smooth, tapered distal endpoint so no flap is left to lift. Tack the endpoint if needed, clear the external carotid orifice by eversion, and pick off loose fragments under heparinized saline. Close, usually with a patch. A patch (bovine pericardium, Dacron, PTFE or vein) widens the lumen and lowers the rates of restenosis and early occlusion compared with routine primary closure. Eversion endarterectomy, in which the internal carotid is divided at the bulb, everted, cleared and reattached, is an alternative that needs no patch. Flush, then restore flow in sequence. Back-bleed the internal and external carotids and forward-flush the common before the last stitches. Then open the external and common carotids first, so any remaining debris or air goes to the face, and open the internal carotid last. Check, close and wake. Confirm flow with Doppler, duplex or angiography per the surgeon, give protamine if that is the surgeon's practice, secure hemostasis, and close the platysma and skin. The patient is woken in the room and a neuro exam is done before leaving it. Questions in the room Which nerves are at risk during a CEA, and what does each injury look like? The hypoglossal (CN XII): the tongue deviates toward the side of injury. The vagus (CN X) and its recurrent laryngeal branch: hoarseness from an ipsilateral vocal cord palsy. The superior laryngeal nerve: voice fatigue and loss of high pitch. The marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve (CN VII), usually from retraction at the angle of the jaw: a drooping corner of the mouth. The glossopharyngeal (CN IX), with very high exposures: dysphagia and aspiration. The hypoglossal and vagus are the classic answers, and hypoglossal, vagal and marginal mandibular injuries are the ones most often reported. Most are traction injuries that recover over weeks to months. What vein lies over the carotid bifurcation, and what do you do with it? The common facial vein, which drains into the internal jugular at about the level of the bifurcation. It is ligated and divided, which opens the view of the bifurcation. Why is the internal carotid usually clamped first and opened last? Because it is the one vessel that leads to the brain. Clamping it first means plaque fragments loosened while the other clamps go on cannot embolize upward. Opening it last means the first rush of blood, carrying any residual debris or air, is directed into the external carotid and the face. What is a stump pressure? The back pressure in the internal carotid once the common and external carotids are clamped. It reflects collateral flow across the circle of Willis. A low value is one reason to place a shunt; thresholds of roughly 40 to 50 mmHg are common, and they vary by surgeon. The heart rate drops as "},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Open AAA repair","u":"operations.html#open-aaa","x":"Open abdominal aortic aneurysm repair Replacement of the aneurysmal aorta with a prosthetic (Dacron or PTFE) tube or bifurcated graft, through either a transperitoneal or a retroperitoneal approach. It remains the durable repair, used when anatomy is unsuitable for a stent graft or in younger, fit patients. Indications An aneurysm at threshold. About 5.5 cm in men and 5.0 cm in women, rapid growth (more than 0.5 cm in six months or about 1 cm in a year), or symptoms. Details are on Presentations . Open rather than EVAR when the anatomy will not hold a stent graft (a short, wide, angulated or thrombus-lined neck, juxtarenal or pararenal extension, or poor iliac access), in younger fit patients with a long life expectancy who want to avoid lifelong surveillance and reintervention, and for infected aneurysms, some connective tissue disorders, and failed EVARs that need explant. Rupture when the anatomy is unsuitable for endovascular repair. When it is suitable, EVAR is now the preferred repair for rupture in SVS and ESVS guidance. Positioning and exposure Transperitoneal. Supine, with a midline laparotomy from xiphoid to pubis. The small bowel is packed to the right and the transverse colon up, the ligament of Treitz is divided to move the duodenum off the aorta, and the retroperitoneum is opened over the aneurysm. The left renal vein crossing in front of the aorta marks the top of the infrarenal neck. This approach reaches both iliacs and the right renal artery easily. Left retroperitoneal. The patient is rolled partly onto the right side, left side up, with a flank incision running from the edge of the rectus toward the tenth or eleventh intercostal space, and the peritoneal sac is swept medially. It suits a hostile abdomen, obesity, a horseshoe kidney, and juxtarenal or suprarenal clamping, but makes the right iliac and right renal arteries harder to reach. Anesthesia is general, with an arterial line and central access, often an epidural for post-operative pain, cell salvage, blood in the room, and active warming. The steps Expose the aorta. Reach the neck of the aneurysm above and the iliac vessels below, identifying the renal arteries, the left renal vein and the inferior mesenteric origin. The iliac veins lie tight behind the iliac arteries, so the iliacs are controlled without blind encircling. Heparinize and clamp. Give heparin, then clamp, often distally first so debris is not showered into the legs, then proximally: infrarenal where possible, suprarenal or supraceliac when the neck is short. The moment of clamping causes a sharp rise in afterload that stresses the heart. Open the sac and control back-bleeding. Open the aneurysm, clear the thrombus, and oversew the back-bleeding lumbar arteries and the inferior mesenteric artery from inside the sac. Sew the graft. Proximal anastomosis to healthy aorta first, then distal: a tube graft to the aortic bifurcation if the iliacs are spared, or a bifurcated graft to the iliacs or femorals if they are aneurysmal or occluded. Flush before completing each anastomosis. Reimplant vessels as needed. Reattach the inferior mesenteric artery or accessory renal arteries when colonic or renal perfusion depends on them, for example when the IMA back-bleeds poorly, the sigmoid looks dusky, or the internal iliacs are compromised. Preserve flow to at least one internal iliac where possible. Unclamp carefully. Release one limb at a time, gradually, while anesthesia loads volume, to blunt the drop in afterload and the washout of acid and potassium. Then look at the sigmoid colon and check the feet. Close the sac and retroperitoneum over the graft. Wrap the old aneurysm wall around the graft and close the retroperitoneum to separate the graft from the duodenum and bowel, then close the abdomen. After a rupture or massive resuscitation the abdomen may be left open temporarily. Questions in the room What happens hemodynamically at aortic cross-clamp and unclamp? Clamping sharply raises afterload and can strain the left ventricle, so it is a high-risk moment for cardiac ischemia; the higher the clamp, the bigger the effect. Unclamping causes a fall in afterload plus washout of acid and potassium from ischemic tissue, producing hypotension (\"declamping shock\"). That is why the surgeon and anesthesiologist coordinate a slow release with volume loading. Which vein crosses in front of the aortic neck, and if it must be divided, where? The left renal vein. If it has to be divided for exposure, it is divided close to the vena cava, medial to its gonadal, adrenal and lumbar tributaries, so the left kidney can still drain through them. Transperitoneal or retroperitoneal: when would you choose each? Transperitoneal gives the widest access to both iliacs, the right renal and the abdominal contents, and is the fastest route in a rupture. Left retroperitoneal is favored for a hostile abdomen (prior surgery, stomas, adhesions), obesity, a horseshoe kidney, an inflammatory aneurysm, and when a juxtarenal or suprarenal clamp is needed. It is often credited with less ileus and fewer pulmonary problems, at the cost of poor access to the right iliac. Post-operative day two after open AAA repair: bloody diarrhea. What happened and what do you do? Sigmoid colon ischemia from interruption of inferior mesenteric flow without adequate collateral supply (the marginal artery of Drummond and the internal iliacs). Resuscitate, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, and get an urgent bedside flexible sigmoidoscopy. Mucosal ischemia is managed supportively; transmural necrosis means a return to the operating room for resection. What is the leading cause of death after AAA repair, early and late? Myocardial infarction. These patients have diffuse atherosclerosis: the aorta may be fixed, but the coronaries remain the leading threat to life. This is why cardiac optimization, aspirin and a statin are central to vascular care. A ruptured aneurysm arrives. What are the first moves? Call the attendi"},{"p":"Open operations","t":"EVAR and endoleaks","u":"operations.html#evar","x":"EVAR and endoleaks High yield Endovascular aneurysm repair excludes the aneurysm from within by deploying a stent graft through the femoral arteries, so blood flows through the graft and the sac is meant to depressurize. It is less invasive than open repair, with lower early mortality, but needs lifelong surveillance imaging and has more late reinterventions. The devices and techniques are on Endovascular and Stent grafts ; what every trainee must know cold are the endoleaks. Indications An aneurysm at the repair threshold with suitable anatomy, and rupture with suitable anatomy. The anatomy is the whole question: an adequate proximal neck for a seal (commonly at least 10 to 15 mm of healthy, fairly parallel aorta below the lowest renal artery, a diameter within the device range, angulation under about 60 degrees, and limited thrombus or calcium), suitable iliac landing zones, and iliofemoral arteries large and straight enough to deliver the device, roughly 6 to 7 mm or more depending on the system. Fenestrated and branched grafts extend this to necks that involve the renal and visceral arteries. Positioning and access Supine in a hybrid room or on a fluoroscopy table, with both groins prepped and the abdomen prepped in case of conversion. Anesthesia may be general, regional, or local with sedation. Both common femoral arteries are accessed percutaneously under ultrasound, with suture-mediated closure devices placed before the large sheaths go in (\"preclose\"), or by open femoral cutdown when the arteries are calcified or small. The steps Access, preclose and heparinize. Ultrasound-guided puncture of both common femoral arteries, closure sutures deployed, then systemic heparin to a target ACT. Wires and a marker angiogram. Exchange for stiff wires on the main-body side, and run an aortogram with a marker pigtail to locate the lowest renal artery and the internal iliac origins. Deploy the main body. Position the fabric just below the lowest renal artery (suprarenal bare stents, if the device has them, sit above), with the contralateral gate oriented for cannulation. Cannulate the gate. From the other groin, wire into the contralateral gate and confirm the position by spinning a pigtail freely inside the main body. Deploy the iliac limbs. Land each limb in the common iliac above the internal iliac origin. If the common iliac is aneurysmal, the options are an iliac branch device or coiling the internal iliac and extending into the external iliac. Balloon the seal zones and junctions. A compliant balloon molds the graft into the proximal neck, the overlaps and the distal seal zones. Completion angiogram. Look for type I and III endoleaks, which are fixed now, and confirm the renal and internal iliac arteries are open. Close and check the legs. Remove the sheaths, tie down the preclose sutures or repair the arteriotomies, and confirm distal pulses or signals. Endoleaks, the defining complication An endoleak is persistent blood flow into the aneurysm sac after stent-graft placement, which keeps the sac pressurized and at risk. The five types are a perennial question. Endoleak classification Type Source Management I Inadequate seal at the proximal (Ia) or distal (Ib) graft attachment Treat promptly (high pressure, rupture risk): extension cuff, balloon molding, or other proximal or distal fixation II Retrograde backfilling from a branch (lumbar artery or the IMA). The most common type Usually observe if the sac is stable; treat, often by embolization, if the sac grows (SVS suggests at about 5 mm or more) III Mechanical failure: separation of graft components (IIIa) or a defect in the fabric (IIIb) Treat promptly (high pressure, rupture risk): relining with new components IV Porosity of the graft fabric, seen at completion while fully anticoagulated Self-limited; observe. Rare with modern grafts V \"Endotension\": sac growth with no leak seen on imaging A diagnosis of exclusion; surveillance, and reintervention if the sac keeps enlarging Questions in the room Which endoleak is the most common, and which types must you treat? Type II is the most common and is usually watched if the sac is stable. Types I and III are high-pressure leaks that need prompt treatment, because they re-pressurize the sac and carry a real rupture risk. What features make a patient a poor EVAR candidate? A short, wide, conical or sharply angulated neck, heavy thrombus or calcium in the neck, and small, calcified or tortuous iliac and femoral arteries that will not take the delivery sheath. Some of these can be overcome with fenestrated or branched devices, adjuncts or an iliac conduit; otherwise open repair is the answer. How do an endoleak, endotension and post-implantation syndrome differ? An endoleak is visible blood flow in the sac. Endotension (type V) is a pressurized, enlarging sac without a demonstrable leak. Post-implantation syndrome is a benign, self-limited fever, leukocytosis and raised CRP in the first days after EVAR, without infection. Lifelong CT or duplex surveillance is mandatory because leaks and sac growth can appear years later. Consent risks Endoleak and the need for further procedures, which are more frequent than after open repair over the long term. Lifelong surveillance imaging, with its contrast and radiation. Access-site problems: hematoma, pseudoaneurysm, dissection, thrombosis, and retroperitoneal bleeding. Graft limb occlusion, graft migration and late rupture. Kidney injury from contrast or renal coverage; buttock claudication or colon ischemia if an internal iliac is covered. Conversion to open repair, MI and death (early mortality is lower than for open repair, often around 1 percent or less electively). After the case Most elective EVAR patients go to the floor or a step-down bed and home on post-operative day one; ruptures and complex repairs go to the CTV ICU. Groins Checked frequently in the first hours per protocol, with bed rest for the period set by the closure method. Look for hemat"},{"p":"Open operations","t":"TEVAR and complex aortic","u":"operations.html#aortic-stent","x":"TEVAR and complex aortic repair Thoracic endovascular aortic repair places a stent graft in the descending thoracic aorta. When the aneurysm involves the visceral or renal branches, fenestrated and branched grafts (FEVAR and BEVAR) preserve flow to those vessels; the device side of these is covered in Endovascular . Indications Descending thoracic aneurysm above the repair threshold, around 5.5 to 6 cm depending on the guideline and on whether repair would be endovascular or open, or with rapid growth or symptoms. Complicated type B dissection: rupture, malperfusion of the gut, kidneys or legs, refractory pain or hypertension, or rapid expansion. Selected uncomplicated high-risk dissections are increasingly treated as well. See Presentations . Blunt traumatic aortic injury, classically at the isthmus just distal to the left subclavian, for higher-grade injuries. Penetrating aortic ulcer and intramural hematoma when complicated or high-risk. Positioning and access Supine, usually under general anesthesia, in a hybrid room. The device goes in through a common femoral artery with a large-bore sheath, percutaneously with preclose or by cutdown, and an iliac conduit is sewn on when the iliacs are too small. A second access (the other femoral, or the left arm) carries a marker pigtail. The proximal landing site is described by aortic arch zones 0 to 4; landing in zone 2 covers the left subclavian origin. Each end needs a seal zone of healthy aorta, commonly at least 2 cm long. The steps Access and heparinize. Large-bore femoral access, a marker pigtail from the other side or the arm, and systemic heparin. Wire and image. A stiff wire into the ascending aorta, and an arch angiogram in a left anterior oblique view to open out the arch and mark the target branch origins. Deploy. Anesthesia lowers the blood pressure briefly while the graft is deployed precisely at the landing zone, with modest oversizing for aneurysms and little or none for dissection. Mold and check. Balloon the seal zones if appropriate (often avoided in dissection), then run a completion angiogram for type I endoleak and branch patency. Withdraw and close carefully. The iliac arteries can rupture as the large sheath comes out, so it is removed under watch and the access is closed or repaired. If the left subclavian will be covered in an elective case, it is commonly revascularized first or at the same sitting (carotid-subclavian bypass or transposition), and always when the left internal mammary feeds a coronary bypass or the left vertebral artery is dominant. Questions in the room Why does long thoracic coverage risk paraplegia? The anterior spinal artery is reinforced along its length by segmental feeders. The largest, the artery of Adamkiewicz, usually arises from a left-sided intercostal or lumbar artery between about T8 and L1. The cord also depends on a collateral network fed by the left subclavian (through the vertebral), the intercostals and lumbars, and the internal iliacs. Covering a long segment, covering the left subclavian, or prior aortic repair removes more of that network. What is spinal cord perfusion pressure, and how do you raise it? Mean arterial pressure minus the cerebrospinal fluid pressure (or the central venous pressure if that is higher). You raise it by raising the MAP and lowering the CSF pressure with a lumbar drain, and you support oxygen delivery with an adequate hemoglobin and oxygenation. What can go wrong if the left subclavian artery is covered? Arm ischemia, posterior circulation stroke (vertebral flow), spinal cord ischemia, and myocardial ischemia if a left internal mammary artery feeds a coronary bypass graft. Where does blunt aortic injury classically occur, and why there? At the isthmus, just distal to the left subclavian artery, where the relatively mobile arch meets the descending aorta tethered by the ligamentum arteriosum. Sudden deceleration concentrates shear at that junction. Consent risks Spinal cord ischemia and paraplegia, from a small percentage up to higher rates with extensive coverage. Stroke, from arch manipulation or left subclavian coverage. Access injury, including iliac rupture; endoleak, migration and reintervention. Retrograde type A dissection, uncommon but often fatal, more of a concern in dissection cases. Arm ischemia, kidney injury, post-implantation syndrome, and death. Lumbar drain complications if one is placed: headache, bleeding or epidural hematoma, infection, and rarely intracranial hemorrhage from overdrainage. After the case TEVAR and complex aortic patients go to the CTV ICU, where the focus is the spinal cord, the brain and the access. Leg strength Serial motor exams of both legs, often hourly at first. Weakness can appear in the first hours or be delayed for days, often after a hypotensive episode. Mean arterial pressure Kept at or above the target the team sets, often 80 to 90 mmHg or higher, with hypotension avoided at all costs. The lumbar drain Managed to a CSF pressure target with a cap on hourly drainage, typically kept for a day or more, then clamped and removed if the legs stay strong, with coagulation checked before removal and neuro checks afterward. Details are on Spinal drains . Stroke and the left arm Neuro checks, and the left arm pulse and pressure if the subclavian was covered. Access and kidneys Groin or conduit site, distal pulses, hemoglobin and creatinine. After FEVAR or BEVAR, add urine output and signs of visceral ischemia, since a branch stent can occlude. Red flag: spinal cord ischemia Covering a long segment of the thoracic aorta can interrupt the intercostal arteries that feed the spinal cord, risking paraplegia. The protective measures are a lumbar cerebrospinal fluid drain to lower the pressure around the cord and deliberate maintenance of a higher mean arterial pressure to keep spinal perfusion up. New leg weakness is an emergency: raise the MAP, drain CSF (or place a drain if none is in), correct anemia and hypoxia, and "},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Aortobifemoral and extra-anatomic","u":"operations.html#aortoiliac","x":"Aortobifemoral and extra-anatomic bypass The open operations for aortoiliac occlusive disease. Aortobifemoral bypass is the durable answer; the extra-anatomic routes avoid the abdomen in patients who cannot have it. Many aortoiliac lesions are now treated endovascularly first, so the open operation is reserved for extensive disease, failed stents, or anatomy that will not take one. Indications Extensive aortoiliac occlusive disease (the Leriche picture) causing CLTI or lifestyle-limiting claudication despite medical and exercise therapy, when endovascular reconstruction is unsuitable or has failed: aortic occlusion up to the renal arteries, diffuse bilateral iliac disease, or coexisting aneurysm. Extra-anatomic bypass instead when the abdomen is hostile, the patient is too frail for an aortic operation, an infected aortic graft has to be removed, or the disease is one-sided (femoral-femoral). Positioning and exposure Supine, with the abdomen and both groins and legs prepped. A midline laparotomy (or left retroperitoneal approach) exposes the infrarenal aorta as for an aneurysm, and vertical groin incisions expose the common femoral, superficial femoral and profunda arteries on both sides. The graft is usually a bifurcated Dacron graft sized to the femorals, commonly 14 by 7 or 16 by 8 mm. The steps: aortobifemoral bypass Open the groins first. Exposing both femoral bifurcations before the abdomen shortens the aortic clamp time and confirms the outflow. Expose the aorta. As for an aneurysm, up to the left renal vein, staying on the right side of the aorta where possible to spare the autonomic nerves on the left. Tunnel to the groins. Make tunnels from the aorta to each groin along the course of the iliac arteries, passing behind the ureters so the graft limbs cannot compress them. Heparinize, clamp and sew the proximal anastomosis. High on the infrarenal aorta, just below the renals, so disease progression does not undermine it. End-to-end suits an occluded or aneurysmal aorta; end-to-side preserves antegrade flow to the inferior mesenteric and internal iliac arteries when the external iliacs are occluded. Pass the limbs and sew the femoral anastomoses. To the common femoral, often extended onto the profunda (profundaplasty) when the superficial femoral is occluded. Flush, release one limb at a time, and check the feet. Then close the retroperitoneum over the graft and close the abdomen and groins. Extra-anatomic options Axillobifemoral bypass A ringed (externally supported) prosthetic graft from the first part of the axillary artery, through an infraclavicular incision, tunneled under the skin along the side of the chest and abdomen to the femoral artery, with a femoral-femoral limb to the other side. The axillary anastomosis is placed medially with some slack so arm abduction cannot tear it. The donor arm is the one with the higher pressure and no subclavian stenosis. Patency is lower and more variable than aortobifemoral bypass. Femoral-femoral crossover A prosthetic graft tunneled under the skin above the pubis, from a healthy femoral artery to the opposite, ischemic one, for a unilateral iliac occlusion. The donor iliac must be free of significant disease (or stented first), otherwise both legs suffer. Femoral endarterectomy and profundaplasty Open removal of common femoral plaque, extended onto the profunda, closed with a patch. It remains the standard treatment for common femoral disease and is often combined with iliac stenting above (a hybrid procedure) or a bypass below. Questions in the room What is Leriche syndrome? Aortoiliac occlusive disease producing the triad of buttock, hip and thigh claudication, erectile dysfunction, and absent or diminished femoral pulses. Why are the graft limbs tunneled behind the ureters? A limb passed in front of a ureter can compress it against the iliac artery behind, causing obstruction and hydronephrosis. Tunneling along the native iliac course, posterior to the ureter, avoids this. End-to-end or end-to-side proximal anastomosis: when would you choose each? End-to-end avoids competitive flow, suits a coexisting aneurysm or an aortic occlusion near the renals, and lies flatter for coverage by retroperitoneum. End-to-side keeps antegrade flow down the native aorta to the inferior mesenteric and internal iliac arteries, which matters when the external iliacs are occluded and those vessels would otherwise lose their supply. How durable is aortobifemoral bypass compared with the extra-anatomic routes? Aortobifemoral bypass has excellent long-term patency, often quoted around 85 to 90 percent at five years. Femoral-femoral and axillobifemoral grafts are longer, run in subcutaneous tunnels and have lower inflow, and their patency is lower and more variable; they are chosen for the patient, not the patency. Consent risks For aortobifemoral bypass, the open aortic list: death, MI, bleeding, kidney injury, colon ischemia, ileus, ureteral or bowel injury, sexual dysfunction from injury to the autonomic plexus, and incisional hernia. Groin wound complications: infection, lymph leak or lymphocele, and seroma. Groin infection is the main route to graft infection. Graft limb thrombosis, distal embolization, and late femoral anastomotic pseudoaneurysm. For extra-anatomic grafts: lower patency, graft infection, and for axillofemoral grafts, arm ischemia, brachial plexus injury and anastomotic disruption. After the case Aortobifemoral patients follow the open AAA pattern in the CTV ICU: urine output, the abdomen, the colon, ileus and potassium. Extra-anatomic patients usually go to the floor. Graft and feet Femoral pulses, distal signals and the look of the feet at each check. For axillofemoral and femoral-femoral grafts, feel the graft pulse along its subcutaneous course. Groins Daily inspection for hematoma, clear lymphatic drainage, redness or separation, typically declaring themselves over the first one to two weeks. A draining groin over a prosthetic graft"},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Lower extremity bypass","u":"operations.html#bypass","x":"Lower extremity bypass Rerouting blood around an occluded segment with a graft, the embodiment of inflow, outflow and conduit. You choose a healthy inflow source above the disease, a target (outflow) vessel with good runoff below it, and a conduit to bridge them. Common configurations are femoropopliteal (above or below the knee) and femoro-tibial or femoro-pedal (distal) bypass for more extensive disease. Indications CLTI (rest pain or tissue loss) with a usable target and conduit, chosen using the Global Vascular Guidelines framework of patient risk, limb threat (WIfI) and anatomic pattern (GLASS). In BEST-CLI (2022), patients with an adequate single-segment great saphenous vein did better with bypass than with endovascular treatment; in BASIL-2 (2023), patients needing infrapopliteal revascularization did better with an endovascular-first strategy. The trials enrolled different patients, so the decision is individualized. See CLTI . Disabling claudication that fails supervised exercise and medical therapy, selectively, with an anatomy not suited to endovascular treatment. See Claudication . Popliteal aneurysm and trauma, where bypass is combined with exclusion or repair. Pearl: conduit choice The great saphenous vein is the best conduit and gives the best long-term patency, especially below the knee. Prosthetic grafts (PTFE or Dacron) work acceptably for above-knee femoropopliteal bypass but perform poorly for distal targets. This is why the team protects the saphenous vein and maps it before harvesting. The full ranking is in Conduits . Positioning and exposure Supine, with the leg prepped circumferentially from the abdomen to the toes (the foot in a sterile bag), the knee slightly flexed and the hip externally rotated on a bump. The vein has been mapped with duplex and marked on the skin beforehand. Common femoral (inflow) A vertical groin incision over the femoral pulse, exposing the common femoral, superficial femoral and profunda. Lymphatics are ligated rather than cut to reduce lymph leaks. Above-knee popliteal A medial distal thigh incision along the front of the sartorius, entering the popliteal space above the knee. Below-knee popliteal A medial incision about a centimeter behind the tibia, sparing the saphenous vein and nerve, with the gastrocnemius retracted backward. Taking the soleus off the tibia extends the exposure to the tibioperoneal trunk. Tibial and pedal targets Posterior tibial and peroneal from the medial side (the peroneal also laterally by resecting a segment of fibula), anterior tibial through an anterolateral incision, and the dorsalis pedis on the dorsum of the foot. The steps Expose the inflow and the target. Usually the common femoral above and the popliteal, tibial or pedal target below. The target is often exposed first to confirm it is soft and suitable before committing the vein. Prepare the conduit. Harvest the vein for a reversed or non-reversed graft, or prepare it in its bed for an in situ graft, ligating side branches and handling it gently. Mark it so it cannot twist in the tunnel. Tunnel. An anatomic tunnel (deep, alongside the native artery) or a subcutaneous one. An in situ vein stays in its own bed. Heparinize and clamp. Systemic heparin, then control of the inflow and target vessels with clamps, bulldogs or vessel loops. Proximal anastomosis. End-to-side to the inflow artery, typically 5-0 or 6-0 polypropylene at the femoral level. Lyse the valves if needed. For in situ and non-reversed grafts, a valvulotome cuts the valve leaflets once arterial pressure distends the vein. Distal anastomosis. End-to-side to the target, with a spatulated end of vein and fine suture (6-0 or 7-0 polypropylene for tibial and pedal vessels). Restore flow and check the result. Graft pulse, Doppler signals at the target and in the foot, and a completion angiogram or duplex to find retained valve cusps, unligated branches (fistulas in an in situ graft), kinks or anastomotic narrowing. Then close in layers. Questions in the room Where do you find the great saphenous vein at the ankle, and which nerve runs with it? Just anterior to the medial malleolus, a reliable cutdown landmark. The saphenous nerve runs with it below the knee, so harvest or exposure there can leave numbness along the medial leg and foot. What makes a good distal target? A vessel soft enough to sew, with continuous runoff into the foot, ideally in-line flow to the pedal arch. Where there is a choice, a target that feeds the angiosome of the wound is preferred, and the best-quality target nearest the inflow allows the shortest conduit. Why does a graft fail early, in the middle period, and late? Within 30 days, failure is usually technical: a kink, a twist, a retained valve, a poor anastomosis, inadequate runoff or inflow. From about 30 days to two years, intimal hyperplasia at the anastomoses or within a vein graft is the main cause. After about two years, progression of atherosclerosis above or below the graft dominates. Why do vein grafts get duplex surveillance? To find a failing graft (a developing stenosis) before it becomes a failed, clotted graft, which is much harder to salvage. A peak systolic velocity over about 300 cm/s or a velocity ratio over about 3.5 at a stenosis, or low velocities throughout the graft, are commonly used triggers for reintervention; criteria vary by lab. Consent risks Graft failure, early or late, and the need for further procedures, which is common over the life of a bypass. Wound complications, which are frequent: hematoma, lymph leak or lymphocele, dehiscence and infection, at the groin and along the vein harvest incision. Graft infection, a particular concern with prosthetic grafts. Leg swelling, which is expected after revascularization. Saphenous nerve numbness. Compartment syndrome after prolonged ischemia, amputation despite bypass, MI and death. After the case Most bypass patients go to the floor; some go to a step-down unit or the CTV ICU depending on thei"},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Embolectomy","u":"operations.html#embolectomy","x":"Embolectomy and thrombectomy Emergency The classic operation for embolic acute limb ischemia. Through a cutdown on the common femoral artery (or the brachial artery for the arm), a Fogarty balloon catheter is passed beyond the clot, the balloon is inflated, and the catheter is withdrawn to drag the embolus out, repeated until inflow and backflow are brisk. A completion angiogram confirms the result. Indications Acute limb ischemia from an embolus, classically a Rutherford IIb limb that needs revascularization within hours, or a IIa limb when thrombolysis is unsuitable. The grading is on Presentations and Quick reference . Thrombosed bypass grafts and saddle emboli at the aortic bifurcation (through both groins). In situ thrombosis on atherosclerotic disease responds poorly to balloon thrombectomy alone and usually needs a bypass, an endovascular fix of the underlying lesion, or catheter-directed lysis (see Thrombolysis ). Positioning and exposure Supine in a hybrid room or with a C-arm, the whole leg prepped (both groins for a saddle embolus), under local with sedation or general anesthesia. The common femoral bifurcation is exposed through a vertical groin incision. A below-knee popliteal exposure allows selective passage into each tibial vessel. For the arm, the brachial artery is exposed at the antecubital fossa under the bicipital aponeurosis. The steps Heparinize. Heparin is usually started at diagnosis (commonly an 80 U/kg bolus, then an infusion per protocol) and topped up in the room. Expose and control. Common, superficial femoral and profunda arteries looped. Arteriotomy. Transverse in a soft common femoral artery (easy to close primarily), or longitudinal if the artery is diseased and a patch or bypass may be needed. Pass the Fogarty, inflow first. Clear the inflow proximally until there is brisk pulsatile flow, then pass the catheter distally down the superficial femoral and the profunda. Inflate gently while withdrawing. Adjust the inflation by feel as the catheter comes back, and repeat until two passes return no clot and back-bleeding is brisk. Back-bleeding alone does not prove the runoff is clear, because collaterals can supply it. Completion angiogram. Residual distal clot is chased with selective tibial embolectomy from below the knee or with intra-arterial thrombolytic; an underlying stenosis is treated with a stent or bypass. Close and assess the compartments. Instill heparinized saline distally, close the arteriotomy primarily or with a patch, and check the calf. A long ischemic time lowers the threshold for fasciotomy at the same sitting. Send the clot. To pathology (and culture if infection is possible), which occasionally reveals a myxoma or septic embolus. Questions in the room What instrument is used for a surgical embolectomy, and who invented it? The Fogarty balloon catheter, developed by Thomas Fogarty. It transformed embolectomy from a major arteriotomy at every site into a controlled extraction through a small cutdown. Typical sizes by vessel are in Instruments . Embolus or thrombosis: how do you tell them apart at the bedside? Embolus: sudden onset, a source such as atrial fibrillation, no prior claudication, and normal pulses in the other leg. Thrombosis: a history of claudication, diminished pulses on the other side, and a less abrupt, often less severe picture because collaterals have developed. The distinction matters because emboli respond well to balloon embolectomy while thrombosis usually needs the underlying lesion fixed. Where do emboli lodge, and where do they come from? At branch points where the lumen narrows: in the leg most often the common femoral bifurcation, then the popliteal, the iliac and the aortic bifurcation; in the arm, the brachial bifurcation. Most arise in the heart (atrial fibrillation, left ventricular thrombus after MI, valve disease); a minority come from proximal aneurysms or plaque. Consent risks Incomplete clot removal or rethrombosis, needing further procedures such as bypass, lysis or fasciotomy. Amputation if the limb cannot be saved. Reperfusion injury: hyperkalemia, acidosis, myoglobinuria and kidney injury, and compartment syndrome. Catheter injury to the artery: dissection, perforation or later stenosis. Bleeding on anticoagulation, wound complications, and death, which is substantial in this population because of the underlying heart disease. After the case The limb and the compartments Hourly pulse or Doppler, motor and sensory checks, and the calf (firmness, pain on passive stretch) for at least the first 24 to 48 hours. Rising pain after reperfusion is compartment syndrome until proven otherwise. Reperfusion labs Potassium, CK, lactate, creatinine and urine color. Myoglobinuria is treated with fluids to a high urine output per protocol. Anticoagulation Heparin infusion restarted when the surgeon allows, usually without a bolus after surgery, then long-term anticoagulation for a cardiac source. See Heparin . The source ECG and telemetry for atrial fibrillation, and an echocardiogram. Recurrent embolism to the same or another limb is a real risk until the patient is anticoagulated. The first call about an acutely cold leg is on On call: the cold leg , and the thrombectomy devices are on Devices ."},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Fasciotomy","u":"operations.html#fasciotomy","x":"Fasciotomy Emergency Releasing the fascia to relieve compartment syndrome, the limb-threatening rise in pressure within a closed muscle compartment, classically after reperfusion of an ischemic leg, after trauma, or after a fracture. The diagnosis is clinical, with pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch as the earliest and most reliable signs. 4 Compartments in the lower leg 2 Incisions to release all four > 30 mmHg Compartment pressure commonly used as a threshold < 30 mmHg Delta pressure (diastolic minus compartment) that supports release Indications Clinical compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, tense compartments, then paresthesia and weakness. Measured pressures support the diagnosis when the exam is unreliable (sedated, intubated or neuropathic patients). Thresholds vary; the delta pressure is more reliable than an absolute value. Prophylactically at revascularization after prolonged ischemia (commonly more than about four to six hours), in a IIb limb, or with combined arterial and venous injury. Positioning and exposure Supine with the leg prepped circumferentially. The standard two-incision technique uses long incisions, because skin can itself be constricting. The lateral incision lies midway between the tibial crest and the fibula, over the septum between the anterior and lateral compartments. The medial incision lies about 2 cm behind the medial edge of the tibia. Thigh, foot and forearm fasciotomies use their own incisions. The steps Mark both incisions. Lateral, between the tibial crest and the fibula; medial, about 2 cm posterior to the medial border of the tibia. Lateral incision: anterior and lateral compartments. Find the intermuscular septum (perforating vessels mark it), then open the anterior compartment fascia in front of it and the lateral compartment fascia behind it, the full length of the leg. Protect the superficial peroneal nerve, which pierces the fascia in the lower third. Medial incision: superficial and deep posterior compartments. Protect the great saphenous vein and saphenous nerve, open the superficial posterior compartment, then take the soleus off the back of the tibia to enter and release the deep posterior compartment. Inspect the muscle. Judge viability by color, consistency, contractility and capacity to bleed, and debride what is clearly dead. Leave the skin open. Hemostasis (the patient is often anticoagulated), then a moist dressing or negative-pressure dressing. Questions in the room Name the four compartments of the lower leg and the structures most at risk. The anterior (deep peroneal nerve and anterior tibial artery; injury causes foot drop and numbness of the first web space), the lateral (superficial peroneal nerve; weak eversion and numbness over the dorsum of the foot), the superficial posterior (gastrocnemius and soleus), and the deep posterior (posterior tibial and peroneal vessels, tibial nerve). A standard leg fasciotomy uses two incisions to release all four. The anterior compartment is the one most often involved. Which compartment is most often missed, and why? The deep posterior, because it lies under the soleus and is only entered by detaching the soleus from the tibia. An incomplete release there leaves the tibial nerve and posterior tibial vessels at risk. Why does compartment syndrome follow revascularization? Ischemia damages the capillary endothelium. When flow returns, fluid leaks into the muscle (reperfusion edema) inside an unyielding fascial envelope, the pressure rises above capillary perfusion pressure, and the muscle becomes ischemic again despite a now-patent artery. Consent risks Return trips to the operating room for debridement and closure, and possibly a skin graft. Bleeding and infection of the open wounds. Nerve injury (superficial peroneal, saphenous) and residual weakness or foot drop. Chronic swelling, scarring, and amputation if muscle proves nonviable. Kidney injury from myoglobin. After the case Wound care Negative-pressure or moist dressings changed per the team, watching for bleeding in an anticoagulated patient. Muscle viability A second look within one to three days if viability was in doubt, with further debridement as needed. Rhabdomyolysis CK, potassium, creatinine and urine color, with fluids to a high urine output per protocol. Closure Delayed primary closure once swelling settles, often within about a week, sometimes helped by gradual approximation (a vessel-loop \"shoelace\"). Otherwise a split-thickness skin graft. Function Physical therapy and an ankle-foot orthosis for foot drop, with splinting to prevent an equinus contracture. Red flag Do not wait for pulselessness, which is a very late finding. If compartment syndrome is suspected after a revascularization or trauma, the threshold to perform a four-compartment fasciotomy is deliberately low, because the cost of a delayed release is dead muscle and a lost limb. When in doubt, measure the pressures or simply decompress."},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Amputations","u":"operations.html#amputation","x":"Amputations When tissue is nonviable or infection is uncontrollable, amputation is reconstructive, not a failure; it removes dead or septic tissue to save the patient and restore function. The level is chosen to balance healing potential (more proximal heals more reliably) against rehabilitation potential (more distal preserves function). Indications Non-salvageable ischemia or infection, intractable rest pain in a limb that cannot be reconstructed, extensive necrosis, or life-threatening sepsis from a necrotic or infected foot. The art is choosing the most distal level that will heal while preserving function. Amputation levels, distal to proximal Level Notes Toe / ray For isolated digital gangrene or infection with good perfusion proximally; a ray amputation takes the toe with part of its metatarsal Transmetatarsal (TMA) Across the forefoot; preserves a functional weight-bearing foot Syme Ankle disarticulation preserving the heel pad; needs good heel perfusion and is less common in vascular patients Below-knee (BKA) Preferred when it will heal, because preserving the knee dramatically improves the odds and lowers the energy cost of walking with a prosthesis Through-knee Knee disarticulation; a long, end-bearing limb with good sitting balance, mainly for patients who will not walk Above-knee (AKA) Heals most reliably but has the worst functional outcome; chosen when a BKA will not heal, with a fixed knee contracture, or when the patient is non-ambulatory Guillotine (open) A quick, open first stage, often at the ankle, for source control of wet gangrene or ascending infection in a septic patient; the formal closed amputation follows once sepsis is controlled Positioning and exposure Supine, with the leg on a bump; a tourniquet is used selectively. Level selection draws on the exam (warm skin, bleeding at the planned edge, a palpable popliteal pulse favors a BKA), toe pressures or transcutaneous oxygen (values above roughly 30 to 40 mmHg favor healing), whether the limb can be revascularized first, and whether the patient will walk. The steps: below-knee amputation Mark the flaps. Tibial transection about a hand's breadth (roughly 10 to 15 cm) below the tibial tuberosity, a short anterior incision, and a long posterior myocutaneous flap of calf muscle that will reach the front without tension. Divide the anterior and lateral compartments. Ligate the anterior tibial vessels. Divide the bones. Saw the tibia and bevel its anterior edge so it does not press through the flap; divide the fibula 1 to 2 cm shorter. Complete the posterior flap. Ligate the posterior tibial and peroneal vessels. Pull each nerve down gently, cut it sharply and let it retract into the soft tissue to limit painful neuromas. Shape and close. Trim bulky soleus, achieve hemostasis, bring the posterior flap forward, and close fascia and skin without tension. Protect the stump. A rigid dressing or knee immobilizer keeps the knee straight and shields the stump from falls. An above-knee amputation uses anterior and posterior flaps, divides the femur in the distal or mid thigh, and anchors the adductors to the bone end (myodesis) so the femur does not drift outward. Questions in the room Why try to preserve the knee with a below-knee amputation, and when is a guillotine amputation used? Keeping the knee joint sharply lowers the energy cost of prosthetic walking and improves the chance a patient walks again, so a BKA is favored whenever it will heal. A guillotine (open) amputation is a quick, open, two-stage approach used to control active infection or wet gangrene in a septic patient; the wound is left open to clear sepsis, then a formal closed amputation is done once the patient is stable and the infection is controlled. Why bevel the tibia and cut the fibula shorter? The sharp anterior edge of the tibia would press through the thin anterior skin and later against the socket, so it is beveled. A fibula left as long as the tibia becomes a painful lateral prominence; cutting it shorter gives a tapered, prosthesis-friendly limb. How is the level chosen? By balancing healing against function: perfusion at the level (exam, toe pressures, transcutaneous oxygen, whether revascularization is possible), the extent of infection and necrosis, knee contracture, and the patient's realistic chance of walking. A bedbound patient with a flexion contracture may be better served by a through-knee or above-knee amputation that heals first time. Consent risks Failure to heal and revision to a higher level. Wound infection, hematoma and dehiscence. Phantom sensation (almost universal) and phantom pain (common), and residual-limb pain from neuromas. Knee or hip flexion contracture, and falls onto the stump. Loss of the other leg over time, DVT, MI and death; early mortality after major amputation is substantial and reflects how sick these patients are. After the case Stump care Keep the knee straight after a BKA (immobilizer, no pillow under the knee) and avoid prolonged hip flexion after an AKA. The first dressing change and staple timing follow the surgeon; staples often stay longer than elsewhere because ischemic skin heals slowly. Watch for dusky edges, eschar, drainage or pain out of proportion. Falls New amputees often forget the leg is gone and stand up at night, landing on the fresh stump. Fall precautions from day one. Phantom and stump pain Multimodal analgesia, a perineural catheter if one was placed, and neuropathic agents as the team prefers; involve the pain service early when it is severe. Infection and glucose Antibiotic duration depends on whether infected tissue or bone remains (only a short course if it was all removed), per ID guidance. Tight glucose control and nutrition support healing. Rehab Physical and occupational therapy from day one, a rehabilitation medicine consult, and disposition planning for inpatient rehab or a skilled facility. A shrinker comes once the incision has healed, and prosthetic fitting follows when the"},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Dialysis access","u":"operations.html#access","x":"Hemodialysis access Creating durable access for hemodialysis, almost always elective, and a large share of a vascular service's elective volume. The traditional principle is autogenous (fistula) before prosthetic (graft) before catheter, because a native arteriovenous fistula lasts longest and has the lowest infection rate. The 2019 KDOQI update softened \"fistula first\" into an individualized plan (the right access for the right patient at the right time), but the order below is still the default. Indications and planning Advanced chronic kidney disease approaching dialysis, ideally with a fistula placed months ahead so it can mature, or a patient already dialyzing through a catheter. Preoperative vein mapping (a vein of about 2.5 to 3 mm or more and an artery of about 2 mm or more are common thresholds), an Allen test or palmar arch assessment, and a history of prior central lines or pacemaker leads that may have narrowed the central veins. Distal before proximal, and usually the non-dominant arm first, to preserve future sites. Vein preservation (no IVs, blood draws or PICC lines in the planned arm) starts long before surgery. Access in order of preference Option What it is Note 1. Radiocephalic AVF Radial artery to cephalic vein at the wrist (the Brescia-Cimino fistula) Most distal, preferred first; preserves proximal sites 2. Brachiocephalic AVF Brachial artery to cephalic vein at the elbow High flow, reliable maturation 3. Brachiobasilic AVF Brachial artery to a transposed basilic vein Needs a transposition, in one or two stages; deeper vein brought superficial 4. AV graft Prosthetic bridge (PTFE) between artery and vein When veins are inadequate; usable sooner but more thrombosis and infection 5. Tunneled catheter Central venous catheter Last resort; highest infection and central-stenosis risk Positioning and exposure Supine with the arm out on an arm board. A regional (brachial plexus) block is common: it dilates the veins, and in a randomized trial it gave better early patency than local anesthesia. The incision sits over the mapped vein and artery: at the wrist for a radiocephalic fistula, at the antecubital fossa for a brachial-based one. The steps: arteriovenous fistula Mobilize the vein. Free a length of cephalic vein, ligate its side branches, divide it distally, and gently distend it with heparinized saline to check for a downstream narrowing. Expose the artery. Radial at the wrist or brachial at the elbow, controlled with vessel loops or bulldogs. Heparinize and open the artery. Often a modest heparin dose per the surgeon, and a short arteriotomy (kept small on the brachial artery to limit steal). Sew the anastomosis. End of vein to side of artery with fine polypropylene (6-0 or 7-0). Check the result. A continuous thrill over the anastomosis is the goal; a pulse without a thrill suggests outflow obstruction. Check the hand: color, capillary refill, and a radial or palmar signal. A brachiobasilic fistula adds a transposition, lifting the deep basilic vein to a superficial position where it can be needled, with care for the medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve. An AV graft (forearm loop or upper-arm straight graft, usually 6 mm PTFE) is tunneled between artery and vein; standard grafts are used after two to three weeks and early-cannulation grafts within days. Questions in the room How do you examine a fistula? A healthy fistula has a soft, continuous thrill and a low-pitched continuous bruit. A strong, water-hammer pulse with little thrill suggests outflow stenosis. Raising the arm should make the fistula collapse; if it stays distended, suspect an outflow stenosis. Occluding the fistula a few centimeters downstream should make the pulse above stronger (augmentation); a weak augmentation suggests an inflow problem. What are the options for steal syndrome? Banding or narrowing the access to reduce flow, DRIL (distal revascularization and interval ligation: the artery just beyond the fistula is ligated and a bypass from higher up feeds the hand), revision using distal inflow, proximalization of the arterial inflow, and ligation of the access when the hand is at risk and the access cannot be saved. Why prefer a fistula over a graft? Once mature, a fistula lasts longer, clots less and is far less likely to become infected. Its weaknesses are a slower start and a meaningful rate of failure to mature; a graft can be used sooner but needs more interventions to stay open. Mnemonic: the rule of 6s A fistula is generally mature and usable when it meets the rule of 6s: a flow above 600 mL/min, a diameter above 6 mm, a depth less than 6 mm below the skin, and at least 6 cm of straight cannulation segment, by about 6 weeks. Consent risks Failure to mature, which is common and may need balloon angioplasty, branch ligation or superficialization. Thrombosis, bleeding and hematoma, and infection (higher with grafts). Steal syndrome and, rarely, ischemic monomelic neuropathy. Aneurysm or pseudoaneurysm, arm swelling from central venous stenosis, and high-output heart failure from very high flow. Nerve injury, notably the medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve with basilic transposition. After the case Most access cases go home the same day; inpatients return to the floor and to their dialysis schedule. Before the patient leaves A thrill, a perfused hand, and a quick motor and sensory exam of the hand, documented, because ischemic monomelic neuropathy presents in the first hours. Steal A cool, pale, painful or numb hand, worse during dialysis, from hours to months after creation. Mild symptoms may settle; rest pain, ulceration or weakness need prompt surgical attention. Arm care No blood pressure cuffs, IVs or blood draws in the access arm. The patient checks the thrill daily and returns if it disappears; a clotted access should be reopened promptly, ideally before the next dialysis, so a catheter is not needed. Maturation A duplex or clinical check at around four to six weeks; failure to mature"},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Other operations","u":"operations.html#other-ops","x":"Other operations to recognize Cases you will hear about on the board or see less often. Know what each is and why it is done. Aortobifemoral, femoral-femoral and axillofemoral bypass now have their own entry in Aortobifemoral and extra-anatomic bypass . Open thrombectomy of a graft salvaging a clotted bypass Reopening a thrombosed graft, often combined with fixing the underlying cause (a stenosis at the anastomosis or in the runoff) so it does not simply clot again. Catheter-directed lysis or a new bypass are the alternatives. Popliteal aneurysm repair The most common peripheral aneurysm, often bilateral and associated with an AAA. Repair is advised at about 2 cm or when it contains thrombus or causes symptoms, because its danger is thrombosis and embolization rather than rupture. Options are exclusion with bypass from a medial approach, a posterior approach with an interposition graft for large compressive aneurysms, or a stent graft. Femoral pseudoaneurysm repair After catheter access. Ultrasound-guided thrombin injection treats most suitable ones; open repair is for large, expanding, infected ones, threatened skin, nerve compression, or failure of injection. Carotid-subclavian bypass or transposition Restores flow to the left subclavian for symptomatic subclavian disease (subclavian steal) or before TEVAR covers its origin. Mesenteric revascularization For acute mesenteric ischemia: superior mesenteric embolectomy through the root of the mesentery, bypass, or retrograde open stenting, usually followed by a planned second-look laparotomy. Chronic mesenteric ischemia is mostly stented, with bypass for failures. See Presentations . Infected aortic graft excision Removal of the infected graft with in situ reconstruction (the patient's own femoral veins, cryopreserved allograft, or an antibiotic-soaked prosthetic) or extra-anatomic bypass, with long-term antibiotics. First-rib resection thoracic outlet Decompression of the thoracic outlet for neurogenic (the most common), venous (Paget-Schroetter, effort thrombosis of the subclavian vein, usually after thrombolysis) or arterial syndromes, removing the first rib (and any cervical rib) to free the neurovascular bundle. Risks include pneumothorax and injury to the brachial plexus, the long thoracic and phrenic nerves, and the subclavian vessels. IVC filter placement For acute venous thromboembolism when anticoagulation is contraindicated. A retrievable filter should be removed once the patient can be anticoagulated. See Presentations . Endovenous ablation Thermal or non-thermal closure of a refluxing saphenous vein for symptomatic venous insufficiency. Before any vein is ablated or harvested, someone should ask whether the patient might one day need it as a bypass conduit."},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Conduits","u":"operations.html#conduits","x":"Conduits The conduit is the third leg of inflow, outflow and conduit, and for an infrainguinal bypass its quality affects patency more than almost any other choice. In the aorta and iliacs, where vessels are large and flow is high, prosthetic grafts do very well; below the knee, the patient's own vein wins. The patency hierarchy below the groin Single-segment great saphenous vein, ideally at least about 3 mm in diameter, from the same leg or, if needed, the other leg. Alternative autologous vein: arm vein (cephalic or basilic), small saphenous vein, or a spliced composite of segments. Prosthetic above the knee: PTFE or Dacron, acceptable for above-knee femoropopliteal bypass when no vein is available. Prosthetic or cryopreserved graft below the knee: the least durable options, sometimes improved by a vein cuff or patch at the distal anastomosis, and used when there is no vein and no endovascular option. Vein grafts Reversed saphenous vein Harvested and flipped end to end, so the valves open in the direction of arterial flow. No valve lysis is needed and it can go through any tunnel, but its narrow end meets the larger inflow artery and its wide end the small target. In situ saphenous vein Left in its bed, with the valves cut by a valvulotome and the side branches ligated (otherwise they become arteriovenous fistulas). It keeps the vein's natural taper, large at the inflow and small at the target, and minimizes handling, but needs the vein to lie where the bypass must go and a long incision or several skip incisions. Non-reversed translocated vein Removed from its bed, valves lysed, and placed in its original orientation (not reversed). It keeps the favorable size match while allowing any tunnel. Arm vein Cephalic and basilic veins, mapped like the saphenous. Thinner-walled and often spliced, with internal webs that may need angioscopy to find; less durable than saphenous vein but better than prosthetic below the knee. Small saphenous vein From the back of the calf, useful for short segments; the sural nerve runs alongside it. Femoral vein The deep femoral vein of the thigh, large and infection-resistant, used to rebuild the aorta and iliacs after an infected graft is removed. Leg swelling and compartment syndrome are the trade-offs. Reversed vs in situ vs non-reversed saphenous vein bypass: what is the difference? All use the saphenous vein. Reversed vein is harvested and flipped so the valves do not obstruct flow. In situ leaves the vein in its bed and the valves are cut with a valvulotome, which keeps the vein in its native size match (large proximally to the large inflow, small distally to the small target). Non-reversed translocated vein is removed and its valves are lysed, but it is placed in its original orientation rather than flipped, keeping the size match. The advantage of in situ and non-reversed techniques is the favorable size match at each end. Prosthetic and biologic grafts PTFE expanded polytetrafluoroethylene The usual prosthetic for above-knee femoropopliteal, femoral-femoral, axillofemoral and AV access grafts. Comes ringed (externally supported) for crossing joints or long tunnels, and in heparin-bonded versions. Performs poorly to tibial targets and is vulnerable to infection. Dacron polyester The standard for aortic tube and bifurcated grafts. Knitted grafts are more porous and are sealed with collagen or gelatin; woven grafts are less porous and favored in the chest and in rupture. Cryopreserved allograft Donor vein or artery used when there is no autologous vein, especially in an infected field. Expensive, with lower patency and a risk of degeneration. Biologic patches and grafts Bovine pericardium patches for carotid and femoral endarterectomy, and bovine or biologic grafts for some access and infected-field cases. Defaults worth stating without hesitation Decision Default answer Best bypass conduit Ipsilateral great saphenous vein; contralateral saphenous vein or arm vein next Aortic and aortoiliac conduit Prosthetic (Dacron or PTFE) tube or bifurcated graft First-choice dialysis access Radiocephalic (Brescia-Cimino) fistula at the wrist, then brachiocephalic, then brachiobasilic transposition, then graft, catheter last CEA closure Patch angioplasty (or eversion) over primary closure to lower restenosis Amputation for ambulation Below-knee over above-knee whenever it will heal Acute embolic occlusion Fogarty embolectomy"},{"p":"Open operations","t":"Instruments","u":"operations.html#instruments","x":"Instruments you'll be handed Vascular trays are built around atraumatic instruments that hold an artery without crushing its lining. Knowing the generic names lets you follow the case and hand or hold the right thing. Exact makes and tray contents vary by hospital, and many have several eponyms. Clamps and vessel control DeBakey clamps The workhorse atraumatic clamps, with fine interlocking rows of teeth along the jaws, in straight, angled and curved forms and sizes from peripheral to aortic. Fogarty clamps Clamps that take soft, disposable jaw inserts, gentle on calcified or delicate arteries and on grafts. Satinsky clamp A curved, side-biting (partially occluding) clamp that isolates part of a vessel wall, such as the aorta or vena cava, while flow continues through the rest. Cooley clamps A family of angled and curved atraumatic clamps in many sizes, used on the iliacs, renals and peripheral arteries; the larger ones go on the aorta. Bulldog clamps Small spring clamps for small vessels such as tibial, radial or branch arteries, placed with an applier or by hand. Vessel loops Thin silicone bands passed around a vessel with a right-angle clamp for gentle retraction and control; a double loop pulled snug occludes a branch. Color conventions (often red for arteries, blue for veins, yellow for nerves) vary. Umbilical tape and Rummel tourniquet A tape around a larger vessel, threaded through a short rubber tube and cinched to occlude it, also used to hold a carotid shunt in place. Dissecting and sewing DeBakey forceps The standard vascular tissue forceps, with fine atraumatic serrations. Hold the adventitia, not the intima. Potts scissors Fine scissors with angled blades (in several angles) for extending an arteriotomy along the vessel. Castroviejo needle driver A fine spring-handled driver, held like a pencil, for 6-0 to 8-0 suture on tibial, pedal and access anastomoses. Right-angle clamp For passing loops and ties around vessels. Endarterectomy elevator A blunt spatula (Freer or Penfield type) for developing the endarterectomy plane. Valvulotome A cutting instrument passed through a vein graft to cut valve leaflets for in situ and non-reversed bypasses. Tunneler A long rod with a sheath or bullet tip for passing a graft through a subcutaneous or anatomic tunnel. Coronary probes or dilators Graduated probes to size a small artery or check an anastomosis. Self-retaining retractors Weitlaner and Gelpi retractors for the groin and neck, and a table-mounted retractor system for laparotomies. Suture Vascular anastomoses are sewn with monofilament polypropylene, double-armed so each end has a needle. It is non-absorbable and slides through tissue smoothly. PTFE suture is sometimes used on PTFE grafts to reduce needle-hole bleeding, and felt pledgets reinforce stitches in fragile aorta. Typical polypropylene sizes by vessel (surgeons vary by a size either way) Vessel Typical size Aorta 3-0 (4-0 for a small aorta) Iliac 4-0 to 5-0 Common femoral 5-0 (4-0 to 6-0) Carotid patch 6-0 Popliteal 5-0 to 6-0 Tibial and pedal 6-0 to 7-0 (8-0 for some pedal vessels) Radial or brachial (access) 6-0 to 7-0 Carotid shunts Javid shunt A tapered shunt held in place with dedicated ring clamps. Pruitt-Inahara shunt A shunt with small balloons at each end that hold it in the vessel, and a side port for flushing or pressure measurement. Straight shunts Simple tubes such as the Sundt or Argyle types, held with Rummel tourniquets. Fogarty embolectomy catheters Balloon catheters sized in French, each with its maximum inflation volume printed on the catheter. Inflate with saline gently while withdrawing, adjusting by feel. Overinflation or dragging too hard tears the intima, causing dissection, rupture or later stenosis. Over-the-wire versions can be steered into individual tibial vessels under fluoroscopy. Typical Fogarty catheter size by vessel Vessel Typical size Aorta 6 to 7 Fr Iliac 5 to 6 Fr Femoral and superficial femoral 4 to 5 Fr Popliteal 3 to 4 Fr Tibial 2 to 3 Fr Brachial 3 to 4 Fr Radial and ulnar 2 to 3 Fr Superior mesenteric 3 to 4 Fr Confirm locally Each attending's preference card lists the suture, shunt, clamps and patch they use. Read it, or ask the scrub tech, before the case; it is the quickest way to learn the local names and to be ready half a step ahead. More on being useful in the room is on Rounds and presenting ."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Endovascular","u":"endovascular.html","x":"How an endovascular case works from the needle to the closure: access, wires and catheters, imaging, radiation and contrast, how a peripheral intervention is planned, the treatment toolkit, aortic stent grafts, clot and embolization work, and the care of the patient before and after an angiogram. Read it before your first day in the angiography suite. The named devices are catalogued separately on Wires, catheters and devices."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Vascular access","u":"endovascular.html#access","x":"Getting in: vascular access Every endovascular case starts with a needle in a vessel, and almost all of it rests on one idea, the Seldinger technique. Where and how the artery is entered decides much of what can go wrong afterward. Note: orientation, not technique This page explains the concepts and vocabulary of endovascular surgery so you can follow a case, look after the patient on either side of it, and answer questions intelligently. It is not a procedural manual. Device selection, sizing, dosing and technique are the operator's decisions, governed by the patient and the device's current instructions for use. You puncture the artery with a needle, pass a guidewire through the needle into the lumen, remove the needle while leaving the wire in place, and then slide a sheath or catheter over the wire. The wire is the rail that everything else rides on. The cardinal rules are that you never let go of the wire and never advance anything against resistance. Most operators start with a micropuncture set: a 21-gauge needle that takes a 0.018 inch wire, then a small coaxial dilator that converts the access to a standard 0.035 inch wire. A misplaced stick with a small needle does far less harm than one with a large needle. Mnemonic: needle, wire, catheter, treat, close The throughline of every case: access by Seldinger, a wire across the lesion, the right catheter to the right place, a balloon, stent or graft to treat it, and a clean closure. If you can say where in that sequence the case is, you can follow it. The common femoral artery, the default door The common femoral artery is the workhorse access site because it is large and, crucially, it lies over the femoral head, so it can be compressed against bone afterward to stop bleeding. The safe puncture is below the inguinal ligament and above the femoral bifurcation, aiming for the artery as it crosses the middle of the femoral head on fluoroscopy. The skin crease is a poor guide; in obese patients it often lies below the bifurcation. Retrograde access. The puncture points against the direction of flow, with the wire heading up toward the aorta. This is the setup for aortography, for treating the aorta and iliacs, and for crossing over to the other leg. Antegrade access. The puncture points with the direction of flow, with the wire heading down the same leg. It is used to treat disease in the thigh and below on the same side. Contralateral “up and over.” One common femoral is punctured retrograde, a catheter is walked up and over the aortic bifurcation, and the wire comes down the opposite leg to treat it. Red flag: a stick too high or too low Where you puncture determines how you bleed. A high stick, above the inguinal ligament, cannot be compressed against the femoral head and can bleed into the retroperitoneum, which is occult and dangerous. A low stick, into the superficial femoral or profunda below the bifurcation, predisposes to a pseudoaneurysm, an arteriovenous fistula, or thrombosis of the smaller vessel. A puncture in the common femoral over the femoral head prevents both. Radial and brachial access The radial artery is increasingly used for visceral and some peripheral work because it has a much lower bleeding and access-site complication rate and lets the patient sit up and walk right away. The left radial is usually preferred for work below the diaphragm: it is a shorter route to the legs, and the catheter enters the arch at the left subclavian without crossing the origins of the innominate and left carotid arteries. Its limits are a small artery, which caps the sheath size, and reach, because many devices are too short to get from the wrist to below the knee. Before radial access, many operators confirm a dual blood supply to the hand with an Allen or Barbeau test, so that the ulnar artery and palmar arch can perfuse the hand if the radial occludes, although how well these tests predict hand ischemia is debated. Heparin and an antispasmodic “cocktail” are given to prevent spasm and radial artery occlusion, and at the end a compression band is weaned off over a couple of hours while keeping the artery open. The brachial artery is an alternative but carries a higher risk of median nerve injury and of a hematoma that compresses the nerve within the tight brachial sheath, so it is used more selectively. Large-bore upper-limb access for complex aortic work usually goes through the axillary or brachial artery, by cutdown or a carefully closed puncture. Pearl: ultrasound guidance Real-time ultrasound to find the common femoral artery, see its bifurcation, and watch the needle enter has become standard, because it reduces high and low sticks, inadvertent venous puncture and access complications. If you are asked how to lower access complications, “an ultrasound-guided, single-wall puncture of the common femoral artery over the femoral head” is the answer. A quick fluoroscopic look at the femoral head before the stick confirms the level. How do you know, on the angiogram, that a femoral puncture is too high? The inferior epigastric artery curves off the distal external iliac at about the level of the inguinal ligament. An arteriotomy at or above the lowest point of that curve is above the ligament, and therefore not compressible. A puncture over the middle of the femoral head and above the femoral bifurcation is the target."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Wires, catheters, sheaths","u":"endovascular.html#wires-catheters","x":"Wires, catheters and sheaths These are the hand tools of the field, and understanding what each does makes a case legible. Two ideas organize everything: French sizing, and the difference between a wire, a catheter and a sheath. This section is the primer; the named products are catalogued on Wires, catheters and devices . Guidewire The rail. It goes first, crosses the lesion, and everything else travels over it. Its diameter is measured in inches (0.035, 0.018, 0.014). Catheter A long hollow tube that rides over the wire to steer it, support it, inject contrast or reach a branch. Sized in French by its outer diameter. Sheath A tube with a hemostatic valve that sits in the puncture, holds it open, and lets catheters and devices go in and out without bleeding or losing access. Sized in French by its inner diameter. Guide catheter A larger, stiffer catheter that reaches further than a short sheath and gives backup for pushing a device across a tough lesion. Like other catheters, it is sized by its outer diameter. 1 Fr about 0.33 mm, so 3 French is about 1 mm 0.035 in the everyday workhorse wire platform 0.018 / 0.014 in small-vessel platforms for tibial and pedal work and low-profile devices What does “French” mean, and how does it differ for a sheath versus a catheter? One French is about 0.33 mm of diameter, so 3 French is roughly 1 mm. A catheter's French is its outer diameter. A sheath's French is its inner diameter, the size of device it will accept, so a sheath always has a larger outer diameter than its stated French (typically by about 1.5 to 2 French) and makes a correspondingly larger hole in the artery. Guidewires Wires differ in diameter, length, stiffness, tip shape and coating. The everyday workhorse is the 0.035 inch system, while smaller 0.018 and 0.014 inch wires are used for the small tibial and pedal vessels and for low-profile devices. Length matters too: an exchange-length wire is long enough to hold onto while a whole catheter is withdrawn over it, roughly twice the length of the catheter. Hydrophilic wires. Called “glide” wires in the room, they have a slippery coating and an angled or steerable tip, ideal for crossing tight or tortuous lesions. The same slipperiness lets them back out, dissect or perforate, so they are usually exchanged for a more supportive wire before bulky devices are delivered, and they are never pulled back through a metal access needle, which can shear off the coating. Stiff and super-stiff wires. Wires such as the Amplatz, and the Lunderquist for aortic work, provide the rigid rail needed to track a bulky stent graft through a tortuous iliac system. Exchanging a soft crossing wire for a stiff delivery wire, through a catheter so the wire position is never lost, is a recurring step. Soft, atraumatic wires. Wires such as a Bentson, with a long floppy tip, are used where you want to avoid injuring the vessel, for example on the first pass up a diseased iliac. Every balloon and stent is built for one wire platform, and its label lists the wire it rides on, the smallest sheath it fits through, and its shaft length. An over-the-wire device carries the wire through its whole length, which gives better push. A rapid-exchange (monorail) device lets the wire exit a short distance from the tip, so a shorter wire can be used and one person can swap devices. Catheters Catheters either inject contrast or select a branch. Flush catheters with a coiled pigtail tip and many side holes sit in a large vessel and deliver a big power-injected bolus for an aortogram without whipping around. Selective catheters have an end hole and preformed shapes designed to hook into specific branches: an angled or Berenstein tip for gentle selection, a Cobra shape for downgoing visceral vessels, and reverse-curve shapes such as the Simmons (Sidewinder) or SOS Omni for sharply angled or upgoing origins. Recurved shapes are also how the opposite iliac is hooked when going up and over. Low-profile support (crossing) catheters follow the wire into an occlusion to give it push. Selective injections are made at lower rates and volumes than flush runs. A high-rate power injection through an end-hole catheter sitting in a small branch can dissect it. Sheaths and guides A sheath holds the puncture open and lets you exchange catheters and devices without losing access or bleeding. A guide catheter is a longer support tube that reaches further and gives backup to push a device across a tough lesion. Long sheaths do the same job from the groin: a crossover sheath parked over the aortic bifurcation supports work in the opposite leg. Most cases progress from a small diagnostic sheath to a larger one once the plan and the device size are settled. Because sheaths are named by their inner diameter and guides by their outer diameter, a guide usually needs to be about one French size larger than the sheath the same device would need. Check the device label rather than relying on the rule. Why is a hydrophilic crossing wire usually swapped for a stiff wire before a stent graft is delivered? A stent graft delivery system is large and stiff. It needs a rail that straightens a tortuous iliac and will not back out or buckle as the device is pushed. A slippery, floppy hydrophilic wire gives little support, can be pulled back as the device advances, and its tip can perforate a branch. The swap is done through a catheter so wire position across the lesion or into the aorta is never lost. Note: the named list For the specific wires, catheters and sheaths you will hear called for in the room, with their sizes and uses, see wires , catheters and sheaths on the devices page."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Angiography and DSA","u":"endovascular.html#angiography","x":"Making the pictures: angiography and DSA An angiogram is a real-time X-ray movie of contrast moving through vessels. The refinement that makes it so clear is digital subtraction angiography (DSA), which takes a plain “mask” image of the field and subtracts it from the contrast images, erasing bone and soft tissue so only the contrast-filled vessels remain. Why does a patient have to hold still for DSA, and what fixes motion on the image? Subtraction assumes the mask and the contrast frames line up exactly, so any movement (including breathing or bowel gas shifting) leaves a blurred “misregistration” artifact. The operator asks for a breath-hold, and can re-mask or apply pixel shifting to realign the images. Techniques you will hear named Road-mapping. A stored contrast image is overlaid on the live fluoroscopy so the operator can steer a wire along a faint map of the vessel without continuous contrast. Angled projections. A bifurcation or an aneurysm neck often hides on a straight-on view and opens up only when the C-arm is angled. The common iliac bifurcation is usually best seen in a contralateral oblique and the common femoral bifurcation in an ipsilateral oblique. The celiac and superior mesenteric origins, which arise from the front of the aorta, are seen best on a lateral view. Bolus chase and stepping runoff. A single injection is followed down the legs as the table moves, or a series of stations is imaged from pelvis to foot. Pressure gradients. Measuring pressure above and below a borderline lesion, sometimes after a vasodilator, helps decide whether an iliac stenosis matters hemodynamically. Thresholds vary between operators. A diagnostic aortogram with runoff Access and sheath. Usually a retrograde common femoral puncture on the less affected side, so the same access can go up and over to treat the worse leg. Aortogram. A flush catheter at about the level of the renal arteries (around L1) shows the renal arteries, the infrarenal aorta and the iliacs. Pelvis. The catheter is pulled down to just above the aortic bifurcation for the iliac and femoral bifurcations, with obliques as needed. Runoff. Images down both legs to the feet, including the tibial vessels and the pedal arch, comparing the two sides. Selective views. If treatment is planned, a selective catheter goes up and over for closer, lower-contrast pictures of the target leg. Pearl: read it the way you present it Describe a runoff as inflow (aorta and iliacs), outflow (common femoral, profunda, superficial femoral and popliteal) and runoff (how many tibial vessels reach the foot, and whether there is a pedal arch). A team that hears “two-vessel runoff via the anterior tibial and peroneal” knows exactly what the target options are. Arterial anatomy is reviewed in Foundations ."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"IVUS and adjunct imaging","u":"endovascular.html#ivus","x":"IVUS and adjunct imaging An angiogram is a silhouette of the lumen in one plane. Intravascular ultrasound and three-dimensional imaging in the room fill in what the silhouette misses: the true size of the vessel, what the wall is made of, and where the branches are. Intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) IVUS is a catheter with an ultrasound transducer at its tip, run over a wire to give cross-sectional images from inside the vessel. Small catheters ride 0.014 or 0.018 inch wires for leg arteries; larger ones on 0.035 inch wires image the aorta, the iliacs and the large veins. Sizing. IVUS measures the true diameter and cross-sectional area of the lumen and of the healthy reference segment. Angiography sees only the lumen in one plane and tends to under-size, so balloons and stents chosen by IVUS are often larger. Plaque and calcium. It shows how much of the circumference is calcified and how deep, which guides whether vessel preparation such as atherectomy or lithotripsy is needed. Wire position. It confirms whether a wire is in the true lumen or subintimal, which matters after crossing an occlusion and when treating aortic dissection, where the stent graft must go into the true lumen. Checking the result. After treatment it shows residual narrowing, dissection, and whether a stent is fully expanded and against the wall. Less contrast. It can replace some contrast runs, which helps patients with kidney disease. Why IVUS dominates venous stenting Iliac vein compression (May-Thurner and other non-thrombotic iliac vein lesions) and post-thrombotic scarring are poorly seen on venography. A compressed iliac vein is flattened between the artery and the spine and spreads sideways, so a front-on venogram can make it look wide and normal; collaterals may be the only clue. IVUS shows the true cross-section, so it is used to diagnose the lesion, choose the stent diameter, and pick landing zones that cover the whole diseased segment. In the VIDIO study, venography missed a meaningful share of lesions that IVUS found. A cross-sectional area reduction of about 50 percent or more is commonly treated as significant, though thresholds vary. Cone-beam CT and fusion imaging Cone-beam CT. The C-arm spins around the patient to build a CT-like three-dimensional image on the table. It is used to check stent graft and bridging stent position and look for endoleak at the end of complex aortic repairs, and to map feeding vessels before embolization. It adds radiation dose, so it is used deliberately. Fusion imaging. The preoperative CTA is turned into a 3D model and overlaid on live fluoroscopy, registered to the bones or to a cone-beam CT. It marks the renal and visceral origins for fenestrated and branched repair and reduces contrast and radiation. Stiff wires and devices straighten and shift the aorta, so the overlay drifts and has to be checked and corrected. Why can an iliac vein look normal on a venogram but be severely narrowed on IVUS? The compression flattens the vein from front to back, so viewed straight on it spreads sideways and the contrast column can look wide, or just faint. A single-plane venogram is a silhouette and cannot show cross-sectional area. IVUS measures the area directly and shows the compression point, which is why it is the reference test for sizing and placing iliac vein stents."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Radiation and contrast","u":"endovascular.html#radiation-contrast","x":"Respecting radiation and contrast The two hazards unique to this room are ionizing radiation and iodinated contrast. Both are asked about constantly, partly because they are safety issues you are expected to take seriously from day one. Radiation safety and ALARA The governing principle is ALARA, keeping dose “as low as reasonably achievable” for both the patient and the team. Dose is controlled along three classic levers and a few practical habits. Mnemonic: time, distance, shielding Cut time (less fluoroscopy, pulsed low frame rates, last-image-hold), increase distance (scattered dose falls with the square of the distance, so a step back helps a lot), and use shielding (a lead apron, thyroid collar, leaded glasses, and table-mounted and ceiling-suspended shields). Wear your dosimeter. Inverse-square law. Doubling your distance from the source quarters your exposure, which is why simply stepping back during an injection is one of the most effective things you can do. Geometry. Keep the X-ray tube under the table and the detector close to the patient, and collimate the beam to the area of interest, which lowers dose and improves the image. Magnification and steep angles raise dose. Scatter. Most of the radiation reaching the team is scattered off the patient, and it is highest on the side where the beam enters, the tube side. On a steep oblique or lateral view, stand on the detector side. Hands out of the beam. Hands near the field during fluoroscopy can be in the primary beam, not just scatter. 50 mSv US annual occupational whole-body dose limit 5 mSv US limit to the embryo or fetus over a declared pregnancy About 2 Gy peak skin dose at which transient skin reddening can begin About 5 Gy reference air kerma often used to trigger patient skin follow-up (local thresholds vary) Red flag: the pregnant or junior team member Radiation injury is cumulative and the lens of the eye and the thyroid are particularly sensitive, which is the reason for leaded glasses and a thyroid shield; international guidance has lowered the recommended lens dose limit to 20 mSv a year averaged over five years. If you are pregnant, disclose it so additional monitoring and shielding can be arranged. Treating the dose casually because you “were only in for a minute” is exactly the attitude the badges exist to correct. Confirm locally Where to wear the dosimeter (commonly at the collar outside the apron, sometimes with a second badge under it), how a rotating trainee gets one, the patient dose level that triggers skin follow-up, and how a pregnancy is declared to radiation safety all vary by hospital. Iodinated contrast Iodinated contrast makes vessels visible but carries two main risks, plus one medication interaction you will be asked about. Contrast-associated kidney injury A rise in creatinine, usually within 48 to 72 hours. Risk is highest with chronic kidney disease (particularly an eGFR below 30; European guidance uses about 45 when intra-arterial contrast reaches the kidneys first, as in an aortogram), diabetes with kidney disease, dehydration, hypotension, and large or repeated contrast loads. The mitigations are to identify at-risk patients, give isotonic IV fluid when indicated, minimize the contrast volume, and avoid other nephrotoxins. In the PRESERVE trial, bicarbonate and N-acetylcysteine were no better than saline and placebo, so they are no longer recommended. Allergic-like reaction From hives to anaphylaxis. A patient with a prior allergic-like reaction to iodinated contrast is generally premedicated with a corticosteroid and an antihistamine, and breakthrough reactions still happen, so the team stays ready. A shellfish or “iodine” allergy is not a contrast allergy and is not by itself a reason to premedicate. Metformin Contrast does not interact with metformin directly, but kidney injury can lead to metformin accumulation and lactic acidosis. Under ACR guidance, patients with an eGFR of 30 or more and no acute kidney injury can continue it; those with acute kidney injury or an eGFR under 30 hold it from the time of contrast for 48 hours and restart once kidney function is confirmed stable. How do you image a patient with severe renal impairment or a serious contrast allergy? Carbon dioxide angiography is the classic answer below the diaphragm. CO2 is injected as a contrast agent, it is not nephrotoxic and causes no allergic reaction, and it is well suited to the aorta and the legs. The critical caveat is that CO2 is not used above the diaphragm, in the arm arteries, or in the cerebral or coronary circulation, because gas can embolize to the brain or heart. IVUS, duplex guidance and small targeted doses of iodinated contrast reduce the load further. Gadolinium is no longer a recommended substitute: at angiographic doses it is itself nephrotoxic and carries a risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Planning an intervention","u":"endovascular.html#planning","x":"Planning a peripheral intervention Most of a peripheral intervention is decided before the needle goes in: what is being treated, from which access, how the lesion will be crossed, and what will be done once it is. Knowing the plan lets you follow the case and anticipate its problems. Read the imaging as inflow, outflow and target Whether the source is a duplex, a CTA or an old angiogram, describe it in the same three parts. Inflow The aorta, iliacs and common femoral artery. Inflow disease is fixed first, because a distal repair fed by a stenotic iliac will fail. Common femoral disease is classically treated with open endarterectomy rather than a stent, often combined with iliac stenting or distal work as a hybrid procedure. Outflow The profunda, superficial femoral and popliteal arteries. Note stenosis versus occlusion, length, calcium, and where the vessel reconstitutes below an occlusion. Target and runoff The tibial vessels and pedal arch. In chronic limb-threatening ischemia the question is which tibial artery can carry straight-line flow to the foot, ideally to the region of the wound. Duplex. Cheap and safe, and it maps the vein for a possible bypass at the same visit. A peak systolic velocity ratio of about 2 or more across a segment suggests a stenosis of at least 50 percent. It is operator-dependent and difficult in the obese abdomen. CTA. Shows the whole tree, the calcium and the access vessels, and is the planning study for the aorta. Heavy calcium blooms in small tibial vessels and can make them look worse than they are. Catheter angiography. Still the reference standard for tibial and pedal anatomy, and usually done in the same sitting as treatment. For chronic limb-threatening ischemia, the Global Vascular Guidelines frame the decision as PLAN: patient risk, limb severity (the WIfI stage) and anatomic pattern (the GLASS stage, which grades femoropopliteal and infrapopliteal disease along the target artery path). The older TASC II A to D classes still come up for aortoiliac and femoropopliteal lesions. For patients with a usable great saphenous vein, the BEST-CLI trial favored bypass, so “endovascular first” is no longer automatic. See CLTI , bypass and the staging systems . Red flag: intervening for the wrong indication Claudication is treated first with supervised exercise, smoking cessation and medical therapy; intervention is for lifestyle-limiting symptoms despite that. SVS guidance recommends against treating isolated below-the-knee disease for claudication alone, because it adds risk without durable benefit. See claudication . Choosing access Access options for a peripheral intervention Access Best for Limits Retrograde contralateral femoral (up and over) The default. A diagnostic aortogram and runoff, then treatment of the opposite iliac, common femoral, superficial femoral and popliteal arteries, all from one puncture. A hostile aortic bifurcation (narrow or steep, a prior aortobifemoral graft, kissing iliac stents, or an aortic stent graft) blocks or complicates the route. The long distance to the tibials costs push and reach. Antegrade ipsilateral femoral Superficial femoral, popliteal and tibial work on the same side. A shorter, straighter route with better push and torque, and reach to the foot. Harder with a pannus or a high bifurcation; needs a healthy proximal superficial femoral to seat the sheath; cannot treat the iliac or common femoral; hemostasis can be harder. Radial (usually left) Hostile groins, obesity, patients who cannot lie flat; iliac, renal and mesenteric work; early walking. The small artery limits sheath size, and many devices are too short to reach below the knee. Brachial or axillary Downgoing visceral branches, for example during fenestrated or branched aortic repair, and large-bore upper-limb access. Nerve injury and hematoma in a tight sheath; arch manipulation carries a stroke risk. Retrograde pedal or tibial A rescue when antegrade crossing fails, or a primary route when the groin is hostile. Usually a small sheath, or no sheath, in the dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial or distal anterior tibial artery under ultrasound or road-map guidance. Retrograde popliteal access, with the patient prone, is a related option. Spasm or injury of the access vessel, which may be the foot's best remaining runoff, can cost the limb. Resident tip When you see a patient booked for an angiogram, look at both groins and write down what you find: scars, a pannus, intertrigo or infection, a prior graft, and the femoral pulses. A hostile groin changes the access plan, and it is far better found the evening before than on the table. Crossing the lesion Stenoses are usually crossed easily. Chronic total occlusions are the challenge, and the strategy escalates. Intraluminal. A wire, supported by a catheter, is worked through the occlusion while staying in the true lumen. This is preferred where possible because it keeps the vessel's structure and the options for later. Subintimal. When that fails, the wire is deliberately passed into the plane within the vessel wall, often as a loop of hydrophilic wire, tracked along the occlusion and brought back into the true lumen beyond it (subintimal angioplasty). The danger is failing to re-enter and extending the dissection past the point of reconstitution. Re-entry device. If the wire will not come back into the true lumen, a re-entry catheter fires a small needle, aimed under fluoroscopy or IVUS, from the subintimal plane into the true lumen just below the occlusion. Retrograde (SAFARI-type) approach. A second access below the occlusion (pedal, tibial or popliteal) lets a wire cross from below. The two channels are connected, or the retrograde wire is snared and pulled out through the femoral sheath to make a through-and-through “flossing” wire, and treatment is delivered from the femoral side so the small distal access stays small. SAFARI stands for subintimal arterial flossing with antegrade-retrograde intervention. Pe"},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"The toolkit","u":"endovascular.html#toolkit","x":"The toolkit: balloons, stents and atherectomy High yield Once you are across a lesion with a wire, a small family of devices does the actual treating. This section covers what each family does and when it is chosen; the named products are on balloons , stents and stent grafts . Balloon angioplasty A balloon is tracked over the wire to the lesion and inflated to crack and stretch the plaque, often called plain balloon angioplasty (PTA, for percutaneous transluminal angioplasty). Balloons are sized to the healthy reference vessel, roughly one to one, and inflated with dilute contrast using a pressure syringe (an inflation device) read in atmospheres. Each balloon has a nominal pressure, at which it reaches its labeled diameter, and a rated burst pressure, which should not be exceeded. Non-compliant balloons hold their size at high pressure; compliant balloons keep growing as they fill and are used for gentle molding, such as of a stent graft's seal zones. Drug-coated balloons. These transfer an antiproliferative drug, most often paclitaxel, to the vessel wall to reduce restenosis, especially in the femoropopliteal segment. A 2018 meta-analysis raised a late mortality signal for paclitaxel devices; after reviewing further data, the FDA concluded in 2023 that the evidence does not support an excess mortality risk. Sirolimus-coated balloons are a newer alternative whose place alongside paclitaxel is still being defined. Cutting and scoring balloons. Small blades or wires on the balloon score resistant, calcified or recurrent lesions so they open at lower pressure. Intravascular lithotripsy. A balloon that emits pressure waves to fracture calcium in the vessel wall before definitive therapy. Stents The single most asked endovascular concept is the difference between the two stent families and where each belongs. Self-expanding versus balloon-expandable stents Feature Self-expanding (nitinol) Balloon-expandable Material Nitinol (nickel-titanium), a shape-memory alloy Stainless steel or cobalt-chromium How it deploys Springs open as the constraining sheath is withdrawn Expanded by inflating the balloon it is mounted on Best where Areas of flexion or external compression (superficial femoral, popliteal, carotid) and long or tapering segments Where precise placement and high radial force matter (ostial renal, common iliac origin) Key property Recovers its shape after being squashed; does not stay crushed Precise and strong, but a deforming force can crush it permanently Placement accuracy Can foreshorten or jump on release Very accurate, good for ostia A covered stent (stent graft) is a stent lined with fabric. It is used to exclude an aneurysm or a pseudoaneurysm, to seal a perforation or rupture, in complex aortoiliac occlusive disease, and sometimes to treat recurrent in-stent restenosis. Drug-eluting stents release an antiproliferative drug over time and are used in the femoropopliteal segment and, borrowed from coronary practice, in short tibial lesions. Why use a self-expanding nitinol stent in the superficial femoral artery rather than a balloon-expandable one? The superficial femoral artery bends, twists, shortens and is compressed by the surrounding muscle with every step. A balloon-expandable stent there could be permanently crushed, whereas a nitinol self-expanding stent springs back to shape after deformation, so it tolerates the mechanical stress of that segment. Pearl: the no-stent zones The common femoral artery and the popliteal artery behind the knee have traditionally been avoided for stenting. Both bend with the hip and knee, and both are where future access punctures and bypass anastomoses go. Common femoral disease is usually treated with open endarterectomy, and a stented popliteal is a hard place to sew a bypass. Atherectomy Atherectomy physically removes or debulks plaque rather than just displacing it, using directional, rotational, orbital or laser devices. It is used mainly for heavily calcified lesions and in-stent restenosis, and to prepare a vessel before angioplasty. The chief hazard to know is distal embolization of the liberated debris, sometimes managed with an embolic protection filter. The evidence that atherectomy improves outcomes over angioplasty alone is limited, and high-volume use of it has drawn scrutiny."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"EVAR and TEVAR","u":"endovascular.html#evar-tevar","x":"Aortic stent grafts: EVAR and TEVAR The largest endovascular operations are the aortic ones. EVAR (endovascular aneurysm repair) treats the abdominal aorta and TEVAR treats the thoracic aorta, in both cases by deploying a fabric-covered stent graft that creates a new internal channel and excludes the aneurysm sac from the circulation. What makes an aneurysm suitable A stent graft needs healthy vessel to seal against at each end, the landing (sealing) zones. For EVAR the key constraints are an adequate infrarenal neck (enough length below the renal arteries, an acceptable diameter, limited angulation, and not too much thrombus or calcium) and iliac arteries large and straight enough to deliver the device. Typical instructions for use ask for roughly 10 to 15 mm of neck length, a neck diameter up to about 32 mm and angulation under about 60 degrees, but each device has its own limits. When the neck is hostile, standard EVAR will not seal, which is the reason fenestrated and branched devices exist. Repair thresholds are on abdominal aortic aneurysm . How EVAR goes, in brief Access both groins. Surgical cutdown, or a percutaneous “pre-close” technique in which suture-mediated closure devices are deployed before the puncture is enlarged. Large sheaths go in over stiff wires and systemic heparin is given. Deploy the main body. Position the graft under fluoroscopy just below the lowest renal artery, confirming the level with an aortogram, and deploy so the fabric seals against the neck. Cannulate the contralateral gate and complete the seal. Catheterize the short stump of the bifurcated graft from the other groin, extend a limb into each iliac, preserving at least one internal iliac artery where possible, and balloon the seal zones and junctions. Completion angiogram. Confirm exclusion of the sac, and look specifically for an endoleak and for kinked or narrowed limbs. Close and check. Close the arteriotomies and check pulses or Doppler signals in both legs before the patient leaves the room. Afterward, watch the groins, both legs (a kinked or occluded graft limb presents as a cold leg or new claudication), the kidneys after contrast, and for post-implantation syndrome, an expected, self-limited fever and leukocytosis in the first days that still requires infection to be considered. Endoleaks are the late failure mode: types I and III are high-pressure leaks that are repaired, and type II, the most common, is usually watched unless the sac grows. The full classification is on EVAR and endoleaks . Red flag: surveillance is forever The trade for EVAR's lower early mortality is lifelong imaging surveillance with CT angiography or duplex, because the repair can fail late through an endoleak (persistent sac flow), graft migration or limb occlusion, and a re-pressurized sac can still rupture. SVS guidance suggests imaging about a month after repair, then at about a year and yearly after that if there is no endoleak or growth, with duplex replacing CT in many patients. A patient lost to follow-up is a patient at risk. TEVAR TEVAR treats descending thoracic aneurysms, complicated type B dissection (rupture, malperfusion, refractory pain or hypertension, or rapid expansion) and increasingly selected uncomplicated type B dissections with high-risk features, and blunt traumatic aortic injury, classically at the isthmus just distal to the left subclavian, where it has largely replaced open repair. In dissection the graft must go into the true lumen to cover the entry tear and re-expand it. Which arch branches the graft covers depends on its landing zone; see aortic landing zones . Red flag: spinal cord ischemia Covering a long length of thoracic aorta can occlude the intercostal arteries that feed the spinal cord, risking paraplegia. Risk rises with the extent of coverage, prior abdominal aortic repair, left subclavian coverage, internal iliac occlusion and hypotension. Prevention includes keeping the mean arterial pressure up (targets per protocol), cerebrospinal fluid drainage in high-risk patients, revascularizing the left subclavian, and staging extensive repairs. Any new leg weakness or numbness after TEVAR is an emergency: raise the blood pressure and call at once. See the spinal drain section and TEVAR and complex aortic . EVAR has lower early mortality than open repair. Why is that not the end of the argument? In the randomized trials (EVAR-1, DREAM, OVER) the early survival advantage of EVAR faded within a few years, and long-term follow-up showed more late reinterventions and, in EVAR-1, more late aneurysm-related deaths after EVAR. The repair can fail silently, so it commits the patient to lifelong surveillance. The choice weighs operative risk and anatomy against durability and the patient's ability to keep up with imaging. Other than spinal cord ischemia, what complications are specific to TEVAR? Stroke, from manipulating wires and devices in the arch or from covering the left subclavian; retrograde type A dissection, particularly when treating dissection; arm ischemia after left subclavian coverage; access injury from the large sheaths; and endoleak or graft migration on surveillance."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Aortic landing zones","u":"endovascular.html#aortic-zones","x":"Aortic landing zones Stent grafts are described by where they land. The zone tells you which branches the fabric covers, and therefore what has to be done to keep those branches perfused. The Ishimaru arch zones, 0 to 4 The Ishimaru map divides the arch and proximal descending aorta at the branch origins. The zone where the proximal end of the graft lands is its proximal seal zone, and every branch that arises from the aorta the fabric crosses is covered. Most thoracic devices need about 2 cm of healthy, parallel-walled aorta to seal, which is why lesions near the left subclavian so often push the landing zone up into the arch. Ishimaru zones and what landing there covers Zone Segment Covered by a graft landing here What it takes 0 Ascending aorta, up to and including the innominate artery origin Innominate, left common carotid and left subclavian Total arch debranching, a branched or fenestrated arch device, or open arch repair such as a frozen elephant trunk 1 Between the innominate and the left common carotid, including the left common carotid origin Left common carotid and left subclavian Carotid-carotid bypass (or similar), plus left subclavian revascularization 2 Between the left common carotid and the left subclavian, including the left subclavian origin Left subclavian Left subclavian revascularization when indicated (below) 3 Proximal descending thoracic aorta, about the first 2 cm beyond the left subclavian No arch branch Nothing for the arch branches; a short margin from the subclavian 4 Descending thoracic aorta beyond zone 3 No arch branch; intercostal arteries Spinal cord protection planning as coverage lengthens Covering the left subclavian Zone 2 landing is common, because many descending thoracic lesions start close to the left subclavian. Covering it without revascularization risks: arm ischemia, usually mild because of collaterals but sometimes disabling; posterior circulation stroke, especially with a dominant left vertebral artery; spinal cord ischemia, because the left vertebral and internal thoracic arteries feed the cord's collateral network; myocardial ischemia when a left internal mammary artery graft supplies the heart. SVS guidance suggests routine left subclavian revascularization before elective TEVAR that covers it, and considers it essential with a left internal mammary coronary graft, a dominant left or absent right vertebral artery, a functioning left arm dialysis access, or extensive coverage with high spinal cord risk; European guidance also favors revascularization in elective cases. In an emergency (rupture or trauma) the subclavian is often covered and revascularized later only if symptoms develop. The options are a carotid-subclavian bypass or subclavian-to-carotid transposition, or a branched stent graft with a side branch into the subclavian. When a bypass is done, the subclavian origin is plugged or covered so the sac cannot refill backward through it. A patient with a left internal mammary to LAD bypass graft needs a zone 2 TEVAR. What has to happen first? The left subclavian must be revascularized before it is covered, by carotid-subclavian bypass or transposition, or the repair done with a branched device. Covering it without revascularization would reverse or stop flow in the mammary graft and cause myocardial ischemia. The SVS/STS zones, 0 to 11 The 2020 SVS/STS reporting standards for aortic dissection extended the map down the whole aorta, so that a dissection's extent or a repair's seal zones can be stated in one shorthand. Zones 0 to 3 match the arch zones above. In this system a type A dissection has its entry tear in zone 0 and a type B dissection has its entry tear in zone 1 or beyond, written with the proximal and distal extent (for example, B3,10 for a type B dissection from zone 3 to the common iliacs). Fenestrated and branched repairs are described the same way: “sealing in zone 6” means above the superior mesenteric artery. SVS/STS aortic zones Zone Segment 0 Ascending aorta, to the innominate origin 1 Innominate to left common carotid 2 Left common carotid to left subclavian 3 First 2 cm of the descending thoracic aorta beyond the left subclavian 4 End of zone 3 to the mid-descending thoracic aorta (about T6) 5 Mid-descending thoracic aorta to the celiac artery 6 Celiac artery to the superior mesenteric artery 7 Superior mesenteric artery to the renal arteries 8 The renal artery segment 9 Infrarenal aorta to the bifurcation 10 Common iliac arteries 11 External iliac arteries Pearl: zones are a checklist on rounds The zone answers the practical question “what did the graft cover?” A zone 2 repair means check the left arm pulse and blood pressure and ask about posterior circulation symptoms. A long repair from zone 2 to zone 5 means think about the spinal cord. A seal in zone 6 or 7 means bridging stents into the visceral and renal arteries, so watch the kidneys, the lactate and the abdomen. Dissection itself is covered on aortic dissection ."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Fenestrated and branched","u":"endovascular.html#complex","x":"Complex aortic repair: FEVAR and BEVAR When the aneurysm reaches the visceral and renal arteries, a plain tube would cover their origins and infarct the kidneys and gut. Complex endovascular repair moves the seal zone up the aorta while keeping those branches open. Fenestrated grafts (FEVAR) have reinforced holes aligned to each branch origin, through which small covered bridging stents are placed into the renal and mesenteric arteries. They suit juxtarenal and pararenal aneurysms, where the graft body sits close to the aortic wall. Branched grafts (BEVAR) carry actual side branches that bridge across a wider aorta to each vessel, for more extensive thoracoabdominal disease. Many devices are custom-made from the patient's CTA, which takes weeks; off-the-shelf branched designs and, at some centers under institutional protocols, surgeon-modified grafts made on the back table fill the gap for urgent cases. These are long cases, done at experienced centers, often with fusion imaging and cone-beam CT. They cover more aorta, so the spinal cord risk rises with the extent of repair, and the bridging stents add their own failure modes: branch stenosis or occlusion, and leaks at the connections between the branch stents and the main graft. Surveillance is at least as demanding as after standard EVAR. Pearl: chimney and snorkel Because custom fenestrated devices take weeks to manufacture, an off-the-shelf workaround for urgent cases is the chimney (snorkel) technique, running parallel stents alongside the main aortic graft to keep a branch vessel perfused. It is ingenious but leaves potential channels (“gutters”) between the stents and the aortic wall through which a type I endoleak can occur, so it is a compromise rather than a first choice. Why can't standard EVAR treat a juxtarenal aneurysm? There is no adequate healthy infrarenal neck below the renal arteries for the graft to seal against, so a standard device either leaks at the top (type Ia endoleak) or would have to cover the renal arteries. The options are open repair, a fenestrated graft that moves the seal above the renals while keeping them perfused, or in selected urgent cases a parallel-graft (chimney) technique. See open AAA repair ."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Thrombolysis and thrombectomy","u":"endovascular.html#thrombolysis","x":"Clot management: thrombolysis and thrombectomy For acute clot, whether a clotted bypass, an acutely ischemic limb, an iliofemoral DVT or a pulmonary embolism, two endovascular strategies exist, and they are often combined. Catheter-directed thrombolysis. A multi-sidehole catheter is threaded into the clot and a fibrinolytic drug (such as tissue plasminogen activator) is infused directly into it over hours, dissolving the thrombus while sparing the patient a full systemic dose. It also tends to reveal the underlying lesion that caused the clot, which can then be treated. The patient is monitored in an ICU or step-down bed, with serial hemoglobin and fibrinogen checks, and comes back for a repeat angiogram (a “lysis check”) after several hours or the next morning. Mechanical and pharmacomechanical thrombectomy. Devices aspirate or macerate clot, either alone for speed or combined with a lytic. Aspiration thrombectomy is increasingly used for acute limb ischemia and for selected pulmonary emboli. The named devices are on thrombectomy devices . Where each fits Acute limb ischemia Rutherford category I and IIa limbs have time for catheter-directed lysis, which suits thrombosis of a native artery or a bypass graft and unmasks the culprit lesion. Category IIb limbs need revascularization now, by open embolectomy or rapid mechanical thrombectomy. Category III limbs are not salvageable, and restoring flow can be dangerous. See acute limb ischemia and embolectomy . Iliofemoral DVT Early thrombus removal is considered for selected patients with acute iliofemoral DVT (recent onset, low bleeding risk, good functional status) and urgently for phlegmasia. ATTRACT found no reduction in post-thrombotic syndrome overall, though iliofemoral patients had less moderate-to-severe disease; CAVA found no benefit, so selection matters. An underlying iliac vein compression is usually stented after clearance, sized by IVUS. See venous thromboembolism . Pulmonary embolism Catheter-directed lysis or aspiration is used for high-risk (massive) PE when systemic lysis is contraindicated or has failed, and for selected intermediate-high-risk PE that is deteriorating, usually through a multidisciplinary PE response team. Red flag: lytic contraindications Fibrinolytics carry a real bleeding risk, so catheter-directed lysis is avoided or used cautiously after recent surgery or trauma, with active or recent bleeding, and with a recent stroke or an intracranial process. A category IIb (immediately threatened) limb usually does not have time for a slow lytic infusion and goes for prompt mechanical clearance or surgery instead. After any revascularization of an ischemic limb, watch for reperfusion injury and compartment syndrome; see fasciotomy . Resident tip For every patient on a lytic infusion, know the drug and rate, the time of the next fibrinogen and lysis check, and the neuro and access-site check schedule. Ask nursing to avoid new arterial punctures and intramuscular injections. Any change in neurologic status means stop the infusion, call the senior, and get a head CT; bleeding at the access site means hold pressure and call. Confirm locally Lytic dosing, whether heparin runs alongside it, the fibrinogen level that prompts a dose reduction or stop, and whether lysis patients go to the CTV ICU or a step-down unit are set by local protocol."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Embolization and filters","u":"endovascular.html#embolization","x":"Embolization and IVC filters Sometimes the goal is to stop flow rather than restore it, either by deliberately occluding a vessel or by catching clot before it reaches the lungs. Embolization Embolization deliberately occludes a vessel using coils, vascular plugs, particles or liquid agents. Vascular surgeons use it to treat a type II endoleak, to control bleeding (trauma, gastrointestinal bleeding or a pseudoaneurysm), to occlude an internal iliac artery before some aortic work, and to treat arteriovenous malformations and pelvic venous reflux. Coils Metal spirals, often with thrombogenic fibers, pushed or detached at a precise spot for permanent occlusion of a named vessel. Vascular plugs Self-expanding nitinol mesh plugs that occlude a larger vessel with a single device, such as an internal iliac artery or the left subclavian origin. Particles Microspheres or polyvinyl alcohol particles that lodge in small distal vessels; used for tumors and diffuse small-vessel bleeding rather than named arteries. Gelatin sponge A temporary agent that recanalizes over weeks, useful in trauma. Liquid agents Glue (cyanoacrylate) and other liquid embolics that cast the vessel or the nidus of a malformation; used for some endoleaks, malformations and bleeding in difficult anatomy. Thrombin injected under ultrasound into a femoral pseudoaneurysm works on the same principle. Pearl: front door and back door Embolizing only the near side of a pseudoaneurysm, an aneurysmal branch or a bleeding vessel lets it refill from the far side through collaterals. Occlude beyond and before the defect, or cover it with a stent graft, so there is no back door. The same logic applies to an internal iliac artery before EVAR: it is occluded at its main trunk, proximal to its branches, so it cannot backfill the sac while the pelvic collaterals stay open. Occluding both internal iliacs risks buttock claudication, erectile dysfunction and colonic or spinal cord ischemia, which is why iliac branch devices are preferred when feasible. Inferior vena cava filters An IVC filter is a device placed in the inferior vena cava to catch emboli from the legs and pelvis. Its clear indication is acute proximal DVT or PE in a patient who cannot be anticoagulated, because of active bleeding or a complication that forces anticoagulation to stop. Recurrent PE despite truly therapeutic anticoagulation is a traditional but more debated indication; adherence, dosing and a change of agent are checked first. A filter does not treat the clot and does not replace anticoagulation once it becomes safe. Trials have not shown benefit from adding a filter to anticoagulation (PREPIC2) or from prophylactic filters in patients without VTE, such as after major trauma. Filters are placed from the femoral or jugular vein, usually infrarenally with the top just below the lowest renal vein; a suprarenal position is reserved for special situations such as thrombus reaching the renal veins. Red flag: retrieve the filter Most filters today are retrievable and are meant to come out once the patient can be anticoagulated, because a filter left indefinitely can migrate, perforate the cava, fracture, or thrombose. The FDA advises removal as soon as protection from PE is no longer needed. “Place it for a clear indication and plan its removal” is the expected attitude. Resident tip When a retrievable filter goes in, put the retrieval plan in the discharge summary and make sure a follow-up visit or tracking entry exists before the patient leaves. Filters are lost to follow-up constantly because everyone assumes someone else arranged it. Confirm locally Who places and retrieves filters, and whether the hospital keeps a filter registry or retrieval clinic, varies by institution. A patient on a heparin drip for a new proximal DVT and PE develops a major GI bleed. What happens next? Stop the heparin, resuscitate and treat the bleeding source. With acute proximal DVT and PE and anticoagulation now contraindicated, this is the clear indication for a retrievable IVC filter. Anticoagulation is restarted as soon as the bleeding risk allows, and the filter is removed once full anticoagulation is established and tolerated."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Closure and complications","u":"endovascular.html#closure-complications","x":"Getting out: closure and access complications High yield At the end of the case the arteriotomy has to be sealed, and most endovascular complications happen right here. Recognizing them quickly is squarely a trainee's job on rounds and overnight. Closing the hole Manual compression. Firm pressure over the arteriotomy, which in a retrograde puncture lies a centimeter or two above the skin nick, typically for 15 to 20 minutes for a small sheath once the heparin effect has fallen to the protocol threshold, and longer for larger sheaths. Still the standard for small sheaths and whenever a closure device is unsuitable. Closure devices. Suture-mediated systems, extravascular plugs (collagen or a sealant) and clips. They let the patient sit up and walk sooner, but they have not clearly reduced complications. They need a common femoral puncture above the bifurcation in a vessel large enough and not too calcified, which is why a sheath angiogram in an ipsilateral oblique view is taken before deployment. Large-bore closure. For EVAR and TEVAR sheaths, two suture devices are placed before the hole is enlarged (“pre-close”) and tied at the end; dedicated large-bore plug devices also exist. A failed pre-close becomes a cutdown and open repair. Radial. A compression band, inflated just enough to stop bleeding while keeping flow through the artery (patent hemostasis), then gradually deflated. The named closure devices are on closure devices . Access-site complications The access-site complications to know Complication How it shows up Typical management Hematoma Local swelling, firmness and bruising at the groin Pressure, mark the edges and observe if stable; check hemoglobin, and get a duplex if it grows or is pulsatile Retroperitoneal bleed Flank, back or lower abdominal pain, hypotension, tachycardia and a falling hemoglobin without an obvious groin hematoma; follows a high stick Resuscitate, hold and reverse anticoagulation, CT; most settle with resuscitation, but ongoing bleeding needs a covered stent or surgery Pseudoaneurysm Pulsatile, tender mass with a bruit; “to-and-fro” flow in the neck on duplex; follows a low stick Small ones often thrombose and can be watched with duplex; ultrasound-guided thrombin injection for most others (compression is an older alternative); surgery for rapid expansion, infection, skin compromise, nerve compression or failure Arteriovenous fistula Continuous bruit and thrill at the puncture (artery and vein both hit) Observation, since many close; repair if it persists, grows or causes symptoms Dissection or thrombosis A new cold, pulseless limb, or lost Doppler signals, after the case Urgent imaging and revascularization Distal embolization Acutely ischemic toes or foot (“trash foot”) Anticoagulation, with aspiration or lysis as needed Infection Redness, drainage or fever days to weeks later, especially after a closure device Antibiotics and imaging; an infected closure device or infected pseudoaneurysm usually needs surgical excision Radial artery occlusion Loss of the radial pulse, usually painless because the ulnar artery supplies the hand Prevented by heparin and patent hemostasis; usually observed A patient becomes hypotensive with back pain after a femoral procedure, but the groin looks unremarkable. What are you worried about? A retroperitoneal hemorrhage from a high arterial puncture above the inguinal ligament, where bleeding tracks into the retroperitoneum and is not visible externally. Resuscitate, send a hemoglobin, reverse anticoagulation, and get a CT, because this can be life-threatening and is easy to miss if you only inspect the skin. How do you tell a pseudoaneurysm from a simple hematoma at the bedside? A hematoma is a firm, non-pulsatile collection, although it can transmit the pulse of the artery beneath it. A pseudoaneurysm is expansile (it pushes your fingers apart, not just up), is often tender, and usually has a bruit. Duplex settles it: a pseudoaneurysm shows swirling flow in the sac and a to-and-fro signal in the neck connecting it to the artery. Resident tip For a bleeding or expanding groin, press firmly with your fingertips a little above the skin nick, where the arteriotomy actually is, rather than on the hole or with a sandbag, and call for help without letting go. Mark the edges of any hematoma with a pen so the next person can tell whether it is growing. The overnight approach is on the groin call ."},{"p":"Endovascular","t":"Before and after an angiogram","u":"endovascular.html#peri-procedure","x":"Before and after an angiogram Most of what a resident does for an endovascular patient happens outside the suite: getting them ready safely, then catching complications afterward. What follows is the common academic-center pattern; the specifics are set by local protocol and the operator. Before Indication and plan. Team Document the indication, the planned access and the intended treatment in the history and physical or pre-procedure note. Consent. Operator or delegate, per policy The procedure, its risks (bleeding and access-site injury, contrast reaction and kidney injury, vessel injury or embolization, radiation, and the possibility of further or open surgery), benefits and alternatives, plus the sedation or anesthesia plan. Baseline exam. You Both groins, and pulses or Doppler signals at every level in both legs, with the ABI if available. Mark the pedal signals with a pen so later checks compare the same spot. Labs. You Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, eGFR and potassium), CBC (hemoglobin and platelets), and PT/INR, with aPTT or anti-Xa if the patient is on heparin. Type and screen for larger cases such as EVAR and TEVAR, a pregnancy test when relevant, and glucose in diabetics. Antithrombotics. Per protocol and operator Aspirin and usually the P2Y12 inhibitor are continued for peripheral intervention. Warfarin, DOACs, low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin drips are held or continued according to the procedure's bleeding risk, the access size and the reason for anticoagulation. As a common pattern, apixaban or rivaroxaban is stopped one day before a low-bleeding-risk procedure and two days before a high-risk one, with longer holds for dabigatran when kidney function is reduced, and the acceptable INR is lower for large-bore access than for a small sheath. Contrast allergy. Per protocol For a prior allergic-like reaction, premedicate. The commonly taught elective regimen is prednisone 50 mg by mouth 13, 7 and 1 hour before, plus diphenhydramine 50 mg 1 hour before, with accelerated IV regimens for urgent cases. Kidneys. You Identify at-risk patients, hold nephrotoxins such as NSAIDs where possible, order IV isotonic fluid per protocol, and handle metformin per the contrast guidance above. Fasting and medications. Per sedation policy NPO follows the sedation or anesthesia policy. ASA guidance allows clear liquids until about 2 hours before and a light meal until 6 hours before, but many units still use NPO after midnight. Morning medications go with a sip of water, and insulin doses are reduced to avoid hypoglycemia while fasting. Lines and site. Nursing A working IV, the planned access site clean and intact, and no IV or blood pressure cuff on an arm planned for radial access or carrying a dialysis fistula. After Know the access details. Site and side, sheath size, closure method (manual, device or pre-close), whether a sheath was left in, the heparin given and last ACT, and the antithrombotic plan. These come from the procedure note or the operator. Access-site and distal checks. Vitals, the access site and the distal pulses or Doppler signals, commonly every 15 minutes for the first hour, every 30 minutes for the next, then hourly for several hours, per protocol. Bedrest. Its length depends on sheath size and closure method. As typical patterns: a few hours flat with the leg straight and the head of the bed low after manual compression of a small femoral sheath; often less after a closure device; longer, sometimes overnight, after large-bore access or if anticoagulation continues; and none after radial access, where the wrist band is weaned instead. The operator's order sets the time. Sheaths left in. A sheath left in place, for example during lysis, is pulled when the ACT or aPTT has fallen to the protocol threshold, followed by manual compression and a fresh bedrest clock. Hydration and kidneys. IV or oral fluids for at-risk patients as planned, and a repeat creatinine at 48 to 72 hours when the risk is high, which may mean after discharge. Medications. Restart home medications, start the post-procedure antiplatelet regimen, restart anticoagulation at the time the operator specifies, and restart metformin per the contrast plan. Before discharge. Walk the patient, then recheck the access site and pulses. Teach them to call for bleeding, new swelling, or a cold, numb or painful leg or hand. Handing a post-angiogram patient to nursing This is [name] , back from a [right SFA angioplasty and stent] through a [left common femoral, 6 French] sheath, closed with [a closure device / manual pressure] . Flat with the [left] leg straight until [time] . Please check the groin and the [right DP and PT Doppler signals, marked on the foot] per the post-procedure protocol. Call me for bleeding or a growing lump at the groin; new back, flank or belly pain; a drop in blood pressure or a rising heart rate; any change in the foot's signals, color, warmth, feeling or movement; low urine output; chest pain, shortness of breath or a rash; and, after any thoracic aortic repair, any new leg weakness or numbness. Call me for anything else that worries you. Resident tip Put the access side, sheath size, closure method and bedrest end time on your sign-out for every post-angiogram patient. When the night nurse calls about a groin, those four facts tell you how worried to be before you walk over. The post-procedure check and procedure note formats are on History, exam and charting . Confirm locally Who obtains consent, pre-procedure lab and INR thresholds, the anticoagulant holding schedule, the contrast premedication regimen, the NPO policy, post-procedure check frequency, bedrest times by sheath size and closure method, and whether patients recover in a procedural unit, on the floor or in the CTV ICU are all set locally."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Wires, catheters and devices","u":"devices.html","x":"The hand tools of endovascular surgery, by name, so that you can follow what is being asked for in the angio suite or the hybrid OR and hand over the right thing. The concepts behind them are in the endovascular playbook ; this page is the catalogue."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Sizes and fit","u":"devices.html#basics","x":"Sizes and fit Three measuring systems are used at once, and almost every \"will it fit?\" question comes down to reading them correctly. French (Fr) Diameter, where 1 Fr is about 0.33 mm, so 3 Fr is about 1 mm and 6 Fr about 2 mm. A catheter's French is its outer diameter. A sheath's French is its inner diameter, the largest device it accepts, so its outer diameter (and the hole in the artery) is about 1.5 to 2 Fr bigger. A guide catheter is sized by its outer diameter, so a 6 Fr guide passes roughly what a 5 Fr sheath does. Inches Wires and the lumens they pass through. The workhorse platform is 0.035 inch; 0.018 and 0.014 inch platforms are used below the knee, in the renal and visceral branches, and with low-profile devices. A device labelled \"0.035\" accepts a wire up to that size. Gauge (G) Needles, where a higher number is smaller. An 18 G needle takes a 0.035 or 0.038 wire; a 21 G micropuncture needle takes a 0.018 wire. Length (cm) Working length of wires, catheters, sheaths and devices. To exchange one catheter for another over a wire without losing position, the wire must be roughly twice as long as the catheter (a 260 or 300 cm \"exchange-length\" wire). Devices for the contralateral tibials need long shafts (often 150 cm or more). The compatibility chain The needle must accept the wire; the wire must pass through the catheter or device; the catheter or device must pass through the sheath's inner diameter; and the sheath's outer diameter determines the hole in the artery and whether the access vessel is big enough. Read every package label in that order. Over the wire or rapid exchange Over-the-wire devices carry the wire through their whole length: better pushability and the ability to swap wires, but they need a long wire. Rapid-exchange (monorail) devices let the wire exit the shaft a short distance from the tip: a short wire is enough and exchanges are fast, but pushability is less. A balloon label such as \"6 x 100 mm, 0.035, 135 cm, 6 Fr\" means a 6 mm diameter by 100 mm length, a 0.035 wire, a 135 cm shaft, and a 6 Fr sheath. Typical pairings (illustrative) Task Typical setup Ultrasound-guided femoral access 21 G micropuncture needle, 0.018 wire, 4 to 5 Fr transitional dilator, then a 0.035 wire and a short sheath Aortogram with runoff 4 to 5 Fr short sheath, 0.035 wire, 4 to 5 Fr flush catheter on a power injector Contralateral SFA intervention 6 Fr crossover sheath (about 45 to 55 cm) over a stiff 0.035 wire; 0.035 or 0.018 devices Tibial intervention 0.014 or 0.018 wire with a support catheter; small-diameter, long balloons on long shafts EVAR Large-bore sheaths sized to the device, over a super-stiff 0.035 wire, usually with suture-mediated preclose Resident tip When the attending asks for something, say it back with the size before you open it: \"a 6 by 100 Mustang, 135 length?\" Opened devices cannot be returned, and the read-back catches most mistakes. Write down every implant (lot sticker) for the brief op note."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Needles and micropuncture","u":"devices.html#needles","x":"Needles and micropuncture Access is the first step and the commonest source of complications, which is why most operators now start small and use ultrasound. Micropuncture kit For example Cook Micropuncture, Merit MAK 21 G needle 0.018 wire 4 to 5 Fr dilator A small needle and wire, then a coaxial transitional dilator whose inner cannula is removed to accept a 0.035 wire. A misplaced stick leaves a small hole that is easy to hold. When you'll hear it: \"Micropuncture and the ultrasound, please.\" 18 G access needle Generic (front-wall or Seldinger needle) 18 G 0.035 or 0.038 wire The traditional single-step needle that takes a standard wire directly. Larger hole if the stick is wrong, so less used where micropuncture is available. When you'll hear it: in emergencies and for large, easy targets. Ultrasound guidance for the femoral, radial, brachial and pedal arteries reduces high and low sticks, back-wall punctures and accidental venous entry. Confirm the needle enters the common femoral artery over the femoral head, then check the position on fluoroscopy before upsizing. The access anatomy is covered in Endovascular ."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Sheaths","u":"devices.html#sheaths","x":"Sheaths A sheath holds the puncture open, has a one-way hemostatic valve and a side port for flushing or pressure, and lets you exchange catheters and devices without losing access or bleeding. Short introducer sheath For example Terumo Pinnacle, Cordis Avanti+ 4 to 8 Fr about 10 to 11 cm The standard first sheath for diagnostic work and ipsilateral iliac or femoral intervention. When you'll hear it: \"Put in a 5 French short sheath.\" Long crossover sheath For example Terumo Destination, Cook Flexor Ansel 5 to 8 Fr 45 to 90 cm curved tip Goes up and over the aortic bifurcation to sit in the opposite iliac or femoral artery, giving support and a short path for contralateral work. Placed over a stiff wire. When you'll hear it: \"Let's get a 6 French Destination up and over.\" Straight long sheath For example Cook Flexor Raabe 5 to 8 Fr straight A long, straight sheath for antegrade access, renal or visceral work, or when extra support is needed without a crossover curve. When you'll hear it: during antegrade femoral or visceral interventions. Radial sheath For example Terumo Glidesheath Slender thin wall hydrophilic A thin-walled sheath that gives a larger inner diameter for a smaller outer diameter, reducing radial spasm and occlusion. Usually paired with an antispasmodic and heparin. When you'll hear it: radial access for visceral or some peripheral cases. Large-bore sheath For example Gore DrySeal Flex, Cook Check-Flo Performer 12 to 26 Fr valved Delivers EVAR and TEVAR components and other big devices. The access vessels must be large enough, and the arteriotomy is closed with preclose sutures or a surgical repair. When you'll hear it: on every aortic stent graft case. Hemostatic valve adapter Generic (Tuohy-Borst, Y-adapter) on guides and catheters A screw-down valve with a side arm that lets a wire or device pass through a guide catheter while you flush or inject. When you'll hear it: \"Tighten the Tuohy,\" when contrast or blood is leaking back."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Guidewires","u":"devices.html#wires","x":"Guidewires The wire is the rail everything else rides on. Wires differ in diameter, length, stiffness, tip shape and coating, and each case usually moves from a soft crossing wire to a stiffer delivery wire. Wires by job Job What you want Examples Getting in and up Soft, atraumatic tip that will not dissect J-tip starter wire, Bentson Crossing Slippery and steerable Glidewire (angled or straight), Glidewire Advantage, 0.018 and 0.014 crossing wires Support and delivery A stiff body with a soft tip Rosen, Amplatz, Supra Core, Lunderquist (aortic) Small vessels Low profile, torque control, graded tip load 0.018 and 0.014 platforms 0.035 inch wires J-tip starter wire Generic, comes in most sheath kits 0.035 about 150 cm 3 mm J The curved tip deflects off plaque and branch origins rather than digging in, so it is a safe first wire into the iliacs. When you'll hear it: \"Run the J up into the aorta.\" Bentson For example Cook 0.035 145 to 180 cm long floppy tip A straight wire with a long, very soft distal segment that buckles rather than perforates. Used to go up through diseased iliacs and through existing stents. When you'll hear it: \"Give me a Bentson.\" Glidewire Terumo 0.035 (also 0.018, 0.038) 150 to 260 cm angled or straight hydrophilic Slippery when wet, steerable with a torque device, and the go-to for crossing tight or tortuous lesions and selecting branches. Stiff versions exist. Too slippery to hold position for bulky devices, so it is usually exchanged. When you'll hear it: \"Angled Glide and a torque device.\" Keep it wet. Glidewire Advantage Terumo 0.035 and 0.018 hydrophilic tip supportive body A hydrophilic distal segment on a stiffer, coated body, so it crosses like a Glidewire but gives more support for delivering balloons and stents. When you'll hear it: \"Let's cross with an Advantage.\" Rosen For example Cook 0.035 145 to 260 cm tight 1.5 mm J moderate stiffness A workhorse support wire with a small J tip that sits safely in branch vessels. Common for delivering iliac and renal stents and crossover sheaths. When you'll hear it: \"Exchange for a Rosen.\" Amplatz For example Boston Scientific Amplatz Super Stiff; Cook Amplatz Extra-Stiff 0.035 stiff body short soft tip Stiff enough to straighten tortuous iliacs and track large sheaths and devices. The short floppy tip means the tip position must be watched. When you'll hear it: \"Get me an Amplatz for the sheath.\" Lunderquist Cook 0.035 extra-stiff curved soft tip 260 cm and longer The very stiff rail for EVAR and TEVAR delivery. It is placed through a catheter, and its tip is kept in view, because it can injure the arch, the aortic valve or the ventricle if pushed. When you'll hear it: \"Lunderquist up to the arch.\" Supra Core Abbott 0.035 supportive flexible tip A peripheral support wire, stiffer than a Rosen and softer than an Amplatz, used for delivering devices across tortuous segments. When you'll hear it: during iliac and femoral interventions needing support. Steerable 0.035 wires For example Medtronic Wholey, Boston Scientific Magic Torque 0.035 shapeable tip torque response Non-hydrophilic wires with good torque control, for steering into a branch while giving moderate support. When you'll hear it: \"Try a Wholey.\" 0.018 and 0.014 inch wires Used for tibial and pedal vessels, renal and visceral branches, and low-profile devices. They come in families graded by tip load (grams of force to buckle the tip): low tip loads for tracking, high tip loads for penetrating chronic total occlusions. 0.018 workhorse For example Boston Scientific V-18 ControlWire, Abbott Command 18 0.018 shapeable tip supportive body Common for femoropopliteal and tibial work with 0.018 balloons and stents. When you'll hear it: \"Go down with a V-18.\" 0.014 workhorse For example Abbott Command 14 0.014 shapeable tip For tibial and pedal vessels and small-vessel devices. Often used through a support catheter. When you'll hear it: below the knee and in pedal loops. CTO wires For example Cook Approach CTO; Asahi peripheral wires such as Astato 0.014 and 0.018 graded tip loads Stiffer-tipped wires for penetrating the cap of a chronic total occlusion, escalated stepwise from lower to higher tip loads. When you'll hear it: \"Escalate to the 12 gram.\" Red flag: wire etiquette Never let go of the wire, and always know where its tip is. Wipe wires with wet gauze and keep hydrophilic wires wet. Never pull a hydrophilic wire back through a metal needle, because the needle can shear off the coating or the tip. When a wire is loose on the table, coil it and keep it in the bowl so it does not slide off the field."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Catheters","u":"devices.html#catheters","x":"Catheters Catheters either deliver a large bolus of contrast (flush catheters) or hook into a particular branch (selective catheters). Each shape exists for a geometry. Flush catheters Pigtail Generic; similar designs include Cook Universal Flush 4 to 5 Fr coiled tip side holes Sits in a large vessel and delivers contrast through many side holes without whipping or injuring the wall. Used for aortograms on a power injector. When you'll hear it: \"Pigtail at L1 for the aortogram.\" Omni Flush AngioDynamics 4 to 5 Fr reverse curve side holes A flush catheter whose curved tip also hooks the opposite common iliac, so the same catheter does the aortogram and then helps walk a wire up and over the bifurcation. When you'll hear it: \"Go up and over with the Omni.\" Selective catheters Which shape for which job Shape Geometry Typical use Kumpe (KMP) Short, gentle angle Crossing iliac and femoral lesions; general peripheral selection Berenstein Short angled tip Gentle selection, steering a wire through a lesion Multipurpose (MPA) Mild distal angle General selection, downgoing vessels Vertebral (VERT) Slight hockey-stick angle Arch vessels, peripheral selection Headhunter (H1) Angled for the arch Innominate and carotid origins Cobra (C1, C2) Double curve, downgoing Renal, celiac, SMA, contralateral iliac Simmons (Sidewinder) 1 to 3 Reverse curve that must be formed in the aorta Difficult arch vessels, sharply angled or upgoing origins, crossing a narrow bifurcation SOS Omni Small reverse curve that forms itself Renal and mesenteric origins, crossing the bifurcation Most shapes come in plain and hydrophilic-coated versions (Terumo's Glidecath line, for example), and in 4 and 5 Fr. Long selective catheters (100 cm or more) are used to reach the opposite leg. Support and microcatheters Support (crossing) catheters For example Cook CXI, Philips Quick-Cross, Terumo NaviCross, Medtronic TrailBlazer, Boston Scientific Rubicon 0.014 to 0.035 tapered tip braided shaft Low-profile catheters that run right up to the wire tip to give it backup for pushing through an occlusion, and that allow a wire exchange beyond a lesion without losing position. When you'll hear it: \"Bring the CXI up behind the wire.\" Microcatheters For example Terumo Progreat, Boston Scientific Renegade about 2.4 to 2.8 Fr through a 4 to 5 Fr catheter Run coaxially through a selective catheter into small branches, mainly to deliver coils, plugs or particles for embolization. When you'll hear it: during type II endoleak and bleeding embolizations. You are in the right common femoral artery and need to treat the left SFA. What do you need to get there? A flush catheter with a curve (such as an Omni Flush) or a reverse-curve catheter (SOS Omni or Simmons) to hook the left common iliac at the bifurcation, a hydrophilic wire to go up and over, then an exchange for a stiffer wire to carry a long crossover sheath into the left iliac or femoral artery."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Balloons","u":"devices.html#balloons","x":"Balloons Angioplasty balloons are tracked over the wire, positioned across the lesion and inflated with an inflation device (an indeflator) that reads pressure in atmospheres. Compliance Non-compliant balloons hold their size at high pressure (for hard, calcified lesions). Semi-compliant balloons grow a little with pressure (the usual angioplasty balloon). Compliant balloons stretch like latex to fill whatever they are in, and are used to occlude the aorta or mold a stent graft, never for angioplasty. Nominal pressure The pressure at which the balloon reaches its labelled diameter. Rated burst pressure The highest pressure the manufacturer supports. Going past it risks rupture of the balloon and the vessel. Sizing Usually about 1:1 with the normal reference vessel diameter, over a length that covers the lesion. Plain balloons (POBA) For example Boston Scientific Mustang (0.035) and Sterling (0.018); Abbott Armada 35 and 18; BD Ultraverse 0.035 or 0.018 3 to 12 mm 20 to 200+ mm Plain old balloon angioplasty, for iliac, femoral and popliteal disease and for vessel preparation before stents or drug-coated balloons. When you'll hear it: \"Open a 5 by 100 Sterling.\" Small-vessel balloons For example Boston Scientific Coyote 0.014 1.5 to 4 mm long lengths Low-profile, long balloons for tibial and pedal arteries, often on 150 cm shafts. When you'll hear it: below the knee in CLTI. Drug-coated balloons For example Medtronic IN.PACT Admiral, BD Lutonix, Boston Scientific Ranger paclitaxel femoropopliteal Transfer an antiproliferative drug to the vessel wall to reduce restenosis, mainly in the femoropopliteal segment, after the lesion has been prepared with a plain balloon. A 2018 meta-analysis raised a late mortality signal for paclitaxel devices; after further data, the FDA concluded in 2023 that the evidence does not support excess mortality. When you'll hear it: \"Prep with a plain balloon, then the DCB.\" Cutting and scoring balloons For example Boston Scientific cutting balloon; Philips AngioSculpt blades or nitinol scoring elements Score the plaque in a controlled way so it dilates at lower pressure with less dissection: resistant, fibrotic or recurrent lesions, including vein graft and dialysis access stenoses. When you'll hear it: on a stubborn waist that will not open. Intravascular lithotripsy Shockwave (for example M5+ and S4) sonic pressure waves low-pressure balloon Emitters inside a balloon deliver pulses that crack deep and superficial calcium, so a heavily calcified vessel can then be dilated. Also used to prepare calcified iliacs for large-bore sheaths. When you'll hear it: \"This is too calcified; get the Shockwave.\" Compliant aortic balloons For example Cook Coda, Medtronic Reliant; ER-REBOA (Prytime Medical) compliant large diameter Mold stent graft seal zones and junctions after deployment, or occlude the aorta for control of a rupture (REBOA or an occlusion balloon through a sheath). When you'll hear it: \"Molding balloon to the neck and the overlaps.\""},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Stents","u":"devices.html#stents","x":"Stents Self-expanding nitinol stents for segments that bend and get compressed; balloon-expandable stents for precise placement and radial strength; covered stents to seal. The comparison table is in Endovascular . Self-expanding nitinol For example Abbott Absolute Pro, BD LifeStent, Boston Scientific Innova 0.035 5 to 10 mm long lengths Spring open as the sheath is pulled back and recover their shape after being squashed, which suits the SFA and iliacs. Can foreshorten or jump on release, so they are deployed slowly. When you'll hear it: \"6 by 120 self-expander in the SFA.\" Interwoven nitinol Abbott Supera braided wire high crush resistance A woven stent with high radial strength and flexibility, used in the distal SFA and popliteal artery. It needs good vessel preparation and careful deployment so it is neither stretched nor compressed. When you'll hear it: across the knee joint or in heavy calcium. Drug-eluting peripheral For example Cook Zilver PTX, Boston Scientific Eluvia paclitaxel femoropopliteal Self-expanding stents that release paclitaxel to reduce restenosis in the femoropopliteal segment. When you'll hear it: for longer or recurrent SFA disease. Balloon-expandable For example Boston Scientific Express LD, Abbott Omnilink Elite 0.035 precise high radial force Mounted on a balloon and expanded to size, landing exactly where placed. Used at ostia (common iliac origins, renal arteries) where precision and strength matter, not in segments that flex. When you'll hear it: \"Kissing balloon-expandable stents at the bifurcation.\" Covered stents Gore Viabahn (self-expanding); Gore Viabahn VBX (balloon-expandable); Getinge iCast (Advanta V12 outside the US); Bentley BeGraft ePTFE or PTFE cover Stents lined with graft material to exclude an aneurysm or pseudoaneurysm, seal a rupture or perforation, treat in-stent restenosis, and bridge to branch vessels in fenestrated and branched repairs. When you'll hear it: \"Get a covered stent ready\" when a vessel ruptures during angioplasty. Carotid stents and TCAR TCAR: ENROUTE system (Silk Road Medical, now Boston Scientific). Transfemoral: for example Abbott Xact, Cordis Precise self-expanding embolic protection TCAR uses direct carotid access with flow reversal through a femoral venous sheath for embolic protection. Transfemoral stenting uses a distal filter. Dual antiplatelet therapy is needed after any carotid stent. When you'll hear it: \"Flow reversal on,\" during TCAR. Embolic protection filters For example Medtronic SpiderFX, Abbott Emboshield NAV6, Boston Scientific FilterWire EZ 0.014 or 0.018 filter basket A basket on a wire placed beyond the lesion to catch debris during carotid stenting, atherectomy or some thrombectomies, then captured and removed. When you'll hear it: before atherectomy in a single-vessel runoff. Venous stents For example BD Venovo, Boston Scientific Vici, Medtronic Abre, Cook Zilver Vena large diameter self-expanding Dedicated stents for iliofemoral venous obstruction such as May-Thurner, sized with IVUS. When you'll hear it: after thrombus removal for left iliac vein compression."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Aortic stent grafts","u":"devices.html#stent-grafts","x":"Aortic stent grafts EVAR and TEVAR devices are modular: a main body, then limbs and extensions, assembled inside the patient through large-bore sheaths. Main body The proximal part that seals in the aortic neck below the renal arteries, with a short \"contralateral gate\" to be cannulated from the other groin. Limbs and extensions Iliac limbs complete the bifurcated graft; extensions lengthen a limb, and proximal cuffs extend the seal upward. Fixation Suprarenal fixation uses a bare stent with barbs above the renal arteries (for example Endurant and Zenith); infrarenal fixation anchors below them (for example Excluder). Oversizing Grafts are oversized relative to the neck, typically about 10 to 20 percent for aneurysms, so the fabric seals against the wall. Less oversizing is used in dissection. EVAR grafts Gore Excluder and Excluder Conformable; Medtronic Endurant II and IIs; Cook Zenith Flex; Endologix AFX2 and Alto; Terumo Aortic TREO bifurcated modular The standard infrarenal devices. AFX2 is a unibody graft that sits on the native aortic bifurcation; Alto seals with a polymer-filled ring for short necks; Excluder Conformable can be angled to fit angulated necks. When you'll hear it: named by brand at the planning conference, sized from the CTA. Iliac branch device For example Gore Excluder Iliac Branch Endoprosthesis internal iliac preservation Extends the repair into the common iliac while keeping the internal iliac perfused, avoiding buttock claudication and pelvic ischemia from coiling it. When you'll hear it: with common iliac aneurysms. Fenestrated and branched For example Cook Zenith Fenestrated; Gore Excluder Thoracoabdominal Branch Endoprosthesis (FDA approved 2024) custom or off-the-shelf bridging stents Holes (fenestrations) or side branches line up with the renal and mesenteric arteries, which are then connected with covered bridging stents. Physician-modified grafts are a further option at some centers. When you'll hear it: for juxtarenal and thoracoabdominal aneurysms. TEVAR grafts Gore TAG and Conformable TAG; Medtronic Valiant Navion; Cook Zenith Alpha Thoracic; Terumo Aortic RelayPro. Branch: Gore TAG Thoracic Branch Endoprosthesis thoracic tube grafts Tube grafts for the descending thoracic aorta: aneurysms, complicated type B dissections, traumatic transections and penetrating ulcers. The thoracic branch device preserves the left subclavian when landing in zone 2. When you'll hear it: with landing zone and left subclavian planning. Pearl Every aortic device has instructions for use with minimum neck length, diameter range, angulation limits and access vessel size. \"Is this on-label?\" is a fair question at the planning conference, and the answer comes from the CTA measurements, not the device brochure."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Thrombectomy and lysis","u":"devices.html#thrombectomy","x":"Thrombectomy and lysis Tools for removing clot from arteries, grafts, veins and the pulmonary arteries, used alone or combined. Fogarty embolectomy catheter Edwards Lifesciences 2 to 7 Fr balloon tip open or over-the-wire Passed beyond the clot through an arteriotomy, inflated gently and withdrawn to drag clot out. Typical sizes are 2 to 3 Fr for tibial vessels, 3 to 4 Fr for the popliteal, 4 to 5 Fr for the femoral and SFA, and 5 to 6 Fr for the iliac; the full table is in Open operations . Inflate with saline by feel, not to a set pressure, to avoid injuring the intima. When you'll hear it: \"A 4 Fogarty down the popliteal.\" Aspiration thrombectomy For example Penumbra Indigo system with Lightning continuous suction several catheter sizes Large-lumen catheters connected to continuous aspiration, increasingly used for acute limb ischemia and pulmonary embolism. Watch the blood loss in the canister. When you'll hear it: \"Let's aspirate first.\" Rheolytic thrombectomy Boston Scientific AngioJet saline jets can deliver lytic High-speed saline jets fragment and aspirate clot, and can \"power-pulse\" a lytic into it. It causes hemolysis (red urine) and can cause bradycardia, so run times are limited and hydration matters. When you'll hear it: with a dark red urine bag afterwards. Mechanical venous and PE thrombectomy Inari (now Stryker) ClotTriever and FlowTriever large bore no lytic needed Coring and aspiration devices that remove large volumes of clot from iliofemoral veins (ClotTriever) and the pulmonary arteries (FlowTriever) without thrombolytics. When you'll hear it: for extensive DVT or intermediate- to high-risk PE. Lysis infusion catheters For example Medtronic Cragg-McNamara, AngioDynamics Unifuse; ultrasound-assisted: Boston Scientific EKOS multi-sidehole infusion length options Placed through the clot to infuse a low dose of thrombolytic over hours, usually in the ICU, with neuro checks, access-site checks and fibrinogen and hemoglobin monitoring per protocol. When you'll hear it: \"Leave a lysis catheter overnight and re-look in the morning.\""},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Atherectomy","u":"devices.html#atherectomy","x":"Atherectomy Devices that remove or modify plaque rather than just displacing it, used mainly in heavily calcified or bulky lesions and as vessel preparation. Directional Medtronic HawkOne cuts and collects plaque A rotating blade shaves plaque into a collection chamber in one direction at a time, steered toward the bulk of the disease. Orbital Diamondback 360 (Cardiovascular Systems, now Abbott) eccentric diamond crown An orbiting crown sands calcium, widening its orbit with speed. Used for calcified femoropopliteal and tibial disease. Rotational with aspiration Boston Scientific Jetstream rotating tip active aspiration A front-cutting rotating tip with aspiration, which removes debris and some thrombus. Laser For example Philips Turbo-Elite, AngioDynamics Auryon photoablation Laser energy vaporizes plaque and thrombus, and is used for in-stent restenosis and some occlusions. Image-guided directional Avinger Pantheris optical coherence tomography A directional device with an imaging core that shows the vessel layers in real time while cutting. Red flag: distal embolization Atherectomy liberates debris. A foot that is worse after the case, or a lost runoff vessel on the completion angiogram, may be embolization; many operators use a filter in limited runoff. Compare the distal exam with the pre-procedure baseline."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Re-entry devices","u":"devices.html#reentry","x":"Re-entry devices When a wire crosses an occlusion in the subintimal plane and cannot get back into the true lumen beyond it, a re-entry device aims a needle or wire back in. Fluoroscopically oriented For example Cordis Outback Elite, Medtronic Enteer, Boston Scientific OffRoad needle or balloon based Oriented with markers on orthogonal fluoroscopic views, then a needle or wire is directed into the true lumen beyond the occlusion. When you'll hear it: \"We're subintimal and can't re-enter; open an Outback.\" IVUS-guided Philips Pioneer Plus built-in IVUS An intravascular ultrasound transducer shows the true lumen directly, so the needle is aimed under imaging rather than by fluoroscopic projection. The alternative strategies, including retrograde pedal or tibial access, are described under planning in Endovascular ."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Embolization and filters","u":"devices.html#embolization-tools","x":"Embolization tools and IVC filters Sometimes the goal is to stop flow: a type II endoleak, a bleeding vessel, an internal iliac before an aortic repair, or a visceral aneurysm. Coils Pushable: for example Cook Nester and Tornado. Detachable: for example Boston Scientific Interlock, Medtronic Concerto, Penumbra Ruby platinum or steel with fibers Pushable coils are cheaper and pushed out of the catheter; detachable coils can be positioned, withdrawn if wrong, and released precisely. Occlusion depends partly on the patient's ability to clot. Vascular plugs For example Abbott Amplatzer Vascular Plug II and IV; Medtronic MVP nitinol mesh single device A self-expanding mesh plug that occludes a larger vessel with one device and precise placement, such as an internal iliac artery. Liquid agents n-butyl cyanoacrylate glue; ethylene vinyl alcohol (Medtronic Onyx) through microcatheters Fill a space or a network of vessels and do not depend on the patient's clotting, which helps in coagulopathic bleeding. Need experience to control. Gelfoam and particles Gelatin sponge; particles such as PVA and microspheres temporary or distal Gelfoam gives temporary occlusion (trauma bleeding); particles occlude small distal vessels (tumors, some bleeding). IVC filters Retrievable: for example BD Denali, Argon Option Elite, Cook Celect and Günther Tulip. Permanent: Greenfield, B. Braun VenaTech infrarenal jugular or femoral delivery Catch emboli in a patient with VTE who cannot be anticoagulated. Retrievable filters should come out once anticoagulation is possible; long dwell times bring migration, perforation, fracture and caval thrombosis. When you'll hear it: \"Who owns the retrieval?\" Put it in the discharge summary."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"Closure devices","u":"devices.html#closure","x":"Closure devices At the end of the case the arteriotomy is closed with a device or held with manual pressure. Device closure lets patients sit up and walk sooner but does not lower the overall complication rate in every setting. Suture-mediated Abbott Perclose ProGlide and ProStyle suture closure preclose for large bore Deploys a pre-tied suture through the arterial wall around the arteriotomy. For large-bore sheaths, two devices are usually placed before the sheath goes in (the \"preclose\" technique) and tied at the end. When you'll hear it: \"Two ProGlides at ten and two.\" Collagen anchor Terumo Angio-Seal intra-arterial anchor collagen plug A small absorbable anchor inside the artery and a collagen plug outside sandwich the hole. It absorbs over weeks to months, which limits early re-puncture at the same spot (see the instructions for use). When you'll hear it: after diagnostic and small-sheath interventions. Extravascular sealant Cordis Mynx polyethylene glycol sealant A sealant deployed on the outside of the artery while a small balloon temporarily occludes the hole from inside, leaving nothing in the lumen. Clip Abbott StarClose SE nitinol clip extravascular A small clip grips the outside of the arteriotomy and closes it. Large-bore plug Teleflex MANTA large-bore arteriotomies A collagen-based device designed for large-bore holes after aortic and structural heart procedures. Radial compression band Terumo TR Band inflatable band Compresses the radial puncture with just enough pressure for hemostasis while keeping the artery patent (patent hemostasis), deflated stepwise per protocol. When you'll hear it: on the nursing handoff after radial cases. Manual compression Still the standard for many small sheaths. Pull the sheath when anticoagulation allows (an ACT threshold per protocol), then press firmly over the arteriotomy, which lies just above the skin puncture, for about 15 to 20 minutes, longer for bigger sheaths or anticoagulated patients. Check the distal pulses afterwards. Closure devices are generally avoided when the puncture is at or below the bifurcation, in a small or heavily calcified artery, or in an infected groin. A patient has a hematoma and a falling hemoglobin four hours after a closure device was used in the groin. What do you check and who do you call? Hold firm pressure over the arteriotomy, mark the hematoma edge, check the distal pulses and vitals, send a hemoglobin, and call the senior. Think about a retroperitoneal bleed if the puncture may have been high (hypotension and flank pain with a modest groin), a failed device, and a pseudoaneurysm. The playbook is on On call ."},{"p":"Wires, catheters and devices","t":"IVUS, CO2 and injectors","u":"devices.html#imaging-tools","x":"IVUS, CO 2 and other adjuncts Tools that help you see: inside the vessel, without iodine, or with a precisely timed contrast bolus. IVUS catheters For example Philips Visions PV; Boston Scientific OptiCross 0.014 to 0.035 compatible An ultrasound transducer on a catheter gives cross-sectional images from inside the vessel: true lumen versus false lumen, vessel diameter for sizing, and stent apposition. Indispensable in venous stenting and helpful in dissection and contrast-sparing work. CO 2 delivery system Dedicated closed delivery systems below the diaphragm only A closed system delivers medical CO 2 as a contrast agent without room-air contamination, for patients with renal impairment or contrast allergy. Not used above the diaphragm or in the arm arteries. Power injector Generic rate (mL/s) volume pressure limit (psi) Delivers a set volume at a set rate for aortograms and runoff studies. The operator sets the rate, total volume and pressure limit for the catheter in use; check the catheter's maximum pressure rating. When you'll hear it: \"15 for 30,\" meaning 15 mL per second for a total of 30 mL. Resident tip Keep a running total of the contrast volume and the fluoroscopy time and dose during the case, and put them in the brief op note. They matter for the creatinine check the next morning and for radiation follow-up."},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Questions you'll be asked","u":"questions.html","x":"The classic teaching questions from rounds, the operating room and the ICU, each with the answer an attending wants to hear. Try to answer every one out loud before you reveal it."},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"How to use this page","u":"questions.html#how-to","x":"How to use this page These are the questions a good teacher uses to find the edge of what you know. None of it is meant to intimidate. Rehearse the answers out loud and you will field them with composure, on rounds or across the table. Answer out loud first. Say the answer in full sentences, the way you would on rounds, before you open the question. Recognizing an answer is not the same as producing one. Reveal and compare. Check whether you hit the point the attending is listening for, not just the fact. Use \"Show all answers\" at the top of a section when you want to read straight through. Re-read the linked chapter when you miss one. Many answers link to the chapter that explains the reasoning. A fact sticks once you know why it is true. Pearl: when you do not know \"I am not certain, but here is how I would think about it\" is a strong answer. Reasoning out loud from first principles beats a confident guess every time, on a service where guessing can harm a patient. Resident tip The night before a case, read the operation on Open operations or Endovascular , then do the anatomy questions below. Questions at the table come from the structures in the wound, and five minutes of rehearsal the night before is worth an hour of reading afterwards."},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Anatomy","u":"questions.html#anatomy-q","x":"Anatomy The questions you will most often be asked while scrubbed, because the anatomy is right in front of both of you. The full treatment is in Foundations . Show all answers Hide all Name the branches of the abdominal aorta from top to bottom, with their vertebral levels. The celiac trunk at about T12, the superior mesenteric artery at L1, the paired renal arteries at L1 to L2, and the inferior mesenteric artery at L3, then the bifurcation into the common iliac arteries at about L4. The smaller branches that complete the list are the inferior phrenic, middle adrenal, gonadal and lumbar arteries and the median sacral artery. Trace the arterial supply from the aorta to the dorsalis pedis. Aorta, common iliac, external iliac, then common femoral as it passes under the inguinal ligament, superficial femoral through the adductor canal, popliteal, then the anterior tibial, which becomes the dorsalis pedis on the dorsum of the foot. What are the three tibial vessels, and which two feed the pedal arch? Anterior tibial, posterior tibial and peroneal. The dorsalis pedis (the continuation of the anterior tibial) and the posterior tibial, through its plantar branches, form the pedal arch. The peroneal ends above the ankle, but its perforating and communicating branches can reconstitute either vessel. It is often the last tibial vessel spared in a diabetic leg and remains a usable bypass target. What is the order of the aortic arch branches? From right to left: the brachiocephalic (innominate) trunk, the left common carotid and the left subclavian. The brachiocephalic divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian. The most common variant is a shared origin of the brachiocephalic and left common carotid, loosely called a bovine arch, which matters when you catheterize the arch or plan a TEVAR landing zone. Which nerve travels in the adductor (Hunter's) canal with the femoral vessels? The saphenous nerve, along with the nerve to vastus medialis. The superficial femoral artery becomes the popliteal as it leaves the distal end of the canal through the adductor hiatus. Below the knee the saphenous nerve runs with the great saphenous vein, which is why vein harvest or ablation in the calf can leave numbness along the medial leg. What is the main collateral around an occluded superficial femoral artery? The profunda femoris (deep femoral artery). Its perforating branches, and the descending branch of the lateral circumflex femoral artery, link with the genicular arteries around the knee to reconstitute the popliteal. A healthy profunda can keep a limb viable despite an occluded SFA, which is why it is protected and, when diseased, repaired with a profundaplasty. Which nerves are at risk in a carotid endarterectomy, and how does each injury show? Hypoglossal. The tongue deviates toward the side of the injury, with dysarthria and trouble chewing and swallowing. Vagus or recurrent laryngeal. Ipsilateral vocal cord paralysis, with hoarseness and a weak cough. The vagus usually lies posteriorly between the carotid and the jugular and can be caught in a clamp. Marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve. A drooping corner of the mouth, usually from retraction near the angle of the jaw. Superior laryngeal. Voice fatigue and loss of high pitch. Glossopharyngeal. Dysphagia and aspiration, mainly with a high exposure. Greater auricular. Numbness of the earlobe, from a high incision. Bilateral recurrent laryngeal or hypoglossal injury can obstruct the airway, so cord and tongue function are checked before a staged operation on the other side. See Open operations . Name the four compartments of the lower leg and their contents. Anterior: the extensors, the deep peroneal nerve and the anterior tibial vessels. Lateral: the peroneus muscles and the superficial peroneal nerve. Superficial posterior: gastrocnemius, soleus and plantaris. Deep posterior: the deep flexors, the posterior tibial and peroneal vessels and the tibial nerve. The anterior compartment is the one most often affected after revascularization, and numbness in the first web space (the deep peroneal nerve) is an early sign. A two-incision fasciotomy releases all four; see Open operations . Where does the great saphenous vein join the deep system, and why does it matter? At the saphenofemoral junction, where it passes through the saphenous opening (fossa ovalis) into the common femoral vein, a few centimeters below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. It matters because the great saphenous vein is the preferred conduit for both peripheral and coronary bypass, so it is mapped and protected. Before anyone ablates or strips it, someone should ask whether the patient may need it for a bypass later. The junction is also the landmark that decides how a superficial thrombophlebitis is treated (see the venous questions ). What is the arc of Riolan, and why does it matter during aortic surgery? A collateral (the meandering mesenteric artery) between the middle colic branch of the SMA and the left colic branch of the IMA, running close to the root of the mesentery. With the marginal artery of Drummond it connects the SMA and IMA territories. A large meandering artery on imaging means one territory is feeding the other because of occlusive disease. If the SMA is occluded and the bowel depends on a patent IMA, ligating or covering that IMA during aortic repair can infarct the bowel, so the IMA may need to be reimplanted. Which vein marks the carotid bifurcation during an endarterectomy? The common facial vein, which crosses the bifurcation on its way into the internal jugular. It is ligated and divided to expose the bifurcation. The hypoglossal nerve usually crosses the internal and external carotid a short distance above, so dissection heading up the neck from there needs care."},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Physiology and hemodynamics","u":"questions.html#physiology-q","x":"Physiology and hemodynamics The why behind the numbers. These come up on rounds when a duplex or an ABI is being discussed. Background is in Foundations . Show all answers Hide all How does Poiseuille's law explain why a stenosis matters? Flow is proportional to the fourth power of the radius and inversely proportional to length and viscosity. A small reduction in lumen diameter causes a large drop in flow, and a slightly larger conduit improves flow a great deal. Halving the radius cuts flow to about one-sixteenth, while doubling the length only halves it, which is why radius dominates. At what point does a stenosis become hemodynamically significant? At roughly a 50 percent reduction in diameter, which is about a 75 percent reduction in cross-sectional area. That is where a measurable pressure and flow gradient appears at rest. With exercise, when distal resistance falls and flow rises, lesser stenoses can become significant, which is why a claudicant can have a normal resting ABI. Use Laplace's law to explain aneurysm rupture risk. Wall tension is proportional to pressure times radius. As an aneurysm enlarges, wall stress climbs at the same blood pressure, which raises rupture risk and drives further expansion, a vicious cycle. It is why diameter is the main predictor of rupture and why blood pressure control matters in anyone with an aneurysm. How do you calculate and interpret an ABI? For each leg, divide the higher of the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial systolic pressures by the higher of the two brachial systolic pressures. 1.00 to 1.40 is normal, 0.91 to 0.99 borderline, 0.90 or less diagnostic of PAD, and 0.40 or less severe disease, the range where rest pain and tissue loss are common. Above 1.40 means non-compressible, calcified vessels, so use a toe pressure. CLTI is a clinical diagnosis (rest pain or tissue loss with proven PAD), not an ABI cutoff. The table is in Quick reference . Why can the ABI be falsely normal or high in a diabetic patient? Medial calcific sclerosis stiffens the tibial arteries so the cuff cannot compress them, and the pressure reads artificially high. The same happens in end-stage kidney disease. The digital arteries are usually spared, so the toe pressure and toe-brachial index are more reliable: a TBI below about 0.70 is abnormal, and a toe pressure under 30 mmHg predicts poor wound healing. What do triphasic, biphasic and monophasic Doppler signals tell you? A normal peripheral artery at rest is triphasic: forward flow in systole, brief reversal in early diastole because of high distal resistance, then a small forward component. Loss of the reversal (biphasic) can be normal in older patients or with vasodilation, or can reflect mild disease. A monophasic, dampened signal means significant disease upstream of the probe, even when the signal is loud. Describe the signal at each site rather than writing \"dopplerable\". A patient has classic claudication but a normal resting ABI. How do you make the diagnosis? With an exercise (treadmill) ABI. During exercise, a stenosis that was trivial at rest becomes flow-limiting and the ankle pressure falls. A fall in ABI of more than about 20 percent, or in ankle pressure of more than about 30 mmHg, supports PAD; exact criteria vary by lab. If the test is normal, think about the mimics: neurogenic claudication from spinal stenosis, venous claudication, and hip or knee arthritis. What happens hemodynamically at aortic cross-clamp and unclamp? Clamping raises afterload, so proximal pressure and cardiac work rise, which stresses a diseased heart. Unclamping drops afterload suddenly and washes potassium, acid and other metabolites out of the ischemic lower body, causing declamping hypotension. That is why the clamp is released slowly, one limb at a time, with volume on board and anesthesia warned in advance. How long can skeletal muscle tolerate ischemia? About 4 to 6 hours of warm ischemia before irreversible injury. Nerve is more sensitive, which is why sensory loss comes early and motor loss signals a limb in serious trouble; skin tolerates the longest. This is the clock that makes acute limb ischemia an emergency and lowers the threshold for fasciotomy after revascularization. Why give a beta-blocker before a vasodilator in aortic dissection? To reduce the shear force on the wall (the rate of rise of pressure). A vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia, which increases shear and can propagate the dissection, so heart rate and contractility are controlled first, usually with IV esmolol or labetalol. The 2022 ACC/AHA targets are a heart rate of 60 to 80 and a systolic pressure under 120 mmHg (older teaching said a heart rate under 60), adding a vasodilator such as nicardipine if needed. See Presentations ."},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Clinical and operative","u":"questions.html#clinical-q","x":"Clinical and operative Decision questions: who needs what, and how fast. Each maps to a presentation in Presentations . Show all answers Hide all Embolic versus thrombotic acute limb ischemia: how do you tell them apart? Embolic occlusion is sudden, in a leg with no prior symptoms and normal pulses on the other side, often from atrial fibrillation, and classically lodges at the common femoral bifurcation. Thrombotic occlusion is more gradual, in a patient with known PAD and diseased pulses on both sides, and developed collaterals soften the picture. It matters because an embolus usually goes straight for a Fogarty embolectomy, while a thrombosis on diseased vessels usually needs imaging to plan lysis, endovascular therapy or bypass. The table is in Quick reference . A patient has an immediately threatened limb (Rutherford IIb). What do you do? Give a heparin bolus at once unless contraindicated, call the senior, and revascularize immediately, because a IIb limb is salvageable only with prompt restoration of flow. There is no time for a conventional catheter-directed lytic infusion running over many hours. The options are open embolectomy or thrombectomy, bypass, or rapid endovascular mechanical thrombectomy where the team and suite are ready, and the team should be prepared to do a four-compartment fasciotomy. Category III is irreversible and usually means primary amputation. See On call . When do you repair an asymptomatic AAA? At 5.5 cm in men and 5.0 cm in women (the SVS suggests considering repair in women from 5.0 to 5.4 cm), with rapid growth (1 cm or more in a year; some also use 0.5 cm in six months), or if it becomes symptomatic at any size. Below threshold, the work is surveillance, smoking cessation and cardiovascular risk reduction. Surveillance intervals are in Quick reference . How do you manage a hypotensive patient with a suspected ruptured AAA? If unstable, straight to the operating room or hybrid suite with no CT. Use permissive hypotension, keeping the systolic only high enough to keep the patient mentating (often around 70 to 90 mmHg), because aggressive fluid resuscitation can disrupt the contained rupture and worsens coagulopathy. Activate massive transfusion. If the patient is stable enough for a CTA, use it to decide EVAR suitability; endovascular repair is preferred when the anatomy allows, and an aortic occlusion balloon can buy time. The SVS suggests a door-to-intervention time under 90 minutes. Which carotid patients benefit most from intervention? Symptomatic patients (stroke, TIA or amaurosis in the matching territory) with 70 to 99 percent stenosis benefit clearly, and benefit is greatest when they are treated within about two weeks of the event. Symptomatic 50 to 69 percent is a moderate, individualized benefit, and below 50 percent is managed medically. Asymptomatic high-grade disease is treated more selectively: everyone gets intensive medical therapy, and intervention is reserved for selected patients with a good life expectancy and a low procedural risk. CREST-2 (2025) has sharpened this debate; see Landmark trials . What is the best conduit for an infrainguinal bypass? The patient's own great saphenous vein, ideally a single segment, especially for below-knee and tibial targets. When it is absent or inadequate, the alternatives are the other leg's saphenous vein, arm vein or spliced vein. Prosthetic grafts are acceptable for an above-knee femoropopliteal bypass but do poorly below the knee. In BEST-CLI, bypass with an adequate single-segment saphenous vein beat endovascular therapy for CLTI, which is why vein mapping is part of the workup. See Conduits and access . What is CLTI, and how do you stage the threatened limb? Chronic limb-threatening ischemia is ischemic rest pain, or tissue loss (a non-healing ulcer or gangrene), present for more than two weeks in a patient with objectively confirmed PAD. The Global Vascular Guidelines stage the limb with WIfI (wound, ischemia and foot infection, each graded 0 to 3), which estimates amputation risk and the likely benefit of revascularization, and describe the arterial anatomy with GLASS to help choose between bypass and endovascular therapy. See WIfI at a glance . Which type B aortic dissections need intervention? Complicated ones: rupture or impending rupture, or malperfusion of the gut, kidneys, spinal cord or a limb. Most definitions add pain or hypertension that persists despite maximal medical therapy, and rapid expansion. These get TEVAR to cover the entry tear. Uncomplicated type B dissection is treated with anti-impulse therapy and close imaging, and TEVAR is considered when high-risk features are present, such as a large aortic or false-lumen diameter. Type A is a cardiac surgical emergency. A patient in atrial fibrillation has severe abdominal pain but a soft abdomen. What are you worried about, and what are the first moves? Acute mesenteric ischemia, most often an SMA embolus. Pain out of proportion to the exam is the classic early picture; peritonitis, acidosis and a high lactate come late, and a normal lactate early on should not reassure you. Get a CTA immediately, start heparin, resuscitate, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, and arrange emergent revascularization (open embolectomy or endovascular) with laparotomy to assess the bowel and resect what is dead. A planned second look is common. See Presentations . When do you suspect compartment syndrome after revascularization, and what do you do? Suspect it after any prolonged ischemic time, especially a IIb limb, or after combined arterial and venous injury. Pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch are the early signs, with a tense compartment and numbness in the first web space; loss of pulses is late and unreliable. Compartment pressures help when the patient cannot be examined: a perfusion gradient (diastolic pressure minus compartment pressure) under 30 mmHg supports the diagnosis. Treatment is prompt four-compartment fasciotomy, "},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Endovascular and devices","u":"questions.html#endo-q","x":"Endovascular and devices The questions you will hear in the angiography suite, about sizing, wires, contrast and access. The background is in Endovascular and Wires, catheters and devices . Show all answers Hide all What do French and inches measure in endovascular work? French measures diameter in thirds of a millimeter, so 1 Fr is about 0.33 mm and 3 Fr is about 1 mm. A catheter is sized by its outer diameter, but a sheath is sized by its inner diameter (the largest device it accepts), so the hole in the artery is roughly 2 Fr larger than the number on the sheath. Wires are sized in inches by diameter: 0.035 is the workhorse, 0.018 and 0.014 are for small vessels and tibials. Balloon and stent diameters and lengths are in millimeters, and catheter and wire lengths in centimeters. Where should a common femoral puncture be, and what goes wrong with a high or low stick? Over the medial third of the femoral head, below the inguinal ligament and above the femoral bifurcation, so the artery can be compressed against bone afterwards. Find the femoral head with fluoroscopy and see the bifurcation and the front wall with ultrasound; the skin crease is unreliable, especially in obese patients. On angiography, a puncture above the lowest curve of the inferior epigastric artery is too high. High stick. Enters the external iliac above the ligament, where bleeding cannot be compressed and tracks into the retroperitoneum. Low stick. Enters the SFA or profunda, which is smaller and has no bony backstop, so pseudoaneurysm, arteriovenous fistula and thrombosis are more likely. Hypotension and back pain after a femoral puncture, with a normal-looking groin. What is it? Retroperitoneal hemorrhage, usually from a high stick above the inguinal ligament or a back-wall puncture. Resuscitate, stop or reverse anticoagulation, send a hemoglobin and a type and crossmatch, and get a CT if the patient is stable enough. Many settle with resuscitation and reversal; ongoing instability goes for endovascular control (a covered stent or balloon tamponade) or open repair. It is occult and can be fatal, so a soft groin should not reassure you. See On call . What are a Bentson, a Glidewire and a Rosen for? Bentson. A 0.035 starting wire with a long, very floppy tip. The classic first wire through the needle or sheath and up the iliacs, because it is unlikely to raise a dissection. Glidewire. A hydrophilic, slippery wire, often with an angled tip, for steering through tortuous vessels, selecting branches and crossing tight stenoses and occlusions. It dissects easily, and it should not be pulled back through a metal access needle, which can shear it. Rosen. A 0.035 wire with a tight J tip and a moderately stiff shaft, a workhorse for exchanging and delivering sheaths, balloons and stents with less risk of the tip perforating. Large devices such as an EVAR graft go up a stiffer wire such as an Amplatz or Lunderquist. The usual sequence is to cross with a steerable wire, then exchange through a catheter for a supportive wire before advancing a device. See Wires . Why a self-expanding stent in the SFA rather than balloon-expandable? The SFA bends, twists and is compressed with walking and in the adductor canal. A nitinol self-expanding stent springs back after deformation, whereas a balloon-expandable stent can be permanently crushed or kinked. Balloon-expandable stents are chosen where precise placement and high radial force matter in a vessel that does not flex, such as ostial renal and common iliac lesions. The same logic is why stents are avoided across the common femoral and the popliteal behind the knee when possible. How do you image the arterial tree in a patient with a serious contrast allergy or poor kidneys? Carbon dioxide angiography below the diaphragm, since CO2 is neither nephrotoxic nor allergenic. It must not be used above the diaphragm (the cerebral, coronary or thoracic arterial circulation) because gas embolism can cause stroke or myocardial ischemia. Its other limits: Lower image quality, especially in small tibial and pedal vessels. The gas floats, so non-dependent vessels fill better than dependent ones. It needs a closed, dedicated delivery system to keep room air out. Gas can become trapped in a large aneurysm sac or the mesenteric vessels and cause ischemia. Many teams use CO2 for the inflow and a small volume of diluted iodinated contrast for the tibial runoff. How do you protect the kidneys of a patient with CKD who needs an angiogram? Recognize the risk from the eGFR, then hydrate with isotonic saline before and after unless the patient is volume overloaded. Use the least contrast that answers the question: diluted contrast, planned runs, CO2 below the diaphragm, and duplex or non-contrast imaging to plan beforehand. Avoid nephrotoxins such as NSAIDs, manage metformin per local protocol, and check the creatinine afterwards. N-acetylcysteine and sodium bicarbonate gave no benefit over saline in the PRESERVE trial and are not recommended. What are the ways to reduce radiation dose in the angiography suite? Time, distance and shielding, under the principle of ALARA (as low as reasonably achievable). Minimize fluoroscopy time with low-dose pulsed fluoroscopy and low frame-rate runs. Step back, because scatter falls with the square of the distance. Wear a lead apron, thyroid shield and eye protection, and use the ceiling and table shields. Collimate tightly, keep the image detector close to the patient, limit magnification and steep angles, and stand on the detector side of the C-arm, since scatter is highest on the tube side. See Endovascular . Which endoleak is most common, and which ones must be treated? Type II (retrograde filling of the sac from a lumbar artery or the IMA) is the most common and is usually observed; it is treated if the sac keeps growing, with thresholds that vary (commonly 5 to 10 mm). Types I (a failed seal at an attachment site) and III (component separation or a fabric tear) are"},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Venous","u":"questions.html#venous-q","x":"Venous Venous consults are common and the questions are predictable: the extent of the clot, whether anticoagulation is enough, and when to do more. See Presentations for the full story. Show all answers Hide all A young woman has an extensive left iliofemoral DVT. What anatomic problem do you look for, and how is it handled? May-Thurner syndrome (iliac vein compression): the left common iliac vein is compressed between the overlying right common iliac artery and the lumbar spine, which is one reason iliofemoral DVT is more common on the left. Anticoagulate. In selected patients with severe symptoms and a low bleeding risk, catheter-directed thrombus removal clears the clot, after which venography and intravascular ultrasound show the compression and a dedicated venous stent treats it. Compression is common on imaging in people without symptoms and is not treated by itself. What does CEAP stand for, and what are the clinical classes? Clinical, etiologic (primary, secondary or congenital), anatomic (superficial, deep or perforator) and pathophysiologic (reflux, obstruction or both). The clinical classes are C0 nothing visible, C1 telangiectasias or reticular veins, C2 varicose veins, C3 edema, C4 skin changes, C5 healed ulcer and C6 active ulcer. The 2020 revision split C4 into C4a (pigmentation or eczema), C4b (lipodermatosclerosis or atrophie blanche) and C4c (corona phlebectatica), and added \"r\" for recurrent varicose veins or ulcers (C2r, C6r). The table is in Quick reference . What is phlegmasia cerulea dolens, and how is it managed? Massive iliofemoral DVT that blocks almost all venous outflow from the leg, producing a painful, swollen, blue leg. Phlegmasia alba dolens is the earlier, pale stage. Unrelieved, the venous hypertension raises compartment pressures, compromises arterial inflow and can progress to venous gangrene; an underlying malignancy is common. Start therapeutic heparin immediately, elevate the leg and resuscitate, then arrange urgent thrombus removal (catheter-directed pharmacomechanical thrombectomy or lysis, or open venous thrombectomy), with fasciotomy for compartment syndrome. It threatens both the limb and the patient's life. When is an IVC filter indicated? The clear indication is acute proximal DVT or PE in a patient who cannot be anticoagulated (active bleeding, recent intracranial hemorrhage, imminent major surgery) or who has a complication that forces anticoagulation to stop. A filter does not treat the clot, so start anticoagulation as soon as it is safe. Adding a filter to anticoagulation did not reduce recurrent PE in PREPIC2, so routine use alongside anticoagulation is not recommended, and apparent failure of anticoagulation should first prompt a check of adherence and dosing. If a retrievable filter goes in, document a retrieval plan and date, because filters left long term cause caval thrombosis, strut penetration and fracture. A patient has superficial thrombophlebitis of the great saphenous vein in the thigh. When does it become a DVT problem? Get a duplex first, because superficial vein thrombosis often coexists with DVT and the scan defines the length and the distance from the saphenofemoral junction. Short segment, away from the junction. Warm compresses, an NSAID and compression. 5 cm or longer. Prophylactic-dose anticoagulation, classically fondaparinux 2.5 mg daily for 45 days. Within about 3 cm of the junction. Treated like a DVT with therapeutic anticoagulation, because it can propagate into the common femoral vein. Recommended duration varies by guideline. How do you work up a suspected leg DVT? Estimate the pretest probability with the Wells score. If DVT is unlikely (Wells under 2), a negative high-sensitivity D-dimer rules it out without imaging. If DVT is likely, or the D-dimer is positive, get a duplex: a whole-leg study, or a proximal compression study repeated in about a week if negative. A non-compressible vein is the key finding. D-dimer is much less useful after surgery and in cancer, pregnancy and old age, because it is so often positive. The score is in Quick reference . How long do you anticoagulate a DVT? At least three months for any proximal DVT. A DVT provoked by a major transient risk factor such as surgery is treated for three months and then stopped. An unprovoked DVT, or one with a persistent risk factor, is treated for three months and then assessed for extended therapy if the bleeding risk allows. Cancer-associated clot is treated as long as the cancer is active. A DOAC is preferred over warfarin for most patients, but warfarin is preferred in antiphospholipid syndrome. Isolated calf DVT can be anticoagulated or followed with serial duplex for propagation. Who should be considered for catheter-directed thrombus removal for an acute DVT? Not most patients. In ATTRACT, adding pharmacomechanical catheter-directed thrombolysis to anticoagulation for proximal DVT did not prevent post-thrombotic syndrome overall and increased major bleeding, though it reduced the severity of the syndrome. It is reserved for selected patients: phlegmasia or a threatened limb, and acute (roughly under two weeks) iliofemoral DVT with severe symptoms, good functional status and a low bleeding risk. Femoropopliteal DVT alone is treated with anticoagulation. A young swimmer has a swollen, blue, heavy arm. What is it, and what is the plan? Paget-Schroetter syndrome: effort thrombosis of the subclavian and axillary vein from compression in the costoclavicular space (venous thoracic outlet syndrome). Duplex or venography confirms it. Management is anticoagulation, usually early catheter-directed thrombolysis, then decompression of the thoracic outlet by first rib resection, with timing that varies by surgeon and venography afterwards to decide whether the vein needs angioplasty. Stenting the vein before decompression is avoided because the stent gets crushed."},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Post-op and ICU","u":"questions.html#icu-q","x":"Post-op and ICU The complications that turn up on the first nights after surgery, in the CTV ICU and on the floor. Each has a recognizable pattern and a first move you should know before you are paged. See The CTV ICU and On call and cross-cover . Show all answers Hide all What are the thresholds for intra-abdominal hypertension and abdominal compartment syndrome, and when do you worry about it? Intra-abdominal hypertension is a sustained intra-abdominal pressure of 12 mmHg or more. Abdominal compartment syndrome is a sustained pressure above 20 mmHg with new organ dysfunction. Pressure is measured through the bladder, supine, at end-expiration, zeroed at the mid-axillary line, after instilling no more than 25 mL of saline. On a vascular service the classic setting is a ruptured AAA repair, open or endovascular, with massive resuscitation and a retroperitoneal hematoma. Watch for a tense abdomen, rising peak airway pressures, falling urine output and hypotension. Sedation, paralysis and gastric and rectal decompression buy little time; the definitive treatment is decompressive laparotomy. Bloody diarrhea on day two after open AAA repair. What do you suspect and how do you confirm? Ischemic colitis, usually of the sigmoid and left colon, from loss of IMA flow on top of poor internal iliac and collateral flow and perioperative hypotension. Any diarrhea in the first days, especially bloody, or a rising lactate, fever, leukocytosis or unexplained acidosis, should prompt a bedside flexible sigmoidoscopy. Mucosal ischemia is managed with bowel rest, fluids, antibiotics and repeat endoscopy; transmural necrosis or peritonitis needs urgent colectomy, usually with an end colostomy. It is more common after rupture than elective repair and can follow EVAR. Transmural necrosis is often lethal, so the threshold to scope is low. See On call . A patient wakes up after TEVAR with new leg weakness. What do you do? Treat it as spinal cord ischemia and act within minutes, because it can be reversed. Raise the mean arterial pressure with fluids and vasopressors (commonly to 90 mmHg or higher). Place a lumbar CSF drain if there is none, or lower the drain level, to bring CSF pressure down (often to 10 mmHg or less, within the hourly drainage limit). Transfuse to keep the hemoglobin up, optimize oxygenation and cardiac output, and do frequent neurologic checks. If a drain was recently placed, check coagulation and consider spinal imaging for a hematoma. Every target here is set per local protocol; document the time of onset and every change. See Spinal drains . Two hours after a carotid endarterectomy, the neck is swelling and the voice is changing. What do you do? Assume an expanding hematoma that threatens the airway. Call for help at once (the senior, anesthesia and the OR), sit the patient up, give oxygen, and assess stridor and work of breathing. If the airway is failing and cannot be secured immediately, open the incision at the bedside, including the platysma closure, to release the hematoma, then go to the OR to find the bleeding. Intubation can be very difficult because of tracheal deviation and laryngeal edema, so the most experienced airway provider should do it. A small, stable hematoma is observed with blood pressure control. See On call . A few days after endarterectomy for a tight stenosis, the patient has a severe headache on the operated side and is hypertensive. What is the concern? Cerebral hyperperfusion syndrome. A hemisphere that lost its autoregulation behind a tight stenosis suddenly receives full pressure. It presents with ipsilateral headache, often frontal or behind the eye, then seizures, focal deficits and intracerebral hemorrhage, usually within the first week after CEA or stenting. Risk factors are a high-grade stenosis, poor collateral reserve (such as a contralateral occlusion), recent stroke and perioperative hypertension. Get a head CT to exclude hemorrhage, control blood pressure tightly to the target the team sets, and treat seizures. Prevention is careful blood pressure control after surgery. What happens metabolically when you restore flow to a limb that has been ischemic for hours? Reperfusion releases potassium, acid, myoglobin and inflammatory mediators from damaged muscle. Expect hyperkalemia and acidosis (with a risk of arrhythmia or arrest as flow returns), rhabdomyolysis with pigment kidney injury, compartment syndrome and sometimes lung injury. Check potassium, a blood gas, CK and urine output. Treat hyperkalemia with IV calcium to stabilize the myocardium, insulin with dextrose and an inhaled beta-agonist, bicarbonate for severe acidosis and dialysis if refractory, and give generous IV fluid to keep urine output up. Warn anesthesia before flow is restored. A heparinized patient's platelets fall on day 7 and a graft clots. What is going on? Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, an antibody against heparin and platelet factor 4 complexes that activates platelets, so it causes thrombosis rather than bleeding. The typical picture is a fall of more than 50 percent, 5 to 10 days after starting heparin (sooner with heparin exposure in the past few months). Score it with the 4T score. If HIT is likely: Stop all heparin, including flushes and heparin-bonded lines. Start a non-heparin anticoagulant, such as argatroban or bivalirudin. Send the PF4 antibody immunoassay, confirmed with a functional test such as the serotonin-release assay. Avoid routine platelet transfusion, do not start warfarin until the platelets recover, and record HIT as an allergy. The 4T score is in Quick reference . What is the most common cause of death after vascular surgery? Cardiac events, especially myocardial infarction, because the vascular patient almost always has coronary disease too. Perioperative MI is often silent, showing up as hypotension, a new arrhythmia, heart failure or simply a rising troponin, so many centers check troponins after major vascular surgery in high-risk patients. Continu"},{"p":"Questions you'll be asked","t":"Classic one-liners","u":"questions.html#oneliners","x":"Classic one-liners Trigger and answer: the single-fact associations that come fast and are expected to be reflexive. Each answer should take you one breath. Show all answers Hide all Arterial and embolic Most common source of an arterial embolus? The heart, usually atrial fibrillation; mural thrombus after an MI is next. Most common site an embolus lodges in the leg? The common femoral bifurcation. The 6 Ps of acute limb ischemia? Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia. Paresthesia and paralysis are the ominous late signs. Leriche syndrome? Buttock and thigh claudication, erectile dysfunction and absent femoral pulses, from aortoiliac occlusive disease. Buerger's disease? Thromboangiitis obliterans, an inflammatory occlusion of small and medium arteries in young smokers, classically men. The cure is absolute cessation of all tobacco. Strongest modifiable risk factor for AAA, and the single best PAD intervention? Smoking, and smoking cessation. Subclavian steal? Proximal subclavian stenosis or occlusion with reversed flow down the vertebral artery, more often on the left. Look for unequal arm pressures, and arm claudication or vertebrobasilar symptoms with arm exercise. Amaurosis fugax? Transient monocular blindness, \"a shade coming down\", from a retinal artery embolus. The ipsilateral carotid is now symptomatic. Aorta and visceral Who gets screened for AAA? A one-time ultrasound in men 65 to 75 who have ever smoked (USPSTF). The SVS also suggests screening women 65 to 75 who have smoked, and first-degree relatives of patients with an AAA. Pain out of proportion to the exam? Acute mesenteric ischemia until proven otherwise. Why does an SMA embolus spare the proximal jejunum? Because it lodges a few centimeters beyond the origin, distal to the first jejunal branches. A thrombosis at the origin takes the whole midgut. Chronic mesenteric ischemia triad? Postprandial pain, food fear and weight loss. Median arcuate ligament syndrome? Compression of the celiac artery by the median arcuate ligament of the diaphragm. Treat by surgical release, not a stent alone, which gets crushed. Most common peripheral aneurysm, and its danger? Popliteal. It thromboses and embolizes rather than ruptures, and it is often bilateral and associated with an AAA, so image the other knee and the aorta. Most common visceral artery aneurysm, and the pregnancy caveat? Splenic artery. It can rupture in pregnancy, so it is treated at any size in a woman of childbearing potential. Venous and upper limb Phlegmasia cerulea dolens? Massive iliofemoral DVT with a swollen, painful, blue leg. A limb-threatening emergency. May-Thurner syndrome? The left common iliac vein compressed by the right common iliac artery. Paget-Schroetter syndrome? Effort thrombosis of the subclavian vein in a young, active arm. Venous ulcer location versus arterial ulcer location? Medial malleolus (the gaiter area) versus the toes, heel and pressure points. Cornerstone treatment of a venous versus an arterial ulcer? Compression versus revascularization. Never compress an ischemic leg; check the ABI first. Access and drugs The Rule of 6s? A mature fistula by about 6 weeks: flow over 600 mL/min, diameter over 6 mm, depth under 6 mm, and about 6 cm of straight vein to cannulate. What reverses heparin, and at what dose? Protamine, roughly 1 mg per 100 units of heparin still active. Give it slowly because of hypotension and anaphylaxis. What reverses dabigatran, and what reverses the factor Xa inhibitors? Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran. Andexanet alfa or four-factor PCC is used for apixaban and rivaroxaban, depending on local protocol and availability. Intraoperative heparin dose before clamping? About 80 to 100 units per kg IV, with an activated clotting time above roughly 250 seconds before the clamp goes on."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Quick reference","u":"reference.html","x":"Every table, classification and number worth memorizing, on one page and built to be screenshotted on rounds. Each section points back to the chapter that explains it, because a number is easier to keep when you know where it comes from."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Perfusion and chronic staging","u":"reference.html#staging","x":"Perfusion and chronic staging How to grade a leg with chronic arterial disease. Explained in Foundations and Presentations . Ankle-brachial index ABI Interpretation > 1.40 Non-compressible, calcified vessels (diabetes, kidney failure); use toe pressures 1.00–1.40 Normal 0.91–0.99 Borderline 0.41–0.90 Mild to moderate PAD (the usual claudication range) ≤ 0.40 Severe PAD; rest pain and tissue loss are common TBI < 0.70 Abnormal toe-brachial index; use it when the ABI is unreliable A toe pressure (or TcPO2) under 30 mmHg predicts poor wound healing. When the resting ABI is normal but the story is claudication, an exercise ABI that falls by more than about 20 percent supports PAD. Fontaine and Rutherford (chronic ischemia) Fontaine Rutherford Clinical I 0 Asymptomatic IIa 1 Mild claudication (walks more than about 200 m) IIb 2–3 Moderate to severe claudication (under about 200 m) III 4 Ischemic rest pain IV 5–6 Tissue loss: minor (5, ulcer or focal gangrene) or major (6, beyond the transmetatarsal level) Rutherford 4 to 6 (Fontaine III and IV) is chronic limb-threatening ischemia, staged with WIfI below."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"WIfI at a glance","u":"reference.html#wifi-ref","x":"WIfI at a glance The SVS threatened limb classification used by the Global Vascular Guidelines to stage CLTI. Grade wound, ischemia and foot infection 0 to 3 each; the combination maps to clinical stages 1 to 4, which estimate the one-year amputation risk and the likely benefit of revascularization. Use a WIfI chart or calculator for the stage. Explained in Presentations . W: wound Grade Wound 0 No ulcer and no gangrene (ischemic rest pain only) 1 Small, shallow ulcer on the distal leg or foot; no exposed bone unless limited to the distal phalanx; no gangrene 2 Deeper ulcer with exposed bone, joint or tendon, not involving the heel, or a shallow heel ulcer without calcaneal involvement; gangrene limited to the digits 3 Extensive deep ulcer of the forefoot or midfoot, or a deep full-thickness heel ulcer with or without calcaneal involvement; extensive gangrene of the forefoot or midfoot, or full-thickness heel necrosis I: ischemia Grade ABI Ankle pressure Toe pressure or TcPO2 0 ≥ 0.80 > 100 mmHg ≥ 60 mmHg 1 0.60–0.79 70–100 mmHg 40–59 mmHg 2 0.40–0.59 50–70 mmHg 30–39 mmHg 3 ≤ 0.39 < 50 mmHg < 30 mmHg When the vessels are calcified and the ABI or ankle pressure is unreliable, grade ischemia by toe pressure or TcPO2. fI: foot infection Grade Foot infection 0 No signs of infection 1 Local infection of skin and subcutaneous tissue only; erythema 0.5 to 2 cm around the wound 2 Local infection with erythema over 2 cm, or involving deeper structures (abscess, osteomyelitis, septic arthritis, fasciitis); no systemic signs 3 Local infection with systemic inflammatory signs: two or more of temperature over 38 or under 36 °C, heart rate over 90, respiratory rate over 20 (or PaCO2 under 32 mmHg), white count over 12,000 or under 4,000 (or 10 percent bands) Infection is present when there are at least two of: local swelling or induration, erythema, tenderness or pain, warmth, and purulent discharge. Arterial anatomy is staged separately with GLASS, which grades the femoropopliteal and infrapopliteal disease along the target path."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Acute limb ischemia","u":"reference.html#ali-ref","x":"Acute limb ischemia Emergency Grade the limb first, because the category decides how fast you move. Explained in Presentations and On call . Rutherford categories of acute limb ischemia Category Sensory and motor loss Doppler (artery / vein) Implication I, viable None; none Audible / audible Not immediately threatened; time to image IIa, marginally threatened Minimal (toes) or none; none Often inaudible / audible Salvageable if treated promptly IIb, immediately threatened More than toes, with rest pain; mild to moderate weakness Usually inaudible / audible Salvageable only with immediate revascularization III, irreversible Profound, anesthetic; paralysis, rigor Inaudible / inaudible Major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage; primary amputation Embolic versus thrombotic occlusion Feature Embolic Thrombotic Onset Sudden, dramatic More gradual, acute on chronic Source and history Heart (atrial fibrillation, mural thrombus after MI); no prior claudication Plaque or graft; known claudication or PAD Other leg Normal pulses Often diminished Classic site Common femoral bifurcation Site of prior stenosis or graft Collaterals Few (no time to form) Often well developed Usual treatment Fogarty embolectomy Imaging, then lysis, endovascular therapy or bypass Mnemonic: the 6 Ps Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis, poikilothermia. Paresthesia and paralysis are the late, ominous signs. Start heparin the moment acute limb ischemia is suspected, unless it is contraindicated."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Aorta and carotid","u":"reference.html#aorta-carotid","x":"Aorta and carotid Repair thresholds, dissection and carotid revascularization. Explained in Presentations (aneurysm, dissection and carotid ). Aneurysm repair thresholds Trigger Threshold Aneurysm definition (infrarenal) ≥ 3.0 cm, or 1.5 times the expected normal diameter AAA, men ≥ 5.5 cm AAA, women ≥ 5.0 cm (SVS: consider from 5.0 to 5.4 cm) Rapid growth ≥ 1 cm in a year (some also use 0.5 cm in 6 months) Symptomatic or ruptured Any size Common iliac aneurysm ≥ 3.5 cm Popliteal aneurysm ≥ 2.0 cm, or smaller with mural thrombus Splenic artery aneurysm ≥ 3 cm, growing or symptomatic; any size in a woman of childbearing potential Aortic dissection Stanford DeBakey Involves Default A I Ascending, arch and descending Emergency surgery A II Ascending only Emergency surgery B III Descending only, beyond the left subclavian Anti-impulse therapy first; TEVAR if complicated Complicated type B means rupture or malperfusion; most definitions add refractory pain or hypertension and rapid expansion. Anti-impulse targets (2022 ACC/AHA) are a heart rate of 60 to 80 and a systolic pressure under 120 mmHg, beta-blocker first; older teaching used a heart rate under 60. The SVS/STS reporting standards time a dissection as hyperacute (under 24 hours), acute (1 to 14 days), subacute (15 to 90 days) or chronic, and describe its extent using the aortic zones below. Carotid revascularization Group Stenosis Recommendation Symptomatic 70–99% Clear benefit; treat within about 2 weeks of the event Symptomatic 50–69% Moderate, individualized benefit Symptomatic < 50% Medical therapy Asymptomatic ≥ 60–70% Selective: low procedural risk and good life expectancy; intensive medical therapy for everyone Intervention assumes a perioperative stroke and death rate under about 6 percent for symptomatic and 3 percent for asymptomatic patients. Duplex grading is in Duplex criteria ; CREST-2 is in Landmark trials ."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Aortic zones and endoleaks","u":"reference.html#aortic-ref","x":"Aortic zones, surveillance and endoleaks The vocabulary of TEVAR planning, aneurysm follow-up and the post-EVAR scan. Explained in Endovascular and Open operations . Arch landing zones (Ishimaru) Zone Location Landing here means 0 Ascending aorta, up to and including the innominate origin All arch branches covered; needs debranching or a branched or total arch repair 1 Between the innominate and left common carotid, covering the left carotid origin Left carotid (and usually left subclavian) revascularization 2 Between the left common carotid and left subclavian, covering the left subclavian origin Left subclavian covered; usually revascularized electively 3 Proximal descending thoracic aorta just beyond the left subclavian No arch branch covered 4 Descending thoracic aorta beyond zone 3 No arch branch covered Left subclavian revascularization is essential when the left internal mammary feeds a coronary graft, the left vertebral is dominant, or there is a left arm dialysis access. The SVS/STS reporting standards continue the numbering down the aorta to the external iliac (zones 5 to 11). AAA surveillance intervals (SVS 2018) Diameter Repeat imaging 2.5–2.9 cm Rescreen in about 10 years 3.0–3.9 cm Every 3 years 4.0–4.9 cm Every 12 months 5.0–5.4 cm Every 6 months ESVS intervals differ slightly; follow the local protocol. After EVAR, the SVS suggests imaging in the first month and at 12 months, then yearly duplex if there is no endoleak and the sac is stable. Endoleaks after EVAR Type Source Action I Seal failure at an attachment site: Ia proximal, Ib distal, Ic iliac occluder Treat promptly; the sac is pressurized II Back-filling from lumbar arteries or the IMA; the most common Observe; treat if the sac keeps growing III Component separation or fabric defect Treat promptly; the sac is pressurized IV Graft fabric porosity, seen at completion Self-limited once anticoagulation wears off V Endotension: sac growth with no visible leak Closer surveillance; reintervene if enlarging"},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Duplex criteria","u":"reference.html#duplex-ref","x":"Duplex criteria Velocity thresholds for the two duplex reports you will read most. Criteria vary by lab and by machine, and accredited labs validate their own, so quote the category on the report and check your lab's criteria before quoting a number. Internal carotid stenosis (SRU consensus, 2003) Stenosis ICA peak systolic velocity Supporting findings Normal < 125 cm/s No visible plaque; ICA/CCA ratio < 2.0; EDV < 40 cm/s < 50% < 125 cm/s Visible plaque; ratio < 2.0; EDV < 40 cm/s 50–69% 125–230 cm/s Ratio 2.0–4.0; EDV 40–100 cm/s ≥ 70%, short of near occlusion > 230 cm/s Ratio > 4.0; EDV > 100 cm/s Near occlusion High, low or undetectable Markedly narrowed lumen on color Doppler; ratio and EDV unreliable Occlusion Undetectable No patent lumen Peak systolic velocity and visible plaque are the primary criteria; the ratio and end-diastolic velocity (EDV) support them. A contralateral occlusion raises velocities on the open side, and stented arteries need their own, higher thresholds. Infrainguinal vein graft surveillance Category Duplex findings Action Normal PSV < 180 cm/s, velocity ratio < 2.0, mid-graft velocity > 45 cm/s Routine surveillance Moderate stenosis PSV 180–300 cm/s, ratio > 2.0 Repeat duplex in weeks; intervene if progressing High risk PSV > 300 cm/s, ratio > 3.5, mid-graft velocity < 45 cm/s, or ABI drop > 0.15 Angiography and repair before the graft occludes The velocity ratio compares the peak systolic velocity at the lesion with the segment just upstream. Vein graft surveillance commonly runs at a baseline study soon after surgery, then about 3, 6 and 12 months, then yearly; a failing graft is far easier to save than a failed one. In a native artery, a ratio of 2.0 or more generally means a stenosis of 50 percent or more."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Venous and ulcers","u":"reference.html#venous-ref","x":"Venous and ulcers The venous classification and the ulcer comparison. Explained in Presentations and Foundations . CEAP clinical class (2020 revision) Class Finding C0 No visible or palpable venous disease C1 Telangiectasias or reticular veins C2 Varicose veins (C2r if recurrent) C3 Edema C4 Skin changes: C4a pigmentation or eczema; C4b lipodermatosclerosis or atrophie blanche; C4c corona phlebectatica C5 Healed venous ulcer C6 Active venous ulcer (C6r if recurrent) E, A and P add etiology (primary, secondary, congenital), anatomy (superficial, deep, perforator) and pathophysiology (reflux, obstruction). A suffix of s or a marks the limb as symptomatic or asymptomatic. Venous versus arterial ulcer Feature Venous Arterial Location Medial malleolus (gaiter area) Toes, heel, pressure points, lateral malleolus Appearance Shallow, irregular, exudative, granulating Punched-out, dry, pale base Pain Mild; better with elevation Severe; worse with elevation, better dependent Surrounding skin Hemosiderin staining, lipodermatosclerosis, warm Shiny, hairless, cool Pulses Present Diminished or absent Cornerstone Compression, after excluding arterial disease Revascularization Red flag Confirm arterial inflow with an ABI before compressing any leg, because compressing an ischemic limb can cause tissue loss. Full compression is generally used when the ABI is above about 0.8; reduced compression may be used down to about 0.5 under specialist supervision, and compression is avoided below that. Thresholds vary between guidelines."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Scores","u":"reference.html#scores-ref","x":"Scores Three scores you will be asked to calculate on the spot: DVT probability, HIT probability and cardiac risk before surgery. Wells score for DVT Criterion Points Active cancer (treated within 6 months, or palliative) +1 Paralysis, paresis or recent plaster cast of the leg +1 Bedridden 3 days or more, or major surgery within 12 weeks +1 Localized tenderness along the deep veins +1 Entire leg swollen +1 Calf 3 cm or more larger than the other side (10 cm below the tibial tuberosity) +1 Pitting edema confined to the symptomatic leg +1 Collateral superficial veins (not varicose) +1 Previously documented DVT +1 Alternative diagnosis at least as likely −2 A score of 2 or more means DVT is likely: get a duplex. Under 2 means unlikely: a negative high-sensitivity D-dimer rules it out without imaging. 4T score for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia T 2 points 1 point Thrombocytopenia Fall over 50% and nadir 20 or more (×10⁹/L) Fall of 30–50%, or nadir 10–19 Timing Clear onset on days 5–10, or within 1 day if heparin was given in the past 30 days Probably days 5–10 but unclear, onset after day 10, or within 1 day with heparin 30–100 days ago Thrombosis New proven thrombosis, skin necrosis at injection sites, or a systemic reaction to an IV heparin bolus Progressive or recurrent thrombosis, red skin lesions, or suspected but unproven thrombosis Other cause None apparent Possible Score 0 for a T when neither column fits (for example a fall under 30%, a fall before day 4 with no recent heparin, or a definite other cause). Total 0 to 3 is low probability, 4 to 5 intermediate and 6 to 8 high. At intermediate or high probability, stop all heparin, start a non-heparin anticoagulant and send the PF4 immunoassay. Revised Cardiac Risk Index (one point each) Factor Counts when High-risk surgery Intraperitoneal, intrathoracic or suprainguinal vascular Ischemic heart disease Prior MI, positive stress test, angina, nitrate use or pathologic Q waves Heart failure History, or signs on exam or chest film Cerebrovascular disease Prior stroke or TIA Diabetes Treated with insulin Kidney disease Creatinine over 2.0 mg/dL Two or more points means elevated risk, which prompts an assessment of functional capacity, with further testing only if it would change management. The RCRI tends to underestimate risk in vascular surgery patients, and vascular-specific tools exist."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Anticoagulant reversal","u":"reference.html#anticoag-ref","x":"Anticoagulant reversal at a glance What reverses what, for bleeding or an urgent operation. Doses are the commonly taught ranges; the hospital pharmacy protocol governs. Explained in Foundations . Reversal by agent Agent Reversal Practical points Unfractionated heparin Protamine about 1 mg per 100 units given in the last 2 to 3 hours; maximum about 50 mg per dose Give slowly: hypotension, bradycardia and anaphylaxis, more likely with prior protamine, NPH insulin or fish allergy. Recheck ACT or aPTT. Enoxaparin Protamine 1 mg per 1 mg enoxaparin within 8 hours of the dose; 0.5 mg per 1 mg at 8 to 12 hours Only partial reversal of anti-Xa activity Warfarin Vitamin K 5 to 10 mg IV plus 4F-PCC (dosed by INR and weight, or a fixed dose, per protocol) PCC works in minutes, vitamin K over hours; plasma only if PCC is unavailable Apixaban, rivaroxaban Andexanet alfa or 4F-PCC (often about 50 units/kg), per local protocol Choice and availability vary. Time of last dose and renal function decide whether reversal is needed. Dabigatran Idarucizumab 5 g IV Also removed by hemodialysis Argatroban, bivalirudin None; stop the infusion Short half-lives: argatroban about 45 minutes (liver), bivalirudin about 25 minutes (kidney) Alteplase (catheter-directed lysis) Stop the infusion; cryoprecipitate and an antifibrinolytic per protocol Check fibrinogen; stop any heparin running with it Aspirin, P2Y12 inhibitors No specific reversal Platelet transfusion only for life-threatening bleeding or urgent surgery, per protocol; it works poorly against ticagrelor 4F-PCC is four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate. Confirm locally Which reversal agents are stocked, how PCC is dosed, and who must approve andexanet or idarucizumab vary by hospital. Check the pharmacy protocol in PowerChart before you need it at 2 a.m. Resident tip Before you call about reversal, know five things: the drug, the dose, the time of the last dose, the creatinine clearance and where the bleeding is. Those decide whether reversal is needed at all, and they are the first questions the senior and the pharmacist will ask."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Conduits and access","u":"reference.html#conduits-ref","x":"Conduits and access The defaults worth stating without hesitation. Explained in Open operations (bypass and dialysis access ). Bypass conduit, in order of preference Conduit When and why Ipsilateral great saphenous vein First choice for infrainguinal bypass, especially below the knee; single segment, generally at least about 3 mm; reversed, non-reversed or in situ Contralateral great saphenous vein Next, unless the other leg is likely to need its own bypass Arm vein, small saphenous or spliced vein When the saphenous vein is absent or inadequate; spliced vein still beats prosthetic below the knee Prosthetic (PTFE, Dacron) Acceptable above the knee and for aortic and iliac reconstruction; poor patency to below-knee and tibial targets Dialysis access, in order of preference Access Notes Radiocephalic fistula (Brescia-Cimino) Wrist; non-dominant arm and most distal site first Brachiocephalic fistula Elbow; when the forearm vessels are unsuitable Brachiobasilic fistula with transposition The deep basilic vein is brought to the surface, in one or two stages Arteriovenous graft When no vein is suitable; usable sooner (about 2 to 3 weeks, or within days for early-cannulation grafts) but lower long-term patency and more infection Tunneled catheter Last resort or bridge; highest infection and central venous stenosis risk Fistula first, with a nuance: an autogenous fistula has the best long-term patency and lowest infection rate, but the 2019 KDOQI guideline frames access as an individualized life plan (the right access for the right patient at the right time), so a graft can be the better choice for some. Typical mapping minimums are a vein of about 2.5 mm and an artery of about 2 mm, though thresholds vary. Save the veins: no IVs, blood draws or PICCs in the non-dominant arm of a patient with advanced kidney disease. Mnemonic: the Rule of 6s A fistula is ready by about 6 weeks: flow over 600 mL/min, diameter over 6 mm, depth under 6 mm, and about 6 cm of straight vein to cannulate. Other defaults Decision Default answer CEA closure Patch angioplasty over primary closure, to lower restenosis Amputation level for walking Below-knee over above-knee whenever it will heal Acute embolic occlusion Fogarty balloon embolectomy"},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Landmark trials","u":"reference.html#trials-ref","x":"Landmark trials in one line each The headline result of each trial you will hear named on rounds. These are summaries for orientation; the details, subgroups and limitations matter when you apply them. Carotid Trial Headline NASCET (1991, 1998) Symptomatic stenosis: endarterectomy sharply reduced ipsilateral stroke at 70–99% (about 26% to 9% at 2 years), gave a modest benefit at 50–69%, and none below 50%. ACAS (1995) Asymptomatic stenosis of 60% or more: endarterectomy roughly halved the projected 5-year ipsilateral stroke risk (about 11% to 5%), with very low perioperative risk. ACST-1 (2004, 2010) Asymptomatic severe stenosis: immediate endarterectomy roughly halved 5-year stroke risk compared with deferral, and the benefit lasted to 10 years in patients under 75. CREST (2010) Stenting versus endarterectomy: similar overall composite outcome; more periprocedural strokes with stenting, more MIs with endarterectomy, and older patients did better with endarterectomy. CREST-2 (2025) Asymptomatic stenosis of 70% or more, against intensive medical therapy alone: adding stenting lowered the rate of stroke or death; adding endarterectomy did not show a statistically significant benefit. Event rates were low in every arm. Aneurysm Trial Headline EVAR-1 (2004 to 2016) AAA of 5.5 cm or more, fit for open repair: EVAR had lower 30-day mortality, but the survival advantage was lost within a few years, with more reinterventions and higher late aneurysm-related mortality. DREAM (2004, 2010) AAA of 5 cm or more: EVAR had lower early mortality and complications, with similar long-term survival and more reinterventions. OVER (2009 to 2019) US veterans: EVAR had lower perioperative mortality, and long-term survival was similar to open repair. Peripheral arterial disease and antithrombotics Trial Headline BASIL (2005) Severe limb ischemia: bypass-first and angioplasty-first had similar amputation-free survival; patients who lived beyond 2 years appeared to do better with bypass first. BEST-CLI (2022) CLTI suitable for either approach: with an adequate single-segment great saphenous vein, bypass reduced major adverse limb events or death compared with endovascular therapy; without it, outcomes were similar. BASIL-2 (2023) CLTI needing infrapopliteal revascularization: an endovascular-first strategy had better amputation-free survival than vein bypass first, driven mainly by fewer deaths. COMPASS (2017) Stable coronary or peripheral artery disease: low-dose rivaroxaban (2.5 mg twice daily) plus aspirin reduced cardiovascular death, stroke and MI, and major limb events in PAD, with more major bleeding. VOYAGER PAD (2020) After lower-extremity revascularization: rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily plus aspirin reduced acute limb ischemia, major amputation, MI, ischemic stroke and cardiovascular death combined, with more bleeding. Venous Trial Headline EVRA (2018) Venous leg ulcers with superficial reflux: early endovenous ablation plus compression healed ulcers faster than compression with deferred ablation. ATTRACT (2017) Acute proximal DVT: adding pharmacomechanical catheter-directed thrombolysis to anticoagulation did not prevent post-thrombotic syndrome and increased major bleeding, though it reduced its severity."},{"p":"Quick reference","t":"Numbers to memorize","u":"reference.html#numbers","x":"Numbers worth memorizing The figures that come up fast and are expected to be reflexive. Thresholds that already live in a table above (ABI, aneurysm repair, carotid, the Rule of 6s, protamine) are not repeated here. 65 to 75 AAA screening age: one-time ultrasound in men who have ever smoked (the SVS adds women who have smoked) 50% / 75% Critical stenosis: about 50 percent diameter equals about 75 percent cross-sectional area Flow ∝ r⁴ Poiseuille: halve the radius and flow falls about sixteenfold T ∝ P × r Laplace: wall tension climbs as an aneurysm grows 80–100 U/kg Intraoperative heparin before clamping, then an ACT above roughly 250 seconds 80 U/kg, 18 U/kg/h Common weight-based heparin bolus and infusion for VTE, per local nomogram 4–6 hours Warm ischemia skeletal muscle tolerates before irreversible injury < 30 mmHg Compartment perfusion gradient (diastolic minus compartment pressure) that supports fasciotomy > 20 mmHg Sustained bladder pressure that, with new organ dysfunction, defines abdominal compartment syndrome Day 5–10 Typical onset of HIT after heparin starts; sooner with recent exposure < 90 minutes SVS door-to-intervention target for a ruptured AAA T12 to L4 Celiac T12, SMA L1, renals L1 to L2, IMA L3, bifurcation about L4 2 of 3 Chronic mesenteric ischemia usually needs significant disease in at least two of the celiac, SMA and IMA 1 Fr = 0.33 mm So 3 French is about 1 mm. Catheters are sized by outer diameter, sheaths by inner 0.035 / 0.018 / 0.014 Wire platforms in inches: workhorse, small vessel, tibial"}];
